// Human Body articles, part 4: tuberculosis, dengue, HIV, pain, emotions, pregnancy and birth.
ADDART({id:"tb",t:"Tuberculosis",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🫁",w:"Tuberculosis",r:["lungs","antibiotics","vacc","microbe","pandemic"],
p:[["An ancient disease","Tuberculosis, or TB, is a disease caused by bacteria that usually attacks the lungs."],["Spread through the air","TB spreads when a person with the disease coughs or sneezes and others breathe in the germs."],["Curable","TB can be cured with a course of several medicines taken for months."]],
l:"Tuberculosis, often called TB, is an infectious disease caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It usually affects the lungs but can harm other parts of the body. TB has troubled humans for thousands of years and is still one of the world’s deadliest infectious diseases: in 2024 about 10.7 million people fell ill with it and about 1.2 million died, according to the World Health Organization. Yet TB is preventable and curable, and countries including the Philippines run large programs to find and treat it.",
s:[["How TB spreads","When someone with active TB in the lungs coughs, sneezes, speaks or sings, tiny droplets carrying bacteria float in the air. People nearby may breathe them in. Not everyone infected becomes sick: the immune system often walls the bacteria off, a state called latent TB. About a quarter of the world’s people are thought to carry latent TB, which may become active later, especially if the immune system weakens."],["Symptoms","Active TB of the lungs causes a cough lasting more than two weeks, sometimes with blood, along with chest pain, fever, night sweats, tiredness and weight loss. Because it once seemed to waste people away, TB was called consumption. Doctors diagnose it with chest X-rays, tests of coughed-up mucus called sputum and quick molecular tests."],["Treatment","TB is treated with a combination of antibiotics, usually taken every day for about six months. Taking every dose is important; stopping early can let the bacteria survive and become resistant. Health workers often support patients through treatment so they can finish it."],["Prevention","The BCG vaccine, first used in 1921, protects babies and young children against severe forms of TB and is given at birth in many countries, including the Philippines. Good ventilation, covering coughs, finding and treating cases early and treating latent infection in people at high risk all help stop TB from spreading."]],
k:[["Cause","Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria"],["New cases (2024)","About 10.7 million"],["Deaths (2024)","About 1.2 million"],["Vaccine","BCG, first used 1921"],["World TB Day","24 March"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Signs of TB are found in Egyptian mummies"],["1882","Robert Koch identifies the TB bacterium"],["1921","The BCG vaccine is first given"],["1943","Streptomycin, the first effective TB antibiotic, is discovered"],["1993","WHO declares TB a global emergency"],["2023","The UN holds a high-level meeting on ending TB"]],
s2:[["Robert Koch","On 24 March 1882, the German doctor Robert Koch announced that he had found the bacterium that causes TB. At the time, TB caused about one in seven deaths in Europe. His discovery proved that TB is an infection, not inherited, and it earned him the Nobel Prize in 1905. World TB Day is held on the anniversary of his announcement."],["Sanatoriums","Before antibiotics, there was no cure for TB. Many patients were sent to sanatoriums, hospitals in the mountains or countryside, where they rested and breathed fresh air. Some recovered, but many did not. The Philippine president Manuel L. Quezon died of TB in 1944 while in exile in the United States during World War II."],["Antibiotics change everything","In 1943 scientists in the laboratory of Selman Waksman in the United States discovered streptomycin, the first antibiotic that worked against TB. Other medicines followed, and doctors learned that using several together prevented resistance. Deaths from TB fell sharply in many countries."],["Drug-resistant TB","When TB bacteria survive incomplete treatment, they can become resistant to the main medicines. Multidrug-resistant TB is harder and longer to treat. Newer medicines and shorter treatment plans developed in the 2010s and 2020s have improved cure rates, but drug-resistant TB remains a serious challenge."],["TB around the world","Most TB cases occur in Asia and Africa. The countries with the most cases include India, Indonesia, China, the Philippines and Pakistan. TB is closely linked to poverty, crowded housing, malnutrition and HIV, which weakens the immune system. Many people with TB are never diagnosed, so finding missing cases is a major goal."],["Ending TB","The United Nations has set a goal of ending the TB epidemic by 2030. This requires faster diagnosis, shorter treatments, support for patients and better vaccines. Several new TB vaccines are being tested in clinical trials, which could protect adults and teenagers better than BCG."]],
sp:["Ancient bones tell a story","Scientists have found signs of TB in the spines of Egyptian mummies thousands of years old and in skeletons from ancient Peru, showing that TB existed in the Americas before Europeans arrived. By studying the DNA of TB bacteria from ancient remains, researchers are learning how the disease spread around the world with human migrations."],
g:[["Bacterium","A tiny single-celled organism; some cause disease"],["Latent TB","Infection without symptoms that can become active later"],["BCG","A vaccine that protects children against severe TB"],["Drug resistance","When germs survive the medicines meant to kill them"]],
f:["TB is caused by bacteria, not a virus.","TB is treated with several medicines for about six months.","World TB Day is on 24 March."],
fun:"Signs of tuberculosis have been found in Egyptian mummies thousands of years old.",
tl:"Ang tuberculosis o TB ay sakit na dulot ng bakterya na kadalasang umaatake sa baga. Kumakalat ito sa hangin kapag umuubo ang may sakit. Nagagamot ito sa pag-inom ng ilang gamot sa loob ng mga anim na buwan."});

ADDART({id:"dengue",t:"Dengue",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦟",w:"Dengue fever",r:["malaria","microbe","vacc","pandemic","immune"],
p:[["Spread by mosquitoes","Dengue is a disease caused by a virus spread by the bites of Aedes mosquitoes."],["Common in the tropics","It is common in warm, rainy parts of Asia, Africa and the Americas, including the Philippines."],["Prevention starts at home","Removing standing water where mosquitoes breed helps prevent dengue."]],
l:"Dengue is a viral infection spread to people by the bite of infected Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti. It is the most common mosquito-borne viral disease in the world, found in more than 100 countries in tropical and subtropical regions. Most infections are mild or cause no symptoms, but some people develop severe dengue, which can be life-threatening. Cases have risen sharply in recent decades, with a record number reported in 2024. There is no specific cure, but early care greatly reduces deaths.",
s:[["The virus and the mosquito","Dengue is caused by four closely related types of dengue virus. Aedes aegypti mosquitoes, with white markings on their legs, bite mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon. They breed in small pools of clean, still water, in flower pots, old tires, buckets, gutters and water containers around homes."],["Symptoms","Symptoms usually begin four to ten days after a bite. They include high fever, severe headache, pain behind the eyes, muscle and joint pain, nausea and rash. The aches can be so intense that dengue was once called breakbone fever. Most people recover within about a week."],["Severe dengue","A small share of patients develop severe dengue, usually just as the fever goes down. Warning signs include severe stomach pain, repeated vomiting, bleeding from the gums or nose, blood in vomit and extreme tiredness or restlessness. Severe dengue needs urgent hospital care. A second infection with a different dengue type raises the risk of severe illness."],["Treatment","There is no medicine that kills the dengue virus. Patients rest, drink plenty of fluids and take paracetamol for fever and pain. Doctors usually advise avoiding aspirin and ibuprofen, which can increase bleeding. In hospital, careful fluid treatment saves the lives of most patients with severe dengue."]],
k:[["Cause","Dengue virus, four types"],["Spread by","Aedes mosquitoes, mainly Aedes aegypti"],["Countries affected","More than 100"],["Old name","Breakbone fever"],["ASEAN Dengue Day","15 June"]],
d:[["1779–1780","Epidemics matching dengue occur in Asia, Africa and the Americas"],["1906","Mosquitoes are shown to spread dengue"],["1950s","Severe dengue outbreaks are recorded in the Philippines and Thailand"],["2011","ASEAN Dengue Day is first observed"],["2019","The Philippines declares a national dengue epidemic"],["2024","A record number of dengue cases is reported worldwide"]],
s2:[["A growing problem","Dengue cases reported to the World Health Organization have increased many times over since 2000, and about half the world’s population now lives in areas at risk. Reasons include rapid growth of cities, travel, poor water storage and waste disposal, and warmer temperatures that help mosquitoes spread to new areas."],["Dengue in the Philippines","Dengue is a major health concern in the Philippines, with cases rising in the rainy season from about June to November. In 2019 the government declared a national dengue epidemic. Health campaigns encourage the 4S strategy: search and destroy breeding sites, use self-protection, seek early consultation and support community action."],["Vaccines","Dengue vaccines are tricky to develop because they must protect against all four virus types. The first vaccine, Dengvaxia, was found to raise the risk of severe disease in people never previously infected, and in 2017 the Philippines stopped its school vaccination program, sparking controversy and a fall in public trust in vaccines. A newer vaccine, TAK-003 (Qdenga), was recommended by WHO in 2023 for some age groups in high-risk areas."],["Bacteria against dengue","Scientists in the World Mosquito Program release Aedes mosquitoes carrying natural bacteria called Wolbachia, which make it harder for the mosquitoes to spread dengue. Releases in cities such as Yogyakarta in Indonesia have greatly reduced dengue cases in trials."],["Stopping mosquitoes at home","The best protection is preventing mosquitoes from breeding and biting. People can cover water containers, empty and scrub vases and buckets weekly, clear gutters, dispose of old tires and cans, use window screens and bed nets, wear long sleeves and use insect repellent. Community clean-up drives help protect whole neighborhoods."],["Related diseases","The same Aedes mosquitoes can spread other viruses, including chikungunya, Zika and yellow fever. Controlling these mosquitoes helps protect against all of them."]],
sp:["Mosquito eggs that wait","Aedes aegypti lays its eggs on the inside walls of containers just above the water line. The eggs can survive dry for many months. When rain or fresh water covers them, they hatch, and within about a week new adult mosquitoes can emerge. This is why scrubbing the inside of containers, not just emptying them, is important to stop dengue."],
g:[["Virus","A tiny germ that can only multiply inside living cells"],["Aedes aegypti","The main mosquito that spreads dengue"],["Severe dengue","A dangerous form of dengue with bleeding or shock"],["Breeding site","A place where mosquitoes lay eggs"]],
f:["Dengue mosquitoes bite mostly during the day.","There are four types of dengue virus.","Mosquito eggs can survive dry for months."],
fun:"Dengue was once called breakbone fever because of its painful aches.",
tl:"Ang dengue ay sakit na dulot ng virus na ikinakalat ng kagat ng lamok na Aedes. Karaniwan ito sa mga bansang tropikal gaya ng Pilipinas. Maiiwasan ito sa pag-alis ng tubig na naiipon kung saan nangingitlog ang lamok."});

ADDART({id:"hiv",t:"HIV and AIDS",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🎗️",w:"HIV/AIDS",r:["immune","microbe","pandemic","blood","vacc"],
p:[["A virus that attacks defenses","HIV is a virus that damages the immune system, the body’s defense against infections."],["AIDS","Without treatment, HIV can lead to AIDS, when the immune system becomes very weak."],["Treatable","Modern medicines let people with HIV live long, healthy lives and prevent passing it on."]],
l:"HIV, the human immunodeficiency virus, attacks the immune system, especially white blood cells called CD4 cells that help fight infections. Without treatment, HIV gradually weakens the immune system and can lead to AIDS, acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, in which the body cannot fight off infections and certain cancers. Since the 1980s, more than 40 million people have died of AIDS-related illnesses. Today there is no cure, but daily medicines control the virus so well that people living with HIV can expect a near-normal lifespan.",
s:[["How HIV spreads","HIV is passed from one person to another through certain body fluids: blood, sexual fluids and breast milk. It can spread through sexual contact, sharing needles or from a mother to her baby during pregnancy, birth or breastfeeding. It is not spread by hugging, shaking hands, sharing cups, toilets or swimming pools, or by mosquitoes."],["What HIV does","HIV enters CD4 cells and uses them to make copies of itself, destroying them in the process. Over years, the number of CD4 cells falls. When it drops very low, or certain serious infections appear, a person is said to have AIDS. Many people with HIV feel well for years, so testing is the only way to know."],["Treatment","Antiretroviral therapy, a combination of medicines, stops HIV from multiplying. Taken every day, it keeps the virus at very low levels and lets the immune system recover. When the virus is so low that tests cannot detect it, it cannot be passed on through sex, a message summed up as undetectable equals untransmittable, or U=U."],["Prevention","HIV can be prevented by using condoms, never sharing needles and testing regularly. People at high risk can take PrEP, medicine that prevents infection. Treating pregnant women with HIV can almost always prevent the virus from passing to their babies. Donated blood is screened for HIV."]],
k:[["Virus","Human immunodeficiency virus"],["Living with HIV (2024)","About 40 million people"],["Deaths since the 1980s","More than 40 million"],["Treatment","Antiretroviral therapy"],["World AIDS Day","1 December"]],
d:[["1981","Doctors in the US report the first cases of what became known as AIDS"],["1983","Scientists at the Pasteur Institute identify the virus"],["1987","AZT becomes the first approved HIV medicine"],["1988","The first World AIDS Day is held"],["1996","Combination therapy transforms HIV treatment"],["2025","A twice-yearly injection to prevent HIV is approved in the US"]],
s2:[["Discovering the virus","In 1981 doctors noticed rare infections and cancers in previously healthy young men in the United States. In 1983 Françoise Barré-Sinoussi and Luc Montagnier at the Pasteur Institute in Paris identified the virus that causes AIDS; they won the Nobel Prize in 2008. HIV is thought to have crossed into humans from chimpanzees in Central Africa in the early 1900s."],["A global epidemic","HIV spread worldwide in the 1980s and 1990s. Africa south of the Sahara has been hit hardest and still has about two-thirds of the people living with HIV. By the late 1990s, AIDS was a leading cause of death in many African countries, leaving millions of children orphaned."],["From death sentence to manageable condition","In the 1980s an HIV diagnosis usually meant death within years. The first medicine, AZT, came in 1987, and in 1996 combination therapy proved that HIV could be controlled. Programs such as the Global Fund, created in 2002, and others helped bring treatment to millions in poorer countries, and AIDS-related deaths have fallen by more than half since their peak."],["HIV in the Philippines","While HIV infections fell in many countries, the Philippines has had one of the fastest-growing HIV epidemics in the Asia-Pacific region, especially among young people. The Philippine HIV and AIDS Policy Act of 2018 expanded free testing and treatment and allowed young people aged 15 and older to get tested."],["Fighting stigma","People with HIV have often faced fear, discrimination and rejection, which discourage testing and treatment. The red ribbon, created in 1991, became a symbol of awareness and support. Health workers stress that people with HIV can work, study, play sports and have families, and that treatment protects others."],["Toward a cure and vaccine","Scientists have not yet created an HIV vaccine, because the virus changes rapidly. A handful of people have been cured after bone marrow transplants for cancer from donors with a rare genetic resistance, but this risky method cannot be used widely. In 2025 a long-acting injection given twice a year was approved in the United States to prevent HIV."]],
sp:["U=U","In the 2010s, large studies following thousands of couples found that people with HIV whose treatment kept the virus undetectable did not pass HIV to their partners through sex. This led health organizations to endorse the message Undetectable = Untransmittable. It encourages people to get tested and stay on treatment, and helps reduce fear and stigma."],
g:[["Immune system","The body’s defense against germs"],["CD4 cell","A white blood cell that HIV attacks"],["Antiretroviral","A medicine that stops HIV from multiplying"],["Stigma","Unfair negative beliefs about a group of people"]],
f:["HIV is not spread by hugging or sharing cups.","Daily medicines can keep HIV under control.","World AIDS Day is on 1 December."],
fun:"The red ribbon, a worldwide symbol of HIV awareness, was created in 1991.",
tl:"Ang HIV ay virus na sumisira sa immune system, ang depensa ng katawan laban sa impeksiyon. Kung hindi gagamutin, maaari itong humantong sa AIDS. Sa modernong gamot, maaaring mabuhay nang mahaba at malusog ang taong may HIV."});

ADDART({id:"pain",t:"Pain",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🤕",w:"Pain",r:["nervous","brain","surgery","skin"],
p:[["A warning signal","Pain is the body’s alarm system, warning us of injury or danger."],["Made in the brain","Nerves send signals, but the feeling of pain is created by the brain."],["Many treatments","Medicines, rest, exercise and other methods can relieve pain."]],
l:"Pain is an unpleasant feeling that warns of actual or possible damage to the body. When we touch something hot or cut a finger, special nerve endings send signals through the spinal cord to the brain, which produces the feeling of pain and makes us pull away or protect the injury. Pain can be short-lived, called acute pain, or last for months or years, called chronic pain. It is shaped not only by injury but also by emotions, attention and past experiences.",
s:[["How pain works","The skin, muscles, joints and organs contain nerve endings called nociceptors, which respond to harmful heat, cold, pressure or chemicals. They send electrical signals along nerves to the spinal cord and up to the brain. Reflexes can make you jerk your hand away from a flame before you even feel the pain."],["Acute and chronic pain","Acute pain comes on suddenly, from injuries, burns or surgery, and fades as the body heals. Chronic pain lasts longer than about three months and may continue after healing, as with back pain, arthritis or migraines. Chronic pain affects a large share of adults worldwide and can make work, sleep and daily life difficult."],["The brain and pain","The brain decides how much something hurts. Fear and worry can make pain feel worse, while distraction, laughter and feeling safe can make it feel milder. Athletes and soldiers sometimes do not notice serious injuries until later. In the 1960s, the gate control theory proposed that signals in the spinal cord can block pain signals, which is one reason rubbing a bumped elbow helps."],["Relieving pain","Medicines such as paracetamol, ibuprofen and aspirin relieve mild to moderate pain. Strong painkillers called opioids, such as morphine, treat severe pain but can be addictive and must be used carefully. Rest, ice or heat, exercise, physical therapy and mental techniques such as relaxation also help."]],
k:[["Pain sensors","Nociceptors"],["Chronic pain","Lasting more than about three months"],["First anesthetic surgery","1840s"],["Aspirin first sold","1899"],["Nobel Prize for heat and touch sensors","2021"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Hippocrates recommends willow bark for pain"],["1804","Friedrich Sertürner isolates morphine from opium"],["1846","Ether anesthesia is demonstrated in Boston"],["1899","Aspirin goes on sale"],["1965","Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall publish the gate control theory"],["2021","David Julius and Ardem Patapoutian win the Nobel Prize for discovering sensors for heat and touch"]],
s2:[["People who feel no pain","A few people are born unable to feel pain, a rare condition caused by changes in certain genes. It might sound lucky, but it is dangerous: they may break bones, burn themselves or bite their tongues without noticing. Their experiences show how important pain is for protecting the body."],["Phantom pain","Many people who have lost an arm or leg still feel it, and some feel pain in the missing limb, called phantom limb pain. In the 1990s the neuroscientist V. S. Ramachandran used a simple mirror box to trick the brain into seeing the missing limb move, which relieved pain for some patients."],["Why chili peppers feel hot","Capsaicin, the chemical in chili peppers, activates the same nerve sensors that detect dangerous heat, so the brain feels burning even though no harm is done. The discovery of this sensor, called TRPV1, by David Julius helped explain how we sense temperature and pain, and earned a share of the 2021 Nobel Prize."],["Referred pain","Sometimes pain is felt in a different place from its cause. During a heart attack, pain may be felt in the left arm, jaw or back, because nerves from these areas share pathways in the spinal cord. Doctors use knowledge of referred pain to diagnose problems."],["Painkillers and their risks","Opioid painkillers are powerful but can cause addiction and dangerous breathing problems. In some countries, overuse of opioids has caused a crisis of addiction and overdose deaths. At the same time, many people in poorer countries cannot get enough pain relief, even for cancer. Doctors aim to balance relief with safety."],["The placebo effect","When people are given a pill with no active medicine but believe it will help, their pain often decreases. This placebo effect is real: the brain releases its own natural painkillers, called endorphins. Scientists test new medicines against placebos to find out whether they truly work."]],
sp:["Willow bark to aspirin","For thousands of years, people in Egypt, Greece and elsewhere chewed willow bark or drank willow tea to ease pain and fever. In the 1800s chemists found that its active substance was related to salicylic acid. In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at the German company Bayer made a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid, sold as aspirin from 1899. It became one of the most widely used medicines in history."],
g:[["Nociceptor","A nerve ending that detects harmful stimuli"],["Chronic pain","Pain lasting longer than about three months"],["Opioid","A strong painkiller related to morphine"],["Endorphin","A natural painkiller made by the body"]],
f:["Pain is produced by the brain.","Some people are born unable to feel pain.","Aspirin comes from a substance found in willow bark."],
fun:"Chili peppers feel hot because they trigger the same nerve sensors as real heat.",
tl:"Ang sakit o kirot ay babala ng katawan sa pinsala o panganib. Nagpapadala ang mga nerbiyo ng signal, ngunit ang utak ang lumilikha ng pakiramdam ng sakit. May iba't ibang paraan para maibsan ito, gaya ng gamot, pahinga at ehersisyo."});

ADDART({id:"emotions",t:"Emotions",c:"Health and the Body",i:"😊",w:"Emotion",r:["brain","mental","hormone","memory","sleep"],
p:[["Feelings that move us","Emotions such as joy, fear, anger and sadness are reactions of the mind and body to what happens around us."],["Brain and body","Emotions involve the brain, hormones, heartbeat, breathing and facial expressions."],["Managing feelings","Understanding and managing emotions helps people stay healthy and get along with others."]],
l:"Emotions are complex reactions that involve feelings, thoughts, body changes and behavior. Joy, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust are among the most familiar, but people experience many more, such as pride, shame, love, jealousy and hope. Emotions help us respond to danger, connect with others and make decisions. They are produced by networks in the brain and influenced by hormones, culture and personal experience. Learning to recognize and manage emotions is an important part of mental health.",
s:[["Emotions in the brain","Many brain areas work together to create emotions. The amygdala, a small almond-shaped structure, helps detect threats and triggers fear. The prefrontal cortex, behind the forehead, helps us think about and control emotional reactions. Chemical messengers such as dopamine, serotonin and oxytocin also shape how we feel."],["Emotions in the body","Emotions change the body. Fear makes the heart race, breathing quicken and muscles tense, preparing for action, the fight-or-flight response described by the American scientist Walter Cannon in 1915. Embarrassment can make cheeks blush, and sadness can bring tears. Long-term stress can affect sleep, digestion and the immune system."],["Facial expressions","Charles Darwin wrote about emotional expressions in humans and animals in 1872. In the 1960s and 1970s, the psychologist Paul Ekman found that people in many cultures, including an isolated community in Papua New Guinea, recognized some facial expressions such as happiness, fear and anger. Other researchers argue that emotions and their expressions vary more across cultures than this suggests."],["Managing emotions","Everyone feels strong emotions. Helpful ways to manage them include naming the feeling, taking slow breaths, going for a walk, talking with someone trusted, writing in a journal and getting enough sleep. Feelings are not good or bad in themselves, but how we act on them matters."]],
k:[["Key brain area for fear","Amygdala"],["Control center","Prefrontal cortex"],["Darwin’s book","The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals (1872)"],["Stress response","Fight or flight"],["Emotional intelligence","Concept proposed in 1990"]],
d:[["1872","Darwin publishes his book on emotional expression"],["1884","William James proposes that emotions follow body changes"],["1915","Walter Cannon describes the fight-or-flight response"],["1970s","Paul Ekman studies facial expressions across cultures"],["1990","Peter Salovey and John Mayer describe emotional intelligence"],["2015","The film Inside Out shows emotions as characters"]],
s2:[["Emotional intelligence","In 1990 the psychologists Peter Salovey and John Mayer described emotional intelligence: the ability to recognize, understand and manage our own emotions and to understand those of others. The idea became popular through a 1995 book by Daniel Goleman. Schools in many countries now teach social and emotional skills."],["Words for feelings","Languages have words for emotions that others lack. Filipino has kilig, the fluttery thrill of romance, and gigil, the urge to squeeze something unbearably cute. German has Schadenfreude, pleasure at someone else’s misfortune, and Japanese has amae, the comfort of depending on someone’s kindness. Learning such words can help people notice and describe their own feelings."],["Why we cry","Humans are the only animals known to cry tears from emotion. Emotional tears may signal to others that we need help or comfort, and many people feel calmer after crying. Tears from sadness, joy and laughter all come from the same glands that keep our eyes moist."],["Emotions and decisions","People once thought emotions got in the way of good thinking. But studies of patients with damage to emotional areas of the brain, described by the neuroscientist Antonio Damasio, found that they struggled to make even simple decisions. Emotions help us weigh what matters."],["Empathy","Empathy is the ability to understand and share the feelings of others. It helps people cooperate, make friends and care for each other. Babies begin showing simple signs of empathy, such as crying when other babies cry, and empathy develops through childhood."],["When emotions become overwhelming","Everyone feels sad, worried or angry at times, but when such feelings are very intense, last a long time or make daily life hard, they may be signs of conditions such as depression or anxiety. These are common and treatable, and talking to a parent, teacher, doctor or counselor is an important first step."]],
sp:["Inside Out","The 2015 animated film Inside Out, made with advice from psychologists including Paul Ekman and Dacher Keltner, shows the emotions of a girl named Riley as characters: Joy, Sadness, Fear, Anger and Disgust. The film’s message, that sadness is important too because it helps us get comfort and support, has been used by teachers and therapists to help children talk about feelings."],
g:[["Emotion","A reaction involving feelings, thoughts and body changes"],["Amygdala","A brain area involved in fear and emotion"],["Empathy","Understanding and sharing others’ feelings"],["Fight or flight","The body’s response to danger"]],
f:["The amygdala helps the brain detect danger.","Darwin wrote a book about emotions in 1872.","Filipino has special words such as kilig and gigil."],
fun:"The Filipino word gigil describes the urge to squeeze something extremely cute.",
tl:"Ang emosyon tulad ng saya, takot, galit at lungkot ay reaksiyon ng isip at katawan sa mga nangyayari. Kasama rito ang utak, mga hormone at ekspresyon ng mukha. Mahalaga ang pag-unawa at pamamahala ng emosyon sa kalusugan ng isip."});

ADDART({id:"pregnancy",t:"Pregnancy and Birth",c:"Health and the Body",i:"👶",w:"Pregnancy",r:["cell","hormone","mendel","nutri","vacc"],
p:[["A new life grows","Pregnancy is the time, about nine months, when a baby develops inside its mother’s womb."],["From one cell","Every person begins as a single fertilized cell that divides into trillions."],["Care for mother and baby","Good care before, during and after birth keeps mothers and babies healthy."]],
l:"Pregnancy is the period during which a baby, at first called an embryo and later a fetus, grows inside the uterus, or womb. It lasts about 40 weeks, counted from the start of the mother’s last menstrual period, and is divided into three trimesters. During this time, a single fertilized cell develops into a baby with a beating heart, a working brain and all its organs. Birth usually happens through labor, though sometimes by surgery. Good prenatal care helps protect the health of both mother and child.",
s:[["The beginning","A pregnancy begins when an egg cell from the mother is fertilized by a sperm cell from the father, forming one cell called a zygote, which carries genes from both parents. It divides again and again as it travels to the uterus and attaches to its wall. The developing baby is called an embryo for the first eight weeks after fertilization, and a fetus after that."],["How the baby grows","By about six weeks of pregnancy, a tiny heart can be seen beating on an ultrasound scan. By the end of the first trimester, the fetus has arms, legs, fingers and toes. In the second trimester it begins to move, kick and hear sounds. In the third trimester it grows quickly, its lungs mature and it usually turns head-down, ready for birth."],["Lifeline: the placenta","The placenta is an organ that grows in the uterus during pregnancy. Connected to the baby by the umbilical cord, it passes oxygen and nutrients from the mother’s blood to the baby and carries away waste. The baby floats in a sac of amniotic fluid, which cushions it and keeps it warm."],["Birth","Labor begins when muscles of the uterus start to tighten regularly, gradually opening the cervix. Then the mother pushes the baby out through the birth canal, followed by the placenta. Sometimes doctors deliver babies by caesarean section, an operation through the abdomen, when this is safer. Babies born before 37 weeks are called premature and may need special care."]],
k:[["Length","About 40 weeks"],["Stages","Three trimesters"],["Embryo becomes fetus","About 8 weeks after fertilization"],["Premature birth","Before 37 weeks"],["Apgar score","Newborn check, created 1952"]],
d:[["c. 1350 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes a pregnancy test using barley and wheat"],["1847","James Young Simpson uses chloroform during childbirth"],["1952","Virginia Apgar introduces her newborn score"],["1958","Ian Donald publishes the use of ultrasound in pregnancy"],["1978","Louise Brown is the first baby born through IVF"],["2000","The UN sets goals to reduce maternal deaths"]],
s2:[["Prenatal care","During pregnancy, health workers check the mother’s blood pressure, weight and blood, track the baby’s growth and give vaccines and supplements such as iron and folic acid. A healthy diet, rest and avoiding smoking, alcohol and unsafe medicines help the baby develop well. Regular checkups help find problems early."],["Ultrasound","Ultrasound scans use high-frequency sound waves to create pictures of the baby inside the womb. In 1958 the Scottish doctor Ian Donald published research showing how ultrasound could be used in pregnancy. It is safe, painless and lets doctors check the baby’s growth, position and heartbeat."],["The Apgar score","In 1952 the American anesthesiologist Virginia Apgar created a quick test to check newborns one and five minutes after birth. It scores the baby’s color, heart rate, reflexes, muscle tone and breathing from 0 to 2 each. The Apgar score is now used in hospitals around the world."],["Midwives","Midwives are trained health workers who care for women during pregnancy, birth and afterward. In many countries, including the Philippines, traditional birth attendants called hilot once helped most births in villages. Today health programs encourage giving birth in health facilities with skilled attendants, which greatly reduces deaths."],["Safer motherhood","Every year, hundreds of thousands of women die from causes related to pregnancy and childbirth, mostly in low-income countries, and most of these deaths are preventable with skilled care. Maternal deaths worldwide have fallen substantially since 2000, but reducing them further remains a major global health goal."],["Twins and multiples","Sometimes more than one baby grows at once. Identical twins form when one fertilized egg splits in two, so they share the same genes. Fraternal twins develop from two separate eggs and are no more alike than other brothers and sisters. Twin births are more common in some populations, such as in parts of West Africa."]],
sp:["The first IVF baby","On 25 July 1978, Louise Brown was born in Oldham, England, the first person conceived through in vitro fertilization, IVF, in which an egg is fertilized by sperm in a laboratory dish and then placed in the mother’s uterus. The scientists Robert Edwards and Patrick Steptoe developed the method; Edwards won the Nobel Prize in 2010. More than ten million babies have since been born through IVF."],
g:[["Embryo","A baby in the earliest stage of development"],["Fetus","A developing baby from about eight weeks after fertilization until birth"],["Placenta","The organ that nourishes a baby in the womb"],["Trimester","One of three stages of pregnancy, each about three months long"]],
f:["Pregnancy lasts about 40 weeks.","Every person begins as a single cell.","Identical twins share the same genes."],
fun:"More than ten million babies have been born through IVF since 1978.",
tl:"Ang pagbubuntis ay panahon, mga siyam na buwan, kung kailan lumalaki ang sanggol sa sinapupunan ng ina. Nagsisimula ang bawat tao bilang iisang selula. Mahalaga ang maayos na pangangalaga bago, habang at pagkatapos manganak."});

;
/* data/tech3.js */
// Technology articles, part 3: bicycles, submarines, solar power, hydroelectric power and refrigeration.
ADDART({id:"bicycle",t:"The Bicycle",c:"Technology",i:"🚲",w:"Bicycle",r:["wheel","cars","rail","jeepney","flight"],
p:[["Two wheels and pedals","A bicycle is a human-powered vehicle with two wheels, moved by pushing pedals."],["Efficient travel","Cycling is one of the most energy-efficient ways to travel ever invented."],["Around the world","There are about a billion bicycles in the world, used for transport, work, sport and fun."]],
l:"A bicycle is a vehicle with two wheels, one behind the other, powered by a rider pushing pedals that turn the back wheel through a chain. Invented in Europe in the 1800s, the bicycle changed daily life by giving ordinary people a cheap, fast way to travel. Today there are roughly a billion bicycles worldwide. They carry people to work and school, deliver goods, race in great events such as the Tour de France and help cities cut traffic and pollution.",
s:[["The running machine","In 1817 the German inventor Karl Drais built a wooden two-wheeled machine with no pedals. Riders sat on it and pushed along the ground with their feet. Called the running machine or dandy horse, it was a fashionable craze for a short time and proved that a person could balance on two wheels in a line."],["Pedals and penny-farthings","In the 1860s French makers added pedals to the front wheel, creating the velocipede, nicknamed the boneshaker because of its rough ride. To go faster, builders made front wheels bigger and bigger, leading in the 1870s to the high-wheeled penny-farthing, which was fast but dangerous to fall from."],["The safety bicycle","In 1885 the English inventor John Kemp Starley built the Rover safety bicycle, with two wheels of similar size and a chain driving the rear wheel. In 1888 John Boyd Dunlop developed a practical air-filled tire. These inventions created the basic design of bicycles still used today and set off a worldwide cycling boom."],["How a bicycle works","When a rider pushes the pedals, a toothed wheel called the chainring turns a chain, which turns a smaller cog on the rear wheel. Gears let riders change how hard they must pedal for hills or flat roads. Brakes press pads against the wheel rims or discs. Staying upright depends on steering and forward motion."]],
k:[["Bicycles worldwide","About 1 billion"],["First two-wheeler","Karl Drais, 1817"],["Safety bicycle","John Kemp Starley, 1885"],["Air-filled tire","John Boyd Dunlop, 1888"],["World Bicycle Day","3 June"]],
d:[["1817","Karl Drais builds the running machine"],["1860s","Pedal velocipedes appear in France"],["1885","The Rover safety bicycle is introduced"],["1888","Dunlop develops the air-filled tire"],["1903","The first Tour de France is held"],["2018","The UN declares 3 June World Bicycle Day"]],
s2:[["Freedom on wheels","In the 1890s the bicycle gave many women new independence, letting them travel on their own and encouraging more practical clothing. The American campaigner Susan B. Anthony said in 1896 that the bicycle had done more to free women than anything else in the world."],["Bicycles at work","Around the world, bicycles carry goods and people. In the Philippines, pedicabs, called padyak or trisikad, are bicycles with sidecars that carry passengers on short trips. In many countries, delivery riders, postal workers and farmers depend on bicycles to earn a living."],["Cycling cities","Some cities are famous for cycling. In the Netherlands and Denmark, separate bike lanes make cycling safe, and in Amsterdam and Copenhagen a large share of trips are made by bicycle. Many cities, from Bogotá to Paris, have built bike lanes and bike-sharing systems to reduce traffic and pollution."],["Racing","Bicycle racing has been popular since the 1860s. The Tour de France, first held in 1903, is a three-week race of more than 3,000 kilometers through France and neighboring countries. Cycling has been part of the modern Olympic Games since 1896, including road, track, mountain bike and BMX events."],["From bicycles to airplanes","The Wright brothers, Wilbur and Orville, ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. Their skills with light frames, chains and balance helped them build the first successful powered airplane in 1903. Early cars also borrowed bicycle parts, such as chains and air-filled tires."],["Electric bikes","Electric bicycles, or e-bikes, have a battery-powered motor that helps the rider pedal. They make longer trips and hills easier and have become very popular, especially in China and Europe. They also raise new questions about road safety and rules."]],
sp:["The most efficient machine","A person on a bicycle uses less energy to travel a kilometer than a person walking, and far less than any car, train or plane per person. Cycling turns the power of leg muscles into motion very efficiently because the wheels roll with little friction. That is why the bicycle is often called the most efficient means of transport ever invented."],
g:[["Chainring","The front toothed wheel turned by the pedals"],["Gear","A toothed wheel that changes pedaling effort"],["Velocipede","An early pedal-powered bicycle"],["Pedicab","A bicycle that carries passengers in a sidecar or cab"]],
f:["The first two-wheeler had no pedals.","The Tour de France began in 1903.","The Wright brothers were bicycle makers."],
fun:"The first bicycle, built in 1817, had no pedals at all.",
tl:"Ang bisikleta ay sasakyang may dalawang gulong na pinaaandar ng pagpedal. Isa ito sa pinakamatipid sa enerhiya na paraan ng paglalakbay. Sa Pilipinas, ginagamit ang padyak o trisikad para magsakay ng pasahero."});

ADDART({id:"submarine",t:"Submarines",c:"Technology",i:"🚢",w:"Submarine",r:["ships","pacific","nuclear","ww2"],
p:[["Ships that dive","A submarine is a vessel that can travel underwater as well as on the surface."],["Sinking and rising","Submarines dive and surface by filling tanks with water or air."],["Exploring the deep","Small submarines called submersibles explore the deepest parts of the ocean."]],
l:"A submarine is a watercraft able to operate independently underwater. Inventors dreamed of underwater boats for centuries, but practical submarines appeared only in the late 1800s. Military submarines played major roles in both world wars and the Cold War, and today nuclear-powered submarines can stay submerged for months. Smaller research submersibles have carried people to the deepest point in the ocean, nearly 11 kilometers down, revealing strange life and landscapes in the deep sea.",
s:[["How submarines dive","Submarines have ballast tanks that can be filled with water or air. To dive, they let water in, becoming heavier than the water around them. To rise, they blow compressed air into the tanks, pushing the water out. Small fins called diving planes help steer the submarine up and down, like wings."],["Early submarines","In the 1620s the Dutch inventor Cornelis Drebbel built a rowed underwater boat that traveled beneath the River Thames in England. During the American Revolution, the one-person Turtle tried to attach a bomb to a British ship in 1776 but failed. In 1864 the H. L. Hunley became the first submarine to sink an enemy warship, though it sank too."],["Submarines at war","German U-boats sank many ships in both world wars, trying to cut off supplies to Britain. American submarines sank much of Japan’s merchant fleet in the Pacific during World War II. After the war, submarines carrying nuclear missiles became a key part of Cold War strategy."],["Life on board","Submarine crews live in tight spaces for weeks or months, sharing small bunks and working in shifts. Machines make fresh water from seawater and produce oxygen. Because there is no daylight, lighting changes to mark day and night. Sonar, which uses sound, helps submarines detect objects in the dark sea."]],
k:[["Early underwater boat","Cornelis Drebbel, 1620s"],["First nuclear submarine","USS Nautilus, 1954"],["Deepest dive","Challenger Deep, about 10,900 m"],["Diving method","Ballast tanks"],["Underwater navigation","Sonar"]],
d:[["1620s","Drebbel’s underwater boat travels beneath the Thames"],["1776","The Turtle attacks a British warship"],["1864","The H. L. Hunley sinks a warship"],["1954","USS Nautilus, the first nuclear submarine, is launched"],["1960","Trieste reaches the bottom of the Challenger Deep"],["2012","James Cameron makes a solo dive to the Challenger Deep"]],
s2:[["Nuclear submarines","In 1954 the United States launched USS Nautilus, the first nuclear-powered submarine. A nuclear reactor provides power for years without refueling and needs no air, so such submarines can stay underwater for months. In 1958 Nautilus became the first vessel to travel under the ice to the North Pole. Several countries now operate nuclear submarines."],["The deepest dive","On 23 January 1960, the Swiss engineer Jacques Piccard and US Navy officer Don Walsh descended in the bathyscaphe Trieste to the bottom of the Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench, about 10,900 meters down. In 2012 the filmmaker James Cameron reached it alone, and in 2019 the explorer Victor Vescovo dived to the deepest points of all five oceans."],["Alvin","The research submersible Alvin, first launched in 1964, has made thousands of dives. In 1977 scientists aboard Alvin discovered hydrothermal vents on the seafloor near the Galápagos Islands, surrounded by giant tube worms and other life that depends on chemical energy rather than sunlight. In 1986 Alvin visited the wreck of the Titanic."],["Submarines and submersibles","A submarine can travel on its own for long periods. A submersible is a smaller craft, often supported by a ship at the surface, used for research, filming or rescue. Remotely operated vehicles, robots without people aboard, now do much deep-sea exploration more safely and cheaply."],["Dangers of the deep","The deep sea is extremely dangerous: at 4,000 meters, the water pressure is about 400 times that at the surface. Submarines must be built to strict standards. In 2023 the private submersible Titan imploded during a dive to the Titanic wreck, killing all five people aboard, a tragedy that led to calls for stronger safety rules."],["Submarines in stories","Jules Verne’s 1870 novel Twenty Thousand Leagues Under the Seas imagined the electric submarine Nautilus, commanded by the mysterious Captain Nemo, long before such vessels existed. The novel inspired many inventors, and the first nuclear submarine was named after Verne’s ship."]],
sp:["The Turtle","The Turtle, built by the American inventor David Bushnell in 1775, was a wooden, egg-shaped submarine just big enough for one person, who turned hand cranks to move it and pumped water to dive. In 1776, during the American Revolution, Sergeant Ezra Lee tried to attach an explosive to the British warship HMS Eagle in New York Harbor but could not fix it to the hull. It was the first recorded use of a submarine in war."],
g:[["Ballast tank","A tank filled with water or air to make a vessel sink or rise"],["Sonar","Using sound waves to detect objects underwater"],["Submersible","A small underwater craft, often supported by a ship"],["Periscope","A tube with mirrors for seeing above the surface"]],
f:["Submarines dive by filling tanks with water.","USS Nautilus was the first nuclear submarine.","People have reached the deepest point in the ocean."],
fun:"The first nuclear submarine was named after the submarine in a Jules Verne novel.",
tl:"Ang submarino ay sasakyang-dagat na kayang maglakbay sa ilalim ng tubig. Lumulubog at umaangat ito sa pagpuno ng tubig o hangin sa mga tangke. Ang maliliit na submersible ay ginagamit sa paggalugad ng pinakamalalim na bahagi ng karagatan."});

ADDART({id:"solarpower",t:"Solar Power",c:"Technology",i:"☀️",w:"Solar power",r:["renew","sun","elec","battery","chip"],
p:[["Electricity from sunlight","Solar power turns sunlight into electricity using solar panels or concentrated sunlight."],["Clean and growing","Solar power produces no pollution while running and is the fastest-growing source of electricity."],["Cheaper than ever","The cost of solar panels has fallen dramatically since 2010."]],
l:"Solar power is energy from the Sun converted into electricity or heat. Most solar electricity comes from photovoltaic cells, usually made of silicon, which produce electricity when sunlight strikes them. Others use mirrors to concentrate sunlight and boil water to drive turbines. Solar power releases no greenhouse gases while operating and has become one of the cheapest ways to make electricity. Installations range from tiny panels on calculators and rooftops to vast solar farms covering many square kilometers.",
s:[["How solar cells work","A solar cell is made of layers of a semiconductor, usually silicon, treated so that one layer has extra electrons and the other has spaces for them. When light hits the cell, it knocks electrons loose, and they flow through a circuit as electric current. This is called the photovoltaic effect. Many cells are joined to make a panel."],["The history of solar cells","In 1839 the French physicist Edmond Becquerel discovered that light could create electricity in certain materials. In 1954 scientists at Bell Laboratories in the United States made the first practical silicon solar cell, converting about 6 percent of sunlight into electricity. In 1958 the satellite Vanguard 1 became the first to use solar power."],["Concentrated solar power","Concentrated solar power plants use mirrors to focus sunlight onto a receiver, heating a fluid that makes steam to drive turbines. Some store heat in molten salt, so they can keep making electricity after sunset. The Noor complex in Ouarzazate, Morocco, is one of the largest such plants."],["The solar boom","Since 2010, the cost of solar panels has fallen by about 90 percent, thanks to mass production, mostly in China, and better technology. Solar has become the world’s fastest-growing source of new electricity, and global solar capacity passed 2 terawatts in 2024. China, the European Union, the United States and India are among the largest users."]],
k:[["Main technology","Photovoltaic (PV) cells"],["Common material","Silicon"],["First practical cell","Bell Labs, 1954"],["First solar satellite","Vanguard 1, 1958"],["Global capacity","More than 2 terawatts (2024)"]],
d:[["1839","Edmond Becquerel discovers the photovoltaic effect"],["1954","Bell Labs makes the first practical silicon solar cell"],["1958","Vanguard 1 uses solar cells in space"],["2008","The Philippines passes its Renewable Energy Act"],["2016","Solar Impulse 2 completes a round-the-world solar flight"],["2024","Global solar capacity passes 2 terawatts"]],
s2:[["Rooftop solar","Millions of homes, schools and businesses have solar panels on their roofs. In many places, owners can sell extra electricity to the power grid, a system called net metering. Rooftop solar lets people make their own electricity and lowers their bills, especially in sunny countries."],["Solar in the Philippines","The Philippines, with strong tropical sunshine, has been building more solar power under its Renewable Energy Act of 2008. Large solar farms have been built in Luzon and the Visayas, and projects such as Terra Solar in Nueva Ecija and Bulacan are planned among the largest solar and battery plants in the world. Small solar systems also bring electricity to remote island villages."],["Light for everyone","Hundreds of millions of people, mostly in Africa and parts of Asia, still lack electricity. Small solar home kits and solar lanterns provide light, phone charging and power for radios and fans, often replacing dangerous kerosene lamps. Solar mini-grids can power whole villages."],["When the Sun goes down","Solar panels produce no electricity at night and less on cloudy days. To provide steady power, solar is combined with batteries, other power sources and better electricity grids. Falling battery prices have made solar-plus-storage projects increasingly common."],["Flying on sunshine","Between 2015 and 2016, the airplane Solar Impulse 2, piloted by Bertrand Piccard and André Borschberg, flew around the world using only solar power, storing energy in batteries to fly through the night. The journey showed what clean energy could achieve, though solar airliners remain far off."],["Panels and the environment","Solar panels produce clean electricity for 25 years or more, but making them uses energy and materials, and old panels must be recycled. Large solar farms need land, so some are built over reservoirs, on rooftops and parking lots or combined with farming, called agrivoltaics."]],
sp:["Solar power in space","Almost all satellites and spacecraft, including the International Space Station, are powered by solar panels. The ISS has huge solar arrays covering an area larger than a basketball court. Spacecraft traveling to Jupiter, such as Juno, use extra-large panels because sunlight there is about 25 times weaker than at Earth. Missions beyond Jupiter usually rely on nuclear power instead."],
g:[["Photovoltaic","Producing electricity directly from light"],["Solar panel","A group of solar cells joined together"],["Net metering","Selling extra solar electricity back to the grid"],["Terawatt","One trillion watts"]],
f:["Solar cells are usually made of silicon.","The first practical solar cell was made in 1954.","Solar panels power the International Space Station."],
fun:"A solar-powered airplane flew all the way around the world in 2015 and 2016.",
tl:"Ang solar power ay enerhiya mula sa araw na ginagawang kuryente gamit ang mga solar panel. Malinis ito at isa sa pinakamabilis lumagong pinagkukunan ng kuryente. Malaki ang ibinaba ng presyo ng mga solar panel mula 2010."});

ADDART({id:"hydropower",t:"Hydroelectric Power and Dams",c:"Technology",i:"💧",w:"Hydroelectricity",r:["renew","elec","bridges","wcycle","nile"],
p:[["Power from flowing water","Hydroelectric power uses the energy of falling or flowing water to make electricity."],["Dams and reservoirs","Most hydropower plants use dams that store water in large lakes called reservoirs."],["A major renewable source","Hydropower is one of the world’s largest sources of renewable electricity."]],
l:"Hydroelectric power, or hydropower, is electricity generated from moving water. Most plants use a dam to hold back a river, creating a reservoir. Water released from the reservoir flows through turbines, spinning them to drive generators. Hydropower has been used for more than a century and supplies a large share of the world’s electricity; countries such as Norway, Brazil and Paraguay get most of their power from it. Dams also provide water and flood control, but they can harm rivers and displace communities.",
s:[["From water wheels to turbines","People have used water wheels to grind grain for more than 2,000 years, in ancient Greece, Rome, China and the Islamic world. In the 1800s engineers developed efficient water turbines. In 1878 the English industrialist William Armstrong lit his house, Cragside, with hydroelectric power, and in 1882 the first commercial hydroelectric plant opened in Appleton, Wisconsin."],["How a hydropower plant works","Water stored high behind a dam has potential energy. When gates open, water rushes down through a large pipe called a penstock and spins the blades of a turbine. The turbine turns a generator, which produces electricity. The water then flows back into the river below. The higher the drop and the more water, the more electricity."],["Kinds of hydropower","Large dams store huge amounts of water. Run-of-river plants use a river’s natural flow with little or no storage. Pumped-storage plants pump water uphill to an upper reservoir when electricity is plentiful and release it to make power when demand is high, working like giant batteries. Small hydro plants can power villages in mountain areas."],["Benefits","Hydropower produces no air pollution while running, can be switched on quickly to meet demand and can last for many decades. Dams can also store water for farms and cities, control floods and create lakes for fishing and recreation."]],
k:[["Largest power station","Three Gorges Dam, China"],["Three Gorges capacity","About 22,500 megawatts"],["First commercial plant","Appleton, Wisconsin, 1882"],["Mostly hydro-powered countries","Norway, Brazil, Paraguay and others"],["Key part","Turbine"]],
d:[["c. 3rd century BCE","Water wheels are used in ancient Greece"],["1882","The first commercial hydroelectric plant opens"],["1895","Niagara Falls power plant begins sending AC electricity"],["1936","Hoover Dam is completed in the United States"],["1984","Itaipu Dam begins producing power"],["2012","Three Gorges Dam reaches full capacity"]],
s2:[["Three Gorges Dam","The Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River in China is the world’s largest power station, with a capacity of about 22,500 megawatts. Completed in stages, it reached full operation in 2012. It also helps control floods. But building it flooded towns and farmland and required more than a million people to move, and it changed the river’s ecology."],["Itaipu","The Itaipu Dam on the Paraná River is shared by Brazil and Paraguay. For years it produced more electricity annually than any other plant. It supplies most of Paraguay’s electricity and a significant share of Brazil’s, and the two countries share its output under a treaty signed in 1973."],["Dams in the Philippines","Dams are important for water and power in the Philippines. Angat Dam in Bulacan supplies most of Metro Manila’s water and also generates electricity. San Roque Dam in Pangasinan and Magat Dam in Isabela provide power and irrigation, and the Agus River plants in Mindanao, including those near Maria Cristina Falls, supply much of the island’s electricity."],["Problems with dams","Dams block fish such as salmon from swimming upstream to breed, trap sediment that would enrich farmland downstream and change water temperatures. Reservoirs can flood forests, farms and the homes of Indigenous peoples. In dry years, low water can reduce power output. Some old dams have been removed to restore rivers."],["Dam safety","Dams must be carefully built and maintained, because a failure can release catastrophic floods. In 1975 the failure of the Banqiao Dam in China during a typhoon caused one of the worst dam disasters in history. Today engineers monitor dams with sensors and inspections."],["Hydropower and climate","Hydropower helps reduce emissions from fossil fuels, and pumped storage helps balance wind and solar power. But droughts and changing rainfall linked to climate change can reduce water levels in reservoirs, affecting electricity supplies in some regions."]],
sp:["Lighting Niagara","In 1895 the Adams Power Plant at Niagara Falls began producing electricity using alternating current, the system championed by Nikola Tesla and George Westinghouse. In 1896 it began sending power about 40 kilometers to the city of Buffalo, New York. Its success helped make alternating current the standard for electricity around the world."],
g:[["Turbine","A machine with blades turned by flowing water, steam or air"],["Reservoir","An artificial lake that stores water"],["Penstock","A pipe carrying water to a turbine"],["Pumped storage","Storing energy by pumping water uphill"]],
f:["The Three Gorges Dam is the world’s largest power station.","Water wheels have been used for over 2,000 years.","Angat Dam supplies most of Metro Manila’s water."],
fun:"Pumped-storage hydropower plants work like giant water batteries.",
tl:"Ang hydroelectric power ay kuryenteng nagmumula sa dumadaloy o bumabagsak na tubig. Karamihan ay gumagamit ng dam na nag-iimbak ng tubig. Sa Pilipinas, mahalaga ang mga dam gaya ng Angat, San Roque at Magat."});

ADDART({id:"refrigeration",t:"Refrigeration and Air Conditioning",c:"Technology",i:"🧊",w:"Refrigeration",r:["thermo","elec","climchange","vacc","steam"],
p:[["Keeping things cool","Refrigerators and air conditioners move heat from one place to another to keep things cold."],["Fresh food and medicine","Refrigeration keeps food fresh, medicines safe and buildings comfortable."],["A cool revolution","Before refrigerators, people stored ice cut from frozen lakes or kept food in cool cellars."]],
l:"Refrigeration is the process of removing heat from a space or substance to make it colder. Refrigerators, freezers and air conditioners all work by pumping heat from inside to outside, using special fluids called refrigerants. Modern refrigeration, developed in the 1800s and spread widely in the 1900s, transformed how people eat, live and work. It keeps food from spoiling, allows vaccines to reach remote places and makes hot cities comfortable, but its energy use and chemicals also affect the environment.",
s:[["Before refrigerators","People have long found ways to keep things cool. Ancient Persians built yakhchāls, domed structures that stored ice through hot summers. Many cultures kept food in cool cellars, springs or caves. In the 1800s a large ice trade shipped blocks cut from frozen lakes in New England to cities as far away as the Caribbean and India."],["How a refrigerator works","A refrigerant fluid flows through pipes inside and outside the fridge. Inside, it evaporates, absorbing heat from the food, just as sweat cools your skin when it evaporates. A compressor then squeezes the gas, making it hot, and it releases that heat through coils at the back, turning back into a liquid. The cycle repeats, pumping heat out."],["Inventors of cold","In 1755 the Scottish professor William Cullen showed that evaporating a liquid could produce a little ice. In 1834 Jacob Perkins patented a vapor-compression machine. In the 1870s the German engineer Carl von Linde built efficient refrigeration machines for breweries. Electric home refrigerators became common in wealthy countries from the 1920s and 1930s."],["Air conditioning","In 1902 the American engineer Willis Carrier designed a system to control temperature and humidity in a printing plant in New York. Air conditioning spread to cinemas, offices and homes. It made it easier to live and work in hot places and changed architecture, as buildings with sealed windows and glass walls became possible."]],
k:[["Main parts","Compressor, condenser, evaporator"],["Working fluid","Refrigerant"],["First artificial cooling demo","William Cullen, 1755"],["Modern air conditioning","Willis Carrier, 1902"],["Ozone treaty","Montreal Protocol, 1987"]],
d:[["c. 400 BCE","Persians use yakhchāls to store ice"],["1755","William Cullen demonstrates artificial cooling"],["1834","Jacob Perkins patents a vapor-compression refrigerator"],["1902","Willis Carrier designs modern air conditioning"],["1987","The Montreal Protocol protects the ozone layer"],["2016","The Kigali Amendment limits HFC refrigerants"]],
s2:[["Frozen food","The American inventor Clarence Birdseye noticed that fish caught by Inuit people in the Canadian Arctic froze almost instantly and tasted fresh when thawed. In the 1920s he developed quick-freezing methods for food. Frozen foods became widespread after home freezers spread, changing how people shop and cook."],["The cold chain","A cold chain is a chain of refrigerated storage and transport that keeps food and medicines cold from where they are made to where they are used. Many vaccines must be kept between 2 and 8°C, and some at far colder temperatures. Solar-powered fridges help deliver vaccines to remote clinics without reliable electricity."],["Refrigerants and the ozone layer","In the 1930s refrigerators began using CFCs, chemicals that seemed safe and stable. In the 1970s and 1980s scientists discovered that CFCs were destroying the ozone layer, which shields Earth from harmful ultraviolet light. The Montreal Protocol of 1987, signed by every country in the world, phased them out, and the ozone layer is slowly recovering."],["Cooling and climate","Replacement chemicals called HFCs do not harm the ozone layer but are powerful greenhouse gases. The Kigali Amendment of 2016 commits countries to reduce them. Air conditioners also use large amounts of electricity, and demand is rising fast as temperatures and incomes grow, so engineers are developing more efficient machines and natural refrigerants."],["Cooling in hot countries","In tropical countries such as the Philippines, refrigeration is vital for preserving fish, meat and produce, and ice plants supply fishing fleets. Air conditioning is common in malls, offices and many homes. Energy-efficient appliances and building designs with shade and ventilation help reduce electricity use."],["Einstein’s fridge","In 1930 Albert Einstein and his former student Leo Szilard patented a refrigerator with no moving parts, inspired by news of a family killed by toxic gas leaking from their fridge. It was never widely produced, but engineers have since revisited the design."]],
sp:["The ice king","In 1806 the Boston merchant Frederic Tudor shipped a cargo of ice cut from frozen ponds to the Caribbean island of Martinique. Many people laughed, but Tudor learned to insulate ice with sawdust and built icehouses in hot ports. His company eventually shipped ice as far as Calcutta in India. He became known as the Ice King, and the ice trade boomed until mechanical refrigeration replaced it."],
g:[["Refrigerant","A fluid that carries heat in a cooling system"],["Compressor","A pump that squeezes refrigerant gas"],["Cold chain","Refrigerated storage and transport from start to finish"],["CFC","A chemical once used in fridges that damages the ozone layer"]],
f:["Refrigerators pump heat out rather than making cold.","Air conditioning was invented for a printing plant.","The Montreal Protocol protects the ozone layer."],
fun:"Albert Einstein co-invented a refrigerator with no moving parts.",
tl:"Ang refrigeration ay paglilipat ng init para mapalamig ang isang lugar o bagay. Pinananatili nitong sariwa ang pagkain at ligtas ang gamot. Bago naimbento ang refrigerator, nag-iimbak ang mga tao ng yelo mula sa nagyelong lawa."});

;
/* data/arts4.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 4: the circus, musical theatre, sculpture, calligraphy, drums and percussion.
ADDART({id:"circus",t:"The Circus",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎪",w:"Circus",r:["theatre","puppetry","ballet","elephant"],
p:[["Daring and delight","A circus is a traveling show of acrobats, clowns, jugglers, tightrope walkers and other performers."],["Born in a ring","The modern circus began in London in 1768, performed in a round ring."],["New forms","Today many circuses use only human performers and combine acrobatics with theater and music."]],
l:"A circus is a company of performers who present feats of skill, strength, balance and comedy, usually in a ring or under a big tent. Acrobats, aerialists, jugglers, tightrope walkers, clowns and, in the past, trained animals have thrilled audiences for centuries. The modern circus began in London in 1768, but acrobatic traditions are far older, especially in China. Since the 1970s, new circus companies such as Cirque du Soleil have blended acrobatics with theater, dance and music, often without animals.",
s:[["Ancient roots","The word circus comes from Latin for circle; in ancient Rome, the Circus Maximus was a huge arena for chariot races. Acrobats, jugglers and tumblers performed at fairs and courts in many lands. In China, acrobatic performances have a history of more than 2,000 years, featuring balancing, plate spinning and human pyramids."],["The first modern circus","In 1768 Philip Astley, a former cavalry soldier, began showing trick horse riding in London in a ring about 13 meters across, a size that let riders stand on galloping horses. He added clowns, acrobats and jugglers between the riding acts. The ring size he chose is still common today. Soon circuses spread across Europe and to the Americas."],["Circus acts","Classic circus acts include the flying trapeze, invented by the Frenchman Jules Léotard in 1859, whose name lives on in the leotard; the tightrope; juggling; contortion; trick cycling; and the human cannonball. Clowns provide comedy and keep the show moving. Many performers come from circus families who have trained for generations."],["The big top","In the 1800s American circuses began touring in huge canvas tents, called big tops, traveling by wagon and later by railroad. Showmen such as P. T. Barnum and the Ringling brothers created giant circuses with parades, menageries of animals and three rings with acts happening at once."]],
k:[["Modern circus founded","1768, London"],["Founder","Philip Astley"],["Standard ring","About 13 m across"],["Flying trapeze","Jules Léotard, 1859"],["Cirque du Soleil founded","1984, Quebec"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE","Acrobatic shows flourish in Han dynasty China"],["1768","Philip Astley opens his riding show in London"],["1782","The Royal Circus gives the art its modern name"],["1859","Jules Léotard performs the first flying trapeze act"],["1984","Cirque du Soleil is founded in Canada"],["2017","Ringling Bros. and Barnum & Bailey Circus closes after 146 years"]],
s2:[["Chinese acrobatics","Chinese acrobatic troupes are famous for astonishing balance and flexibility, stacking chairs, spinning plates, diving through hoops and forming human towers. The town of Wuqiao in Hebei Province has been called a cradle of Chinese acrobatics, and its schools have trained performers for centuries."],["Clowns","Clowns have entertained audiences since ancient times. The English performer Joseph Grimaldi, in the early 1800s, created the classic white-faced clown, and clowns are still sometimes called Joeys in his honor. Different traditions include the elegant whiteface, the clumsy auguste and the sad tramp clown. Hospital clowns visit sick children to bring laughter."],["Animals in the circus","For a long time, circuses featured lions, tigers, elephants and bears performing tricks. Concerns grew about cruelty, cramped travel and unnatural behavior, and many countries now ban or restrict wild animals in circuses. Bolivia became one of the first to ban all circus animals in 2009. Many circuses now focus entirely on human skill."],["Contemporary circus","In the 1970s and 1980s, new circus companies in France, Canada and Australia began combining acrobatics with storytelling, dance, live music and dramatic lighting. Cirque du Soleil, founded in Quebec in 1984 by former street performers, became the most famous, with shows seen by hundreds of millions of people."],["Circus around the world","The Moscow State Circus became world famous for its skilled performers during the Soviet era. In Mongolia, contortionists continue a long tradition. In Ethiopia and Kenya, youth circus groups give young people skills and confidence. Many countries have circus schools that train professional performers."],["Training and safety","Circus artists train for years to master their skills, building strength, flexibility and balance. Safety nets, harnesses and spotters protect them while they learn. Circus skills such as juggling and balancing are now taught in schools and community programs because they build coordination, teamwork and confidence."]],
sp:["Jules Léotard","Jules Léotard was a French gymnast whose father ran a swimming pool in Toulouse, where he practiced swinging between ropes and bars over the water. In 1859, at the Cirque Napoléon in Paris, he performed the first flying trapeze act, leaping from one swinging bar to another. He became a star, and the song The Daring Young Man on the Flying Trapeze was written about him. The tight one-piece garment he wore is still called a leotard."],
g:[["Aerialist","A performer who does acts high in the air"],["Big top","The large tent of a traveling circus"],["Contortionist","A performer who bends the body into unusual positions"],["Ringmaster","The host who introduces the circus acts"]],
f:["The modern circus began in London in 1768.","The leotard is named after a trapeze artist.","Chinese acrobatics are over 2,000 years old."],
fun:"The leotard is named after Jules Léotard, the inventor of the flying trapeze act.",
tl:"Ang sirko ay palabas ng mga akrobat, payaso, juggler at iba pang mahusay na tagapagtanghal. Nagsimula ang modernong sirko sa London noong 1768. Ngayon, maraming sirko ang gumagamit na lamang ng mga taong tagapagtanghal at hindi ng hayop."});

ADDART({id:"musicals",t:"Musical Theatre",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎤",w:"Musical theatre",r:["opera","theatre","ballet","cinema","opm"],
p:[["Stories told in song","Musical theatre combines songs, spoken words, dance and acting to tell a story on stage."],["Broadway and the West End","New York’s Broadway and London’s West End are the most famous centers of musical theatre."],["Loved worldwide","Musicals are performed in many languages and countries, and many have become films."]],
l:"Musical theatre is a form of theater in which songs, music and dance are woven into the storytelling. Characters sing to express feelings that words alone cannot, and big dance numbers bring scenes to life. Musicals grew from opera, operetta, vaudeville and music hall in the 1800s and early 1900s and flourished on New York’s Broadway and in London’s West End. Shows such as West Side Story, Les Misérables and The Lion King have been performed around the world, and Filipino performers have become stars of the genre.",
s:[["Origins","Musical theatre has many ancestors. European operettas, light operas with spoken dialogue, were popular in the 1800s; in England, Gilbert and Sullivan wrote comic operettas such as The Pirates of Penzance. In America, variety shows called vaudeville, along with minstrel shows, burlesque and revues, featured songs, comedy and dance."],["The book musical","In 1927 Show Boat, by Jerome Kern and Oscar Hammerstein II, told a serious story about race and love along the Mississippi River, with songs that grew from the plot. In 1943 Rodgers and Hammerstein’s Oklahoma! fully combined story, song and dance, setting the model for the book musical, in which every element serves the story."],["The golden age","From the 1940s to the 1960s, Broadway produced classics such as South Pacific, The King and I, My Fair Lady, The Sound of Music and West Side Story, which retold Romeo and Juliet among rival gangs in New York with music by Leonard Bernstein and dance by Jerome Robbins. Many became popular films."],["Megamusicals","From the 1970s and 1980s, spectacular shows from London, often with sung-through scores, became global hits: Andrew Lloyd Webber’s Cats and The Phantom of the Opera, and Les Misérables and Miss Saigon by Claude-Michel Schönberg and Alain Boublil. They were staged in dozens of countries and languages."]],
k:[["Main centers","Broadway (New York), West End (London)"],["First modern book musical","Show Boat (1927)"],["Longest-running Broadway show","The Phantom of the Opera (1988–2023)"],["Top award (US)","Tony Award, since 1947"],["First Asian woman to win a Tony","Lea Salonga, 1991"]],
d:[["1879","Gilbert and Sullivan’s The Pirates of Penzance premieres"],["1927","Show Boat opens on Broadway"],["1943","Oklahoma! opens"],["1957","West Side Story opens"],["1989","Miss Saigon opens in London, starring Lea Salonga"],["2015","Hamilton opens on Broadway"]],
s2:[["Lea Salonga","The Filipino singer and actress Lea Salonga was chosen at 17 to play Kim in Miss Saigon in London in 1989. When the show moved to Broadway, she won the 1991 Tony Award for Best Actress in a Musical, the first Asian woman to win a Tony. She later played Éponine and Fantine in Les Misérables and was the singing voice of Disney’s Jasmine and Mulan."],["Filipinos on the world stage","The search for Miss Saigon’s cast in Manila launched the international careers of many Filipino performers, and Filipino actors have since appeared in musicals in London, New York, Asia and Australia. At home, companies such as Repertory Philippines, founded in 1967, Tanghalang Pilipino and the Philippine Educational Theater Association (PETA) stage musicals in English and Filipino. PETA’s Rak of Aegis, built on songs by the band Aegis, became a hit original musical."],["Musicals on film","When sound films arrived in 1927, film musicals quickly followed. Hollywood made classics such as The Wizard of Oz, Singin’ in the Rain and The Sound of Music. Animated musicals such as The Little Mermaid and Frozen are hugely popular. India’s film industry, including Bollywood, has long made films in which song and dance are central."],["Hamilton","In 2015 Lin-Manuel Miranda’s Hamilton told the story of the American founding father Alexander Hamilton using hip hop, rap, R&B and pop, with a cast mainly of actors of color. It became a cultural phenomenon, won 11 Tony Awards and the Pulitzer Prize for Drama, and brought many young people to musical theatre."],["Making a musical","A musical is created by a team: a composer writes the music, a lyricist writes the song words and a book writer writes the story and dialogue. A director, choreographer, designers and musicians bring it to the stage. Actors in musicals must be triple threats, able to sing, dance and act."],["Musicals worldwide","Musical theatre thrives beyond New York and London. South Korea, Japan and Germany have large musical industries, Japan’s all-female Takarazuka Revue has performed since 1914, and Chinese and Australian productions tour widely. Schools and community theaters everywhere stage musicals, giving young people a chance to perform."]],
sp:["The Phantom of the Opera","Andrew Lloyd Webber’s The Phantom of the Opera, based on a French novel by Gaston Leroux, tells of a masked musical genius haunting the Paris Opera House. It opened in London in 1986 and on Broadway in 1988, where it ran for 35 years and nearly 14,000 performances, the longest run in Broadway history, before closing in 2023. It has been seen by well over a hundred million people worldwide."],
g:[["Book musical","A musical in which songs and dances grow from the story"],["Lyricist","The writer of a song’s words"],["Choreographer","The person who creates dances"],["Sung-through","A musical with little or no spoken dialogue"]],
f:["Show Boat (1927) was an early modern musical.","Lea Salonga won a Tony Award in 1991.","The Phantom of the Opera ran 35 years on Broadway."],
fun:"Japan’s Takarazuka Revue, with an all-female cast, has performed musicals since 1914.",
tl:"Ang musical theatre ay pagtatanghal na pinagsasama ang awit, salita, sayaw at pag-arte para magkuwento. Kilala ang Broadway at West End. Si Lea Salonga ang unang babaeng Asyano na nanalo ng Tony Award noong 1991."});

ADDART({id:"sculpture",t:"Sculpture",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🗿",w:"Sculpture",r:["michel","caveart","architecture","ukiyoe"],
p:[["Art in three dimensions","Sculpture is art made in three dimensions, by carving, modeling, casting or assembling materials."],["Among the oldest arts","The oldest known sculptures are carved figures about 40,000 years old."],["Every culture","From Egypt’s Sphinx to China’s Terracotta Army, sculpture appears in every civilization."]],
l:"Sculpture is the art of making three-dimensional forms, either freestanding figures that can be seen from all sides or reliefs that project from a flat background. Sculptors carve stone and wood, model clay and wax, cast metal and assemble found objects. Sculpture is one of humanity’s oldest arts, used to honor gods and rulers, remember the dead, decorate buildings and express ideas. Famous examples include the Great Sphinx, the Terracotta Army, the moai of Rapa Nui, Michelangelo’s David and modern works in public parks around the world.",
s:[["Techniques","Carving removes material from a block of stone or wood with chisels and other tools. Modeling builds up soft material such as clay or wax. Casting pours molten metal, often bronze, into a mold; the ancient lost-wax method used a wax model that melted away. Assemblage joins different materials and objects together."],["Ancient sculpture","Some of the oldest known sculptures, such as the Lion-man of Hohlenstein-Stadel in Germany, carved from mammoth ivory, are about 40,000 years old. Ancient Egyptians carved colossal statues of pharaohs and the Great Sphinx. The Nok culture of Nigeria made striking terracotta heads more than 2,000 years ago, and ancient Greeks created lifelike marble and bronze figures."],["Great sculptures of Asia","In 1974 farmers digging a well in Xi’an, China, discovered the Terracotta Army, about 8,000 life-size clay soldiers buried with the first emperor of China around 210 BCE. Buddhist sculpture spread across Asia, from the giant Buddhas of Leshan in China to the relief carvings of Borobudur in Indonesia and Angkor in Cambodia."],["Renaissance and beyond","Italian Renaissance sculptors revived classical styles. Donatello cast a bronze David in the 1400s, and Michelangelo carved his marble David, over 5 meters tall, between 1501 and 1504. Later, Gian Lorenzo Bernini made marble seem to move, and in the 1800s Auguste Rodin created powerful works such as The Thinker."]],
k:[["Main techniques","Carving, modeling, casting, assembling"],["Oldest known","Lion-man figure, about 40,000 years old"],["Terracotta Army","About 8,000 figures, found 1974"],["Michelangelo’s David","Carved 1501–1504"],["Common materials","Stone, wood, clay, bronze"]],
d:[["c. 38,000 BCE","The Lion-man figure is carved in Germany"],["c. 2500 BCE","The Great Sphinx of Giza is carved"],["c. 210 BCE","The Terracotta Army is buried in China"],["1504","Michelangelo completes David"],["1886","The Statue of Liberty is dedicated in New York"],["1935","The Oblation is unveiled at the University of the Philippines"]],
s2:[["The moai of Rapa Nui","On Rapa Nui, or Easter Island, in the Pacific, the Polynesian inhabitants carved nearly a thousand giant stone statues called moai, mostly between about 1250 and 1500. Many represent honored ancestors. Some are over 9 meters tall. Islanders say the statues walked to their places; experiments show they could be moved upright by rocking them with ropes."],["The Benin Bronzes","The Kingdom of Benin, in present-day Nigeria, produced thousands of brass and bronze plaques and sculptures from the 1200s onward, recording kings, warriors and court life. In 1897 British forces looted them. They were scattered among museums worldwide, and since the 2020s several countries have begun returning them to Nigeria."],["Sculpture in the Philippines","Philippine sculpture includes the carved bulul figures of the Ifugao, which guard rice harvests, and the religious santos of the Spanish colonial period. Guillermo Tolentino created the Oblation at the University of the Philippines and the Bonifacio Monument in Caloocan, and the modernist Napoleon Abueva, a National Artist, worked in wood, stone and metal."],["Modern sculpture","In the 1900s sculptors explored new forms. Constantin Brâncuși simplified shapes to their essence, Henry Moore created flowing reclining figures with holes, and Alexander Calder invented mobiles, hanging sculptures that move in the air. Louise Bourgeois built giant spider sculptures. Some artists began using light, sound and even living plants."],["Public sculpture","Statues and monuments in squares and parks honor heroes, mark events and give cities their character, such as the Statue of Liberty in New York, Christ the Redeemer in Rio de Janeiro and the Merlion in Singapore. Communities sometimes debate which figures should be honored, and some statues have been moved or removed."],["How sculpture is cared for","Outdoor sculptures face weathering, pollution and damage. Conservators clean bronze, repair cracked stone and protect wood from insects. Some famous originals, such as Michelangelo’s David, have been moved indoors to protect them, with copies placed outside."]],
sp:["The Terracotta Army","When farmers digging a well near Xi’an in 1974 found fragments of clay figures, they uncovered one of the greatest archaeological discoveries of the 20th century. The pits hold about 8,000 soldiers, along with horses and chariots, made to guard Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of a unified China, in the afterlife. Each soldier’s face is different, and they were originally painted in bright colors, which faded quickly after exposure to air."],
g:[["Relief","Sculpture that projects from a flat background"],["Lost-wax casting","Casting metal using a wax model that melts away"],["Chisel","A sharp tool for carving stone or wood"],["Mobile","A hanging sculpture that moves"]],
f:["The oldest sculptures are about 40,000 years old.","The Terracotta Army has about 8,000 figures.","Michelangelo’s David is over 5 meters tall."],
fun:"Each soldier in China’s Terracotta Army has a different face.",
tl:"Ang eskultura ay sining na may tatlong dimensiyon, ginagawa sa pag-ukit, paghulma, paghulog ng metal o pagbubuo ng mga materyales. Kabilang sa mga tanyag na halimbawa ang Terracotta Army, ang moai at ang Oblation ni Guillermo Tolentino."});

ADDART({id:"calligraphy",t:"Calligraphy",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🖌️",w:"Calligraphy",r:["print","haikuart","goldage","ukiyoe","phlit"],
p:[["Beautiful writing","Calligraphy is the art of beautiful handwriting, done with brushes or pens."],["Many traditions","Chinese, Arabic, Japanese, Indian and European calligraphy are among the great traditions."],["Art and meaning","In calligraphy, the shape of the letters is as important as the words themselves."]],
l:"Calligraphy, from Greek words meaning beautiful writing, is the art of forming letters and characters with skill and expression. Calligraphers use brushes, reed pens, quills or metal nibs, and ink on paper, silk or parchment. In East Asia and the Islamic world, calligraphy has long been considered one of the highest forms of art. Medieval European scribes copied books by hand in elegant scripts. Today calligraphy survives in art, wedding invitations, logos and the revival of traditional scripts such as Philippine baybayin.",
s:[["Chinese calligraphy","In China, calligraphy, shufa, has been honored for more than 2,000 years. Writers use a soft brush and ink to form characters with strokes of varying thickness, speed and energy. The Four Treasures of the Study are the brush, ink, paper and inkstone. Wang Xizhi, who lived in the 300s, is revered as the sage of calligraphy."],["Japanese and Korean calligraphy","Calligraphy spread from China to Korea and Japan. In Japan, shodō, the way of writing, combines Chinese characters with Japanese kana scripts and is closely linked to Zen Buddhism. Many Japanese schoolchildren practice it, especially at New Year. Korean calligraphy, seoye, uses both Chinese characters and the Korean hangul alphabet."],["Arabic calligraphy","In the Islamic world, calligraphy became the most honored art, used to write the Qur’an and decorate mosques, palaces, tiles and pottery. Styles include the angular Kufic and the flowing Naskh and Thuluth. In the 900s the scholar Ibn Muqla created rules of proportion for letters. Arabic calligraphy was added to UNESCO’s list of intangible cultural heritage in 2021."],["European calligraphy","In medieval Europe, monks and scribes copied books by hand with quill pens, creating beautiful manuscripts such as the Book of Kells, made around 800 in Ireland or Scotland. Under Charlemagne, a clear script called Carolingian minuscule was developed, which later became the basis for our lowercase letters. The printing press reduced the need for scribes, but calligraphy remained an art."]],
k:[["Meaning","Beautiful writing"],["Chinese tools","Brush, ink, paper, inkstone"],["Revered Chinese master","Wang Xizhi (4th century)"],["Arabic styles","Kufic, Naskh, Thuluth"],["Philippine script","Baybayin"]],
d:[["353 CE","Wang Xizhi writes the Preface to the Orchid Pavilion poems"],["c. 800","The Book of Kells is created"],["900s","Ibn Muqla sets rules for Arabic letter proportions"],["1593","The Doctrina Christiana is printed in Manila with baybayin"],["2009","Chinese calligraphy is recognized by UNESCO"],["2021","Arabic calligraphy is recognized by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["The Orchid Pavilion","In 353 CE, Wang Xizhi and friends gathered at the Orchid Pavilion to drink wine and write poems. Wang wrote a preface for the collection, Lantingji Xu, which became the most celebrated work in Chinese calligraphy. The original was reportedly buried with the Tang emperor Taizong, who loved it, but copies have been studied for centuries."],["Baybayin","Before Spanish colonization, many peoples in the Philippines wrote with baybayin, a script in which each character stands for a consonant with a vowel. The Doctrina Christiana, printed in Manila in 1593, included Tagalog in baybayin. Today artists and young Filipinos are reviving baybayin in calligraphy, tattoos, logos and on banknotes and government seals. Related scripts are still used by the Hanunuo Mangyan and Tagbanwa peoples."],["Indian and Tibetan calligraphy","India has many scripts, including Devanagari, Bengali, Tamil and Gurmukhi, each with calligraphic traditions in religious texts and art. Persian calligraphy flourished in Mughal India. Tibetan monks copy sacred texts in elegant scripts, and calligraphy decorates prayer flags and temple walls."],["Calligraphy and the computer","In 2005 Steve Jobs, co-founder of Apple, said that a calligraphy class he took at Reed College inspired the attractive range of typefaces in early Macintosh computers. Digital fonts today owe much to centuries of calligraphy, and many are designed by type designers trained in hand lettering."],["Learning calligraphy","Calligraphy takes patience and practice. Beginners learn to hold the brush or pen correctly, control pressure and make basic strokes before forming letters. Many people find it calming, like meditation. Popular forms today include brush lettering and modern calligraphy used in invitations and art."],["Calligraphy as art","Calligraphy can be read, but it is also enjoyed as pure art. Some Chinese and Japanese calligraphers write in a wild cursive style, almost abstract. Contemporary artists from the Middle East, such as practitioners of calligraffiti, combine Arabic letters with street art and modern painting."]],
sp:["The Four Treasures","Chinese calligraphers rely on the Four Treasures of the Study. The brush, made of animal hair, can produce strokes from hair-thin to bold. Ink comes as a solid stick, ground with water on the inkstone, a smooth stone dish. Paper, invented in China around 2,000 years ago, absorbs the ink. Fine sets of these tools have been treasured as works of art in themselves."],
g:[["Calligraphy","The art of beautiful handwriting"],["Script","A system or style of writing"],["Baybayin","A pre-colonial Philippine writing system"],["Manuscript","A book or document written by hand"]],
f:["Calligraphy means beautiful writing.","Our lowercase letters come from a script developed under Charlemagne.","Baybayin is being revived in the Philippines."],
fun:"A calligraphy class helped inspire the fonts on early Apple computers.",
tl:"Ang kaligrapiya ay sining ng magandang pagsulat gamit ang pinsel o pluma. Kabilang sa mga dakilang tradisyon ang Tsino, Arabe, Hapones at Europeo. Sa Pilipinas, muling binubuhay ang baybayin bilang sining."});

ADDART({id:"drums",t:"Drums and Percussion",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🥁",w:"Percussion instrument",r:["orchestra","phdance","jazz","musicw"],
p:[["The heartbeat of music","Percussion instruments make sound when struck, shaken or scraped."],["Among the oldest instruments","Drums have been played for thousands of years in nearly every culture."],["Rhythm everywhere","From West African talking drums to Japanese taiko and Philippine kulintang, percussion drives music."]],
l:"Percussion instruments produce sound when they are hit, shaken or scraped. They include drums, which have a skin or membrane stretched over a frame, and many other instruments such as gongs, bells, xylophones, rattles and cymbals. Percussion provides the rhythm at the heart of most music and is used in ceremonies, celebrations, communication and dance around the world. Some percussion instruments, such as the xylophone, gongs and steelpan, can also play melodies.",
s:[["Kinds of percussion","Instruments with a stretched skin, such as drums, are called membranophones. Instruments that vibrate as a whole, such as gongs, bells, cymbals and xylophones, are called idiophones. Some percussion instruments have a definite pitch, like the timpani, and others, like the snare drum, do not."],["Ancient drums","Drums are among the oldest instruments. Archaeologists in China have found drums made with alligator skin dating back several thousand years. Ancient Egypt, Mesopotamia and Mesoamerica all used drums in religious ceremonies and festivals. Bronze drums made by the Dong Son culture of Vietnam more than 2,000 years ago spread across Southeast Asia."],["Drums of Africa","Drums are central to many African musical traditions. The djembe, a goblet-shaped drum from West Africa, produces bass, tone and slap sounds. Talking drums, such as the Yoruba dùndún of Nigeria, can change pitch by squeezing cords, imitating the tones of speech so drummers can send messages and praise poetry."],["The drum kit","The drum kit, or drum set, developed in the United States in the early 1900s so that one player could play several drums and cymbals using hands and feet. It includes a bass drum played with a pedal, a snare drum, tom-toms, a hi-hat and cymbals. It became the rhythmic engine of jazz, rock, pop and many other styles."]],
k:[["Drums (membranophones)","Skin stretched over a frame"],["Idiophones","Gongs, bells, cymbals, xylophones"],["Philippine gong ensemble","Kulintang"],["Trinidad’s national instrument","Steelpan"],["Orchestral drums","Timpani"]],
d:[["c. 5500 BCE","Early drums are used in Neolithic China"],["c. 500 BCE","Dong Son bronze drums are made in Vietnam"],["Early 1900s","The drum kit develops in the United States"],["1930s–1940s","The steelpan is developed in Trinidad and Tobago"],["1981","The taiko group Kodo is founded on Sado Island, Japan"],["2005","UNESCO recognizes the gong culture of Vietnam’s Central Highlands"]],
s2:[["Kulintang","In the southern Philippines, especially among the Maguindanao, Maranao and Tausug peoples, the kulintang ensemble features a row of small bronze gongs played with sticks to make melodies, accompanied by larger gongs and a drum called the dabakan. Similar gong-chime traditions are found in Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei. In the Cordillera, the Ifugao, Kalinga and other peoples play flat gongs called gangsa."],["Taiko","Taiko are Japanese drums of many sizes, from small hand drums to huge drums over a meter wide. Once used in temples, festivals and battles, taiko became a powerful performing art in the 20th century. Ensembles such as Kodo, founded in 1981, perform energetic, athletic shows, and taiko groups now exist around the world."],["The steelpan","The steelpan, or steel drum, was developed in Trinidad and Tobago in the 1930s and 1940s, when musicians began hammering discarded oil drums into tuned instruments that can play melodies and chords. Steel bands play at Trinidad’s Carnival, and the steelpan is the country’s national instrument."],["Tabla","The tabla is a pair of hand drums from the Indian subcontinent, central to Hindustani classical music. Players use their fingers and palms to produce an astonishing range of sounds, and they learn rhythms by reciting syllables called bols. The great tabla player Zakir Hussain helped bring the tabla to audiences worldwide."],["Drums in festivals","Drums bring energy to festivals everywhere. In the Philippines, drums and percussion drive the dancing at the Ati-Atihan in Kalibo, the Sinulog in Cebu and the Dinagyang in Iloilo. In Brazil, samba schools parade with huge drum sections called baterias during Carnival. In Korea, farmers’ music, nongak, features drums and gongs."],["Percussion in the orchestra","The orchestra’s percussion section includes timpani, tuned kettledrums, as well as the bass drum, snare drum, cymbals, triangle, xylophone, glockenspiel and many others. A percussionist may play several instruments in one piece. Some composers have written entire works for percussion ensembles."]],
sp:["Drums that talk","In West Africa, the hourglass-shaped talking drum is tied with cords from top to bottom. When the drummer squeezes the drum under the arm, the skins tighten and the pitch rises. Because languages such as Yoruba use rising and falling tones to change meaning, skilled drummers can imitate speech, sending messages, announcing events and reciting proverbs and praise poems that listeners can understand."],
g:[["Percussion","Instruments played by striking, shaking or scraping"],["Membranophone","An instrument with a vibrating stretched skin"],["Idiophone","An instrument whose whole body vibrates"],["Kulintang","A Philippine ensemble of small tuned gongs"]],
f:["Drums are among the oldest instruments.","Talking drums can imitate speech.","Kulintang is a row of small tuned gongs."],
fun:"The steelpan of Trinidad and Tobago was first made from discarded oil drums.",
tl:"Ang mga instrumentong percussion ay tumutunog kapag pinalo, inalog o kinaskas. Kabilang dito ang tambol, gong at xylophone. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang kulintang, isang hanay ng maliliit na gong mula sa Mindanao."});

;
/* data/earth4.js */
// Planet Earth articles, part 4: Mount Fuji, the Rocky Mountains, waterfalls, monsoons and the deep ocean.
ADDART({id:"fuji",t:"Mount Fuji",c:"Geography",i:"🗻",w:"Mount Fuji",r:["japancountry","volc","ukiyoe","kilimanjaro","taal"],
p:[["Japan’s highest peak","Mount Fuji, at 3,776 meters, is the highest mountain in Japan."],["A sleeping volcano","It is an active volcano that last erupted in 1707."],["Sacred and beautiful","Fuji is a sacred symbol of Japan and has inspired artists for centuries."]],
l:"Mount Fuji, called Fujisan in Japanese, is the highest mountain in Japan, rising 3,776 meters on the island of Honshu about 100 kilometers southwest of Tokyo. Its almost perfectly symmetrical, snow-capped cone has made it a national symbol, celebrated in poetry, paintings and photographs. Fuji is an active stratovolcano that last erupted in 1707. Considered sacred in Shinto and Buddhist traditions, it was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2013 as a sacred place and source of artistic inspiration.",
s:[["A young volcano","Mount Fuji sits where three tectonic plates meet. It is made of layers of lava and ash built up over several older volcanoes during the past 100,000 years or so; the present cone is relatively young. Its last eruption, the Hōei eruption of 1707, came weeks after a huge earthquake and covered Edo, now Tokyo, in ash. Scientists monitor Fuji closely."],["A sacred mountain","Fuji has been worshipped for centuries. Shinto shrines called Sengen shrines honor the goddess Konohanasakuya-hime, associated with the mountain. Pilgrims climbed Fuji as a religious practice, and in the Edo period, groups called Fuji-kō organized pilgrimages and built small model Fujis for those who could not make the journey."],["Climbing Fuji","Fuji can be climbed in the summer season, usually July to early September, when the trails are free of snow. Hundreds of thousands of people climb each year, often overnight to watch the sunrise, called goraikō, from the summit. Because of crowding and safety concerns, the authorities introduced climbing fees and limits on daily climbers in 2024."],["The Fuji Five Lakes","At the northern foot of Fuji lie five lakes formed when lava flows dammed rivers: Kawaguchi, Yamanaka, Sai, Shōji and Motosu. They offer famous reflections of the mountain. The surrounding area, including forests that grew on old lava flows, is part of Fuji-Hakone-Izu National Park."]],
k:[["Height","3,776 m"],["Location","Honshu, between Shizuoka and Yamanashi prefectures"],["Type","Stratovolcano"],["Last eruption","1707"],["World Heritage Site","2013"]],
d:[["c. 100,000 years ago","Older volcanoes begin building the Fuji area"],["864–866","The Jōgan eruption creates large lava flows"],["1707","The Hōei eruption covers Edo in ash"],["c. 1831","Hokusai’s Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji are published"],["1872","Women are officially allowed to climb Fuji"],["2013","Fuji becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Fuji in art","Mount Fuji has appeared in Japanese art for more than a thousand years. Katsushika Hokusai’s print series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji, including The Great Wave, and Utagawa Hiroshige’s views made the mountain famous around the world. The ancient poetry collection Man’yōshū, from the 700s, already praised Fuji’s beauty."],["Women on the mountain","For centuries, women were forbidden to climb to the summit of Fuji for religious reasons. The ban was officially lifted in 1872. According to accounts, an Englishwoman, Fanny Parkes, climbed it in 1867, and a Japanese woman, Tatsu Takayama, had reached the summit disguised as a man in 1832."],["Weather on Fuji","The summit is cold and windy, with temperatures often below freezing even in summer nights. A weather station operated on the summit for decades, and in 1964 a weather radar was built there to track typhoons approaching Japan. Lenticular clouds, shaped like flying saucers, often form over the peak."],["Fuji and the bullet train","On clear days, passengers on the Tōkaidō Shinkansen, the high-speed train between Tokyo and Osaka, get spectacular views of Fuji. Fuji can be seen from Tokyo on clear winter days, about 100 kilometers away."],["If Fuji erupts","An eruption of Fuji could spread ash over Tokyo, disrupting trains, flights, power and water for millions of people. The Japanese government has prepared hazard maps and evacuation plans and advises residents to keep emergency supplies, even though no one knows when Fuji will erupt again."],["Overcrowding and protection","Fuji’s popularity has brought crowds, litter and traffic. Some climbers attempt the steep ascent without rest, risking altitude sickness, or in unsuitable clothing. Recent rules on fees, numbers and timing aim to protect both climbers and the mountain’s environment."]],
sp:["A perfect cone","Mount Fuji’s graceful shape comes from repeated eruptions of fluid lava and ash, piling up evenly around the central vent. From many directions, its slopes appear almost perfectly symmetrical, rising gently at the base and steeply near the top. Snow covers the peak for much of the year, usually from autumn to late spring. This classic shape has made Fuji one of the most recognizable mountains on Earth."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A tall, cone-shaped volcano of layered lava and ash"],["Goraikō","Watching the sunrise from Fuji’s summit"],["Pilgrimage","A journey to a sacred place"],["Lenticular cloud","A lens-shaped cloud that forms over mountains"]],
f:["Mount Fuji is 3,776 meters high.","Fuji last erupted in 1707.","Women were banned from climbing Fuji until 1872."],
fun:"On clear winter days, Mount Fuji can be seen from Tokyo, about 100 kilometers away.",
tl:"Ang Bundok Fuji ang pinakamataas na bundok sa Hapon, na may taas na 3,776 metro. Isa itong aktibong bulkan na huling pumutok noong 1707. Banal ito para sa mga Hapones at naging inspirasyon ng maraming alagad ng sining."});

ADDART({id:"rockies",t:"The Rocky Mountains",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Rocky Mountains",r:["usacountry","andes","geysers","alps","canyon"],
p:[["Backbone of a continent","The Rocky Mountains stretch more than 4,800 kilometers through western Canada and the United States."],["The Continental Divide","Rivers on one side of the Rockies flow toward the Pacific; on the other, toward the Atlantic and Arctic."],["Wild places","The Rockies are home to grizzly bears, elk, wolves and famous national parks."]],
l:"The Rocky Mountains, or Rockies, are a major mountain range in western North America, running more than 4,800 kilometers from northern British Columbia in Canada to New Mexico in the United States. Their highest peak is Mount Elbert in Colorado, at 4,401 meters. The Rockies form much of the Continental Divide, which separates rivers flowing to the Pacific from those flowing to the Atlantic and Arctic oceans. They are home to Indigenous nations, rich wildlife and celebrated national parks such as Banff, Jasper, Yellowstone and Glacier.",
s:[["How the Rockies formed","The Rockies were mostly raised between about 80 and 55 million years ago, during a mountain-building event called the Laramide orogeny, when the Pacific plates pushed under North America far inland. Later, erosion by rivers and Ice Age glaciers carved valleys, lakes and sharp peaks."],["Rivers of the West","Many great rivers begin in the Rockies, including the Colorado, the Missouri, the Columbia, the Arkansas and the Rio Grande. Snow that melts each spring feeds these rivers, which provide water for farms and cities across a huge area of dry land."],["Indigenous peoples","The Rockies have been home to many Indigenous nations for thousands of years, including the Shoshone, Ute, Blackfoot, Crow, Arapaho, Ktunaxa and Stoney Nakoda. They hunted bison and elk, gathered plants and traveled mountain passes for trade. Many places in the mountains remain sacred to them."],["Wildlife","The Rockies support grizzly and black bears, elk, moose, bighorn sheep, mountain goats, cougars, lynx and wolves. Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone by the 1920s and reintroduced in 1995, changing the behavior of elk and helping trees and streams recover, an example of how one species can affect a whole ecosystem."]],
k:[["Length","More than 4,800 km"],["Highest peak","Mount Elbert, 4,401 m"],["Countries","Canada and the United States"],["Formed","About 80–55 million years ago"],["Famous parks","Banff, Jasper, Yellowstone, Glacier, Rocky Mountain"]],
d:[["c. 80 million years ago","The Laramide orogeny begins raising the Rockies"],["1805","The Lewis and Clark expedition crosses the Rockies"],["1858–1859","The Pikes Peak gold rush draws settlers to Colorado"],["1885","Banff becomes Canada’s first national park; the Canadian Pacific Railway is completed"],["1909","The Burgess Shale fossils are discovered"],["1995","Gray wolves are reintroduced to Yellowstone"]],
s2:[["National parks","The Rockies contain some of North America’s most famous parks. Yellowstone, partly in the Rockies, was the world’s first national park in 1872. Banff, created in 1885, was Canada’s first, with turquoise lakes such as Lake Louise and Moraine Lake. Rocky Mountain National Park in Colorado and Glacier National Park in Montana protect high peaks, forests and alpine meadows."],["The Burgess Shale","In 1909 the American scientist Charles Walcott discovered the Burgess Shale in the Canadian Rockies, in what is now Yoho National Park. Its rocks preserve the soft bodies of strange sea creatures from about 508 million years ago, giving a rare view of early animal life during the Cambrian explosion."],["Crossing the mountains","The Rockies were a great barrier to travel. In 1805 the American Lewis and Clark expedition, guided in part by the Shoshone woman Sacagawea, crossed them on the way to the Pacific. Railways such as the Canadian Pacific, completed in 1885, used tunnels and passes, and many Chinese laborers worked on building them in dangerous conditions."],["Gold and silver","Gold and silver rushes in the 1800s brought miners and settlers to the Rockies, founding towns such as Denver and Leadville in Colorado. Many towns boomed and then were abandoned when the mines ran out, leaving ghost towns that tourists visit today."],["Changing mountains","Glaciers in the Rockies are melting as the climate warms. Glacier National Park had about 150 glaciers in 1850 and far fewer today. Warmer winters also help bark beetles spread, killing large areas of pine forest, and drier conditions increase the risk of wildfires."],["Recreation","Millions of visitors come to the Rockies each year to hike, climb, ski, fish, camp and watch wildlife. Ski resorts such as Aspen, Vail, Whistler and Banff’s Lake Louise attract skiers from around the world, and trails such as the Continental Divide Trail run thousands of kilometers along the mountains."]],
sp:["The Continental Divide","Along the crest of the Rockies runs the Continental Divide, an imaginary line separating watersheds. A raindrop falling on one side may flow to the Pacific Ocean, while one falling a few meters away on the other side may flow to the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic. At a place called Triple Divide Peak in Montana, water can flow to three oceans: the Pacific, the Atlantic and, through Hudson Bay, the Arctic."],
g:[["Orogeny","A period of mountain building"],["Continental Divide","A line separating rivers flowing to different oceans"],["Watershed","An area of land that drains into one river or sea"],["Alpine","Relating to high mountains above the tree line"]],
f:["The Rockies stretch more than 4,800 km.","Mount Elbert is the highest peak of the Rockies.","Wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995."],
fun:"From Triple Divide Peak in Montana, water can flow to three different oceans.",
tl:"Ang Rocky Mountains ay malaking hanay ng bundok sa kanlurang Hilagang Amerika, na umaabot nang higit 4,800 kilometro mula Canada hanggang Estados Unidos. Dito nagsisimula ang maraming malalaking ilog at dito matatagpuan ang mga tanyag na pambansang parke."});

ADDART({id:"waterfalls",t:"Waterfalls",c:"Geography",i:"💦",w:"Waterfall",r:["hydropower","wcycle","canyon","amazon","nile"],
p:[["Water in free fall","A waterfall is a place where a river or stream drops over a steep edge."],["Shaped by erosion","Waterfalls form where water wears away soft rock faster than hard rock."],["Natural wonders","Angel Falls, Victoria Falls, Iguazu and Niagara are among the most famous."]],
l:"A waterfall is a point in a river or stream where water flows over a vertical drop or a series of steep drops. Waterfalls form where rivers cross bands of hard and soft rock, at the edges of plateaus, in valleys carved by glaciers or along fault lines. They range from small cascades to mighty falls like Victoria Falls in Africa, which sends up spray visible from many kilometers away. The tallest waterfall, Angel Falls in Venezuela, plunges 979 meters. Waterfalls attract millions of visitors and can provide hydroelectric power.",
s:[["How waterfalls form","Many waterfalls form where a river flows over a layer of hard rock lying on top of softer rock. The water wears away the soft rock underneath faster, undercutting the hard layer until pieces break off. Over time, the waterfall slowly moves upstream, leaving a gorge. At its base, falling water digs a deep plunge pool."],["Kinds of waterfalls","Plunge waterfalls drop straight down without touching the rock. Horsetail falls stay in contact with the rock. Cascades tumble down a series of rock steps. Tiered falls drop in several distinct stages. Some waterfalls are tall and narrow, while others, like Victoria Falls, are wide curtains of water."],["The tallest","Angel Falls in Venezuela is the world’s tallest uninterrupted waterfall, with a total height of 979 meters and a main drop of about 807 meters, falling from the flat-topped mountain Auyán-tepui. Its Pemon Indigenous name is Kerepakupai Merú. It is named after the American pilot Jimmie Angel, who flew over it in 1933."],["The widest and biggest","Victoria Falls, on the border of Zambia and Zimbabwe, is about 1.7 kilometers wide and 108 meters high, forming one of the largest curtains of falling water on Earth. Iguazu Falls, on the border of Argentina and Brazil, is made of about 275 separate falls. By volume of water, falls on the Congo River in Africa are among the largest."]],
k:[["Tallest","Angel Falls, Venezuela, 979 m"],["Largest sheet of water","Victoria Falls, about 1.7 km wide"],["Most separate drops","Iguazu Falls, about 275"],["Famous in North America","Niagara Falls"],["Philippine example","Maria Cristina Falls, Iligan"]],
d:[["1855","David Livingstone names Victoria Falls after Queen Victoria"],["1895","Niagara Falls begins producing hydroelectric power"],["1901","Annie Edson Taylor goes over Niagara Falls in a barrel"],["1933","Jimmie Angel flies over Angel Falls"],["1984","Iguazu National Park in Argentina becomes a World Heritage Site"],["1989","Victoria Falls becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Mosi-oa-Tunya","Victoria Falls is known locally as Mosi-oa-Tunya, the smoke that thunders, because its spray rises hundreds of meters and its roar can be heard from far away. The Scottish missionary David Livingstone, the first European to describe it, in 1855, named it after Queen Victoria. In the wet season, more than 500 million liters of water can pour over it every minute."],["Niagara Falls","Niagara Falls, on the border of Canada and the United States, is made of three falls: Horseshoe Falls, American Falls and Bridal Veil Falls. Much of its water is diverted to power plants. Erosion once moved the falls upstream by about a meter a year, but engineering works have slowed it greatly. In 1901 a 63-year-old teacher, Annie Edson Taylor, survived going over the falls in a barrel."],["Waterfalls of the Philippines","The Philippines has many waterfalls. Maria Cristina Falls in Iligan powers hydroelectric plants that supply much of Mindanao. Pagsanjan Falls in Laguna is famous for boat rides through a gorge. Kawasan Falls in Cebu, Tinago Falls in Iligan and Asik-Asik Falls in Cotabato, which seems to pour out of a cliff of plants, attract many visitors."],["Waterfalls and power","The energy of falling water can turn turbines to make electricity. Niagara Falls was one of the first places to generate hydroelectric power on a large scale in the 1890s. Many waterfalls around the world power plants, though this can reduce the flow that visitors see."],["Life around waterfalls","The constant spray of large waterfalls creates rain forests around them, as at Victoria Falls and Iguazu, where rare plants and animals thrive. Some fish and birds, such as the great dusky swift at Iguazu, nest behind the falling water. Gobies in Hawaii climb waterfalls using suction cups on their bellies."],["Frozen and hidden falls","In very cold winters, some waterfalls freeze into towers of ice, which climbers ascend with ice axes. The biggest waterfall on Earth is actually underwater: the Denmark Strait cataract, between Greenland and Iceland, where cold, dense water sinks about 3,500 meters beneath warmer water."]],
sp:["The world’s biggest waterfall is underwater","Beneath the Denmark Strait, between Greenland and Iceland, very cold, heavy water from the Nordic seas flows over a ridge and plunges down into the deep Atlantic. It falls about 3,500 meters, more than three times the height of Angel Falls, carrying millions of cubic meters of water each second. No one can see it, since it happens entirely beneath the sea surface, but it is part of the great ocean currents that move heat around the planet."],
g:[["Plunge pool","A deep pool at the base of a waterfall"],["Gorge","A narrow valley with steep rock walls"],["Cascade","A waterfall that tumbles down rock steps"],["Erosion","The wearing away of rock and soil"]],
f:["Angel Falls is the tallest waterfall.","Victoria Falls is called the smoke that thunders.","The biggest waterfall on Earth is under the sea."],
fun:"The largest waterfall on Earth is hidden under the sea between Greenland and Iceland.",
tl:"Ang talon ay lugar kung saan bumabagsak ang tubig ng ilog sa matarik na gilid. Ang Angel Falls ang pinakamataas, at ang Victoria Falls ang isa sa pinakamalaki. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang Maria Cristina Falls at Pagsanjan Falls."});

ADDART({id:"monsoon",t:"Monsoons",c:"Geography",i:"🌧️",w:"Monsoon",r:["climate","phtyph","storms","rice","indocean"],
p:[["Seasonal winds","A monsoon is a seasonal change in wind direction that brings wet and dry seasons."],["Rain for billions","Monsoon rains water the crops that feed billions of people in Asia and Africa."],["Habagat and amihan","In the Philippines, the southwest monsoon is habagat and the northeast monsoon is amihan."]],
l:"A monsoon is a large-scale seasonal reversal of winds, bringing a rainy season and a dry season to much of the tropics. The word comes from the Arabic mawsim, meaning season. The most powerful monsoons occur in South and Southeast Asia, where moist ocean winds bring heavy rain in summer. Monsoons are vital for farming and water supplies for billions of people, but too much rain causes floods and landslides, while a weak monsoon can bring drought and hunger.",
s:[["How monsoons work","In summer, land heats up faster than the ocean. Warm air rises over the land, creating low pressure, and moist air from the ocean flows in to replace it. As the moist air rises over land and mountains, it cools and drops heavy rain. In winter, the pattern reverses: land cools faster, and dry winds blow from land toward the sea."],["The South Asian monsoon","India receives most of its yearly rain during the southwest monsoon, from about June to September. The monsoon usually reaches the southern state of Kerala around the start of June and spreads north. Its arrival is eagerly awaited, celebrated in songs and festivals, because farmers depend on it to plant rice, cotton and other crops."],["Monsoons in the Philippines","The Philippines has two main monsoons. The southwest monsoon, habagat, usually from about June to September, brings warm, moist air and heavy rains, especially to the western side of the country. The northeast monsoon, amihan, from about November to February, brings cooler, drier air. Typhoons can strengthen the habagat, causing severe flooding."],["Other monsoons","Monsoons also affect West Africa, where the rains are crucial for farmers in the Sahel; northern Australia; East Asia, where the rainy season is called meiyu in China and tsuyu in Japan; and parts of the southwestern United States and Mexico, which have a summer monsoon of thunderstorms."]],
k:[["Word origin","Arabic mawsim, season"],["South Asian rainy season","About June–September"],["Philippine southwest monsoon","Habagat"],["Philippine northeast monsoon","Amihan"],["Wettest places","Mawsynram and Cherrapunji, India"]],
d:[["1st century BCE","Greek sailors learn to use monsoon winds to sail to India"],["1686","Edmond Halley explains monsoons by the heating of land and sea"],["1875","The India Meteorological Department is founded"],["1972","PAGASA, the Philippine weather agency, is established"],["2005","Record monsoon rains flood Mumbai"],["2012","Habagat rains flood much of Metro Manila"]],
s2:[["Sailing with the monsoon","For thousands of years, sailors in the Indian Ocean used the monsoon winds to travel. They sailed from Arabia and East Africa to India with the southwest monsoon and returned with the northeast monsoon. This seasonal rhythm shaped trade in spices, textiles and other goods linking Asia, Africa and the Middle East."],["The wettest places on Earth","Mawsynram and Cherrapunji in the hills of Meghalaya, India, are among the wettest places on Earth, receiving more than 11 meters of rain a year on average, mostly during the monsoon, as moist winds from the Bay of Bengal rise over the hills. People there use umbrellas called knups, woven from bamboo and leaves."],["Floods and landslides","Monsoon rains can cause disastrous floods. In 2010 monsoon floods covered about a fifth of Pakistan, affecting some 20 million people. In the Philippines, habagat rains in 2012, enhanced by a typhoon, submerged large parts of Metro Manila. Deforestation, poor drainage and building on floodplains make floods worse."],["When the monsoon fails","If the monsoon is late or weak, crops fail and water runs short. In the past, failed monsoons led to terrible famines in India and elsewhere. Today irrigation, reservoirs, weather forecasting and food storage reduce the risk, but farmers still depend greatly on the rains."],["El Niño and the monsoon","El Niño, a warming of the eastern Pacific Ocean that occurs every few years, often weakens the South Asian monsoon and brings drier conditions to the Philippines, Indonesia and Australia. La Niña, its opposite, often brings more rain. Forecasters watch the Pacific to predict monsoon strength."],["Monsoons and climate change","Scientists expect a warmer climate to make monsoon rainfall more intense and variable, with heavier downpours and longer dry spells in some regions. This could increase the risk of both floods and droughts for the billions of people who depend on monsoon rains."]],
sp:["Amihan and Habagat in Filipino life","The words amihan and habagat are part of everyday Filipino life. Weather forecasts on television announce when the habagat season begins, signaling the rainy season and school class suspensions during heavy rains. The amihan brings cool, breezy months around Christmas, when many festivals take place. Fishermen and farmers have long planned their work around these seasonal winds."],
g:[["Monsoon","A seasonal reversal of winds bringing wet and dry seasons"],["Habagat","The southwest monsoon in the Philippines"],["Amihan","The northeast monsoon in the Philippines"],["El Niño","A periodic warming of the eastern Pacific Ocean"]],
f:["Monsoon comes from the Arabic word for season.","India gets most of its rain during the summer monsoon.","Amihan brings cooler air to the Philippines."],
fun:"People in Meghalaya, India, one of the wettest places on Earth, use woven umbrellas called knups.",
tl:"Ang monsoon o habagat at amihan ay pana-panahong pagbabago ng direksiyon ng hangin na nagdadala ng tag-ulan at tag-araw. Mahalaga ang ulan nito sa pagsasaka ng bilyon-bilyong tao, ngunit maaari rin itong magdulot ng baha."});

ADDART({id:"trench",t:"The Deep Ocean and Ocean Trenches",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Deep sea",r:["pacific","submarine","tect","geysers"],
p:[["The largest habitat","The deep ocean is the largest living space on Earth, dark, cold and under huge pressure."],["Ocean trenches","Trenches are the deepest places on Earth, formed where tectonic plates dive beneath each other."],["Mostly unexplored","Much of the deep seafloor has never been mapped in detail or seen by people."]],
l:"The deep ocean, below about 200 meters, is where sunlight fades and the waters become cold, dark and crushingly heavy. It makes up most of the ocean and is the largest habitat on Earth, home to strange creatures adapted to darkness and pressure. The deepest parts are ocean trenches, long, narrow valleys formed where one tectonic plate sinks beneath another. The Mariana Trench in the Pacific reaches nearly 11 kilometers deep at the Challenger Deep, and the Philippine Trench is among the deepest in the world.",
s:[["Zones of the ocean","Scientists divide the ocean into zones by depth. The sunlit zone, down to about 200 meters, has enough light for plants to grow. The twilight zone, to about 1,000 meters, is dim. Below lie the midnight zone, the abyssal zone, from about 4,000 meters, and the hadal zone, below about 6,000 meters, found only in trenches."],["How trenches form","Trenches form at subduction zones, where a heavy ocean plate slides beneath another plate and sinks into the mantle. The bending plate forms a deep, curved valley. Subduction zones also produce powerful earthquakes, tsunamis and chains of volcanoes, such as those of Japan, Indonesia and the Philippines."],["The Mariana Trench","The Mariana Trench in the western Pacific, east of the Mariana Islands, is the deepest known place on Earth. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, is about 10,900 meters below sea level. If Mount Everest were placed there, its peak would still be more than 2 kilometers underwater. The pressure is over 1,000 times that at the surface."],["Life in the dark","Deep-sea animals have remarkable adaptations. Many make their own light, called bioluminescence, to find food, attract mates or confuse predators. Anglerfish dangle glowing lures. Gulper eels have huge mouths. Many creatures feed on marine snow, flakes of dead material drifting down from above."]],
k:[["Average ocean depth","About 3,700 m"],["Deepest point","Challenger Deep, about 10,900 m"],["Deepest zone","Hadal zone, below 6,000 m"],["Philippine Trench","More than 10,000 m deep"],["Pressure at the deepest point","Over 1,000 times surface pressure"]],
d:[["1872–1876","HMS Challenger’s expedition explores the deep sea"],["1875","The Challenger expedition records the depth of the Mariana Trench"],["1960","Trieste reaches the Challenger Deep"],["1977","Hydrothermal vents and their life are discovered"],["2012","James Cameron dives alone to the Challenger Deep"],["2023","A snailfish is filmed at over 8,300 m, the deepest fish yet seen"]],
s2:[["The Philippine Trench","The Philippine Trench runs about 1,300 kilometers along the eastern side of the Philippines, where the Philippine Sea Plate sinks beneath the islands. It is one of the deepest trenches in the world, reaching more than 10,000 meters at a point called the Emden Deep. In 2021 Filipino scientist Deo Florence Onda and explorer Victor Vescovo dived to its deepest point, the first Filipino to do so."],["The Challenger expedition","From 1872 to 1876, the British ship HMS Challenger sailed around the world studying the oceans, measuring depths by lowering weighted ropes and collecting thousands of new species. It recorded a depth of about 8,200 meters near the Mariana Islands, and the Challenger Deep is named after it. The expedition is considered the birth of modern oceanography."],["Hydrothermal vents","In 1977 scientists in the submersible Alvin discovered hot springs on the seafloor near the Galápagos Islands, surrounded by giant tube worms, clams and crabs. These creatures rely on bacteria that make food from chemicals in the vent water, not from sunlight. Some scientists think life on Earth may have begun at such vents."],["The deepest fish","Snailfish are small, pale fish with soft bodies that live in trenches. In 2023 scientists filmed a snailfish at a depth of over 8,300 meters in a trench off Japan, the deepest fish ever recorded. Scientists think fish cannot survive much deeper because of the effects of pressure on their bodies."],["Mapping the seafloor","Most of the seafloor has never been mapped in high detail; parts of the Moon and Mars are mapped better. Ships use sonar to measure depth by timing echoes. The international Seabed 2030 project aims to map the whole ocean floor in detail, and by the mid-2020s about a quarter had been mapped."],["Threats to the deep","Even the deep sea is affected by people. Plastic has been found in the Mariana Trench, and deep-sea animals contain pollutants from the surface. Some companies want to mine metal-rich nodules from the seafloor for batteries, but scientists warn this could destroy fragile habitats that take thousands of years to recover. Countries are debating rules at the International Seabed Authority."]],
sp:["Giants of the deep","The giant squid, which can grow longer than a school bus, lived for centuries in sailors’ legends of sea monsters. Scientists had only dead or dying specimens until 2004, when Japanese researchers photographed a live giant squid in its natural habitat, and in 2012 it was filmed in the deep sea for the first time. Its eyes, as big as dinner plates, are among the largest of any animal, helping it see in the dim depths."],
g:[["Trench","A long, deep valley in the ocean floor"],["Subduction","When one tectonic plate sinks beneath another"],["Bioluminescence","Light produced by living things"],["Hadal zone","The deepest ocean zone, found in trenches"]],
f:["The Challenger Deep is nearly 11 km deep.","Many deep-sea animals make their own light.","The Philippine Trench is one of the deepest on Earth."],
fun:"If Mount Everest were placed in the Challenger Deep, its peak would still be underwater.",
tl:"Ang malalim na karagatan ay madilim, malamig at may napakalakas na presyon. Ang mga trench ang pinakamalalim na lugar sa Earth, gaya ng Mariana Trench. Ang Philippine Trench ay isa rin sa pinakamalalim sa mundo."});

;
/* data/space4.js */
// Space articles, part 4: Mars rovers, the asteroid belt, spacesuits and how the Solar System formed.
ADDART({id:"marsrovers",t:"Mars Rovers",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🤖",w:"Mars rover",r:["mars","astrobio","robot","spaceagencies","rocket"],
p:[["Robots on Mars","Mars rovers are robotic vehicles that drive across the surface of Mars to study it."],["Searching for water and life","Rovers have found evidence that Mars once had lakes, rivers and conditions suitable for life."],["Controlled from Earth","Scientists on Earth send commands, but signals take several minutes each way."]],
l:"Mars rovers are wheeled robots sent to explore the surface of Mars. Equipped with cameras, drills, lasers and laboratories, they study rocks, soil and air to understand the planet’s history and whether it ever supported life. Since 1997, NASA has landed five successful rovers, and China landed its Zhurong rover in 2021. Together they have shown that ancient Mars had liquid water, lakes and the chemical ingredients for life, transforming our picture of the Red Planet.",
s:[["Sojourner","The first rover to work on Mars was Sojourner, a six-wheeled robot the size of a microwave oven, carried by NASA’s Mars Pathfinder lander in July 1997. It explored rocks near the landing site for almost three months, proving that a rover could operate on another planet."],["Spirit and Opportunity","In January 2004, the twin rovers Spirit and Opportunity landed on opposite sides of Mars. Planned to last 90 Martian days, Spirit worked for over six years, and Opportunity for over fourteen, driving more than 45 kilometers, farther than a marathon. They found minerals and rocks that form in water, showing Mars was once wet."],["Curiosity","Curiosity, about the size of a small car, landed in Gale Crater in August 2012, lowered by a rocket-powered sky crane. Powered by a nuclear battery, it has been climbing Mount Sharp, a mountain of layered rock. It found that Gale Crater once held a long-lasting lake with the chemistry needed for microbial life, and detected organic molecules."],["Perseverance","Perseverance landed in Jezero Crater, an ancient lake with a river delta, in February 2021. It searches for signs of past life and collects rock samples in sealed tubes for possible return to Earth. In 2024 it found a rock with unusual spots that scientists consider a possible sign of ancient life, though other explanations remain possible."]],
k:[["First rover","Sojourner, 1997"],["Longest-working","Opportunity, 2004–2018"],["Largest","Perseverance and Curiosity"],["Chinese rover","Zhurong, 2021"],["Signal delay","About 3 to 22 minutes each way"]],
d:[["1971","The Soviet Mars 3 lander carries a small rover but fails soon after landing"],["1997","Sojourner explores Mars"],["2004","Spirit and Opportunity land"],["2012","Curiosity lands in Gale Crater"],["2021","Perseverance and China’s Zhurong land; Ingenuity makes the first powered flight on another planet"],["2024","Perseverance finds a rock with possible signs of ancient life"]],
s2:[["Ingenuity","Perseverance carried Ingenuity, a small helicopter weighing about 1.8 kilograms. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet, rising about 3 meters in the thin Martian air. Designed for just five test flights, it flew 72 times before a damaged rotor ended its mission in January 2024."],["Zhurong","China’s Tianwen-1 mission orbited Mars and, in May 2021, landed the Zhurong rover in a plain called Utopia Planitia, making China the second country to operate a rover on Mars. Named after a god of fire in Chinese mythology, Zhurong studied the soil and underground layers with radar, finding signs of past water."],["Driving on Mars","Because radio signals take minutes to travel between Earth and Mars, rovers cannot be driven with a joystick. Engineers plan each day’s moves, send commands and wait for results. Modern rovers can also navigate on their own, using cameras to spot hazards. Rovers usually move only tens or hundreds of meters a day."],["Seven minutes of terror","Landing on Mars is extremely difficult. A spacecraft enters the atmosphere at about 20,000 km/h and must slow to a gentle touchdown in about seven minutes, without help from Earth, using a heat shield, parachute and rockets. Engineers call this the seven minutes of terror. Many earlier Mars landers crashed."],["Dust storms and endings","Mars has huge dust storms that can cover the whole planet. In 2018 a global storm blocked sunlight from Opportunity’s solar panels, and it fell silent; its last message was interpreted by many as saying its battery was low and it was getting dark. Spirit got stuck in soft sand in 2009 and stopped communicating in 2010."],["Future rovers","The European Space Agency is preparing the Rosalind Franklin rover, which will drill up to 2 meters below the surface, where signs of life might be protected from radiation. Space agencies are also studying ways to bring Perseverance’s samples back to Earth and, in the longer term, to send human explorers to Mars."]],
sp:["The sky crane","Curiosity and Perseverance were too heavy for airbags, which had cushioned earlier rovers bouncing onto the surface. Engineers invented the sky crane: a rocket-powered platform hovered about 20 meters above the ground and lowered the rover on cables. When the wheels touched down, the cables were cut and the platform flew away to crash at a safe distance. The bold idea worked perfectly both times."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that drives across the surface of a planet or moon"],["Sky crane","A rocket platform that lowers a rover on cables"],["Organic molecule","A carbon-based molecule, a building block of life"],["Delta","Sediment deposited where a river enters a lake or sea"]],
f:["Opportunity worked on Mars for over 14 years.","Ingenuity was the first helicopter to fly on another planet.","China landed its Zhurong rover on Mars in 2021."],
fun:"The Opportunity rover was planned to last 90 days but worked for more than 14 years.",
tl:"Ang Mars rover ay robot na sasakyan na naglalakbay sa ibabaw ng Mars para pag-aralan ito. Natuklasan ng mga rover na dati ay may mga lawa at ilog sa Mars. Kabilang dito ang Sojourner, Opportunity, Curiosity, Perseverance at ang Zhurong ng Tsina."});

ADDART({id:"asteroidbelt",t:"The Asteroid Belt",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪨",w:"Asteroid belt",r:["comet","solar","jupiter","pluto","meteors"],
p:[["Rocks between the planets","The asteroid belt is a ring-shaped region between Mars and Jupiter filled with rocky and metallic bodies."],["Leftovers","Asteroids are leftovers from the formation of the Solar System about 4.6 billion years ago."],["Mostly empty space","Despite the millions of asteroids, they are spread so far apart that spacecraft pass through safely."]],
l:"The asteroid belt is a broad ring of space between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter where most of the Solar System’s asteroids orbit the Sun. It contains the dwarf planet Ceres, large asteroids such as Vesta and Pallas, and millions of smaller rocks. Asteroids are remnants of the early Solar System, material that never formed a planet, probably because Jupiter’s powerful gravity stirred it up. Despite its crowded image in films, the belt is mostly empty space, and the total mass of all its asteroids is only a few percent of the Moon’s.",
s:[["Discovery","On 1 January 1801, the Italian astronomer Giuseppe Piazzi discovered Ceres, which was at first thought to be a missing planet between Mars and Jupiter. Soon Pallas, Juno and Vesta were found. As more and more were discovered, astronomers realized they were a new class of small bodies, which William Herschel called asteroids, meaning star-like."],["Why no planet formed","As the planets formed from a disk of dust and gas, the material in this region should have clumped together into a planet. But the gravity of nearby Jupiter stirred up the small bodies, making them collide too fast to stick together. Many were thrown out of the region, leaving only a small fraction of the original material."],["Kinds of asteroids","Most asteroids fall into three main types. Dark, carbon-rich C-type asteroids are the most common, especially in the outer belt. Stony S-type asteroids are found more in the inner belt. Metallic M-type asteroids may be the iron cores of shattered early worlds."],["Ceres","Ceres, about 940 kilometers across, is the largest object in the belt and contains roughly a third of its total mass. In 2006 it was classified as a dwarf planet. It has a rocky core, an icy mantle and possibly a layer of salty water deep inside. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft found bright patches of salt in Occator Crater."]],
k:[["Location","Between Mars and Jupiter"],["Largest object","Ceres, about 940 km wide"],["First discovered","Ceres, 1 January 1801"],["Total mass","A few percent of the Moon’s"],["Main types","C-type, S-type, M-type"]],
d:[["1801","Giuseppe Piazzi discovers Ceres"],["1807","Vesta is discovered"],["1866","Daniel Kirkwood discovers gaps in the belt caused by Jupiter"],["2011","Dawn begins orbiting Vesta"],["2015","Dawn begins orbiting Ceres"],["2023","NASA’s Psyche spacecraft launches toward a metal-rich asteroid"]],
s2:[["The Dawn mission","NASA’s Dawn spacecraft, launched in 2007 and powered by ion engines, orbited Vesta from 2011 to 2012 and then Ceres from 2015 until 2018. It was the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond the Earth–Moon system. It revealed Vesta’s giant impact basin and Ceres’s bright salt deposits and icy interior."],["Kirkwood gaps","In 1866 the American astronomer Daniel Kirkwood noticed gaps in the asteroid belt where few asteroids orbit. Asteroids at these distances would circle the Sun in a simple ratio to Jupiter’s orbit, for example three times for every one of Jupiter’s, so Jupiter’s regular tugs push them out of those orbits."],["Psyche","Psyche is an asteroid about 220 kilometers wide that appears to be rich in metal, perhaps the exposed core of an early planet. NASA’s Psyche spacecraft, launched in October 2023, is due to arrive in 2029. Studying it could reveal what lies deep inside rocky planets like Earth, where we cannot go."],["Not like the movies","In films, spaceships dodge through crowded fields of tumbling rocks. In reality, asteroids in the belt are usually hundreds of thousands of kilometers apart. Spacecraft such as Pioneer 10, Voyager and New Horizons have flown through the belt without any danger of collision."],["Trojans and near-Earth asteroids","Not all asteroids are in the belt. Trojan asteroids share Jupiter’s orbit, gathered in two swarms ahead of and behind the planet; NASA’s Lucy mission, launched in 2021, is visiting several of them. Near-Earth asteroids have orbits that bring them close to Earth; Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx brought back samples from two such asteroids, Ryugu and Bennu."],["Mining asteroids","Some asteroids contain valuable metals such as iron, nickel and platinum, and others contain water ice that could be turned into rocket fuel. Companies and space agencies have studied asteroid mining, but it remains far in the future because of the great cost and difficulty."]],
sp:["Bright spots on Ceres","As Dawn approached Ceres in 2015, it spotted mysterious bright spots shining from inside Occator Crater. Up close, they turned out to be deposits of sodium carbonate, a kind of salt, left behind when salty water rose from below and evaporated. The discovery suggests that Ceres may still have pockets of liquid brine deep beneath its surface, making this small world more active than once thought."],
g:[["Asteroid","A small rocky or metallic body orbiting the Sun"],["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Kirkwood gap","A gap in the belt caused by Jupiter’s gravity"],["Ion engine","A spacecraft engine that uses electrically charged particles"]],
f:["Ceres was the first asteroid discovered.","The asteroid belt is mostly empty space.","Dawn orbited both Vesta and Ceres."],
fun:"All the asteroids in the belt together weigh only a few percent as much as our Moon.",
tl:"Ang asteroid belt ay rehiyon sa pagitan ng Mars at Jupiter na puno ng mga batong umiikot sa araw. Ito ay mga natira mula sa pagbuo ng Solar System. Ang Ceres ang pinakamalaking bagay rito."});

ADDART({id:"spacesuits",t:"Spacesuits",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧑‍🚀",w:"Space suit",r:["astronauts","iss","apollo","space"],
p:[["A personal spacecraft","A spacesuit protects an astronaut from the dangers of space, like a small spacecraft shaped for a person."],["Air, pressure and temperature","Suits supply oxygen, keep the body under pressure and protect against extreme heat and cold."],["Walking in space","Astronauts wear special suits for spacewalks outside spacecraft and on the Moon."]],
l:"A spacesuit is a garment that keeps a person alive in the vacuum of space or in the low pressure of high altitudes. Space has no air to breathe, no pressure to keep body fluids from boiling and temperatures that swing from scorching in sunlight to freezing in shadow. Suits worn for spacewalks are complex machines, with layers of protective fabric, a pressurized inner bladder, a helmet, gloves and a backpack that supplies oxygen, removes carbon dioxide, controls temperature and provides communications.",
s:[["Why suits are needed","Without a suit, a person exposed to space would lose consciousness within about 15 seconds as oxygen left the blood. The lack of pressure would make body fluids begin to vaporize. Unfiltered sunlight would burn, and tiny meteoroids could strike. Temperatures outside can range from above 120°C in sunlight to below minus 150°C in shadow."],["Layers of protection","A spacewalk suit has many layers. Next to the skin, a garment with tubes of cool water removes body heat. A pressure bladder holds air in, and a restraint layer keeps it from ballooning. Outer layers of strong, insulating and reflective fabrics protect against heat, cold, radiation and micrometeoroids."],["Two kinds of suits","Launch and entry suits, such as the Russian Sokol and SpaceX’s suits, protect astronauts inside spacecraft if the cabin loses pressure. Spacewalk suits, such as NASA’s Extravehicular Mobility Unit and Russia’s Orlan, let astronauts work outside for many hours. A spacewalk suit with its backpack weighs well over 100 kilograms on Earth but feels weightless in orbit."],["Working in a suit","Moving in a pressurized suit is tiring, like squeezing a balloon with every motion. Gloves are especially hard to bend, and astronauts’ hands can become sore and fingernails damaged after long spacewalks. Astronauts train underwater in huge pools wearing suits to practice tasks in conditions similar to weightlessness."]],
k:[["First spacewalk","Alexei Leonov, 18 March 1965"],["First moonwalk suits","Apollo A7L, 1969"],["NASA spacewalk suit","Extravehicular Mobility Unit (EMU)"],["Russian spacewalk suit","Orlan"],["First commercial spacewalk","Polaris Dawn, 2024"]],
d:[["1934","Pilot Wiley Post flies high using an early pressure suit"],["1965","Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","Apollo 11 astronauts walk on the Moon"],["1984","Bruce McCandless makes the first untethered spacewalk"],["2008","Zhai Zhigang makes China’s first spacewalk"],["2019","Christina Koch and Jessica Meir make the first all-female spacewalk"]],
s2:[["Leonov’s dangerous spacewalk","On 18 March 1965, the Soviet cosmonaut Alexei Leonov floated outside Voskhod 2 for about 12 minutes, the first spacewalk. But his suit ballooned in the vacuum, and he could not fit back through the airlock. He secretly let air out of the suit, risking decompression sickness, to squeeze back inside. He later became a celebrated space artist."],["Suits for the Moon","The Apollo A7L suits, worn by the 12 astronauts who walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, had about 21 layers and a backpack life-support system. Lunar dust was a big problem: it was sharp and clingy, wearing away fabrics and seals. New suits being developed for future Moon missions must be more flexible and resistant to dust."],["Flying free","In February 1984, the American astronaut Bruce McCandless flew about 100 meters away from the Space Shuttle Challenger using a jet-powered backpack called the Manned Maneuvering Unit, with no safety line. The photo of him floating alone above Earth became one of the most famous images of spaceflight."],["Suits around the world","Different countries have developed their own suits. Russia’s Orlan suits are entered through a door in the back. China’s Feitian suit was first used in 2008, when Zhai Zhigang made China’s first spacewalk. Astronauts from many countries have worn American and Russian suits on the International Space Station."],["Fitting every astronaut","Spacesuits must fit well to work safely. In 2019 a planned all-female spacewalk was postponed because only one medium-size suit torso was ready. In October 2019 Christina Koch and Jessica Meir completed the first all-female spacewalk. Future suits are being designed to fit a wider range of body sizes."],["Close calls","Spacewalks are risky. In 2013 the Italian astronaut Luca Parmitano’s helmet began filling with water from a leak in the cooling system during a spacewalk, covering his eyes, nose and ears. He made his way back to the airlock by memory and touch. Engineers fixed the problem and added absorbent pads and snorkels to helmets."]],
sp:["Wiley Post’s pressure suit","In 1934 the American aviator Wiley Post wanted to fly high into the jet stream, where the air is too thin to breathe. With the B. F. Goodrich company, he developed one of the first practical pressure suits, made of rubber and fabric with a metal helmet that looked like a diving helmet. He flew to heights of over 12,000 meters. His suit helped lay the foundation for the spacesuits of later decades."],
g:[["Spacewalk","Activity outside a spacecraft in space"],["Pressure suit","A suit that keeps the body at safe air pressure"],["Airlock","A chamber that lets astronauts move between spacecraft and space"],["Life support","Systems that provide air, temperature control and other needs"]],
f:["Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk in 1965.","Spacesuits contain water tubes to cool astronauts.","The first all-female spacewalk was in 2019."],
fun:"The first spacewalker had to let air out of his ballooning suit to get back inside.",
tl:"Ang spacesuit ay kasuotang nagpoprotekta sa astronaut sa panganib ng kalawakan. Nagbibigay ito ng oxygen, presyon at proteksiyon laban sa matinding init at lamig. Si Alexei Leonov ang unang naglakad sa kalawakan noong 1965."});

ADDART({id:"solarform",t:"How the Solar System Formed",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Formation and evolution of the Solar System",r:["solar","sun","nebula","moon","kuiper"],
p:[["Born from a cloud","The Solar System formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust."],["A spinning disk","The Sun formed at the center, and the planets grew from a flat disk of material around it."],["Rocky and giant worlds","Rocky planets formed near the Sun, and gas and ice giants formed farther out."]],
l:"The Solar System formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a giant cloud of gas and dust that collapsed under its own gravity. Most of the material fell to the center to form the Sun, while the rest flattened into a spinning disk. Within the disk, dust grains stuck together into pebbles, boulders and eventually planets. This idea, called the nebular hypothesis, was first proposed in the 1700s and is now supported by meteorites, computer models and telescope images of young stars surrounded by planet-forming disks.",
s:[["The solar nebula","The Sun and planets began in a cold cloud of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, with dust containing heavier elements made by earlier generations of stars. Something, perhaps the shock wave from a nearby exploding star, caused part of the cloud to collapse. As it shrank, it spun faster and flattened into a disk, like pizza dough spun in the air."],["The Sun ignites","Most of the mass gathered at the center, becoming hotter and denser until nuclear fusion began, turning hydrogen into helium. The Sun was born. It contains about 99.8 percent of all the mass in the Solar System; everything else, including all the planets, makes up the remaining 0.2 percent."],["Building planets","In the disk, dust grains collided and stuck together, growing into larger bodies called planetesimals, a few kilometers across. Their gravity pulled in more material, and they collided and merged to form protoplanets and eventually planets. This process took from a few million to tens of millions of years."],["Rocky and giant planets","Close to the hot young Sun, only rock and metal could stay solid, so small rocky planets formed: Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. Beyond a boundary called the frost line, water and other substances froze into ice, providing more building material. There, large cores formed quickly and pulled in vast amounts of gas, becoming Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune."]],
k:[["Age","About 4.6 billion years"],["Theory","Nebular hypothesis"],["Sun’s share of mass","About 99.8 percent"],["Oldest solids","Mineral grains in meteorites, about 4.57 billion years old"],["Key boundary","The frost line"]],
d:[["1755","Immanuel Kant suggests the Solar System formed from a rotating cloud"],["1796","Pierre-Simon Laplace develops the nebular hypothesis"],["1950s","Meteorite dating fixes the Solar System’s age at about 4.5 billion years"],["2005","The Nice model proposes that the giant planets moved after forming"],["2014","ALMA images a planet-forming disk around the young star HL Tauri"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth, a pristine building block"]],
s2:[["Clues from meteorites","Some meteorites contain tiny white grains called calcium-aluminum-rich inclusions, the oldest known solids in the Solar System. By measuring radioactive elements in them, scientists have dated them to about 4.567 billion years ago, marking the birth of the Solar System. Meteorites are like time capsules from that era."],["Disks around other stars","Telescopes now show planet-forming disks around young stars. In 2014 the ALMA radio telescope in Chile captured an image of the disk around HL Tauri, with dark rings that may be gaps cleared by forming planets. Such images confirm that our Solar System’s birth was not unusual."],["The birth of the Moon","Not long after Earth formed, about 4.5 billion years ago, a body about the size of Mars, named Theia, is thought to have collided with it. The impact threw debris into orbit, which gathered to form the Moon. This giant-impact hypothesis explains why the Moon has a small iron core and rocks similar to Earth’s."],["Wandering giant planets","Computer models suggest that the giant planets did not stay where they formed. Their gravity interacted with leftover planetesimals, causing Jupiter to move slightly inward and Saturn, Uranus and Neptune outward. Such movements may have scattered comets and asteroids, sending some crashing into the inner planets and helping shape the Kuiper Belt."],["Leftover building blocks","Asteroids, comets and Kuiper Belt objects are leftovers from planet formation. In 2019 NASA’s New Horizons spacecraft flew past Arrokoth, a small Kuiper Belt object made of two lobes gently stuck together. Its shape supports the idea that planetesimals formed by the gentle collapse of clouds of pebbles."],["The Sun’s future","The Sun is about halfway through its life. In about 5 billion years, it will run low on hydrogen in its core and swell into a red giant, probably swallowing Mercury and Venus and perhaps Earth. It will then shed its outer layers, leaving behind a small, hot white dwarf that will slowly cool over billions of years."]],
sp:["Made of stardust","The hydrogen in the Solar System dates back to the Big Bang, but almost all the heavier elements, the carbon in our bodies, the oxygen we breathe, the iron in our blood and the silicon in rocks, were made inside earlier stars that lived and died before the Sun was born. Those stars scattered their elements into space, enriching the cloud from which the Solar System formed. In a real sense, we are made of stardust."],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"],["Planetesimal","A small body that combines with others to form planets"],["Frost line","The distance from a star beyond which ice can form"],["Protoplanetary disk","A disk of gas and dust around a young star"]],
f:["The Solar System is about 4.6 billion years old.","The Sun holds about 99.8 percent of the Solar System’s mass.","The Moon probably formed from a giant collision."],
fun:"The atoms in your body were made inside stars that died before the Sun was born.",
tl:"Nabuo ang Solar System mga 4.6 bilyong taon na ang nakararaan mula sa ulap ng gas at alikabok. Nabuo ang araw sa gitna, at ang mga planeta ay mula sa umiikot na disk sa paligid nito."});

;
/* data/science4.js */
// Science and Numbers articles, part 4: molecules, measurement, simple machines, percentages and friction.
ADDART({id:"molecules",t:"Molecules and Chemical Bonds",c:"Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Molecule",r:["atom","reactions","carbonchem","crystals","mixtures"],
p:[["Atoms joined together","A molecule is a group of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds."],["Sharing and swapping electrons","Atoms bond by sharing electrons or by giving and taking them."],["Shapes matter","The shape of a molecule decides how it behaves, from how water flows to how medicines work."]],
l:"A molecule is the smallest unit of a substance that keeps its chemical properties, made of atoms joined by chemical bonds. A water molecule, H₂O, has two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom; oxygen gas is made of molecules of two oxygen atoms. Molecules range from tiny pairs of atoms to giant chains like DNA containing billions of atoms. Chemical bonds form because atoms become more stable by sharing or transferring their outer electrons. Understanding bonds and molecular shapes explains much of chemistry and biology.",
s:[["Covalent bonds","In a covalent bond, two atoms share a pair of electrons, which holds them together. Atoms can share one, two or three pairs, forming single, double or triple bonds. Water, carbon dioxide, sugar and the proteins in our bodies are held together by covalent bonds. The American chemist Gilbert Lewis described the shared-electron-pair bond in 1916."],["Ionic bonds","In an ionic bond, one atom gives one or more electrons to another. The atom that loses electrons becomes a positive ion, and the one that gains them becomes a negative ion, and opposite charges attract. Table salt, sodium chloride, forms this way: sodium gives an electron to chlorine. Ionic compounds usually form crystals and dissolve in water."],["Forces between molecules","Weaker attractions also act between molecules. Hydrogen bonds, attractions between slightly positive hydrogen atoms and slightly negative atoms such as oxygen, hold water molecules close together, giving water its high boiling point and surface tension. Hydrogen bonds also hold the two strands of DNA together."],["Molecular shapes","Molecules have definite 3D shapes because electron pairs push each other apart. Water is bent, with an angle of about 104.5 degrees; carbon dioxide is straight; and methane is shaped like a pyramid with a triangular base, called a tetrahedron. A molecule’s shape affects its smell, taste and how it fits into other molecules, such as enzymes and receptors."]],
k:[["Example","Water, H₂O"],["Main bond types","Covalent, ionic, metallic"],["Avogadro constant","About 6.022 × 10²³ per mole"],["Water bond angle","About 104.5°"],["Key book","The Nature of the Chemical Bond (Pauling, 1939)"]],
d:[["1808","John Dalton publishes his atomic theory"],["1811","Amedeo Avogadro proposes that gases contain molecules"],["1916","Gilbert Lewis describes the shared electron-pair bond"],["1939","Linus Pauling publishes The Nature of the Chemical Bond"],["1953","The double-helix structure of DNA is described"],["2009","Scientists at IBM image the bonds of a single molecule"]],
s2:[["Avogadro’s idea","In 1811 the Italian scientist Amedeo Avogadro proposed that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of particles. He suggested that gases such as oxygen are made of molecules of two atoms. His idea was largely ignored for about 50 years. The number of particles in one mole of a substance, about 602 sextillion, is called the Avogadro constant in his honor."],["Linus Pauling","The American chemist Linus Pauling used quantum mechanics to explain how atoms bond and why molecules have particular shapes. His 1939 book The Nature of the Chemical Bond became one of the most influential science books ever. He won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1954 and the Nobel Peace Prize in 1962 for campaigning against nuclear weapons testing."],["Seeing a molecule","Molecules are far too small to see with ordinary microscopes. In 2009 scientists at IBM in Zurich used an atomic force microscope, with a tip ending in a single molecule, to image a molecule called pentacene, showing its five rings of atoms and the bonds between them. Such images confirm the structures chemists had worked out for decades."],["Mirror-image molecules","Some molecules exist in two forms that are mirror images, like left and right hands, and cannot be placed exactly on top of each other. This property is called chirality. The two forms can behave differently: one form of the molecule carvone smells like spearmint, while its mirror image smells like caraway. In medicines, one form may heal while the other is useless or harmful."],["Giant molecules","Some molecules are enormous. Polymers such as plastics and proteins are long chains of repeating units. A single DNA molecule in one human chromosome can contain hundreds of millions of base pairs and would stretch several centimeters if unwound. Diamond can be thought of as a single giant network of bonded carbon atoms."],["Same atoms, different molecules","The same atoms can form different molecules with very different properties. Oxygen gas, O₂, is essential for breathing, while ozone, O₃, is a pollutant near the ground but protects life high in the atmosphere by absorbing ultraviolet light. Ethanol and dimethyl ether have the same atoms but different arrangements, one a liquid alcohol, the other a gas."]],
sp:["Why water is special","Water molecules are small but remarkable. Because oxygen pulls electrons more strongly than hydrogen, a water molecule is slightly negative at the oxygen end and slightly positive at the hydrogen ends. These charges let water molecules stick together through hydrogen bonds, so water is liquid at room temperature, dissolves many substances, and expands when it freezes, making ice float. Without these properties, life as we know it would not exist."],
g:[["Molecule","A group of atoms joined by chemical bonds"],["Covalent bond","A bond in which atoms share electrons"],["Ion","An atom or group of atoms with an electric charge"],["Hydrogen bond","A weak attraction involving hydrogen atoms"]],
f:["A water molecule has two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.","Table salt is held together by ionic bonds.","Mirror-image molecules can smell different."],
fun:"One form of the molecule carvone smells like spearmint, and its mirror image smells like caraway.",
tl:"Ang molekula ay grupo ng dalawa o higit pang atom na pinagbubuklod ng chemical bond. Nagkakabit ang mga atom sa pamamagitan ng paghahati o pagbibigay ng electron. Ang hugis ng molekula ang nagpapasya kung paano ito kikilos."});

ADDART({id:"measure",t:"Measurement and Units",c:"Science",i:"📐",w:"International System of Units",r:["timephys","scimeth","euclid","goldage"],
p:[["How much, how long, how heavy","Measurement means finding the size, amount or degree of something using standard units."],["Metric for the world","Most of the world uses the metric system, built on units such as the meter and kilogram."],["Defined by nature","Today the basic units are defined by unchanging constants of nature."]],
l:"Measurement is the process of comparing a quantity, such as length, mass, time or temperature, with a standard unit. Early units were based on body parts and everyday objects, and they varied from place to place. In the 1790s France created the metric system, based on the meter and kilogram, which grew into the International System of Units, SI, used by nearly every country and by scientists everywhere. Since 2019, all seven SI base units have been defined by fundamental constants of nature, making them the same anywhere in the universe.",
s:[["Ancient units","Early people measured with their bodies. The cubit, used in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia, was the length from elbow to fingertip. The foot, the hand and the pace were also common. In the Philippines, traditional measures included the dangkal, a handspan, and the dipa, the length of outstretched arms, while rice was measured by the ganta and the salop."],["The metric system","During the French Revolution, scientists created a new system meant to be for all people, for all time. The meter was defined as one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator through Paris. The gram was based on the mass of water in a small cube. Units were related by multiples of ten, using prefixes such as kilo- for a thousand and milli- for a thousandth."],["The SI base units","The International System of Units, adopted in 1960, has seven base units: the meter for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time, the ampere for electric current, the kelvin for temperature, the mole for amount of substance and the candela for brightness. All other units, such as the newton for force and the watt for power, are built from these."],["Accuracy and precision","Accuracy means how close a measurement is to the true value; precision means how consistent repeated measurements are. Scientists always state the uncertainty of their measurements. Better instruments, such as atomic clocks and laser rulers, allow measurements of astonishing precision."]],
k:[["International system","SI, adopted 1960"],["Base units","Seven"],["Metric system created","France, 1790s"],["Meter defined by","The speed of light (since 1983)"],["World Metrology Day","20 May"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Egyptians use the royal cubit"],["1799","Platinum standards for the meter and kilogram are made in France"],["1875","The Metre Convention is signed by 17 nations"],["1960","The International System of Units (SI) is adopted"],["1967","The second is defined using caesium atoms"],["2019","All SI base units are redefined using constants of nature"]],
s2:[["Goodbye, Le Grand K","From 1889 the kilogram was defined by a single platinum-iridium cylinder, known as Le Grand K, kept in a vault near Paris. Comparisons showed that copies were changing very slightly relative to it. On 20 May 2019, the kilogram was redefined using the Planck constant, a fundamental number of physics, so it no longer depends on any object."],["Measuring time","The second was once defined as a fraction of a day, but Earth’s rotation is slightly irregular. Since 1967, the second has been defined as the time it takes for a certain kind of radiation from caesium atoms to oscillate 9,192,631,770 times. Atomic clocks based on this definition keep GPS, phones and the internet in step."],["The cost of mixed-up units","In 1999 NASA lost the Mars Climate Orbiter because one team used imperial units, pound-force, while another used metric newtons. The spacecraft approached Mars too low and was destroyed. The mistake showed why scientists and engineers insist on using the same units."],["Metric around the world","Almost every country officially uses the metric system, including the Philippines. The United States still uses customary units such as inches, pounds and Fahrenheit in daily life, though science and medicine there use metric. Many countries mix systems informally, measuring height in feet or televisions in inches."],["Temperature scales","Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius in most of the world, where water freezes at 0 and boils at 100 at sea level. The Fahrenheit scale is used in the United States. Scientists use the kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, the lowest possible temperature, equal to minus 273.15°C."],["Measuring the very big and very small","Scientists use prefixes to describe huge and tiny quantities, from nanometers, billionths of a meter, used for computer chip features, to gigawatts for power stations. In 2022 new prefixes were added, ronna- and quetta- for enormous numbers and ronto- and quecto- for tiny ones, to keep up with growing data and science."]],
sp:["Measuring the Earth to make the meter","In 1792 two French astronomers, Jean-Baptiste Delambre and Pierre Méchain, set out to measure the distance between Dunkirk and Barcelona, which lie on the same line of longitude, to calculate the length of the meter. Working through revolution and war, they climbed church towers and hills to measure angles. The survey took about seven years. Méchain worried for the rest of his life about a small error he had found in his measurements."],
g:[["Unit","A standard quantity used in measurement"],["SI","The International System of Units"],["Precision","How consistent repeated measurements are"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature"]],
f:["The SI has seven base units.","The kilogram was redefined in 2019.","A unit mix-up caused the loss of a Mars spacecraft."],
fun:"The meter was originally based on the size of the Earth.",
tl:"Ang pagsukat ay paghahambing ng dami gaya ng haba, bigat at oras sa isang pamantayang yunit. Ginagamit ng halos lahat ng bansa ang sistemang metriko. Sa Pilipinas, dating ginamit ang dangkal, dipa at ganta."});

ADDART({id:"machines",t:"Simple Machines",c:"Science",i:"⚙️",w:"Simple machine",r:["newton","energyphys","wheel","bicycle"],
p:[["Making work easier","Simple machines are basic devices that make it easier to lift, move or split things."],["Six classic machines","They are the lever, wheel and axle, pulley, inclined plane, wedge and screw."],["Inside every machine","Complex machines, from bicycles to cranes, are built from simple machines."]],
l:"A simple machine is a basic mechanical device that changes the size or direction of a force, making work easier. The six classical simple machines are the lever, the wheel and axle, the pulley, the inclined plane, the wedge and the screw. People have used them since prehistoric times to build monuments, lift water and make tools. Simple machines do not create energy; they trade force for distance, letting a small force move a large load over a longer distance. Almost every complex machine combines several of them.",
s:[["The lever","A lever is a rigid bar that turns around a fixed point called a fulcrum. Pushing down on the long end of a lever can lift a heavy load near the fulcrum. Seesaws, crowbars, scissors, bottle openers and even your arm work as levers. The Greek scientist Archimedes explained the law of the lever in the 200s BCE."],["Wheel and axle and pulley","A wheel and axle is a large wheel attached to a smaller rod, so turning the wheel turns the axle with more force, as in a doorknob, steering wheel or screwdriver. A pulley is a wheel with a groove for a rope; using several pulleys together lets a person lift a heavy weight with less force, as in cranes and flagpoles."],["Inclined plane, wedge and screw","An inclined plane, or ramp, lets you raise a heavy object by pushing it up a gentle slope rather than lifting it straight up. A wedge is two inclined planes back to back, used to split or cut, like an axe or knife. A screw is an inclined plane wrapped around a rod, turning rotation into strong forward motion."],["Mechanical advantage","The mechanical advantage of a machine tells how much it multiplies force. A lever with the effort three times farther from the fulcrum than the load has a mechanical advantage of three. But there is a trade-off: to lift the load a certain height, you must push three times as far. The total work, force times distance, stays the same, apart from losses to friction."]],
k:[["Number of classic machines","Six"],["Lever pivot","Fulcrum"],["Law of the lever","Archimedes, 3rd century BCE"],["Key idea","Trade force for distance"],["Measure of effect","Mechanical advantage"]],
d:[["c. 2.6 million years ago","Early humans make stone tools that work as wedges"],["c. 3500 BCE","The wheel and axle appear in Mesopotamia"],["c. 2500 BCE","Ramps help build the pyramids of Egypt"],["3rd century BCE","Archimedes explains levers and pulleys"],["1st century CE","Heron of Alexandria describes five simple machines"],["1600","Galileo explains machines in terms of work"]],
s2:[["Archimedes and the lever","Archimedes of Syracuse understood levers so well that he reportedly said, give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth. According to ancient writers, he used a system of pulleys to let a single person pull a fully loaded ship onto land. His work laid the foundation for the science of mechanics."],["The Archimedes screw","The Archimedes screw is a large screw inside a tube. When its lower end is placed in water and turned, water is carried upward along the thread. Used for more than 2,000 years to irrigate fields and drain land, it is still used today in water treatment plants and even to generate electricity from flowing water."],["Building the pyramids","No one knows exactly how the pyramids of Egypt were built, but ramps, levers and sledges were almost certainly used. A wall painting in the tomb of Djehutihotep shows workers pouring water in front of a sledge carrying a giant statue; experiments in 2014 showed that wet sand can greatly reduce the force needed to pull a sledge."],["Compound machines","Most machines combine several simple machines. A bicycle uses wheels and axles, levers in the brakes and pedals, and pulleys in the chain and gears. Scissors are two levers with wedge-shaped blades. A crane combines pulleys, levers and a wheel and axle to lift enormous loads."],["Simple machines in the body","The human body uses simple machines. Bones act as levers, joints act as fulcrums and muscles provide the force. When you raise your forearm, your elbow is the fulcrum. Your front teeth work like wedges to bite into food."],["Rube Goldberg machines","The American cartoonist Rube Goldberg drew absurdly complicated machines that performed simple tasks through long chains of levers, pulleys, ramps and falling objects. Today students build real Rube Goldberg machines in competitions, learning about simple machines, energy and engineering while having fun."]],
sp:["The screw that lifts water","Tradition credits Archimedes with inventing the water screw in the 200s BCE, though similar devices may have been used earlier in Egypt and Mesopotamia; some historians think they watered the Hanging Gardens of Babylon. Turning a hand crank, a single worker could lift a steady stream of water from a river or canal into a field. The same simple principle now moves grain, plastic pellets and even fish in modern factories and farms."],
g:[["Lever","A bar that turns on a fixed point"],["Fulcrum","The pivot point of a lever"],["Mechanical advantage","How much a machine multiplies force"],["Compound machine","A machine made of two or more simple machines"]],
f:["There are six classic simple machines.","Scissors are made of two levers.","A screw is an inclined plane wrapped around a rod."],
fun:"Archimedes said that with a long enough lever and a place to stand, he could move the Earth.",
tl:"Ang simple machine ay payak na kagamitang nagpapadali ng pag-angat, paggalaw o paghati ng mga bagay. Anim ang klasikong simple machine: lever, wheel and axle, pulley, inclined plane, wedge at screw."});

ADDART({id:"percent",t:"Percentages and Ratios",c:"Mathematics",i:"💯",w:"Percentage",r:["stats","prob","alg","inflation","fib"],
p:[["Parts of a hundred","A percentage is a number expressed as a part of 100, written with the sign %."],["Comparing amounts","Ratios compare two quantities, such as 3 cups of flour to 1 cup of sugar."],["Everywhere in life","Percentages and ratios are used in shopping, cooking, sports, maps, science and money."]],
l:"A percentage is a way of expressing a number as a fraction of 100; the word comes from the Latin per centum, meaning by the hundred. Fifty percent, written 50%, means 50 out of every 100, or one half. A ratio compares two or more quantities, such as the number of boys to girls in a class. Percentages and ratios are among the most useful ideas in everyday mathematics, used to compare prices, calculate discounts and interest, mix ingredients, read maps and understand news and statistics.",
s:[["Calculating percentages","To find a percentage of a number, multiply the number by the percentage and divide by 100. For example, 20% of 50 is 50 × 20 ÷ 100 = 10. To turn a fraction into a percentage, divide and multiply by 100: 3 out of 4 is 0.75, or 75%. A percentage can also be written as a decimal: 12% is 0.12."],["Ratios and proportions","A ratio such as 2:3 means that for every 2 of one thing there are 3 of another. Recipes, paint mixes and medicines rely on ratios. When two ratios are equal, they form a proportion. If 2 kilograms of rice cost 100 pesos, a proportion shows that 5 kilograms cost 250 pesos."],["Percent change","Percent change shows how much something has grown or shrunk compared with its original size. If a price rises from 80 to 100, the increase is 20, which is 25% of 80. Beware: if a price rises by 50% and then falls by 50%, it ends lower than it began, because the second change is calculated from a bigger number."],["Scales and maps","Maps and models use ratios called scales. A map scale of 1:50,000 means 1 centimeter on the map equals 50,000 centimeters, or 500 meters, on the ground. Model cars, building plans and globes are all made to scale."]],
k:[["Meaning","Out of one hundred"],["Symbol","%"],["Word origin","Latin per centum"],["Ratio example","2:3"],["Map scale example","1:50,000"]],
d:[["c. 6 CE","Emperor Augustus taxes goods sold at one hundredth of their value"],["1400s","Italian merchants abbreviate per cento, leading to the % sign"],["1494","Luca Pacioli describes the Rule of 72 for doubling money"],["1683","Jacob Bernoulli studies compound interest and discovers the number e"],["1800s","Percentages become common in business and statistics"],["1900s","Percentages appear in weather forecasts and opinion polls"]],
s2:[["The percent sign","The % sign developed gradually from abbreviations of the Italian per cento used by merchants in the 1400s. Over time, the abbreviation changed into a symbol that looks like a small fraction with zeros, reflecting its meaning of parts per hundred. Related symbols include ‰, per mille, meaning parts per thousand."],["Discounts and taxes","Shops advertise discounts as percentages, such as 30% off. If a shirt costs 500 pesos, 30% off saves 150 pesos, so it costs 350. Governments collect taxes as percentages; the Philippines’ value-added tax, for example, is 12% on many goods and services. Understanding percentages helps people make smart choices."],["Interest and the Rule of 72","Banks pay interest on savings and charge interest on loans as a percentage of the money. With compound interest, interest is earned on earlier interest too, so money grows faster over time. A handy shortcut, the Rule of 72, says that dividing 72 by the yearly interest rate gives roughly the number of years for money to double: at 6%, about 12 years."],["Percentage points","A percentage point is the simple difference between two percentages. If unemployment rises from 4% to 5%, it has risen by 1 percentage point, but by 25% relative to the starting value. News reports sometimes confuse the two, so careful readers check which is meant."],["Ratios in art and nature","Artists and architects have long used ratios to create pleasing proportions. Leonardo da Vinci’s drawing Vitruvian Man shows ideal human proportions. Musical harmony depends on ratios of frequencies: two notes an octave apart have a frequency ratio of 2:1. The golden ratio, about 1.618, appears in some art and natural patterns."],["Percentages in science and sport","Scientists use percentages to describe concentrations, such as 21% oxygen in the air, and probabilities, such as a 70% chance of rain. Sports statistics use percentages for shooting accuracy in basketball and win rates. Percentages make it easy to compare groups of different sizes."]],
sp:["Up 50%, down 50%","Imagine a toy costs 100 pesos. Its price rises by 50%, to 150 pesos. Then it falls by 50%. Is it back to 100? No: 50% of 150 is 75, so the new price is 75 pesos. Because each percentage is calculated from a different starting amount, equal percentage rises and falls do not cancel out. This surprising fact matters for prices, investments and populations."],
g:[["Percentage","A number expressed as parts of 100"],["Ratio","A comparison of two quantities"],["Proportion","An equation stating that two ratios are equal"],["Percentage point","The difference between two percentages"]],
f:["Percent means out of one hundred.","A 1:50,000 map scale means 1 cm stands for 500 m.","A 50% rise followed by a 50% fall leaves you lower than you started."],
fun:"If a price rises 50% and then falls 50%, it ends up lower than it started.",
tl:"Ang porsiyento ay bilang na ipinapahayag bilang bahagi ng 100, isinusulat na %. Ang ratio ay paghahambing ng dalawang dami. Ginagamit ang mga ito sa pamimili, pagluluto, mapa, agham at pera."});

ADDART({id:"friction",t:"Friction",c:"Science",i:"🛷",w:"Friction",r:["newton","energyphys","machines","thermo","cars"],
p:[["A force that resists sliding","Friction is the force that resists the movement of one surface sliding across another."],["Useful and wasteful","Friction lets us walk, hold objects and stop cars, but it also wastes energy and wears things out."],["Heat from rubbing","Rubbing surfaces together turns motion into heat, which is why rubbing your hands warms them."]],
l:"Friction is a force that acts between surfaces in contact, resisting their motion relative to each other. Without friction, we could not walk, write, tie knots or drive cars; everything would slide like on extremely slippery ice. But friction also turns useful energy into heat, wears down machine parts and makes engines less efficient. Engineers reduce unwanted friction with smooth surfaces, lubricants and bearings, and increase helpful friction with tire treads, brake pads and rough soles.",
s:[["What causes friction","Even surfaces that look smooth are rough at a microscopic scale, covered with tiny bumps. When two surfaces press together, these bumps catch and stick, and atoms on the surfaces attract each other. Pushing one surface across the other requires force to break these contacts, producing friction."],["Static and kinetic friction","Static friction holds still objects in place; it is why a heavy box does not start moving until you push hard enough. Once the box slides, kinetic friction resists its motion and is usually smaller, which is why it is easier to keep something moving than to start it. Rolling friction, for wheels, is much smaller still."],["The laws of friction","Leonardo da Vinci studied friction around 1500 and noticed that it depends on how hard surfaces are pressed together, not on how large the touching area is. In 1699 the French scientist Guillaume Amontons rediscovered these rules, and Charles-Augustin de Coulomb developed them further in 1785. They are still used today as good approximations."],["Fluid friction","Objects moving through air or water also experience friction, called drag or air resistance. Drag increases with speed and with the size of the surface facing the flow. Streamlined shapes, like those of fish, birds, airplanes and racing cars, reduce drag. Parachutes use large surfaces to increase drag and slow a fall."]],
k:[["Kinds","Static, kinetic, rolling, fluid"],["Depends on","Surfaces and how hard they press together"],["Early study","Leonardo da Vinci, c. 1500"],["Laws published","Amontons (1699), Coulomb (1785)"],["Science of friction","Tribology"]],
d:[["Prehistory","People make fire by rubbing sticks together"],["c. 1500","Leonardo da Vinci studies friction"],["1699","Guillaume Amontons publishes the laws of friction"],["1785","Charles-Augustin de Coulomb extends the laws"],["1826","John Walker invents the friction match"],["1966","The word tribology is introduced for the science of friction"]],
s2:[["Making fire","For tens of thousands of years, people have made fire using friction, spinning a wooden stick against another piece of wood until the heat lights tinder. In 1826 the English chemist John Walker invented the friction match, which bursts into flame when struck. Safety matches, which light only on a special strip, followed in the 1840s and 1850s."],["Friction on the road","Car tires depend on friction to grip the road for accelerating, turning and stopping. Treads channel water away so tires keep contact with wet roads; if water builds up under a tire, it can skid, called aquaplaning. Brakes work by pressing pads against discs or drums, turning the car’s motion into heat."],["Reducing friction","Engineers reduce friction with lubricants such as oil and grease, which form a slippery layer between moving parts. Ball bearings replace sliding with rolling. Air hockey tables float pucks on a cushion of air, and magnetic levitation trains float above their tracks, eliminating wheel friction entirely."],["The cost of friction","Friction and wear cost the world enormous amounts of energy and money. A 2017 study estimated that about a fifth of the world’s energy use goes to overcoming friction in vehicles, machines and industry. Better lubricants, coatings and designs could save energy and cut emissions."],["Why ice is slippery","Ice is slippery because a thin, watery layer forms on its surface, letting skates and shoes slide easily. Scientists still debate exactly why this layer forms; pressure, heat from friction and the special nature of water molecules at the surface all seem to play roles. Skaters glide because friction on ice is so low."],["Friction in space","Spacecraft returning to Earth hit the atmosphere at tremendous speed, and the compressed air in front of them becomes extremely hot. Heat shields protect them. Meteors glow for the same reason. In the vacuum of space itself, there is almost no air resistance, so objects keep moving once pushed."]],
sp:["Water on the sand","A painting in the ancient Egyptian tomb of Djehutihotep, from about 1900 BCE, shows a huge statue being dragged on a sledge while a man pours water onto the sand in front of it. For a long time, people thought this was a ritual. In 2014 physicists at the University of Amsterdam showed that the right amount of water stiffens sand so that the sledge does not dig in, roughly halving the force needed to pull it. The Egyptians had discovered a clever way to reduce friction."],
g:[["Friction","A force that resists sliding between surfaces"],["Lubricant","A substance that reduces friction"],["Drag","Friction from air or water on a moving object"],["Tribology","The science of friction, wear and lubrication"]],
f:["Friction turns motion into heat.","It is easier to keep something moving than to start it.","Ancient Egyptians wet sand to reduce friction."],
fun:"Ancient Egyptians poured water on sand to make dragging giant statues easier.",
tl:"Ang friction ay puwersang lumalaban sa pagdulas ng isang ibabaw sa isa pa. Dahil dito, nakalalakad tayo at nakahihinto ang sasakyan, ngunit nag-aaksaya rin ito ng enerhiya. Kapag pinagkiskis ang dalawang bagay, nagkakaroon ng init."});

;
