/* data/animals8.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 8: bird migration, hornbills, salamanders, the tuatara, classification and extinction.
ADDART({id:"birdmigr",t:"Bird Migration",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🕊️",w:"Bird migration",r:["birds","eagle","wetlands","penguin","phbio"],
p:[["Great journeys","Many birds fly long distances each year between breeding grounds and wintering grounds."],["Natural compasses","Birds navigate using the Sun, stars, Earth’s magnetic field, landmarks and even smell."],["The Philippines on the route","The Philippines lies on a major flyway used by millions of migrating shorebirds and waterbirds."]],
l:"Bird migration is the regular seasonal movement of birds, often between northern breeding grounds in summer and warmer wintering areas. Nearly 2,000 bird species migrate, some traveling thousands of kilometers each way. Migration lets birds take advantage of seasonal food and safe nesting places. Birds prepare by eating heavily and storing fat, then fly by day or night, finding their way with remarkable navigation skills. Many migratory birds are declining because of habitat loss, so countries along their routes cooperate to protect them.",
s:[["Why birds migrate","In spring, the far north offers long days and an explosion of insects and plants, ideal for raising chicks. In autumn, as food disappears and cold sets in, birds fly to warmer places. Migration takes great energy and risk, but the rewards of plentiful food and fewer predators make it worthwhile for many species."],["Champions of migration","The Arctic tern makes the longest known migration, traveling from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year, a round trip of 70,000 kilometers or more. In 2022 a young bar-tailed godwit flew nonstop from Alaska to Tasmania, about 13,500 kilometers, in 11 days without landing, eating or drinking."],["Finding the way","Birds use several compasses. They follow the position of the Sun by day and the stars by night, and they can sense Earth’s magnetic field, possibly through special molecules in their eyes. They also remember landmarks such as coastlines, rivers and mountains, and some seabirds use smell. Young birds of some species migrate alone, guided by instinct."],["Flyways","Migrating birds follow broad routes called flyways. The East Asian–Australasian Flyway runs from Siberia and Alaska through China, Korea, Japan and Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, to Australia and New Zealand. Tens of millions of shorebirds use it, resting and feeding at wetlands and mudflats along the way."]],
k:[["Migratory species","Nearly 2,000"],["Longest migration","Arctic tern, 70,000 km or more a year"],["Longest nonstop flight","Bar-tailed godwit, about 13,500 km"],["Navigation","Sun, stars, magnetic field, landmarks"],["World Migratory Bird Day","Second Saturday of May and October"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes about birds disappearing in winter"],["1822","A stork with an African arrow in its neck is found in Germany"],["1899","Hans Christian Mortensen begins ringing birds to track them"],["1979","The Bonn Convention on migratory species is signed"],["1994","Olango Island becomes the Philippines’ first Ramsar wetland site"],["2022","A bar-tailed godwit sets a nonstop flight record"]],
s2:[["The arrow stork","People once had strange ideas about where birds went in winter; some believed swallows slept underwater. In 1822 a white stork was shot in Germany with an 80-centimeter African spear through its neck. It had survived the wound and flown back from Africa, proving that storks travel there. Germans call such birds Pfeilstörche, arrow storks."],["Tracking birds","In 1899 the Danish teacher Hans Christian Mortensen began fitting birds with numbered metal rings to learn where they traveled. Today scientists use tiny satellite tags, GPS trackers and geolocators weighing less than a gram, as well as radar and microphones that record birds calling as they fly over at night."],["Migrants in the Philippines","Each year, migratory birds such as plovers, sandpipers, egrets, herons and ducks arrive in the Philippines from northern Asia, mostly from about September to April. Olango Island in Cebu, Candaba Swamp in Pampanga and the coasts of Manila Bay are important resting and feeding sites. The Philippines has protected several wetlands under the international Ramsar Convention."],["Dangers on the journey","Migrating birds face storms, predators, hunting and exhaustion. Many die colliding with lit-up buildings and windows at night, so some cities dim their lights during migration seasons. The loss of coastal wetlands, such as the mudflats of the Yellow Sea, where shorebirds refuel, has caused steep declines in some species."],["Getting ready","Before migrating, many birds enter a state of restlessness and eat huge amounts, sometimes doubling their body weight with fat for fuel. Some shrink their digestive organs during flight to save weight. Bar-headed geese fly over the Himalayas at great altitudes where oxygen is scarce, helped by special hemoglobin in their blood."],["Not only birds","Many other animals migrate, including whales, sea turtles, salmon, wildebeest, monarch butterflies and dragonflies. But birds make some of the most spectacular and widespread journeys, connecting countries and continents."]],
sp:["The godwit’s record","On 13 October 2022, a five-month-old bar-tailed godwit fitted with a satellite tag took off from Alaska and flew without stopping for 11 days, landing in Tasmania after about 13,560 kilometers. It never landed to rest, feed or drink, flapping its wings the whole way and using winds to help. Before leaving, godwits fatten up so much that fat makes up more than half their body weight."],
g:[["Migration","Regular seasonal movement from one place to another"],["Flyway","A broad route used by migrating birds"],["Ringing","Putting a numbered band on a bird’s leg to track it"],["Ramsar site","A wetland protected under an international treaty"]],
f:["The Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic each year.","Birds can sense Earth’s magnetic field.","The Philippines lies on the East Asian–Australasian Flyway."],
fun:"A young bar-tailed godwit flew nonstop from Alaska to Tasmania in 11 days.",
tl:"Ang migrasyon ng ibon ay taunang paglalakbay nila sa pagitan ng pugaran at taglamigang lugar. Ginagamit nila ang araw, bituin at magnetic field ng Earth para hanapin ang daan. Dumadaan sa Pilipinas ang milyon-milyong migratoryong ibon."});

ADDART({id:"hornbill",t:"Hornbills",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦜",w:"Hornbill",r:["birds","trees","phbio","borneo","eagle"],
p:[["Big beaks","Hornbills are birds with huge curved bills, often topped by a hollow helmet called a casque."],["Walled-in nests","Many female hornbills seal themselves inside tree holes while nesting, fed by their mates."],["Forest gardeners","By eating fruit and spreading seeds, hornbills help forests grow."]],
l:"Hornbills are a family of about 60 bird species found in the tropical forests and savannas of Africa and Asia, including the Philippines. They are known for their long, downcurved bills, often brightly colored and topped with a casque. Hornbills eat fruit, insects and small animals, and they play a vital role in spreading the seeds of forest trees. Many species have unusual nesting habits, with the female walling herself into a tree hole. Because they need large old trees, many hornbills are threatened by deforestation and hunting.",
s:[["The bill and casque","Despite its size, a hornbill’s bill is light, made of keratin over a honeycomb of bone. The casque on top is usually hollow and may help amplify calls or display maturity. The helmeted hornbill is unusual: its casque is solid, like ivory. Hornbills are also the only birds whose first two neck vertebrae are fused, perhaps to support the heavy bill."],["Sealed in the nest","In most hornbill species, the female enters a hole in a tree and seals the entrance with mud, droppings and food, leaving only a narrow slit. The male feeds her and later the chicks through the slit, sometimes for months. Inside, the female molts her flight feathers. This protects the nest from snakes, monkeys and other predators."],["Farmers of the forest","Hornbills eat large amounts of fruit, especially figs, and fly long distances, dropping seeds far from the parent tree. Some trees depend on hornbills to spread their seeds. Because of this, hornbills are sometimes called farmers of the forest, and their disappearance can change which trees grow."],["Hornbills of the Philippines","The Philippines has about ten hornbill species, most found nowhere else. They include the large rufous hornbill, called kalaw for its loud call, the Visayan hornbill, the Mindanao wrinkled hornbill and the Sulu hornbill, one of the world’s rarest birds, surviving only in tiny numbers on Tawi-Tawi. Most Philippine hornbills are threatened by forest loss and hunting."]],
k:[["Species","About 60"],["Found in","Africa and Asia"],["Special feature","Large bill with casque"],["Philippine species","About 10, mostly endemic"],["Critically endangered example","Helmeted hornbill, Sulu hornbill"]],
d:[["1758","Linnaeus names the first hornbill species"],["1978","Thai scientists begin a long-term hornbill research and protection project"],["2001","The Philippines passes its Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act"],["2015","The helmeted hornbill is listed as critically endangered"],["2016","International trade in helmeted hornbill casques gets stronger protection"],["2020s","Conservationists count fewer than about 40 Sulu hornbills"]],
s2:[["The helmeted hornbill","The helmeted hornbill of Borneo, Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula has a solid casque, sometimes called red ivory, which is carved into ornaments. Poaching for this trade caused a sharp decline, and in 2015 the species was listed as critically endangered. Its call, a series of hoots ending in a maniacal laugh, can be heard far through the forest."],["The great hornbill","The great hornbill, found from India to Southeast Asia, is one of the largest hornbills, with a wingspan of about 1.5 meters and a bright yellow casque. Its wingbeats make a loud whooshing sound. It is the state bird of Kerala and Arunachal Pradesh in India, and many Indigenous peoples consider hornbills sacred."],["Ground hornbills","Africa’s ground hornbills are large black birds with red throat skin that walk on the savanna hunting insects, snakes and small mammals. Southern ground hornbills live in groups that help raise one chick at a time and can live for decades. They are considered sacred or bringers of rain by some African peoples."],["Hornbills in culture","In Borneo, the rhinoceros hornbill is a symbol of the Dayak peoples, and the Malaysian state of Sarawak is called the Land of the Hornbills. Hornbill feathers and carvings are used in ceremonies. In the film The Lion King, the character Zazu is a red-billed hornbill."],["Protecting hornbills","Conservationists protect nesting trees, install artificial nest boxes, patrol forests against poachers and work with local communities. In Thailand and India, villagers are paid to guard hornbill nests. In the Philippines, breeding centers and forest protection programs help species such as the Visayan hornbill."],["A loving family","Many hornbill pairs stay together for years. During nesting, the male may bring hundreds of fruits a day to his sealed-in mate and chicks, regurgitating them one by one through the slit. If the male dies, the female and chicks may starve, which is one reason hunting adult hornbills is so harmful."]],
sp:["The Sulu hornbill","The Sulu hornbill is one of the rarest birds on Earth. Once found on several islands of the Sulu Archipelago, it now survives only in the remaining forests of Tawi-Tawi, where surveys have counted fewer than about 40 birds. It is black with a white tail, and males have pale bills. Logging and hunting have pushed it to the edge of extinction, and local conservationists are working to protect its last forests."],
g:[["Casque","A helmet-like growth on top of a hornbill’s bill"],["Seed disperser","An animal that spreads plant seeds"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Critically endangered","At extremely high risk of extinction"]],
f:["Female hornbills seal themselves into tree holes.","The Philippines has about ten hornbill species.","Hornbills spread the seeds of forest trees."],
fun:"Zazu in The Lion King is a red-billed hornbill.",
tl:"Ang hornbill o kalaw ay ibong may malaki at kurbadong tuka, na kadalasang may casque sa ibabaw. Ikinukulong ng babaeng hornbill ang sarili sa butas ng puno habang nangingitlog. Mahalaga sila sa pagkalat ng buto ng mga puno sa gubat."});

ADDART({id:"salamander",t:"Salamanders and Newts",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Salamander",r:["frog","lizard","evo","cell","wetlands"],
p:[["Amphibians with tails","Salamanders are amphibians with long bodies, tails and usually four short legs."],["Masters of regrowth","Many salamanders can regrow lost legs, tails and even parts of their hearts and eyes."],["Moist places","Most live in damp forests, streams and ponds, and many breathe through their skin."]],
l:"Salamanders are a group of about 800 species of amphibians with slender bodies, long tails and moist skin. Newts are a kind of salamander. Although they look somewhat like lizards, salamanders are amphibians, related to frogs, and have no scales. They live mainly in the Northern Hemisphere, especially in the Americas, in damp forests, streams, caves and ponds. Salamanders are famous for their ability to regenerate lost body parts, and they range from tiny species a few centimeters long to giant salamanders nearly 2 meters long.",
s:[["Salamander life","Most salamanders lay eggs in water or damp places. Many have aquatic larvae with feathery external gills, which later change into adults that live on land. Salamanders eat insects, worms, snails and other small animals, catching them with sticky tongues. They are mostly active at night or in wet weather."],["Breathing through skin","The largest family of salamanders, the lungless salamanders, has no lungs at all. They breathe entirely through their thin, moist skin and the lining of their mouths. This is why they must stay damp. Most salamanders also absorb some oxygen through their skin, making them sensitive to pollution."],["Regeneration","Salamanders can regrow lost limbs, tails, parts of the spinal cord, heart, eyes and jaws. When a limb is lost, cells at the wound form a bud that grows into a perfect new limb, with bones, muscles and nerves. Scientists study salamanders, especially axolotls, hoping to learn how to help humans heal injuries."],["Where they live","Salamanders are most diverse in North and Central America; the Appalachian Mountains of the United States are a world hotspot. They also live in Europe, northern Africa and Asia, including China, Japan and Vietnam. None are native to Australia, and they are absent from most of Africa and from tropical islands such as the Philippines."]],
k:[["Species","About 800"],["Group","Amphibians"],["Largest","Chinese giant salamanders, up to about 1.8 m"],["Largest family","Lungless salamanders"],["Special ability","Regrowing body parts"]],
d:[["c. 160 million years ago","Salamander fossils show the group was already present"],["1689","The olm, a cave salamander, is first written about in Slovenia"],["1864","Axolotls are brought from Mexico to Paris and studied"],["2013","A new fungus deadly to salamanders is identified in Europe"],["2018","The axolotl’s genome, one of the largest known, is sequenced"],["2019","Scientists suggest Chinese giant salamanders are several species"]],
s2:[["The axolotl","The axolotl, from lakes near Mexico City, keeps its gills and lives in water its whole life, a trait called neoteny, as if staying a larva forever. It can regenerate almost any body part. Wild axolotls are critically endangered, surviving mainly in the canals of Xochimilco, though millions are kept in laboratories and as pets. It is a cultural symbol of Mexico City."],["Giant salamanders","Chinese giant salamanders are the world’s largest amphibians, growing up to about 1.8 meters long. They live in cold mountain streams, breathing through wrinkly skin. Overhunting for food has made them critically endangered. Japan has its own giant salamander, and North America has the hellbender, up to about 70 centimeters long."],["The olm","The olm lives in flooded caves in Slovenia, Croatia and nearby countries. It is blind, with pale pink skin, and can sense light, smells and electric fields. Olms are thought to live for about 100 years and may go years without eating. Local people once believed they were baby dragons washed out of caves by floods."],["Newts","Newts are salamanders that spend much of their adult life in water, especially during breeding. Male newts of some species grow wavy crests and bright colors in spring to attract females. Some newts, like the rough-skinned newt of North America, contain a powerful poison in their skin."],["Fire salamanders","The fire salamander of Europe is black with bright yellow spots, a warning that its skin produces toxins. People once believed salamanders could live in fire, perhaps because they crawled out of logs thrown on fires. This myth gave the salamander a place in legends and heraldry."],["Threats","Salamanders are threatened by habitat loss, pollution, climate change and disease. A fungus called Batrachochytrium salamandrivorans, identified in 2013, has wiped out fire salamander populations in parts of Europe. Scientists are working to stop it from spreading to North America, the world’s salamander hotspot."]],
sp:["The giant genome","The axolotl has one of the largest genomes of any animal, about ten times larger than the human genome. In 2018 scientists finished sequencing it, a major challenge because of its size and repetitive DNA. They hope to find the genes that allow axolotls to regrow limbs perfectly, which might one day help medicine heal wounds and injuries in people."],
g:[["Amphibian","A cold-blooded animal that usually begins life in water"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"],["Neoteny","Keeping juvenile features as an adult"],["Larva","An early, immature stage of an animal"]],
f:["Salamanders are amphibians, not lizards.","Some salamanders have no lungs.","Axolotls can regrow lost limbs."],
fun:"The olm, a blind cave salamander, may live for about 100 years.",
tl:"Ang salamander ay amphibian na may mahabang katawan at buntot. Kaya ng marami sa kanila na muling patubuin ang nawalang paa at buntot. Walang katutubong salamander sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"tuatara",t:"The Tuatara",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Tuatara",r:["lizard","nzcountry","dino","evo","chameleon"],
p:[["A living relic","The tuatara of New Zealand is the last survivor of a group of reptiles that lived alongside the early dinosaurs."],["Not a lizard","Although it looks like a lizard, it belongs to its own ancient order of reptiles."],["Slow and long-lived","Tuatara grow slowly and can live for more than 100 years."]],
l:"The tuatara is a reptile found only in New Zealand. It looks like a lizard but is the only living member of an ancient order called Rhynchocephalia, whose relatives flourished more than 200 million years ago. Tuatara grow up to about 80 centimeters long, are active at cool temperatures and can live for over a century. They nearly disappeared from mainland New Zealand after people brought rats, but survived on offshore islands and are now being returned to protected sanctuaries.",
s:[["An ancient lineage","The tuatara’s ancestors appeared about 240 million years ago, in the Triassic period, and were widespread around the world. All died out except the tuatara, which survived in New Zealand. Its body shows features unlike those of lizards, such as the way its teeth are fused to its jawbone and the structure of its skull."],["The third eye","Young tuatara have a parietal eye, sometimes called a third eye, on top of the head, with a lens and retina. It becomes covered by scales as the animal grows. Scientists think it may help regulate daily and seasonal rhythms by sensing light. Some lizards and frogs also have a parietal eye."],["Life in the cold","Most reptiles need warm temperatures, but tuatara are active at temperatures as low as about 5°C. They come out at night to hunt beetles, wētā insects, spiders, and sometimes small lizards and seabird chicks. They often share burrows with nesting seabirds such as petrels."],["Slow life","Tuatara grow very slowly, not reaching maturity until 10 to 20 years of age. Females lay eggs only every few years, and the eggs take 11 to 16 months to hatch, among the longest of any reptile. The temperature of the nest decides whether hatchlings are male or female: warmer nests produce more males."]],
k:[["Found in","New Zealand only"],["Order","Rhynchocephalia, the only living member"],["Length","Up to about 80 cm"],["Lifespan","Can exceed 100 years"],["Egg incubation","11–16 months"]],
d:[["c. 240 million years ago","The tuatara’s ancestors appear"],["c. 1250–1300","Polynesian settlers, with Pacific rats, arrive in New Zealand"],["1831","The tuatara is first described by scientists"],["1895","Tuatara receive legal protection"],["2005","Tuatara are released into the Zealandia sanctuary in Wellington"],["2008","The first wild-born tuatara on the mainland in about 200 years is found"]],
s2:[["Henry the tuatara","Henry, a tuatara at the Southland Museum in Invercargill, New Zealand, became famous for becoming a father at about the age of 111 in 2009, after surgery to remove a tumor seemed to improve his mood. He had lived at the museum since the 1970s and showed little interest in mating for decades."],["Saved on islands","When Polynesian settlers and later Europeans arrived in New Zealand, they brought rats, cats and other predators that ate tuatara eggs and young. By the 1900s, tuatara survived only on about 30 offshore islands. Conservationists removed rats from many islands and bred tuatara in captivity, helping populations recover."],["Back to the mainland","In 2005, 70 tuatara were released into Zealandia, a fenced, predator-free sanctuary in Wellington. In 2008 rangers found a wild-born hatchling there, the first on the mainland in about 200 years. More sanctuaries have since received tuatara."],["A treasure of the Māori","For Māori, the Indigenous people of New Zealand, the tuatara is a taonga, a treasure, and is linked to Tangaroa and other ancestral figures. Its name means peaks on the back in the Māori language, describing its spiny crest. Iwi, tribes, are kaitiaki, guardians, of tuatara, and work with scientists on their protection."],["Climate risk","Because nest temperature decides the sex of tuatara, climate change is a concern: warmer temperatures could produce too many males. On North Brother Island, males already outnumber females. Scientists are studying ways to help, such as shading nests or moving populations to cooler places."],["Tuatara and evolution","Studies of the tuatara’s genome, published in 2020, revealed that it has an unusual mix of reptile and even mammal-like features. Scientists study tuatara to learn how reptiles evolved and how such an ancient lineage survived for so long."]],
sp:["Peaks on the back","The word tuatara comes from the Māori language: tua means back, and tara means spines or peaks, describing the crest of spines along the animal’s back, especially large in males. When males fight or court females, they raise their crests and puff up their bodies to look bigger. The crest has made the tuatara one of New Zealand’s most recognizable animals, featured on coins and stamps."],
g:[["Rhynchocephalia","An ancient order of reptiles with one living member"],["Parietal eye","A light-sensitive spot on top of the head"],["Predator-free","Without introduced animals that eat native wildlife"],["Taonga","A Māori word for a treasure"]],
f:["The tuatara is not a lizard.","Tuatara can live for over 100 years.","Nest temperature decides whether tuatara hatchlings are male or female."],
fun:"A tuatara named Henry became a father at about 111 years old.",
tl:"Ang tuatara ay reptilyang matatagpuan lamang sa New Zealand. Mukha itong butiki ngunit ito na lamang ang natitirang miyembro ng sinaunang grupo ng mga reptilya. Maaari itong mabuhay nang higit 100 taon."});

ADDART({id:"classification",t:"Classifying Living Things",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🏷️",w:"Taxonomy (biology)",r:["evo","cell","dna","microbe","mendel"],
p:[["Sorting life","Scientists sort living things into groups based on how they are related."],["Two-part names","Every species has a two-part scientific name, such as Homo sapiens for humans."],["Millions of species","About 2 million species have been named, but millions more await discovery."]],
l:"Classification, or taxonomy, is the science of naming, describing and grouping living things. It helps scientists organize the enormous diversity of life and understand how organisms are related. The modern system was founded by the Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus in the 1700s, who gave each species a two-part Latin name and arranged them into a hierarchy of groups. Today scientists use DNA to work out the family tree of life, grouping organisms by shared ancestry. About 2 million species have been described, but estimates of the total range from about 8 million to many more.",
s:[["Linnaeus","Carl Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist, published Systema Naturae in 1735, sorting plants, animals and minerals into groups. In later editions, especially the 10th edition of 1758, he used binomial nomenclature, giving every species a two-part name: a genus name and a species name. Humans became Homo sapiens, meaning wise human."],["The ranks of life","Living things are classified in nested ranks: domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus and species. For example, the Philippine eagle belongs to the domain Eukarya, kingdom Animalia, phylum Chordata, class Aves, order Accipitriformes, family Accipitridae, genus Pithecophaga and species Pithecophaga jefferyi."],["Three domains","In the 1970s, the American microbiologist Carl Woese studied genetic material and found that microbes once lumped together as bacteria were really two very different groups. Life is now divided into three domains: Bacteria; Archaea, microbes often found in extreme environments; and Eukarya, organisms with complex cells, including plants, animals, fungi and protists."],["Classifying with DNA","Scientists once grouped organisms mainly by appearance and anatomy. Today they compare DNA to build family trees, called phylogenies. DNA has revealed surprises: whales are close relatives of hippos, birds are living dinosaurs and fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants."]],
k:[["Founder of modern system","Carl Linnaeus"],["Key book","Systema Naturae (1735)"],["Naming system","Binomial nomenclature"],["Named species","About 2 million"],["Three domains","Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle groups animals by their features"],["1735","Linnaeus publishes Systema Naturae"],["1758","The 10th edition sets the starting point for animal names"],["1859","Darwin’s On the Origin of Species links classification to evolution"],["1977","Carl Woese identifies the Archaea"],["2011","A study estimates about 8.7 million species of complex life"]],
s2:[["How species get names","When scientists discover a new species, they describe it in a scientific publication, give it a Latin or Latin-style name and deposit a type specimen in a museum as a reference. Names often honor a feature, a place or a person. Rules set by international commissions make sure each species has only one valid name."],["New species every year","Scientists describe thousands of new species every year, mostly insects, plants, fungi and other small organisms, but also frogs, lizards and occasionally mammals. The Philippines, with its many islands, is a hotspot of new discoveries, including new species of frogs, lizards, beetles, plants and even forest mice found on single mountains."],["Fun names","Some species have playful names. A horsefly from Australia was named Scaptia beyonceae after the singer Beyoncé, a beetle Agra katewinsletae after the actress Kate Winslet, and a trapdoor spider Aptostichus barackobamai after a former US president. Such names draw attention to the importance of discovering species."],["The puzzle of the platypus","When the first platypus specimen reached Britain from Australia in 1799, some scientists thought it was a fake, made by sewing a duck’s bill onto a mammal’s body. The platypus, a mammal that lays eggs, challenged the categories scientists used and showed that nature does not always fit neatly into boxes."],["Common names and scientific names","Common names vary from place to place: the bird called a kalaw in the Philippines is the rufous hornbill in English. Scientific names are the same worldwide, so scientists in different countries know exactly which organism they mean. Scientific names are written in italics, with the genus capitalized."],["Why classification matters","Classification helps scientists study diseases, find new medicines, protect endangered species and understand evolution. Knowing that a new plant is related to a known medicinal plant, for example, can suggest where to look for useful chemicals. Biodiversity cannot be protected if species are unknown."]],
sp:["Carl Linnaeus","Carl Linnaeus, born in Sweden in 1707, loved plants from childhood. He traveled through Lapland collecting specimens and became a professor at Uppsala University, where he sent students around the world to collect plants and animals. His system of two-part names was so simple and useful that it is still used today. He was so proud of his work that he reportedly said God created, but Linnaeus organized."],
g:[["Taxonomy","The science of naming and classifying living things"],["Species","A group of organisms that can breed with one another"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Binomial nomenclature","The two-part naming system for species"]],
f:["Humans are called Homo sapiens.","Life is divided into three domains.","About 2 million species have been named."],
fun:"A species of horsefly was named after the singer Beyoncé.",
tl:"Ang klasipikasyon o taxonomy ay agham ng pagpapangalan at pagpapangkat ng mga buhay na bagay. Binigyan ni Carl Linnaeus ang bawat species ng dalawang-bahaging pangalan, gaya ng Homo sapiens para sa tao."});

ADDART({id:"extinction",t:"Extinction",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Extinction",r:["dino","evo","eco","climchange","phbio"],
p:[["Gone forever","Extinction happens when the last individual of a species dies."],["Mass extinctions","Five times in Earth’s history, huge numbers of species died out in a short time."],["Preventing extinction","Today people cause many extinctions, but conservation can save species from disappearing."]],
l:"Extinction is the end of a species, when its last member dies. It is a natural part of evolution: about 99 percent of all species that have ever lived are now extinct. Five times in Earth’s history, mass extinctions wiped out a large share of life, including the event that ended the age of dinosaurs. Today human activities, such as habitat destruction, hunting, pollution, invasive species and climate change, are causing species to disappear far faster than the natural rate. Conservation efforts aim to protect threatened species and the habitats they need.",
s:[["The five mass extinctions","Scientists recognize five mass extinctions: at the end of the Ordovician, about 445 million years ago; in the Late Devonian; at the end of the Permian, about 252 million years ago; at the end of the Triassic; and at the end of the Cretaceous, 66 million years ago. The end-Permian event, the Great Dying, killed around 90 percent of marine species."],["Causes of extinction","Natural extinctions can result from climate change, volcanic eruptions, asteroid impacts, competition and disease. Today the main causes are human: destroying forests, wetlands and reefs; overhunting and overfishing; introducing invasive species such as rats and cats to islands; pollution; and climate change."],["Lost species","Famous extinct species include the dodo of Mauritius, last reliably seen around 1662; the passenger pigeon, once numbering billions in North America, whose last member, Martha, died in 1914; and the thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, whose last known individual died in a zoo in 1936. Many plants, insects and small animals have vanished without being noticed."],["The Red List","The International Union for Conservation of Nature keeps the Red List of Threatened Species, which assesses the risk of extinction for tens of thousands of species. Categories range from least concern to vulnerable, endangered, critically endangered, extinct in the wild and extinct. More than 45,000 species are listed as threatened."]],
k:[["Share of species ever lived now extinct","About 99 percent"],["Mass extinctions","Five"],["Worst mass extinction","End-Permian, about 252 million years ago"],["Threatened species (IUCN)","More than 45,000"],["Dodo last seen","About 1662"]],
d:[["c. 252 million years ago","The Great Dying wipes out most life"],["66 million years ago","An asteroid impact ends the age of non-bird dinosaurs"],["c. 1662","The dodo is last reliably seen"],["1914","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, dies"],["1936","The last known thylacine dies"],["1964","The IUCN Red List is established"]],
s2:[["The dodo","The dodo was a large, flightless pigeon found only on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean. It had no natural predators and was unafraid of people. After Dutch sailors arrived in 1598, dodos were hunted, and introduced rats, pigs and monkeys ate their eggs. Within about 70 years they were gone. As dead as a dodo became a saying for something gone forever."],["The passenger pigeon","In the early 1800s, passenger pigeons may have been the most numerous birds in North America, flying in flocks so huge they darkened the sky for hours. Commercial hunting and forest clearing destroyed them within decades. Martha, the last one, died at the Cincinnati Zoo on 1 September 1914. Her story helped inspire wildlife protection laws."],["Lazarus species","Sometimes species thought extinct are found again, called Lazarus species. The coelacanth, a fish known only from fossils and thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive off South Africa in 1938. In the Philippines, the Cebu flowerpecker, feared extinct, was rediscovered in 1992 in a small patch of forest."],["A sixth mass extinction?","Many scientists warn that the current rate of extinction is far higher than the natural rate and that the world may be entering a sixth mass extinction. A major international report in 2019 estimated that about a million species are threatened with extinction, many within decades, unless action is taken."],["Saving species","Conservation has saved many species. Protecting habitats, banning hunting, breeding animals in captivity and removing invasive predators have helped species such as the California condor, the black-footed ferret, the giant panda and humpback whales, and are helping rare species such as the Philippine crocodile. Protected areas on land and sea are vital."],["Island extinctions","Islands are especially vulnerable because their species often live nowhere else and evolved without predators. The Philippines, with thousands of islands and many unique species, is a global conservation priority. Cebu, for example, lost most of its original forest, and several of its unique birds became extinct or nearly so."]],
sp:["The last passenger pigeon","Martha was named after Martha Washington, the wife of the first US president. She lived at the Cincinnati Zoo, where visitors came to see the last of a species that had once numbered in the billions. When she died in 1914, her body was frozen in a block of ice and sent to the Smithsonian Institution in Washington. Her death became one of the most famous examples of how quickly humans can drive even a very common species to extinction."],
g:[["Extinction","The disappearance of a species"],["Mass extinction","The loss of a large share of species in a short time"],["Red List","A list assessing species’ risk of extinction"],["Lazarus species","A species rediscovered after being thought extinct"]],
f:["About 99 percent of all species that ever lived are extinct.","The dodo lived only on Mauritius.","The coelacanth was rediscovered alive in 1938."],
fun:"The coelacanth, a fish thought extinct for 66 million years, was found alive in 1938.",
tl:"Ang ekstinksiyon ay ang tuluyang pagkawala ng isang species. Limang beses nagkaroon ng malawakang ekstinksiyon sa kasaysayan ng Earth. Ngayon, maraming species ang nawawala dahil sa gawain ng tao, ngunit maaari silang iligtas ng konserbasyon."});

;
/* data/history3.js */
// History articles, part 3: the Phoenicians, the Celts, the Mughal Empire, Srivijaya and Majapahit, the Black Death and the Green Revolution.
ADDART({id:"phoenicians",t:"The Phoenicians",c:"History",i:"⛵",w:"Phoenicia",r:["alphabet","rome","greece","egypt","trade"],
p:[["Sailors and traders","The Phoenicians were seafaring traders from city-states on the coast of what is now Lebanon."],["Letters for the world","Their alphabet became the ancestor of the Greek, Latin, Hebrew and Arabic scripts."],["Royal purple","They were famous for a precious purple dye made from sea snails."]],
l:"The Phoenicians were an ancient people who lived in independent city-states along the eastern Mediterranean coast, mainly in present-day Lebanon, from about 1500 to 300 BCE. Cities such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos grew rich through trade and seafaring, sailing across the Mediterranean and beyond and founding colonies such as Carthage in North Africa. The Phoenicians spread goods, ideas and above all their alphabet, the ancestor of many of the world’s writing systems, including the letters you are reading now.",
s:[["City-states of the coast","Phoenicia was never a single country but a group of cities, each with its own king, sharing a language and culture. Hemmed in by mountains, they turned to the sea. Their famous cedar forests supplied timber to Egypt and Mesopotamia for ships, temples and palaces."],["Masters of the sea","Phoenician sailors built strong ships and navigated by the stars, including the North Star, which the Greeks called the Phoenician star. They traded across the Mediterranean, reached Spain and the Atlantic coast and may have sailed to Britain for tin. Greek historian Herodotus reported that Phoenicians sailed around Africa for an Egyptian pharaoh around 600 BCE, though some doubt the story."],["The alphabet","Around 1050 BCE, the Phoenicians used an alphabet of 22 letters, each standing for a consonant sound. It was far simpler than Egyptian hieroglyphs or Mesopotamian cuneiform. The Greeks adopted it and added vowels, and it later developed into the Latin alphabet. Aramaic, Hebrew and Arabic scripts also descend from it."],["Purple and glass","The Phoenicians made a rich purple dye from murex sea snails, which took thousands of snails to produce a small amount. Tyrian purple became a symbol of royalty and power in Rome and elsewhere. They were also skilled glassmakers, metalworkers and ivory carvers."]],
k:[["Homeland","Coast of present-day Lebanon"],["Main cities","Tyre, Sidon, Byblos"],["Most famous colony","Carthage"],["Alphabet","22 consonant letters"],["Famous product","Tyrian purple dye"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Phoenician cities flourish on the coast"],["c. 1050 BCE","The Phoenician alphabet is in use"],["814 BCE","Carthage is founded, according to tradition"],["c. 600 BCE","Phoenicians reportedly sail around Africa"],["332 BCE","Alexander the Great captures Tyre after a long siege"],["146 BCE","Rome destroys Carthage"]],
s2:[["Carthage","According to tradition, Carthage was founded in 814 BCE by settlers from Tyre led by Queen Dido, in present-day Tunisia. It grew into a great trading empire controlling much of the western Mediterranean. Its rivalry with Rome led to the three Punic Wars, ending with Carthage’s destruction in 146 BCE."],["Hannibal","The Carthaginian general Hannibal led an army with war elephants from Spain over the Alps into Italy in 218 BCE, during the Second Punic War. He defeated Roman armies in several battles, including Cannae in 216 BCE, but could not capture Rome. He is remembered as one of history’s greatest military commanders."],["Byblos and the Bible","The city of Byblos exported papyrus from Egypt, and the Greeks named papyrus after it: byblos. From this came biblion, book, and eventually the word Bible. Byblos is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world."],["The siege of Tyre","Tyre stood on an island just off the coast, making it hard to attack. In 332 BCE Alexander the Great besieged it for seven months, building a causeway out to the island. After capturing it, he destroyed much of the city. Silt built up along the causeway over centuries, turning Tyre into a peninsula."],["Religion","The Phoenicians worshipped gods such as Baal, Astarte and Melqart, the patron god of Tyre. Temples stood in every city. Carthage’s religion was influenced by its Phoenician roots, and ancient writers made claims about its practices that historians still debate."],["Legacy","The Phoenicians linked the peoples of the Mediterranean through trade, spreading goods, technologies and ideas. Their greatest legacy is the alphabet. Modern Lebanon proudly remembers its Phoenician heritage, and archaeological sites such as Byblos and Tyre are World Heritage Sites."]],
sp:["From Phoenician letters to ours","The first letter of the Phoenician alphabet was ʾaleph, meaning ox, drawn like an ox’s head; the second was bet, meaning house. The Greeks turned these into alpha and beta, the source of the word alphabet. Over centuries, the ox-head letter rotated and changed into our letter A. Many of the letters we use today can be traced back through Greek and Latin to Phoenician signs."],
g:[["City-state","An independent city ruling its own territory"],["Alphabet","A writing system with letters for sounds"],["Colony","A settlement founded in a new land"],["Murex","A sea snail used to make purple dye"]],
f:["The word alphabet comes from the first two Greek letters, based on Phoenician ones.","Carthage was a Phoenician colony.","Tyrian purple dye was made from sea snails."],
fun:"The word Bible comes from Byblos, a Phoenician city that traded papyrus.",
tl:"Ang mga Phoenician ay sinaunang mangangalakal at mandaragat mula sa baybayin ng Lebanon ngayon. Ang kanilang alpabeto ang pinagmulan ng alpabetong Griyego, Latin, Hebreo at Arabe. Kilala rin sila sa mamahaling pulang-ube na tina."});

ADDART({id:"celts",t:"The Celts",c:"History",i:"🍀",w:"Celts",r:["rome","greece","viking","langevo","fairy"],
p:[["Peoples of ancient Europe","The Celts were groups of Iron Age peoples across much of Europe who shared related languages and cultures."],["Warriors and artists","They were known as fierce warriors and skilled metalworkers who made beautiful jewelry and swirling designs."],["Living languages","Celtic languages such as Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh and Breton are still spoken today."]],
l:"The Celts were a collection of peoples in ancient Europe who spoke related Celtic languages and shared aspects of culture, religion and art. From about 800 BCE, their culture spread across central Europe to Britain and Ireland, Spain, France, northern Italy and even Anatolia. They were never a single nation but many tribes. Roman expansion conquered most Celtic lands, but Celtic languages and traditions survived, especially in Ireland, Scotland, Wales, Brittany, Cornwall and the Isle of Man.",
s:[["Hallstatt and La Tène","Archaeologists name two main phases of early Celtic culture after sites where many objects were found. The Hallstatt culture, named after a salt-mining village in Austria, flourished from about 800 to 450 BCE. The La Tène culture, named after a site in Switzerland, followed from about 450 BCE, famous for its flowing, curving art."],["Life and society","Celtic peoples lived mainly as farmers and herders in villages and hillforts, and some built large fortified towns the Romans called oppida. Society included warriors, craftsmen, farmers and druids, who served as priests, teachers and judges. Bards preserved history and stories in poetry and song."],["Warriors","The Celts were famed and feared as warriors. In about 390 BCE, a Celtic army led by Brennus sacked Rome. Celtic groups also invaded Greece and settled in central Anatolia, where they were called Galatians. Roman writers described them as brave, proud and fond of feasting and bright clothing."],["Conquest by Rome","Julius Caesar conquered Gaul, roughly modern France, between 58 and 50 BCE, defeating the Gallic leader Vercingetorix at Alesia in 52 BCE. Rome later conquered much of Britain. In 60 or 61 CE, Queen Boudica of the Iceni led a major revolt against Roman rule in Britain, burning London before being defeated."]],
k:[["Period","From about 800 BCE"],["Early cultures","Hallstatt and La Tène"],["Religious leaders","Druids"],["Living Celtic languages","Irish, Scottish Gaelic, Welsh, Breton, Cornish, Manx"],["Famous revolt leader","Boudica"]],
d:[["c. 800 BCE","The Hallstatt culture develops"],["c. 450 BCE","The La Tène culture emerges"],["c. 390 BCE","Celts led by Brennus sack Rome"],["52 BCE","Caesar defeats Vercingetorix at Alesia"],["60–61 CE","Boudica leads a revolt in Britain"],["c. 800","Monks create the Book of Kells"]],
s2:[["Druids","Druids were the religious and learned class of Celtic society. They led ceremonies, studied the stars and nature, settled disputes and taught young people, memorizing knowledge rather than writing it down. Much of what we know about them comes from Roman writers, who may have exaggerated. Rome suppressed the druids."],["Celtic art","Celtic artists created beautiful metalwork, such as golden neck rings called torcs, decorated shields and mirrors, with swirling spirals, knots and animal shapes. Later, Christian monks in Ireland and Britain combined these designs with Christian themes in illuminated manuscripts such as the Book of Kells."],["Samhain and Halloween","The Celts marked the year with festivals. Samhain, at the beginning of November, marked the end of the harvest and the start of winter, when the boundary between the world of the living and the dead was thought to be thin. Many traditions of Halloween, such as costumes and bonfires, may have roots in Samhain."],["Celtic languages today","Celtic languages once spoken across Europe survive today in two groups. Irish, Scottish Gaelic and Manx form one; Welsh, Breton and Cornish form the other. Welsh is spoken by hundreds of thousands of people, and Irish is an official language of Ireland and the European Union. Efforts to revive these languages include schools and media."],["Myths and legends","Celtic myths are full of heroes, gods and magical beings. Irish legends tell of the hero Cú Chulainn and of the Tuatha Dé Danann, a godlike people. Welsh tales collected in the Mabinogion and Arthurian legends, possibly rooted in Celtic Britain, have inspired many later stories."],["Who were the Celts?","Historians debate the term Celts. Ancient Greeks and Romans used names such as Keltoi and Galli for various peoples, and the idea of a single Celtic people became popular much later. Today scholars often speak of Celtic-speaking peoples with many different identities and ways of life."]],
sp:["Boudica","Boudica was queen of the Iceni people in eastern Britain. After her husband died, the Romans seized her kingdom, flogged her and mistreated her daughters. In 60 or 61 CE, she led a huge revolt, destroying the Roman towns of Camulodunum (Colchester), Londinium (London) and Verulamium (St Albans). The Roman governor eventually defeated her army. She has since become a symbol of resistance, and a bronze statue of her in a chariot stands in London."],
g:[["Druid","A priest and scholar of the ancient Celts"],["Torc","A rigid metal neck ring worn by Celts"],["Hillfort","A fortified settlement on a hill"],["Gaul","The Roman name for the land of the Celts, roughly France"]],
f:["Celts sacked Rome around 390 BCE.","Welsh and Irish are Celtic languages.","Halloween may have roots in the Celtic festival of Samhain."],
fun:"Some Halloween traditions may come from the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain.",
tl:"Ang mga Celt ay mga sinaunang mamamayan sa Europa na may magkakaugnay na wika at kultura. Kilala sila bilang mandirigma at mahuhusay na panday. Sinasalita pa rin ang mga wikang Celtic gaya ng Irish at Welsh."});

ADDART({id:"mughal",t:"The Mughal Empire",c:"History",i:"🕌",w:"Mughal Empire",r:["tajmahal","islam","hindu","indiacountry","partition"],
p:[["Rulers of India","The Mughal Empire ruled much of the Indian subcontinent from 1526 to the 1700s."],["Wealth and splendor","It was one of the richest empires in the world, famous for its art, gardens and buildings."],["The Taj Mahal","The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal as a tomb for his wife."]],
l:"The Mughal Empire was a powerful empire that ruled most of the Indian subcontinent from the early 1500s until the 1700s, with emperors continuing in name until 1857. Founded by Babur, a descendant of Timur and Genghis Khan from Central Asia, it reached its height under Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan and Aurangzeb. The Mughals ruled a vast, diverse population of Hindus, Muslims and others, created magnificent architecture such as the Taj Mahal and the Red Fort, and blended Persian, Indian and Central Asian cultures.",
s:[["Babur and the founding","Babur, a prince from Fergana in present-day Uzbekistan, invaded northern India and defeated the Sultan of Delhi at the First Battle of Panipat in 1526, using cannons and cavalry tactics. He founded the Mughal dynasty. His memoir, the Baburnama, describes his life, battles and love of gardens with remarkable honesty."],["Akbar the Great","Akbar, who ruled from 1556 to 1605, expanded the empire and built a strong administration. He sought to unite his diverse subjects through a policy of tolerance called sulh-i kul, peace with all, abolished a tax on non-Muslims and held discussions with scholars of many religions. He married Rajput princesses and appointed Hindus to high positions."],["Shah Jahan and the Taj Mahal","Shah Jahan, who ruled from 1628 to 1658, was a great builder. After his beloved wife Mumtaz Mahal died in childbirth in 1631, he built the Taj Mahal in Agra as her tomb. He also built the Red Fort and the Jama Masjid in Delhi. His son Aurangzeb imprisoned him in Agra Fort, where he could see the Taj Mahal from his window."],["Aurangzeb and decline","Aurangzeb, who ruled from 1658 to 1707, expanded the empire to its greatest size but faced revolts and costly wars, especially against the Marathas. His stricter religious policies are debated by historians. After his death, the empire weakened, regional powers rose and in 1739 the Persian ruler Nader Shah sacked Delhi."]],
k:[["Founded","1526"],["Founder","Babur"],["Greatest emperors","Akbar, Jahangir, Shah Jahan, Aurangzeb"],["Famous monument","Taj Mahal"],["Ended","1857"]],
d:[["1526","Babur wins the First Battle of Panipat"],["1556","Akbar becomes emperor"],["1632","Construction of the Taj Mahal begins"],["1707","Aurangzeb dies; the empire begins to decline"],["1739","Nader Shah sacks Delhi"],["1857","The last Mughal emperor is exiled after the Indian Rebellion"]],
s2:[["A rich empire","In about 1700 the Mughal Empire was one of the largest economies in the world, by some estimates producing about a quarter of the world’s goods. It exported fine cotton textiles, silk, spices, indigo and gems. European trading companies, including the British East India Company, came to trade and gradually gained power."],["Art and miniature painting","Mughal emperors supported artists who created detailed miniature paintings, blending Persian and Indian styles, showing court life, battles, animals, plants and portraits. Jahangir, who ruled from 1605 to 1627, was a keen naturalist and commissioned paintings of birds and flowers. Mughal jewelry, carpets and metalwork were also renowned."],["Gardens and buildings","The Mughals loved gardens laid out in four parts with water channels, called charbagh, symbolizing paradise. Mughal architecture combined domes, arches, minarets and fine inlay of colored stones in marble. Examples include Humayun’s Tomb in Delhi, Fatehpur Sikri built by Akbar, the Red Fort and the Shalimar Gardens in Lahore."],["Languages and culture","Persian was the language of the Mughal court and administration. Urdu developed from a mix of Hindustani, Persian, Arabic and Turkic words. Mughal cuisine, with dishes such as biryani and kebabs, influenced the food of South Asia. Music, poetry and festivals flourished under Mughal patronage."],["The British take over","As Mughal power declined, the British East India Company expanded its control after victories such as the Battle of Plassey in 1757. The Mughal emperor became a figurehead. After the Indian Rebellion of 1857, in which rebels proclaimed the elderly emperor Bahadur Shah II their leader, the British exiled him to Rangoon in Burma, ending the dynasty."],["The Peacock Throne","Shah Jahan commissioned the Peacock Throne, covered in gold and set with diamonds, rubies, emeralds and pearls, said to have taken seven years to make. When Nader Shah sacked Delhi in 1739, he carried it off to Persia, along with the famous Koh-i-Noor diamond. The original throne was later lost."]],
sp:["The Taj Mahal","The Taj Mahal in Agra was built between about 1632 and 1653 by some 20,000 workers, including craftsmen from across the empire and beyond. Its white marble dome, minarets and inlaid decoration of semi-precious stones make it one of the most beautiful buildings in the world. It changes color with the light, appearing pinkish at dawn and golden at sunset. It was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983 and is one of the New Seven Wonders of the World."],
g:[["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"],["Miniature painting","A small, detailed painting"],["Charbagh","A four-part Persian-style garden"],["Figurehead","A leader in name with little real power"]],
f:["The Mughal Empire was founded in 1526.","Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal for Mumtaz Mahal.","Akbar promoted tolerance among religions."],
fun:"Shah Jahan spent his last years imprisoned in Agra Fort, looking out at the Taj Mahal.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Mughal ay namuno sa malaking bahagi ng subkontinenteng Indian mula 1526. Isa ito sa pinakamayamang imperyo, kilala sa sining at arkitektura. Ipinatayo ni Shah Jahan ang Taj Mahal para sa kanyang asawa."});

ADDART({id:"seaempires",t:"Srivijaya and Majapahit",c:"History",i:"🛕",w:"Majapahit",r:["sea","indonesiacountry","precol","buddh","silk"],
p:[["Kingdoms of the sea","Srivijaya and Majapahit were powerful maritime empires based in what is now Indonesia."],["Controlling trade","They grew rich by controlling sea routes between China, India and the islands of Southeast Asia."],["Links to the Philippines","Their trade and culture reached the islands of the Philippines, as shown by ancient artifacts."]],
l:"Srivijaya and Majapahit were two of the greatest empires of early Southeast Asia. Srivijaya, centered on Palembang in Sumatra, controlled the Strait of Malacca from about the 600s to the 1200s and was a major center of Buddhist learning. Majapahit, based in eastern Java, flourished from 1293 to the early 1500s and claimed influence over much of the Indonesian archipelago and beyond. Both grew rich from sea trade between China, India and the islands, and their cultures influenced the wider region, including the Philippines.",
s:[["Srivijaya","Srivijaya rose in the 600s around Palembang on the island of Sumatra. By controlling the Strait of Malacca and the Sunda Strait, it taxed and protected ships sailing between China and India. Its kings were Buddhist, and its inscriptions, among the oldest in the Malay language, date from the 680s."],["A center of learning","The Chinese Buddhist monk Yijing stopped in Srivijaya in 671 on his way to India and later returned to study and translate Buddhist texts there, advising other pilgrims to learn in Srivijaya first. Thousands of monks lived and studied in the capital. Buddhist art and temples spread across the region."],["Majapahit","Majapahit was founded in 1293 by Raden Wijaya in eastern Java, after he used a Mongol invasion force sent by Kublai Khan to defeat his rivals and then drove the Mongols out. Under King Hayam Wuruk and his chief minister Gajah Mada in the 1300s, Majapahit reached its height, with influence across much of the archipelago."],["Trade and wealth","Majapahit traded rice, spices and other goods with China, India, the Middle East and other Southeast Asian kingdoms. Its ports bustled with merchants. The court poem Nagarakretagama, written in 1365, describes the capital and lists many places said to pay tribute, though historians debate how much real control Majapahit had."]],
k:[["Srivijaya capital","Palembang, Sumatra"],["Srivijaya period","c. 600s–1200s"],["Majapahit capital","Trowulan, East Java"],["Majapahit period","1293–early 1500s"],["Famous minister","Gajah Mada"]],
d:[["671","The monk Yijing visits Srivijaya"],["683","The Kedukan Bukit inscription records Srivijaya’s power"],["900","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is written in the Philippines"],["1025","The Chola Empire of southern India raids Srivijaya"],["1293","Majapahit is founded after a Mongol invasion of Java"],["1365","The poem Nagarakretagama is written"]],
s2:[["The Laguna Copperplate Inscription","In 1989 a worker near Lumban, Laguna, found a thin copper plate inscribed in the Kawi script, an early script of Java. Dated to the year 900, it records the cancellation of a debt and mentions places in the Philippines and possibly Java. It is the oldest known written document from the Philippines and shows that the islands were connected with the cultures and trade networks of Java and the Malay world."],["Gajah Mada’s oath","Gajah Mada, Majapahit’s powerful chief minister, is said to have sworn the Palapa Oath, vowing not to eat a certain spice until he had united the archipelago under Majapahit. He led military campaigns that expanded its influence. In modern Indonesia, he is a national hero, and the country’s first satellite was named Palapa."],["The Chola raid","In 1025 the Chola Empire of southern India, a rival trading power, launched a naval raid on Srivijaya, attacking its ports and capturing its king. Srivijaya never fully recovered its dominance, and other kingdoms rose in the region over the following centuries."],["Art and religion","Both empires blended local traditions with Hinduism and Buddhism from India. Java’s nearby temples, such as Buddhist Borobudur and Hindu Prambanan, built around the 800s by other Javanese dynasties, show the richness of this culture. Majapahit art included terracotta figures, temples and red-brick architecture at Trowulan."],["The coming of Islam","From the 1300s, Muslim merchants and teachers spread Islam through the ports of Southeast Asia. As Majapahit weakened in the 1400s, Muslim states such as the Sultanate of Malacca and Demak in Java rose. Islam also spread to the southern Philippines, where the Sultanate of Sulu was founded in the 1400s."],["Legacy","Indonesia sees Majapahit as a symbol of national unity, and its red and white colors are thought to have inspired the Indonesian flag. Srivijaya and Majapahit show that Southeast Asia had powerful, sophisticated states long before European arrival, linked by the sea to the wider world."]],
sp:["Driving out the Mongols","In 1292 Kublai Khan sent a large fleet to punish a Javanese king who had insulted his envoy. When the Mongols arrived in 1293, that king had already been killed by a rival. The clever prince Raden Wijaya allied with the Mongols to defeat the rival, then turned on the Mongols themselves and drove them back to their ships. He founded Majapahit, named, according to legend, after a bitter maja fruit found at the site."],
g:[["Maritime empire","An empire whose power is based on the sea"],["Strait","A narrow channel of water linking two seas"],["Tribute","Payment or gifts showing respect to a stronger state"],["Kawi","An old script used in Java and the Philippines"]],
f:["Srivijaya controlled the Strait of Malacca.","Majapahit was founded in 1293.","The Laguna Copperplate Inscription dates from 900."],
fun:"Indonesia’s first communications satellite was named Palapa after Gajah Mada’s oath.",
tl:"Ang Srivijaya at Majapahit ay makapangyarihang imperyong pandagat sa Indonesia ngayon. Yumaman sila sa pagkontrol ng kalakalan sa dagat. Ipinapakita ng Laguna Copperplate Inscription (900) ang ugnayan ng Pilipinas sa kulturang ito."});

ADDART({id:"blackdeath",t:"The Black Death",c:"History",i:"💀",w:"Black Death",r:["pandemic","microbe","silk","mongol","antibiotics"],
p:[["A deadly plague","The Black Death was a devastating plague pandemic that swept across Asia, Europe and Africa in the 1340s and 1350s."],["Spread by fleas","It was caused by bacteria carried by fleas living on rats and other animals."],["A changed world","It killed a large share of Europe’s people and transformed society."]],
l:"The Black Death was a pandemic of bubonic plague that struck western Asia, the Middle East, North Africa and Europe between 1346 and 1353. It was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, spread mainly by fleas carried by rats and by people. It killed an estimated 30 to 60 percent of Europe’s population, tens of millions of people, along with huge numbers elsewhere. The Black Death caused terrible suffering but also brought lasting changes to economies, religion, medicine and society.",
s:[["Origins","Scientists studying DNA from burials have traced the strain behind the Black Death to Central Asia, near Lake Issyk-Kul in present-day Kyrgyzstan, where graves from 1338 and 1339 record deaths from plague. Plague spread along trade routes of the Mongol Empire, including the Silk Roads. In 1346 it struck the Mongol army besieging the trading city of Caffa in Crimea."],["Spread to Europe","Merchant ships from Caffa carried the disease across the Black Sea and Mediterranean. In October 1347, ships arrived at Messina in Sicily with sick and dying sailors. Within months, plague spread through Italy, France, Spain, England and beyond, reaching Scandinavia and Russia by 1351. It also devastated Egypt, Syria and North Africa."],["Symptoms","Bubonic plague caused fever, chills and painful swellings called buboes in the groin, armpits or neck. Many victims died within days. When plague infected the lungs, it could spread directly from person to person through coughing, and this pneumonic form was almost always fatal. Septicemic plague infected the blood."],["Fear and response","People did not know what caused the plague. Some blamed bad air, the stars or divine punishment. Groups called flagellants whipped themselves in public to seek forgiveness. Tragically, Jewish communities were falsely blamed and attacked in many places. Some cities isolated the sick and controlled arrivals, early forms of public health measures."]],
k:[["Years","1346–1353"],["Cause","Bacterium Yersinia pestis"],["Spread by","Fleas on rats; also person to person"],["Europe’s population lost","About 30–60 percent"],["Origin","Central Asia"]],
d:[["1338–1339","Plague deaths are recorded near Lake Issyk-Kul"],["1346","Plague strikes the siege of Caffa"],["1347","Plague reaches Messina in Sicily"],["1348","The Black Death sweeps through France, Italy and England"],["1377","Ragusa (Dubrovnik) introduces quarantine for travelers"],["1894","Alexandre Yersin identifies the plague bacterium"]],
s2:[["Quarantine","In 1377 the city of Ragusa, now Dubrovnik in Croatia, required ships and travelers from plague areas to wait 30 days before entering. Venice and other cities later extended the waiting time to 40 days, quaranta giorni in Italian, giving us the word quarantine. Quarantine remains an important tool against infectious diseases."],["Changes to society","With so many people dead, there were too few workers to farm the land. Surviving laborers demanded higher wages and more freedom, weakening the feudal system in Western Europe. Rulers tried to freeze wages, leading to unrest such as the Peasants’ Revolt in England in 1381. Some historians link these changes to the later rise of new economies."],["Writers of the plague","The Italian writer Giovanni Boccaccio began his Decameron with a vivid description of plague in Florence, then told stories shared by young people who fled the city. The North African historian Ibn Khaldun, who lost his parents to the plague, described how it emptied towns and changed civilizations."],["Discovering the cause","The cause of plague was discovered during a later pandemic. In 1894 in Hong Kong, the Swiss-French scientist Alexandre Yersin identified the bacterium now named Yersinia pestis. In 1898 Paul-Louis Simond showed that rat fleas spread it. DNA from Black Death burials, studied in the 2010s, confirmed that Yersinia pestis caused the medieval pandemic."],["Plague today","Plague still exists in wild rodents in parts of Africa, Asia and the Americas, causing occasional human cases. Today it can be treated successfully with antibiotics if caught early. Madagascar, the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Peru are among the countries that report cases."],["Art and memory","The Black Death inspired art showing the Dance of Death, in which skeletons lead people of all ranks, from kings to peasants, reminding viewers that death comes to everyone. Some historians think the nursery rhyme Ring a Ring o’ Roses refers to the plague, but folklorists have found no evidence for this."]],
sp:["The word quarantine","When plague kept returning to Europe after the Black Death, cities looked for ways to protect themselves. Ragusa made ships wait 30 days, a trentino, before landing. Venice built isolation hospitals on islands in its lagoon and later required ships to wait 40 days. The Italian word for 40, quaranta, gave English the word quarantine. During the COVID-19 pandemic, quarantine again became a familiar word around the world."],
g:[["Plague","A serious infectious disease caused by Yersinia pestis"],["Bubo","A swollen lymph node, a sign of bubonic plague"],["Quarantine","Isolating people who may carry a disease"],["Feudalism","A medieval system in which peasants worked land for lords"]],
f:["The Black Death was caused by bacteria spread by fleas.","It killed about a third to more than half of Europe’s people.","The word quarantine comes from the Italian for 40 days."],
fun:"The word quarantine comes from the 40 days ships had to wait before landing in Venice.",
tl:"Ang Black Death ay matinding salot noong 1340s at 1350s na kumalat sa Asya, Europa at Aprika. Dulot ito ng bakteryang dala ng mga pulgas sa daga. Napatay nito ang malaking bahagi ng populasyon ng Europa."});

ADDART({id:"greenrev",t:"The Green Revolution",c:"History",i:"🌾",w:"Green Revolution",r:["rice","wheat","agri","indiacountry","mexicocountry"],
p:[["More food for the world","The Green Revolution was a period from the 1940s to the 1970s when new crop varieties and farming methods greatly increased harvests."],["Miracle rice","Scientists at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, Laguna, created high-yield rice that spread across Asia."],["Benefits and costs","It saved millions from hunger but also brought environmental and social problems."]],
l:"The Green Revolution was a period of rapid increases in crop yields, especially of wheat and rice, in developing countries from the 1940s to the 1970s. Scientists bred high-yielding varieties that produced far more grain when grown with fertilizers, irrigation and pesticides. Grain production in many countries soared, helping India, Mexico, the Philippines and others avoid feared famines as populations grew. The Green Revolution is credited with saving many millions of lives, but it also raised concerns about the environment, water use and fairness to small farmers.",
s:[["Borlaug and wheat","In 1944 the American scientist Norman Borlaug began working in Mexico on a program to improve wheat. He bred short, sturdy wheat varieties that resisted disease and did not fall over when heavily fertilized. Mexico became self-sufficient in wheat, and the new varieties spread to India and Pakistan in the 1960s. Borlaug won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970."],["IRRI and miracle rice","The International Rice Research Institute, IRRI, was founded in 1960 in Los Baños, Laguna, Philippines, with support from the Ford and Rockefeller foundations and the Philippine government. In 1966 it released IR8, a semi-dwarf rice that could double yields with fertilizer and irrigation. Nicknamed miracle rice, it spread rapidly across Asia."],["India’s transformation","In the 1960s India faced severe food shortages and depended on imported grain. Scientists such as M. S. Swaminathan introduced high-yielding wheat and rice, and states such as Punjab and Haryana saw harvests soar. Within years, India became largely self-sufficient in grain, an achievement that shaped its development."],["How it worked","The Green Revolution combined several elements: high-yielding seeds, chemical fertilizers, irrigation, pesticides and government support through credit, research and guaranteed prices. The new varieties put more energy into grain rather than stalks and could be planted more than once a year in some places."]],
k:[["Period","About 1940s–1970s"],["Key scientist","Norman Borlaug"],["Rice institute","IRRI, Los Baños, founded 1960"],["Miracle rice","IR8, released 1966"],["Nobel Peace Prize","Borlaug, 1970"]],
d:[["1944","Norman Borlaug begins wheat research in Mexico"],["1960","IRRI is founded in Los Baños, Laguna"],["1966","IRRI releases IR8 rice"],["1968","Indian wheat harvests reach record levels"],["1970","Borlaug wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2021","The Philippines approves Golden Rice for planting"]],
s2:[["Feeding a growing world","Between the 1960s and 2000, grain production in developing countries roughly tripled while the area of farmland grew only modestly. In 1968 a famous book predicted that hundreds of millions would starve in the 1970s; instead, food production generally kept pace with population growth in much of Asia and Latin America."],["Environmental concerns","Heavy use of fertilizers and pesticides polluted rivers and harmed wildlife and human health. Irrigation drained groundwater in places such as Punjab. Planting the same few varieties over large areas reduced crop diversity and made crops vulnerable to pests. Today scientists aim for a more sustainable approach."],["Who benefited?","Farmers who could afford seeds, fertilizer and irrigation often gained most, while poorer farmers sometimes fell into debt or lost land. Regions with good water and roads benefited more than others. Much of Africa saw fewer gains, partly because of different crops, soils and conditions, and efforts continue to raise yields there."],["The Philippines and rice","IRRI made the Philippines a world center of rice research. Its scientists, including many Filipinos, have developed hundreds of varieties, including rice that survives floods for two weeks, tolerates salt or drought, and matures faster. IRRI’s gene bank stores more than 100,000 types of rice from around the world."],["Golden Rice","Scientists developed Golden Rice, genetically modified to produce beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A, to help prevent blindness and illness caused by vitamin A deficiency. In 2021 the Philippines became the first country to approve it for commercial planting, but in 2024 a court halted its planting pending legal challenges, reflecting debates about genetically modified crops."],["Toward an evergreen revolution","M. S. Swaminathan called for an evergreen revolution: increasing food production while protecting soil, water and biodiversity. Modern approaches include precise use of fertilizer and water, crop rotation, improved varieties bred with genetic tools, and supporting small farmers, who produce much of the world’s food."]],
sp:["IR8, the miracle rice","IR8 was created at IRRI by crossing a tall, vigorous Indonesian rice called Peta with a short Chinese-Taiwanese variety, Dee-geo-woo-gen. The result was a short, sturdy plant that did not fall over when heavily fertilized and could produce up to 5 to 10 tonnes per hectare, compared with 1 to 2 tonnes for many traditional varieties. Released in 1966, it helped prevent food shortages across Asia. It became a symbol of hope across the continent."],
g:[["High-yielding variety","A crop bred to produce much more grain"],["Fertilizer","A substance added to soil to help plants grow"],["Irrigation","Supplying water to crops artificially"],["Gene bank","A collection that stores seeds for the future"]],
f:["IRRI was founded in Los Baños, Laguna, in 1960.","IR8 was called miracle rice.","Norman Borlaug won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970."],
fun:"IRRI’s gene bank in Los Baños stores more than 100,000 types of rice.",
tl:"Ang Green Revolution ay panahon mula 1940s hanggang 1970s kung kailan lubhang tumaas ang ani dahil sa bagong binhi at paraan ng pagsasaka. Ginawa sa IRRI sa Los Baños, Laguna ang IR8, ang tinaguriang miracle rice."});

;
/* data/cities.js */
// Countries and Places: great cities, part 3: Tokyo, Cairo, Istanbul and Rio de Janeiro.
// Same format as data/countries1.js (see data/animals-load.js).
addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{tokyo:[139.69,35.69,"Japan",36,"Tokyo"],cairo:[31.24,30.04,"Egypt",36,"Cairo"],istanbul:[28.98,41.01,"Turkey",36,"Istanbul"],rio:[-43.2,-22.91,"Brazil",36,"Rio de Janeiro"]})});

ADDART({id:"tokyo",t:"Tokyo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗼",w:"Tokyo",r:["japancountry","japan","meiji","fuji","urban"],
p:[["Japan’s capital","Tokyo is the capital of Japan and the heart of one of the largest urban areas on Earth."],["Old and new","Ancient temples and shrines stand among skyscrapers, neon signs and busy train stations."],["Rebuilt twice","Tokyo was rebuilt after a great earthquake in 1923 and after bombing in World War II."]],
l:"Tokyo is the capital of Japan, located on Tokyo Bay on the island of Honshu. The Tokyo Metropolis has about 14 million people, and the Greater Tokyo Area, with about 37 million by common measures, has long been considered the world’s largest metropolitan area; a 2025 United Nations study using a new method ranked Jakarta and Dhaka larger. Once a fishing village called Edo, it became the seat of the shoguns in 1603 and Japan’s capital in 1868. Tokyo is a world center of finance, technology, fashion, food and popular culture.",
s:[["From Edo to Tokyo","In 1457 the warrior Ōta Dōkan built a castle at Edo. In 1603 Tokugawa Ieyasu made Edo the seat of his shogunate, and it grew into one of the largest cities in the world, with over a million people by the 1700s. When the emperor moved there in 1868, at the start of the Meiji era, it was renamed Tokyo, meaning eastern capital."],["Disaster and rebirth","On 1 September 1923, the Great Kantō earthquake and the fires that followed destroyed much of Tokyo and Yokohama, killing over 100,000 people. In March 1945, during World War II, American firebombing killed around 100,000 people in a single night and burned large areas. Each time, Tokyo was rebuilt, and after the war it grew into a modern megacity."],["A city of trains","Tokyo has one of the world’s busiest and most punctual public transport systems, with dozens of train and subway lines. Shinjuku Station is the busiest railway station in the world, used by millions of people each day. The first Shinkansen bullet train line opened between Tokyo and Osaka in 1964."],["Neighborhoods","Tokyo is made up of many distinct areas. Shibuya is known for its famous scramble crossing, Shinjuku for skyscrapers and nightlife, Ginza for shopping, Akihabara for electronics, anime and games, and Asakusa for the ancient Sensō-ji temple. Harajuku is famous for youth fashion."]],
k:[["Country","Japan"],["Tokyo Metropolis population","About 14 million"],["Greater Tokyo","About 37 million (common estimates)"],["Former name","Edo"],["Tallest structure","Tokyo Skytree, 634 m"]],
d:[["1457","Ōta Dōkan builds Edo Castle"],["1603","Edo becomes the seat of the Tokugawa shogunate"],["1868","Edo is renamed Tokyo and becomes the capital"],["1923","The Great Kantō earthquake devastates the city"],["1964","Tokyo hosts the Summer Olympics; the Shinkansen opens"],["2021","Tokyo hosts the postponed 2020 Summer Olympics"]],
s2:[["Sensō-ji","Sensō-ji in Asakusa is Tokyo’s oldest temple, traditionally founded in 645 CE after fishermen found a statue of the bodhisattva Kannon in a river. Visitors pass through the Kaminarimon, the Thunder Gate, with its giant red lantern, and walk along Nakamise, a street of traditional shops, to reach the main hall."],["The 1964 Olympics","In 1964 Tokyo became the first Asian city to host the Summer Olympics. The Games showed the world Japan’s recovery after the war. In preparation, Tokyo built highways, hotels and the first Shinkansen line. Tokyo hosted the Games again in 2021, delayed by a year because of the COVID-19 pandemic."],["Food capital","Tokyo has more restaurants awarded Michelin stars than any other city. It is famous for sushi, ramen, tempura, soba and many other foods. The famous fish market moved from Tsukiji to a new site at Toyosu in 2018, while the old outer market still bustles with food stalls."],["Pop culture","Tokyo is a center of anime, manga, video games and music. Akihabara is packed with shops selling electronics, figures and games, and Ikebukuro and Nakano are also popular with fans. Character goods, themed cafés and karaoke are part of the city’s lively culture."],["Living with earthquakes","Tokyo lies in one of the world’s most earthquake-prone regions. Buildings follow strict codes, and many skyscrapers use special systems to absorb shaking. Schools hold regular drills, and 1 September, the anniversary of the 1923 earthquake, is Disaster Prevention Day in Japan."],["Green spaces","Despite its size, Tokyo has many parks and gardens. Ueno Park has museums and a zoo, Shinjuku Gyoen is famous for cherry blossoms and the Meiji Jingu shrine stands in a forest planted by volunteers from across Japan in the 1910s and 1920s. The Imperial Palace, on the site of Edo Castle, is surrounded by moats and gardens."]],
sp:["The Shibuya scramble","Outside Shibuya Station lies one of the busiest pedestrian crossings in the world. When the traffic lights turn red in all directions, pedestrians flood the intersection from every side at once, sometimes thousands at a time, crossing straight and diagonally. Despite the crowds, people rarely collide. Nearby stands a statue of Hachikō, a loyal dog who waited at the station for his owner every day for nearly ten years after the owner’s death in 1925."],
g:[["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Shogunate","A government led by a shogun, a military ruler"],["Shinkansen","Japan’s high-speed bullet train"],["Metropolis","A very large city; also Tokyo’s official name as a prefecture"]],
f:["Tokyo was once called Edo.","Shinjuku Station is the busiest station in the world.","Tokyo has more Michelin-starred restaurants than any other city."],
fun:"Tokyo’s name means eastern capital.",
tl:"Ang Tokyo ang kabisera ng Hapon at isa sa pinakamalaking urbanong lugar sa mundo. Dati itong tinatawag na Edo. Itinayong muli ito matapos ang lindol noong 1923 at ang pambobomba noong Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig."});

ADDART({id:"cairo",t:"Cairo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Cairo",r:["egyptcountry","egypt","pyramids","nile","islam"],
p:[["City on the Nile","Cairo is the capital of Egypt, spreading along the Nile River near where it fans out into its delta."],["City of a thousand minarets","Its historic Islamic quarter is filled with mosques, madrasas and markets."],["Gateway to the pyramids","The Pyramids of Giza stand on the city’s western edge."]],
l:"Cairo is the capital and largest city of Egypt and one of the largest cities in Africa and the Arab world, with more than 20 million people in its metropolitan area. It lies on the Nile River, close to the ancient capital of Memphis and the Pyramids of Giza. Founded in 969 CE, Cairo became a great center of Islamic learning, trade and architecture, and its historic quarter is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today it is a bustling, crowded city, a hub of government, culture, film and education in the Arab world.",
s:[["Ancient roots","Long before Cairo was founded, the area was home to Memphis, capital of ancient Egypt, and Heliopolis, a center of sun worship. In 641 CE Arab armies founded the city of Fustat on the Nile. In 969 the Fatimid dynasty built a new walled royal city nearby called al-Qāhira, the Victorious, from which the name Cairo comes."],["A center of learning","The Fatimids founded al-Azhar Mosque in 970, and it grew into one of the oldest continuously operating universities in the world, still a leading center of Islamic scholarship. Students from across the Muslim world have studied there for over a thousand years."],["Medieval splendor","In 1176 Saladin began building the Citadel on a hill overlooking the city. Under the Mamluk sultans, from 1250 to 1517, Cairo was one of the largest and richest cities in the world, filled with mosques, schools, hospitals and markets. Many of these buildings still stand in Islamic Cairo."],["Modern Cairo","In the 1800s, the ruler Khedive Ismail built new districts with wide boulevards modeled on Paris. Cairo grew rapidly in the 1900s as people moved from the countryside. In 1987 it opened the first metro system in Africa. Egypt is building a New Administrative Capital east of Cairo to ease crowding."]],
k:[["Country","Egypt"],["Metropolitan population","More than 20 million"],["Founded","969 CE"],["Name means","The Victorious"],["River","Nile"]],
d:[["641","Arab armies found Fustat"],["969","The Fatimids found al-Qāhira"],["970","Al-Azhar Mosque is founded"],["1176","Saladin begins building the Citadel"],["1979","Historic Cairo becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2025","The Grand Egyptian Museum fully opens near the pyramids"]],
s2:[["The Pyramids of Giza","On the western edge of Greater Cairo stand the Pyramids of Giza, built about 4,500 years ago as tombs for the pharaohs Khufu, Khafre and Menkaure, along with the Great Sphinx. The Great Pyramid is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing. Millions of visitors come to see them each year."],["Museums of Cairo","The Egyptian Museum in Tahrir Square, opened in 1902, holds a vast collection of ancient artifacts. The Grand Egyptian Museum near Giza, one of the largest archaeological museums in the world, fully opened in 2025 and displays the complete treasures of Tutankhamun’s tomb."],["Khan el-Khalili","Khan el-Khalili is a famous bazaar in Islamic Cairo, dating back to the 1300s. Its narrow lanes are lined with shops selling spices, perfumes, lanterns, jewelry, textiles and souvenirs, and coffeehouses where people have gathered for centuries. The Nobel Prize-winning novelist Naguib Mahfouz set many stories in this area."],["Naguib Mahfouz","Naguib Mahfouz, born in Cairo in 1911, wrote novels about the lives of ordinary Cairenes, including the Cairo Trilogy, which follows a family through the early 1900s. In 1988 he became the first Arabic-language writer to win the Nobel Prize in Literature."],["Hollywood of the Arab world","Cairo has long been the center of Arab film, music and television. Egyptian films and songs, such as those of the singer Umm Kulthum, became popular across the Arab world, spreading the Egyptian dialect of Arabic. Cairo’s studios have produced thousands of films."],["Life in a megacity","Cairo is lively, noisy and crowded, with heavy traffic, busy markets and street food such as koshari, a dish of rice, lentils, pasta and tomato sauce. Many people live in informal neighborhoods. The city faces challenges of air pollution, housing and water, but its energy and history make it one of the world’s great cities."]],
sp:["The City of the Dead","In the east of Cairo lies a vast area of tombs and mausoleums built from the Mamluk period onward, called the City of the Dead, or the Qarafa. Some of its tombs are masterpieces of Islamic architecture. Over generations, many families have made homes among the tombs, living alongside the graves of the dead, creating a unique neighborhood where tens of thousands of people live."],
g:[["Delta","Land at a river’s mouth where it splits into branches"],["Madrasa","An Islamic school"],["Bazaar","A market with many shops"],["Fatimids","A dynasty that ruled Egypt from 969 to 1171"]],
f:["Cairo’s name means the Victorious.","Al-Azhar is one of the oldest universities in the world.","Cairo opened Africa’s first metro in 1987."],
fun:"Cairo is sometimes called the city of a thousand minarets.",
tl:"Ang Cairo ang kabisera ng Ehipto, nasa tabi ng Ilog Nile. Isa ito sa pinakamalaking lungsod sa Aprika at mundong Arabe. Nasa gilid nito ang mga Piramide ng Giza, at kilala ito sa mga moske at sa Unibersidad ng al-Azhar."});

ADDART({id:"istanbul",t:"Istanbul",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Istanbul",r:["turkeycountry","byzant","ottoman","rome","silk"],
p:[["City on two continents","Istanbul spans the Bosphorus strait, with parts in both Europe and Asia."],["Three names","It has been called Byzantium, Constantinople and Istanbul."],["Capital of empires","It was the capital of the Byzantine and Ottoman empires for about 1,600 years."]],
l:"Istanbul is the largest city of Türkiye, with about 15.7 million people, making it one of the largest cities in Europe. It lies on both sides of the Bosphorus, a narrow strait connecting the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara, so it is partly in Europe and partly in Asia. Founded as the Greek colony of Byzantium, it became Constantinople, capital of the Roman and Byzantine empires, and later the capital of the Ottoman Empire. Its skyline of domes and minarets, including Hagia Sophia, reflects its long history.",
s:[["Byzantium","According to tradition, Greek colonists from Megara founded Byzantium around 657 BCE on the European side of the Bosphorus. Its position, controlling the passage between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean and the crossing between Europe and Asia, made it valuable to every empire in the region."],["Constantinople","In 330 CE the Roman emperor Constantine the Great made the city his new capital and renamed it Constantinople. After the western Roman Empire fell, it remained the capital of the Eastern Roman, or Byzantine, Empire for over a thousand years, protected by massive walls built in the 400s. It was one of the largest and richest cities in the world."],["The Ottoman capital","On 29 May 1453, the Ottoman sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople after a long siege, ending the Byzantine Empire. The city became the Ottoman capital and was gradually known as Istanbul. Sultans built magnificent mosques and palaces, such as Topkapı Palace and the Süleymaniye Mosque, designed by the great architect Mimar Sinan."],["Modern Istanbul","When the Republic of Turkey was founded in 1923, Ankara became the capital. But Istanbul remained the country’s largest city and center of business, culture and tourism. Bridges and tunnels now link its European and Asian sides, including the Marmaray rail tunnel under the Bosphorus, opened in 2013."]],
k:[["Country","Türkiye"],["Population","About 15.7 million"],["Former names","Byzantium, Constantinople"],["Waterway","Bosphorus Strait"],["Continents","Europe and Asia"]],
d:[["c. 657 BCE","Greek colonists found Byzantium"],["330 CE","Constantine makes the city his capital"],["537","Hagia Sophia is completed"],["1453","The Ottomans capture Constantinople"],["1923","Ankara becomes Turkey’s capital"],["1973","The first Bosphorus Bridge links Europe and Asia"]],
s2:[["Hagia Sophia","Hagia Sophia, meaning holy wisdom, was completed in 537 under the Byzantine emperor Justinian. For nearly a thousand years it was the largest church in the world, famous for its enormous dome that seems to float on light. After 1453 it became a mosque, in 1934 a museum and in 2020 a mosque again, while remaining open to visitors."],["The Blue Mosque","The Sultan Ahmed Mosque, completed in 1616, faces Hagia Sophia across a square. It is called the Blue Mosque because of the more than 20,000 blue tiles decorating its interior. It has six minarets and is still an active place of worship."],["The Grand Bazaar","The Grand Bazaar, begun in the 1450s soon after the Ottoman conquest, is one of the largest and oldest covered markets in the world, with about 4,000 shops along dozens of streets. It sells carpets, jewelry, ceramics, spices, lamps and leather goods."],["The walls of Theodosius","In the 400s, Emperor Theodosius II built massive land walls stretching about 6 kilometers across the peninsula, with moats, towers and double walls. They protected Constantinople from many sieges for about a thousand years, until Ottoman cannons breached them in 1453. Long sections still stand."],["Earthquake risk","Istanbul lies near the North Anatolian Fault, one of the world’s most active faults. A major earthquake in 1999 near İzmit, east of the city, killed more than 17,000 people. Scientists warn that a large earthquake near Istanbul is likely in the coming decades, and the city is strengthening buildings."],["A city of writers and cats","The novelist Orhan Pamuk, winner of the 2006 Nobel Prize in Literature, has written lovingly about his native Istanbul. The city is also famous for its many street cats, cared for by residents who leave food and water for them. They appear in films and have become a symbol of the city."]],
sp:["The fall of Constantinople","In 1453 the young Ottoman sultan Mehmed II besieged Constantinople with a huge army and giant cannons, including one that fired stone balls weighing hundreds of kilograms. When the defenders blocked the Golden Horn harbor with a great chain, Mehmed had ships dragged overland on greased logs into the harbor. On 29 May, Ottoman troops broke through the walls, and the last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died fighting. Mehmed became known as the Conqueror."],
g:[["Strait","A narrow waterway connecting two seas"],["Byzantine Empire","The Eastern Roman Empire, centered on Constantinople"],["Minaret","A tall tower of a mosque"],["Bazaar","A market of many shops"]],
f:["Istanbul lies in both Europe and Asia.","Hagia Sophia was completed in 537.","Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453."],
fun:"In 1453 the Ottomans dragged warships overland to get around a chain blocking the harbor.",
tl:"Ang Istanbul ang pinakamalaking lungsod ng Türkiye, nasa magkabilang panig ng Bosphorus, kaya bahagi ito ng Europa at Asya. Dati itong tinawag na Byzantium at Constantinople at naging kabisera ng mga imperyong Byzantine at Ottoman."});

ADDART({id:"rio",t:"Rio de Janeiro",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏖️",w:"Rio de Janeiro",r:["brazilcountry","soccer","olymp","festivals","amazon"],
p:[["The marvelous city","Rio de Janeiro, in Brazil, is famous for its beaches, mountains and the giant statue of Christ the Redeemer."],["Samba parades","Its Carnival, with samba parades, is one of the biggest festivals in the world."],["Former capital","Rio was the capital of Brazil until 1960 and briefly the seat of the Portuguese Empire."]],
l:"Rio de Janeiro is a city in southeastern Brazil, with about 6.2 million people and over 12 million in its metropolitan area. Set between green mountains and the Atlantic Ocean around Guanabara Bay, it is known as the Cidade Maravilhosa, the marvelous city. Rio is famous for Copacabana and Ipanema beaches, Sugarloaf Mountain, the statue of Christ the Redeemer and its spectacular Carnival. It was Brazil’s capital until 1960 and hosted the 2016 Olympic Games, the first in South America.",
s:[["River of January","Portuguese explorers arrived in January 1502 and mistook the entrance of Guanabara Bay for the mouth of a river, naming it Rio de Janeiro, River of January. The city was founded in 1565 by Estácio de Sá. It grew as a port for gold and diamonds from Minas Gerais and became the capital of colonial Brazil in 1763."],["A royal capital","In 1808, fleeing Napoleon’s invasion of Portugal, the Portuguese royal family and court sailed to Rio, making it the capital of the Portuguese Empire, the only European empire ruled from the Americas. After Brazil’s independence in 1822, Rio was the capital of the Empire of Brazil and then of the republic until Brasília replaced it in 1960."],["Mountains and sea","Rio is famous for its dramatic landscape, recognized by UNESCO in 2012. Sugarloaf Mountain rises from the bay, with cable cars running since 1912. The Tijuca Forest, one of the world’s largest urban forests, was replanted in the 1860s after coffee farming had cleared it. Beaches such as Copacabana and Ipanema are centers of daily life."],["People and inequality","Rio is a city of great contrasts. Wealthy neighborhoods stand near favelas, informal communities built on hillsides, where a large share of the population lives. Favelas are home to vibrant culture, music and community life, but many face poverty and violence. Efforts continue to improve services and safety."]],
k:[["Country","Brazil"],["City population","About 6.2 million (2022 census)"],["Founded","1565"],["Name means","River of January"],["Famous statue","Christ the Redeemer (1931)"]],
d:[["1502","Portuguese explorers arrive at Guanabara Bay"],["1565","Estácio de Sá founds the city"],["1808","The Portuguese royal court moves to Rio"],["1931","The statue of Christ the Redeemer is completed"],["1960","Brasília replaces Rio as Brazil’s capital"],["2016","Rio hosts the first Olympic Games in South America"]],
s2:[["Christ the Redeemer","The statue of Christ the Redeemer stands with outstretched arms on top of Corcovado mountain, 700 meters above the city. Completed in 1931, it is about 30 meters tall, not counting its pedestal, and made of reinforced concrete covered with soapstone tiles. It was designed by the French sculptor Paul Landowski and the Brazilian engineer Heitor da Silva Costa, and was named one of the New Seven Wonders of the World in 2007."],["Carnival","Rio’s Carnival, held before Lent, attracts millions of people. The highlight is the parade of samba schools in the Sambadrome, designed by the architect Oscar Niemeyer and opened in 1984. Each school presents thousands of dancers, drummers and giant floats telling a story. Street parties called blocos fill neighborhoods across the city."],["Samba and bossa nova","Samba, with roots in Afro-Brazilian music, developed in Rio in the early 1900s and became Brazil’s national rhythm. In the late 1950s, musicians such as Antônio Carlos Jobim and João Gilberto created bossa nova, a gentle blend of samba and jazz. The song The Girl from Ipanema became one of the most recorded songs in history."],["The Maracanã","The Maracanã stadium was built for the 1950 FIFA World Cup. In the deciding match, about 200,000 people watched Brazil lose to Uruguay, a shock known as the Maracanaço. The stadium later hosted the 2014 World Cup final and the opening and closing ceremonies of the 2016 Olympics."],["The 2016 Olympics","In 2016 Rio hosted the Summer Olympics, the first in South America, followed by the Paralympics. The Games featured the first-ever Refugee Olympic Team. They brought new transport lines but also debate about costs and their lasting benefits for residents."],["Afro-Brazilian heritage","Rio was once a major port of the transatlantic slave trade. The Valongo Wharf, where hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans landed, was rediscovered in 2011 and named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017. Afro-Brazilian culture deeply shapes Rio’s music, religion, food and Carnival."]],
sp:["Capital of an empire","When Napoleon’s armies invaded Portugal in 1807, the Portuguese royal family boarded ships with thousands of courtiers and sailed to Brazil, arriving in Rio in 1808. Rio suddenly became the capital of a global empire, gaining a royal library, botanical garden, printing press and bank. In 1815 Brazil was raised to a kingdom equal to Portugal. When King João VI returned to Lisbon in 1821, his son Pedro stayed and in 1822 declared Brazil independent."],
g:[["Favela","An informal settlement in a Brazilian city"],["Samba","A Brazilian music and dance style"],["Carnival","A festival before Lent, with parades and parties"],["Bossa nova","A Brazilian music style mixing samba and jazz"]],
f:["Rio de Janeiro means River of January.","Christ the Redeemer was completed in 1931.","Rio hosted South America’s first Olympics in 2016."],
fun:"Rio was once the capital of the Portuguese Empire, the only European empire ruled from the Americas.",
tl:"Ang Rio de Janeiro ay lungsod sa Brazil na kilala sa mga dalampasigan, kabundukan at estatwa ni Kristo na Manunubos. Tanyag ang Carnival nito. Ito ang kabisera ng Brazil hanggang 1960."});

;
/* data/history4.js */
// History articles, part 4: human origins, the Stone Age, the Philippine–American War, the Enlightenment, Charlemagne and medieval Europe, the Khmer Empire.
ADDART({id:"humanorigins",t:"Human Origins",c:"History",i:"🦴",w:"Human evolution",r:["evo","dna","caveart","ethiopiacountry","kenyacountry"],
p:[["Where we came from","Humans evolved in Africa over millions of years from earlier ape-like ancestors."],["Many human species","For most of prehistory, several kinds of humans lived on Earth at the same time."],["Spreading across the world","Modern humans, Homo sapiens, spread out of Africa to every continent except Antarctica."]],
l:"Human origins is the story of how our species, Homo sapiens, evolved. Fossils, stone tools and DNA show that the human family tree began in Africa between about 6 and 7 million years ago, when our ancestors split from the lineage leading to chimpanzees. Over millions of years, many human species appeared, learned to walk upright, make tools and control fire. Homo sapiens arose in Africa about 300,000 years ago and later spread across the world, meeting and sometimes mixing with other humans such as Neanderthals. One of these other species, Homo luzonensis, lived in the Philippines.",
s:[["Our closest relatives","Humans are primates, closely related to chimpanzees and bonobos, with whom we share about 98 to 99 percent of our DNA. We did not evolve from chimpanzees; rather, humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor that lived about 6 to 7 million years ago. Members of the human branch are called hominins."],["Walking upright","One of the earliest changes was walking on two legs. Australopithecus species, which lived in eastern and southern Africa from about 4 to 2 million years ago, walked upright but had small brains. The famous fossil Lucy, found in Ethiopia in 1974, is about 3.2 million years old. Footprints at Laetoli in Tanzania, 3.6 million years old, show upright walking."],["The genus Homo","The first members of our genus, Homo, appeared about 2.5 million years ago. Homo habilis made simple stone tools. Homo erectus, from about 1.9 million years ago, had a larger brain and body like ours, used fire and was the first human species to spread out of Africa, reaching as far as Georgia, China and Java."],["Homo sapiens","Fossils from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco show that Homo sapiens existed about 300,000 years ago. Modern humans developed art, ornaments, complex tools and probably language. From about 60,000 to 70,000 years ago, groups spread out of Africa, reaching Asia, Australia, Europe and eventually the Americas."]],
k:[["Split from chimpanzee lineage","About 6–7 million years ago"],["Famous early fossil","Lucy, about 3.2 million years old"],["First Homo species","About 2.5 million years ago"],["Homo sapiens","About 300,000 years ago"],["Philippine species","Homo luzonensis, announced 2019"]],
d:[["c. 7 million years ago","Sahelanthropus, a possible early hominin, lives in Chad"],["c. 3.2 million years ago","Lucy lives in Ethiopia"],["c. 1.9 million years ago","Homo erectus appears"],["c. 300,000 years ago","Homo sapiens lives in Africa"],["c. 40,000 years ago","Neanderthals disappear"],["2019","Homo luzonensis is announced from Callao Cave, Cagayan"]],
s2:[["Homo luzonensis","In Callao Cave in Cagayan, northern Luzon, archaeologists found teeth and bones of a small human species, announced in 2019 as Homo luzonensis. It lived at least 50,000 years ago and had a mix of ancient and more modern features, such as curved finger and toe bones suited for climbing. Its discovery showed that early humans somehow crossed the sea to Luzon."],["Neanderthals and Denisovans","Neanderthals lived in Europe and western Asia from about 400,000 to 40,000 years ago. They made tools, used fire, buried their dead and probably made art. Denisovans, known mainly from DNA, lived in Asia. Modern humans met and had children with both. Most people of non-African ancestry carry about 1 to 2 percent Neanderthal DNA, and some people in Southeast Asia and Oceania, including some Filipino groups, carry Denisovan DNA."],["The hobbit of Flores","In 2004 scientists announced the discovery of Homo floresiensis on the island of Flores in Indonesia, a tiny human about a meter tall, nicknamed the hobbit. It lived until about 50,000 years ago. Like Homo luzonensis, it shows that isolated islands could produce unusual human species."],["Fire and cooking","Early humans learned to control fire, perhaps a million years ago or more. Fire provided warmth, protection from predators and light, and cooking made food easier to chew and digest, which may have helped human brains grow larger. Gathering around fires may also have encouraged social life and storytelling."],["Reading ancient DNA","Since the 2000s, scientists have been able to recover DNA from fossils tens of thousands of years old. The Swedish geneticist Svante Pääbo led the sequencing of the Neanderthal genome and identified the Denisovans from a finger bone, winning the 2022 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Ancient DNA has transformed the study of human origins."],["Peopling the world","Modern humans reached Australia at least 50,000 years ago, crossing open sea. They reached Europe about 45,000 years ago and the Americas, probably by way of a land bridge from Siberia or along the coast, more than 15,000 years ago. The ancestors of many Filipinos arrived in waves over tens of thousands of years, including later Austronesian seafarers."]],
sp:["One human family","All living humans belong to a single species, Homo sapiens, and are remarkably similar genetically: any two people share about 99.9 percent of their DNA. Because humans lived longest in Africa, African populations hold the greatest genetic diversity. Differences in skin color and other features developed relatively recently as people adapted to different climates. Studies of DNA show that the idea of separate human races has no basis in biology."],
g:[["Hominin","A member of the human branch of the family tree"],["Fossil","Remains or traces of ancient life preserved in rock"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Neanderthal","A human species that lived in Europe and western Asia"]],
f:["Humans and chimpanzees share a common ancestor.","Homo sapiens appeared about 300,000 years ago.","Homo luzonensis lived in the Philippines."],
fun:"Many people today carry a small amount of Neanderthal DNA.",
tl:"Nagmula ang tao sa Aprika sa loob ng milyon-milyong taon. Noong unang panahon, ilang uri ng tao ang sabay na nabuhay. Isa rito ang Homo luzonensis na natagpuan sa Callao Cave, Cagayan."});

ADDART({id:"stoneage",t:"The Stone Age",c:"History",i:"🪓",w:"Stone Age",r:["humanorigins","caveart","agri","metals","banaue"],
p:[["Tools of stone","The Stone Age is the long period of prehistory when people made tools mainly from stone."],["Hunters to farmers","It began with hunter-gatherers and ended after people learned to farm and build villages."],["The longest age","It lasted more than three million years, over 99 percent of human history."]],
l:"The Stone Age is the broad period of prehistory during which humans and their ancestors made tools mainly from stone, along with wood, bone and other materials. It began over 3 million years ago with the first stone tools and lasted until people began working metals, at different times in different places, from about 3300 BCE in the Middle East. Archaeologists divide it into the Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, the Mesolithic, or Middle Stone Age, and the Neolithic, or New Stone Age, when farming and settled villages began.",
s:[["The Old Stone Age","The Paleolithic began with the first stone tools, at least 2.6 million and possibly 3.3 million years ago, and lasted until about 12,000 years ago. People lived as hunter-gatherers, moving with the seasons to hunt animals and gather plants. Tools developed from simple sharp flakes to carefully shaped hand axes, spear points, needles and harpoons."],["Art and belief","Late in the Paleolithic, people created stunning cave paintings, carved figurines, made jewelry from shells and teeth and buried their dead with care. These show imagination, symbolism and possibly religious beliefs. Cave paintings in Indonesia and Spain are among the oldest known, over 40,000 years old."],["The New Stone Age","The Neolithic began about 12,000 years ago in the Middle East, when people began to farm crops and keep animals. Farming developed independently in several regions, including China, New Guinea, Mesoamerica and the Andes. People built permanent villages, made pottery and polished stone tools. This change is called the Neolithic Revolution."],["The end of the Stone Age","The Stone Age ended when people began to use metals: first copper, then bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, and later iron. This happened around 3300 BCE in the Middle East and later elsewhere. Some societies continued to use stone tools much longer, and the Stone Age did not end at the same time everywhere."]],
k:[["Main periods","Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic"],["Oldest stone tools","About 3.3 million years old, Kenya"],["Farming begins","About 12,000 years ago"],["Followed by","Copper and Bronze Ages"],["Philippine site","Tabon Caves, Palawan"]],
d:[["c. 3.3 million years ago","The oldest known stone tools are made in Kenya"],["c. 1.76 million years ago","Hand axes appear"],["c. 9500 BCE","Göbekli Tepe is built in Türkiye"],["c. 7400 BCE","Çatalhöyük, an early town, grows in Anatolia"],["c. 3000 BCE","Construction of Stonehenge begins"],["1991","Ötzi, a 5,300-year-old mummy, is found in the Alps"]],
s2:[["Göbekli Tepe","Göbekli Tepe, in southeastern Türkiye, is a site of huge carved stone pillars arranged in circles, built about 11,500 years ago, before farming was well established. The pillars are decorated with foxes, snakes, boars and birds. It surprised archaeologists by showing that hunter-gatherers could organize large building projects, perhaps for gatherings or rituals."],["Ötzi the Iceman","In 1991 hikers in the Alps between Austria and Italy found the frozen body of a man who died about 5,300 years ago. Called Ötzi, he carried a copper axe, a bow, arrows, a fire-making kit and medicinal mushrooms, and wore clothes of leather and grass. An arrowhead in his shoulder showed that he was killed. He is one of the best-preserved prehistoric humans."],["Stone Age Philippines","The Tabon Caves in Palawan contain some of the oldest human remains in the Philippines, with fossils dated to tens of thousands of years ago, as well as stone tools and burial jars. The Manunggul Jar, a burial jar from about 800 BCE with a lid showing two figures in a boat of souls, is a national treasure. The Angono Petroglyphs in Rizal are carvings made in prehistoric times."],["Stonehenge","Stonehenge, in southern England, is a circle of huge standing stones built in stages from about 3000 to 2000 BCE. Some stones were brought from Wales, over 200 kilometers away. It is aligned with the sunrise at midsummer and sunset at midwinter. Its exact purpose is still debated."],["Life in a Neolithic village","At Çatalhöyük, in Anatolia, thousands of people lived in mud-brick houses packed so closely that they entered through holes in the roofs. They farmed wheat and barley, herded sheep and cattle, painted walls with hunting scenes and buried their dead beneath their floors."],["How we know","Archaeologists learn about the Stone Age by excavating sites, studying tools, bones, seeds and pollen, and using dating methods such as radiocarbon dating. Experimental archaeologists try making stone tools themselves to understand how they were made and used."]],
sp:["The first tools","At a site called Lomekwi 3 in Kenya, scientists announced in 2015 the discovery of stone tools about 3.3 million years old, older than any known member of our genus Homo. Some were large cores and flakes made by striking rocks together. They suggest that earlier hominins, perhaps related to Lucy’s species, were already making tools. Toolmaking was a key step in human evolution."],
g:[["Prehistory","The time before written records"],["Paleolithic","The Old Stone Age"],["Neolithic","The New Stone Age, when farming began"],["Hunter-gatherer","A person who lives by hunting and gathering wild food"]],
f:["The Stone Age lasted more than three million years.","Farming began in the Neolithic.","Ötzi the Iceman is about 5,300 years old."],
fun:"Ötzi the Iceman carried a first-aid kit of medicinal mushrooms.",
tl:"Ang Panahon ng Bato ay mahabang panahon ng prehistorya kung kailan gumamit ang tao ng kasangkapang bato. Nagsimula ito sa mga mangangaso at natapos nang matuto ang tao na magsaka. Sa Pilipinas, mahalaga ang Tabon Caves sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"phamwar",t:"The Philippine–American War",c:"History",i:"⚔️",w:"Philippine–American War",r:["phrev","malolos","spanph","commonwealth","aguinaldo"],
p:[["A war for independence","The Philippine–American War was fought from 1899 between the First Philippine Republic and the United States."],["After Spain","It began after Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States in 1898."],["A long struggle","Organized fighting ended in 1902, but resistance continued for years in some regions."]],
l:"The Philippine–American War was an armed conflict between the First Philippine Republic and the United States that began on 4 February 1899. After the Spanish–American War, Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States in the Treaty of Paris of 1898, even though Filipino revolutionaries had already declared independence. Filipinos fought to defend their republic, first in conventional battles and then through guerrilla warfare. The United States declared the war over in 1902 after the capture of Emilio Aguinaldo, but resistance continued in some areas for years. The war cost many lives and led to decades of American colonial rule.",
s:[["Causes","Filipino revolutionaries had fought Spain since 1896 and declared independence on 12 June 1898. Under the Treaty of Paris, signed on 10 December 1898, Spain ceded the Philippines to the United States for 20 million dollars. The First Philippine Republic was proclaimed in Malolos on 23 January 1899. Tensions grew as American troops occupied Manila and Filipino forces surrounded it."],["Outbreak","On the night of 4 February 1899, American soldiers fired on Filipino soldiers near San Juan, starting fighting around Manila. American forces, better armed and supplied, pushed the Filipino army north, capturing Malolos in March 1899. General Antonio Luna, one of the ablest Filipino commanders, was assassinated by fellow Filipino soldiers in June 1899."],["Guerrilla war","From late 1899, Filipino forces turned to guerrilla warfare, attacking in small groups and relying on support from villages. At Tirad Pass in December 1899, the young general Gregorio del Pilar and his men fought a rearguard action to cover Aguinaldo’s retreat; del Pilar was killed. American forces responded with harsh measures, including burning villages and moving civilians into guarded zones."],["The end of the republic","On 23 March 1901, American forces led by Frederick Funston captured Emilio Aguinaldo in Palanan, Isabela, using a ruse. Aguinaldo swore allegiance to the United States and called on Filipinos to stop fighting. President Theodore Roosevelt declared the war over on 4 July 1902, though fighting continued, including resistance led by Macario Sakay until 1906 and conflicts in Mindanao and Sulu until 1913."]],
k:[["Began","4 February 1899"],["Declared over by the US","4 July 1902"],["Sides","First Philippine Republic and the United States"],["Filipino leader","Emilio Aguinaldo"],["American soldiers killed","About 4,200"]],
d:[["12 June 1898","Filipino revolutionaries declare independence"],["10 December 1898","The Treaty of Paris cedes the Philippines to the US"],["4 February 1899","Fighting breaks out near Manila"],["2 December 1899","The Battle of Tirad Pass"],["28 September 1901","Filipino fighters attack US troops at Balangiga, Samar"],["4 July 1902","The US declares the war over"]],
s2:[["The human cost","About 4,200 American soldiers died, and estimates of Filipino soldiers killed are around 20,000. Many more Filipino civilians died from violence, famine and diseases such as cholera, worsened by the war; estimates commonly start at about 200,000, and some historians give much higher figures. The war left deep scars in many communities."],["Balangiga","On 28 September 1901, townspeople and fighters in Balangiga, Samar, attacked an American garrison, killing about 48 soldiers. In retaliation, American forces under General Jacob Smith launched a brutal campaign on Samar, ordering the area turned into a howling wilderness. US soldiers took three church bells from Balangiga as trophies. After a long campaign by Filipinos, the bells were returned to the Philippines in December 2018."],["Debate in America","The war divided Americans. Supporters argued that the United States had a duty or interest in governing the islands. The Anti-Imperialist League, whose members included the writer Mark Twain, opposed the war and annexation, arguing it contradicted American ideals of self-government. Reports of abuses led to Senate hearings in 1902."],["Heroes of the resistance","Besides Aguinaldo, Luna and del Pilar, leaders such as Miguel Malvar in Batangas, Vicente Lukbán in Samar and Macario Sakay, who led a resistance government until 1906, are remembered as heroes. Sakay surrendered under a promise of amnesty but was hanged in 1907. Many ordinary Filipinos, including women, supported the fighters."],["American colonial rule","After the war, the United States governed the Philippines as a colony. It set up a civil government, public schools teaching in English, staffed at first by American teachers called Thomasites, and a legislature. Filipinos continued to press for independence, which was promised in 1934 and granted on 4 July 1946, after the Commonwealth period and World War II."],["Remembering the war","For many years, the war was little discussed in American textbooks and sometimes called the Philippine Insurrection. Filipino historians and writers have worked to restore its memory as a war for independence. Today it is studied in both countries as an important and painful chapter in their shared history."]],
sp:["The Battle of Tirad Pass","On 2 December 1899, General Gregorio del Pilar, just 24 years old, held the narrow Tirad Pass in the mountains of Ilocos Sur with about 60 men against a much larger American force, to give Emilio Aguinaldo time to escape. After hours of fighting, the Americans found a path around the Filipino position, and del Pilar was killed along with most of his men. He became known as the hero of Tirad Pass, a symbol of youthful sacrifice."],
g:[["Cede","To give up territory, usually by treaty"],["Guerrilla warfare","Fighting in small, mobile groups using surprise attacks"],["Annexation","Taking over a territory and making it part of a country"],["Anti-imperialist","Opposed to building an empire by ruling other lands"]],
f:["The war began on 4 February 1899.","Aguinaldo was captured in Palanan, Isabela, in 1901.","General Gregorio del Pilar died at Tirad Pass aged 24."],
fun:"The Balangiga bells, taken as trophies in 1901, returned to the Philippines in 2018.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Pilipino–Amerikano ay nagsimula noong 4 Pebrero 1899 sa pagitan ng Unang Republika ng Pilipinas at Estados Unidos. Ipinaglaban ng mga Pilipino ang kanilang kasarinlan. Idineklarang tapos ito ng Amerika noong 1902, ngunit nagpatuloy ang paglaban sa ilang lugar."});

ADDART({id:"enlightenment",t:"The Enlightenment",c:"History",i:"💡",w:"Age of Enlightenment",r:["scimeth","frrev","usrev","hrights","democ"],
p:[["The Age of Reason","The Enlightenment was a movement of ideas in the 1600s and 1700s that emphasized reason, science and individual rights."],["Questioning authority","Thinkers challenged the power of kings and churches and argued for freedom and equality."],["Changing the world","Enlightenment ideas inspired revolutions and modern democracy, human rights and science."]],
l:"The Enlightenment was an intellectual and cultural movement that flourished in Europe and the Americas from the late 1600s through the 1700s. Its thinkers believed that reason, observation and science could improve human understanding and society. They questioned traditional authority, argued for religious tolerance, freedom of speech, equality before the law and government by consent of the governed. Enlightenment ideas influenced the American and French revolutions, the abolition of slavery and later independence movements, including those in Latin America and the Philippines.",
s:[["Roots","The Enlightenment grew out of the Scientific Revolution. Isaac Newton’s discovery that simple laws governed the motion of planets and falling objects suggested that reason could uncover the laws of nature, and perhaps of society too. Philosophers such as René Descartes and Francis Bacon emphasized doubt, reasoning and experiment."],["Government by consent","The English philosopher John Locke argued in 1689 that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property, and that governments exist to protect these rights; if they fail, people may replace them. The French thinker Montesquieu proposed separating government powers. Jean-Jacques Rousseau wrote of the social contract and the general will."],["Tolerance and free speech","Voltaire, a French writer, used wit to attack religious intolerance, censorship and injustice. Enlightenment thinkers called for freedom of religion and of the press. Coffeehouses, salons, newspapers and academies spread these ideas among educated people."],["The Encyclopédie","Between 1751 and 1772, Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d’Alembert published the Encyclopédie in France, a huge work summarizing knowledge in science, arts and crafts, written by many contributors. It aimed to change the way people think and was censored by authorities. It inspired later encyclopedias."]],
k:[["Period","Late 1600s to late 1700s"],["Key ideas","Reason, science, rights, tolerance"],["Key thinkers","Locke, Voltaire, Montesquieu, Rousseau, Kant, Smith"],["Famous work","The Encyclopédie (1751–1772)"],["Motto (Kant)","Dare to know"]],
d:[["1687","Newton publishes his Principia"],["1689","Locke publishes Two Treatises of Government"],["1748","Montesquieu publishes The Spirit of the Laws"],["1751","The first volume of the Encyclopédie appears"],["1776","Adam Smith publishes The Wealth of Nations; the US Declaration of Independence"],["1784","Kant publishes What Is Enlightenment?"]],
s2:[["Dare to know","In 1784 the German philosopher Immanuel Kant answered the question What is Enlightenment? He wrote that it was humanity’s escape from immaturity, the inability to use one’s own understanding without guidance from others. Its motto, he said, was sapere aude, dare to know, have the courage to use your own reason."],["Economics","The Scottish thinker Adam Smith, in The Wealth of Nations in 1776, argued that free markets, competition and the division of labor could create wealth, guided as if by an invisible hand. The Scottish Enlightenment, including David Hume, made Edinburgh a center of ideas in philosophy, science and economics."],["Women and the Enlightenment","Women hosted salons where ideas were debated, and some, like Émilie du Châtelet, who translated Newton into French, made scientific contributions. In 1792 Mary Wollstonecraft published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, arguing that women deserved equal education and rights. Many male thinkers, however, still excluded women from equality."],["Limits and contradictions","Enlightenment ideals of liberty and equality were often not applied to everyone. Some thinkers defended or ignored slavery and colonialism, and some held racist views. Yet others used Enlightenment arguments to campaign against slavery, and enslaved people in Haiti fought for the rights proclaimed in the French Revolution."],["Enlightenment ideas in the Philippines","In the 1800s, Enlightenment and liberal ideas reached the Philippines, especially through Filipino students in Europe, the ilustrados. José Rizal, Marcelo H. del Pilar and others drew on ideas of reason, education, equality and rights to call for reform under Spanish rule. These ideas helped shape the Philippine Revolution and the Malolos Constitution."],["Legacy","Many features of the modern world, such as constitutions, human rights, freedom of expression, separation of church and state, public education and the scientific method, have roots in the Enlightenment. Critics, then and now, have argued that reason alone cannot answer every human question, and debate about its legacy continues."]],
sp:["An encyclopedia that changed minds","The Encyclopédie, edited by Denis Diderot, eventually filled 28 volumes with more than 70,000 articles and thousands of illustrations of crafts and machines. Its contributors included Voltaire, Rousseau and Montesquieu. By giving detailed attention to the work of ordinary craftspeople and questioning religious and political authority, it spread Enlightenment ideas across Europe. French authorities banned it more than once, but publication continued in secret."],
g:[["Reason","The power of the mind to think logically"],["Natural rights","Rights that all people have by nature"],["Social contract","An agreement by which people form a society and government"],["Salon","A gathering in a private home for discussion"]],
f:["The Enlightenment emphasized reason and rights.","Diderot edited the Encyclopédie.","Kant’s motto for the Enlightenment was dare to know."],
fun:"The Encyclopédie was banned by French authorities but kept being published in secret.",
tl:"Ang Enlightenment ay kilusan ng mga ideya noong 1600s at 1700s na nagbigay-diin sa katwiran, agham at karapatan ng tao. Nakaimpluwensiya ito sa mga rebolusyon at sa mga ilustrado gaya ni José Rizal."});

ADDART({id:"medieurope",t:"Charlemagne and Medieval Europe",c:"History",i:"🏰",w:"Feudalism",r:["gothic","crusades","viking","byzant","renais"],
p:[["Lords, knights and peasants","Medieval Europe was organized around lords, knights, peasants and the Church."],["Charlemagne","In 800, Charlemagne was crowned emperor, uniting much of western Europe."],["Castles and cathedrals","The Middle Ages produced castles, universities, cathedrals and lasting laws."]],
l:"The Middle Ages, or medieval period, in Europe lasted roughly from the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 to about 1500. In western Europe, society was shaped by the Christian Church, local lords and a system historians often call feudalism, in which land was exchanged for loyalty and military service. Charlemagne, crowned emperor in 800, briefly united much of the region. The period saw castles and knights, but also growing towns, trade, universities, Gothic cathedrals and early forms of parliament.",
s:[["The emperor of the West","Charlemagne, king of the Franks, conquered much of western and central Europe, from northern Spain to Germany and Italy. On Christmas Day 800, Pope Leo III crowned him emperor in Rome. Charlemagne supported learning, gathering scholars at his court; this revival of education and copying of books is called the Carolingian Renaissance."],["Lords and vassals","After Charlemagne’s empire divided, power became local. Kings granted land, called fiefs, to nobles in return for loyalty and military service. These vassals in turn granted land to knights. Historians debate the word feudalism, since arrangements varied widely across Europe, but such ties of land and loyalty were common."],["Life on the manor","Most people were peasants who farmed land on manors owned by lords. Many were serfs, bound to the land and owing labor and payments to their lord. Villages centered on the church, and life followed the farming seasons and religious festivals. Life was hard, and famine and disease were constant dangers."],["The Church","The Catholic Church was the most powerful institution in western Europe. It ran monasteries, schools and hospitals, owned large estates and influenced kings. Monks copied books and preserved learning. In the east, the Byzantine Empire and Orthodox Christianity continued Roman traditions."]],
k:[["Period","About 476 to 1500"],["Charlemagne crowned","25 December 800"],["Key system","Feudalism (land for loyalty)"],["Most people","Peasants, many of them serfs"],["Holy Roman Empire","962–1806"]],
d:[["476","The Western Roman Empire falls"],["800","Charlemagne is crowned emperor"],["962","Otto I founds the Holy Roman Empire"],["1088","The University of Bologna is founded"],["1215","Magna Carta is sealed in England"],["1337–1453","The Hundred Years’ War between England and France"]],
s2:[["Knights and chivalry","Knights were mounted warriors who served lords and kings. They trained from childhood as pages and squires. Chivalry was a code of conduct that expected knights to be brave, loyal, generous and protective of the weak, though real knights often fell short. Tournaments let knights show off their skills."],["Castles","Castles were fortified homes for lords, built first of wood and earth and later of stone, with thick walls, towers, moats and gatehouses. They protected against attack and controlled the surrounding land. As cannons improved in the late Middle Ages, castles became less useful for defense."],["Towns and trade","From about 1000, farming improved, populations grew and towns expanded. Merchants and craftspeople formed guilds to regulate trade and quality. Trade fairs and routes connected Europe with the Byzantine Empire, the Islamic world and Asia, bringing spices, silk and new ideas."],["Universities","Medieval Europe created the first universities: Bologna, founded around 1088, Paris and Oxford in the 1100s, and many others. Students studied law, medicine, theology and the liberal arts, often in Latin. Universities drew on knowledge preserved and expanded by scholars in the Islamic world."],["Joan of Arc","During the Hundred Years’ War, a peasant girl named Joan of Arc said that saints told her to help the French king drive out the English. In 1429 she helped lift the siege of Orléans. Captured and tried by pro-English church judges, she was burned at the stake in 1431 at age 19. She was later declared a saint."],["Were they dark ages?","The early Middle Ages were once called the Dark Ages, suggesting a time of ignorance. Historians now reject this view, pointing to medieval achievements in architecture, art, music, philosophy, law and technology, such as the heavy plow, windmills, mechanical clocks and eyeglasses."]],
sp:["The crowning of Charlemagne","On Christmas Day in the year 800, as Charlemagne knelt in prayer at Old St Peter’s Basilica in Rome, Pope Leo III placed a crown on his head and proclaimed him emperor of the Romans. It was the first time in over 300 years that western Europe had an emperor. The event linked the Frankish kingdom, the papacy and the memory of Rome, and shaped European politics for centuries."],
g:[["Feudalism","A system of land granted in return for loyalty and service"],["Vassal","A person who held land from a lord in exchange for loyalty"],["Serf","A peasant bound to work a lord’s land"],["Guild","An association of merchants or craftspeople"]],
f:["Charlemagne was crowned emperor in 800.","The University of Bologna dates from about 1088.","Joan of Arc helped lift the siege of Orléans in 1429."],
fun:"Medieval Europe invented eyeglasses and mechanical clocks.",
tl:"Ang Middle Ages sa Europa ay mula 476 hanggang mga 1500. Hinubog ang lipunan ng Simbahan, mga panginoon at sistemang piyudal. Kinoronahan si Charlemagne bilang emperador noong 800."});

ADDART({id:"khmer",t:"The Khmer Empire",c:"History",i:"🛕",w:"Khmer Empire",r:["angkor","seaempires","buddh","hindu","sea"],
p:[["Builders of Angkor","The Khmer Empire ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from the 800s to the 1400s."],["Temples and reservoirs","Its capital, Angkor, had magnificent temples and huge reservoirs for water."],["A great city","Angkor was one of the largest cities of the pre-industrial world."]],
l:"The Khmer Empire was a powerful state centered in present-day Cambodia that flourished from 802 until the 1400s. At its height, it controlled much of mainland Southeast Asia, including parts of present-day Thailand, Laos and Vietnam. Its capital region, Angkor, contained hundreds of temples, including Angkor Wat, the largest religious monument in the world, and a vast network of canals and reservoirs. The empire’s kings blended Hindu and Buddhist beliefs, and its art and architecture remain among the greatest achievements of Asia.",
s:[["Founding","In 802 King Jayavarman II proclaimed himself a universal monarch and god-king on the sacred mountain of Phnom Kulen, declaring independence from Java and uniting Khmer lands. His successors built capitals near the Tonlé Sap, the great lake of Cambodia, eventually centered at Angkor."],["Angkor Wat","King Suryavarman II built Angkor Wat in the early 1100s as a temple to the Hindu god Vishnu and possibly his tomb. Its five towers represent Mount Meru, the home of the gods, and its walls are carved with long reliefs of Hindu epics, battles and heavenly dancers. It later became a Buddhist temple and is a symbol of Cambodia, appearing on its flag."],["Jayavarman VII","Jayavarman VII, who ruled from about 1181 to around 1218, drove out invaders from Champa and expanded the empire. A devout Buddhist, he built the city of Angkor Thom, with the Bayon temple famous for its giant smiling stone faces. He also built hospitals and rest houses along roads across the empire."],["Water and rice","Angkor’s rulers built enormous reservoirs called barays, canals and moats to manage water from the monsoon rains. The West Baray is about 8 kilometers long. This system helped supply water for the city and may have supported rice farming and religious ceremonies."]],
k:[["Period","802 to the 1400s"],["Capital","Angkor"],["Most famous temple","Angkor Wat (early 1100s)"],["Religions","Hinduism and Buddhism"],["World Heritage Site","Angkor, 1992"]],
d:[["802","Jayavarman II founds the Khmer Empire"],["c. 1113–1150","Suryavarman II builds Angkor Wat"],["1181","Jayavarman VII becomes king"],["1296–1297","Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan visits Angkor"],["1431","Ayutthaya forces sack Angkor"],["1992","Angkor becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["A giant city","Laser scanning from aircraft, called lidar, used in the 2010s revealed that Angkor was surrounded by a huge urban landscape of roads, canals, houses and fields stretching over about 1,000 square kilometers, making it one of the largest settlements of the pre-industrial world. Most houses were made of wood and have disappeared."],["Zhou Daguan’s account","In 1296 and 1297, the Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan stayed at Angkor and later wrote a description of the city, its king, festivals, markets and daily life. He described golden towers, royal processions with elephants and women traders in the markets. His account is the only detailed eyewitness description of Angkor at its height."],["Decline","From the 1300s, the empire weakened. Wars with the Thai kingdom of Ayutthaya, which sacked Angkor in 1431, changes in trade and religion, and long droughts followed by intense monsoons that damaged the water system may all have contributed. The Khmer court moved south toward Phnom Penh, closer to sea trade."],["Art and dance","Khmer sculptors created graceful statues of gods, Buddhas and kings, and temple walls show thousands of apsaras, heavenly dancers. Cambodia’s classical dance, recognized by UNESCO, preserves movements seen in these carvings. Many statues were looted during periods of war and are now being returned to Cambodia."],["Rediscovery and restoration","Angkor Wat was never fully abandoned, as Buddhist monks continued to use it. In the 1860s, the French explorer Henri Mouhot’s descriptions drew European attention. Since the 1990s, international teams and Cambodian experts have worked to restore temples damaged by time, jungle and war."],["Angkor today","Angkor attracts millions of visitors each year. Sunrise over Angkor Wat is one of the world’s most famous sights. The Ta Prohm temple, where giant tree roots grip the ruins, has been left partly in its jungle state. Tourism supports the local economy but also brings challenges of conservation."]],
sp:["The faces of the Bayon","The Bayon temple, at the heart of Angkor Thom, has more than 200 giant stone faces carved on its towers, gazing calmly in all directions with gentle smiles. They may represent the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, a figure of compassion, or King Jayavarman VII himself, or both. The temple’s walls also show scenes of daily life, markets, fishing and battles with the Cham, giving a vivid picture of the Khmer world."],
g:[["Baray","A large reservoir built by the Khmer"],["God-king","A ruler believed to be divine or linked to the gods"],["Lidar","Laser scanning used to map the ground"],["Apsara","A heavenly dancer in Hindu and Buddhist art"]],
f:["Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world.","Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s flag.","The Bayon has more than 200 stone faces."],
fun:"Angkor Wat appears on the national flag of Cambodia.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Khmer ay namuno sa malaking bahagi ng Timog-silangang Asya mula 800s hanggang 1400s. Itinayo nito ang Angkor Wat, ang pinakamalaking relihiyosong monumento sa mundo."});

;
/* data/history5.js */
// History articles, part 5: the Opium Wars, the colonization of Africa, the American Civil War, China since 1949, the modern Middle East, terrorism after 2001.
ADDART({id:"opium",t:"The Opium Wars",c:"History",i:"⛴️",w:"Opium Wars",r:["china","trade","indiacountry","chinacountry","meiji"],
p:[["Wars over trade","The Opium Wars were two wars in the 1800s in which Britain, and later France, fought China over trade."],["A dangerous drug","Britain sold opium grown in India to China, causing widespread addiction."],["Unequal treaties","China lost, and was forced to sign treaties that gave foreign powers special rights, including Hong Kong."]],
l:"The Opium Wars were two conflicts between China’s Qing dynasty and Western powers: the First Opium War from 1839 to 1842 with Britain, and the Second Opium War from 1856 to 1860 with Britain and France. They grew out of British merchants’ trade in opium, grown in British-ruled India and smuggled into China, which caused addiction and drained China’s silver. China’s defeats forced it to open ports, give up Hong Kong and accept unequal treaties. In China, the wars are remembered as the start of a century of humiliation by foreign powers.",
s:[["The opium trade","In the 1700s and 1800s, Britain bought huge amounts of Chinese tea, silk and porcelain, but China wanted few British goods, so silver flowed into China. To reverse this, British merchants, especially the East India Company and its agents, sold opium grown in India to Chinese smugglers. By the 1830s, millions of Chinese were addicted, and silver was flowing out of China."],["Lin Zexu","The Qing emperor sent the official Lin Zexu to Guangzhou, then called Canton, to stop the trade. In 1839 he seized and destroyed more than 1,000 tonnes of opium at Humen, mixing it with lime and salt and flushing it into the sea. He also wrote to Queen Victoria asking how Britain would feel if another country sold poison to its people."],["The First Opium War","Britain responded by sending warships. Its steam-powered gunboats and modern weapons defeated Chinese forces along the coast and rivers. In the Treaty of Nanjing of 1842, China paid a large indemnity, ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain and opened five ports, including Shanghai, to British trade and residents."],["The Second Opium War","In 1856 Britain and France went to war with China again, seeking more trade rights. In 1860 their troops reached Beijing and looted and burned the Old Summer Palace, a vast complex of palaces and gardens. New treaties opened more ports, legalized the opium trade, allowed Christian missionaries to travel and gave Britain the Kowloon Peninsula."]],
k:[["First Opium War","1839–1842"],["Second Opium War","1856–1860"],["Chinese dynasty","Qing"],["Key treaty","Treaty of Nanjing, 1842"],["Hong Kong returned","1997"]],
d:[["1839","Lin Zexu destroys opium at Humen"],["1842","The Treaty of Nanjing ends the First Opium War"],["1850–1864","The Taiping Rebellion devastates China"],["1856","The Second Opium War begins"],["1860","British and French troops burn the Old Summer Palace"],["1997","Hong Kong is returned to China"]],
s2:[["Lasting privileges","The treaties forced on China gave foreigners extraterritoriality, meaning they were judged by their own countries’ laws rather than China’s, and let foreign powers control tariffs and set up concessions in Chinese cities. Japan and other powers later gained similar privileges. These unequal treaties were not fully abolished until the 1940s."],["The century of humiliation","Chinese historians describe the period from the First Opium War to the founding of the People’s Republic in 1949 as the century of humiliation, marked by defeats, foreign occupation and loss of territory. This memory continues to shape Chinese views of the West and national pride."],["The Taiping Rebellion","While China fought foreign powers, it faced a far deadlier internal conflict. The Taiping Rebellion, led by Hong Xiuquan, who believed he was the brother of Jesus, raged from 1850 to 1864 and killed perhaps 20 to 30 million people, one of the deadliest wars in history. It further weakened the Qing dynasty."],["Hong Kong","Hong Kong grew from a small island into a great trading port under British rule. In 1898 Britain leased the New Territories for 99 years. In 1997 Britain returned Hong Kong to China under the principle of one country, two systems, which promised it a high degree of autonomy. How this has been applied has been a subject of controversy."],["Japan takes note","Japan watched China’s defeats with alarm. When American warships arrived in 1853 demanding trade, Japan eventually chose to modernize rapidly, leading to the Meiji Restoration of 1868, so that it would not suffer China’s fate."],["Legacy of opium","Opium addiction remained a serious problem in China into the 20th century. Lin Zexu became a national hero, and 3 June, the anniversary of his destruction of opium, is remembered in campaigns against drugs. The International Day against Drug Abuse on 26 June also honors his action."]],
sp:["Lin Zexu’s letter","In 1839 Lin Zexu wrote a letter to Queen Victoria. He pointed out that opium was forbidden in Britain and asked why British merchants should sell it in China. Let us suppose that foreigners came from another country and brought opium into England, he wrote; would you not be greatly angered? It is not known whether the queen ever read the letter, but it became a famous statement of China’s case."],
g:[["Opium","An addictive drug made from poppies"],["Indemnity","A payment demanded as compensation"],["Treaty port","A port opened to foreign trade by treaty"],["Extraterritoriality","Exemption from local laws for foreigners"]],
f:["The First Opium War ended with the Treaty of Nanjing in 1842.","Hong Kong was returned to China in 1997.","Lin Zexu destroyed over 1,000 tonnes of opium."],
fun:"Lin Zexu wrote a letter to Queen Victoria asking Britain to stop selling opium to China.",
tl:"Ang Opium Wars ay dalawang digmaan noong 1800s sa pagitan ng Tsina at Britanya, at kalaunan ng Pransiya. Ibinenta ng Britanya ang opyo sa Tsina. Natalo ang Tsina at napilitang ibigay ang Hong Kong at buksan ang mga daungan."});

ADDART({id:"africacolon",t:"The Colonization of Africa",c:"History",i:"🌍",w:"Scramble for Africa",r:["decol","slavetrade","ethiopiacountry","drccountry","nkrumah"],
p:[["The Scramble for Africa","Between about 1880 and 1914, European powers divided almost all of Africa among themselves."],["Borders drawn in Europe","At the Berlin Conference of 1884–1885, European leaders set rules for claiming African land."],["Resistance","Africans resisted colonial rule in many ways, and Ethiopia defeated an Italian invasion."]],
l:"The colonization of Africa refers to the conquest and rule of most of the African continent by European powers, especially during the Scramble for Africa between about 1880 and 1914. Britain, France, Germany, Belgium, Portugal, Italy and Spain claimed territories, often drawing borders with little regard for African peoples. By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia remained independent. Colonial rule brought violence, forced labor and the exploitation of land and resources, as well as railways, schools and new borders that still shape Africa. Most African countries won independence in the 1950s and 1960s.",
s:[["Before the scramble","For centuries, Europeans had traded on Africa’s coasts, including in enslaved people, but had controlled little territory inland, apart from areas such as the Cape in South Africa and Algeria, conquered by France from 1830. Diseases such as malaria and strong African states limited European expansion."],["Why the scramble began","In the late 1800s, European powers competed for raw materials such as rubber, cotton, gold and diamonds, for markets and for national prestige. New technologies, including steamships, railways, machine guns and the malaria medicine quinine, made conquest easier. Some Europeans claimed a civilizing mission, an idea now seen as a justification for domination."],["The Berlin Conference","From November 1884 to February 1885, representatives of European powers and the United States met in Berlin, without any African representatives, to set rules for claiming African territory. Claims had to be backed by effective occupation. The conference recognized the Congo Free State as the personal possession of King Leopold II of Belgium."],["Colonial rule","Colonial governments ruled directly or through local leaders. Africans were forced to pay taxes, work on plantations, mines and roads, and grow cash crops for export. Land was taken for European settlers in places such as Kenya, Algeria and southern Africa. Missions and colonial governments set up some schools and hospitals, mostly serving limited groups."]],
k:[["Main period","About 1880–1914"],["Berlin Conference","1884–1885"],["Independent in 1914","Ethiopia and Liberia"],["Main colonial powers","Britain, France, Germany, Belgium, Portugal, Italy, Spain"],["Independence wave","1950s–1960s"]],
d:[["1830","France begins the conquest of Algeria"],["1884–1885","The Berlin Conference is held"],["1896","Ethiopia defeats Italy at the Battle of Adwa"],["1900","Yaa Asantewaa leads the Asante against the British"],["1904–1908","German forces carry out genocide against the Herero and Nama"],["1957","Ghana becomes independent"]],
s2:[["The Congo Free State","From 1885 to 1908, King Leopold II ruled the Congo Free State as his personal property. To extract rubber and ivory, his agents used forced labor, hostage-taking and terrible violence, including cutting off hands. Millions of Congolese died from violence, famine and disease. International outrage, led by campaigners such as E. D. Morel and Roger Casement, forced Belgium to take over the colony in 1908."],["Adwa","In 1896 Emperor Menelik II of Ethiopia defeated an invading Italian army at the Battle of Adwa, one of the greatest victories of an African state over a European power. Ethiopia kept its independence and became a symbol of African pride, and its colors inspired the flags of many later African nations."],["Fighting back","Africans resisted conquest in many ways. Samori Touré fought the French in West Africa for nearly two decades. The Asante queen mother Yaa Asantewaa led a war against the British in 1900. The Maji Maji rebellion in German East Africa and uprisings in Zimbabwe, Algeria and elsewhere showed widespread opposition. Others resisted through religion, strikes and politics."],["The Herero and Nama genocide","In German South West Africa, now Namibia, the Herero and Nama peoples rose against colonial rule. From 1904 to 1908, German forces killed tens of thousands of them, driving many into the desert and confining others in camps. It is considered the first genocide of the 20th century. Germany formally recognized it as genocide in 2021."],["Lasting effects","Colonial borders cut across ethnic groups and joined rivals, contributing to later conflicts. Economies were organized to export raw materials, a pattern that persisted after independence. European languages became official languages in many countries. Historians continue to study and debate colonialism’s long-term effects on African development."],["The road to independence","After World War II, African independence movements grew rapidly. Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, became independent in 1957, and in 1960 alone 17 African countries gained independence. Some, such as Algeria, Kenya, Angola and Mozambique, won freedom only after long wars. By the 1990s, colonial rule had ended across the continent."]],
sp:["Africa divided on a map","At the Berlin Conference, European diplomats discussed Africa over maps, often of places they had never visited. Many borders were drawn as straight lines along lines of latitude and longitude or rivers, splitting peoples such as the Maasai between Kenya and Tanzania and the Ewe between Ghana and Togo. When African countries became independent, the Organisation of African Unity decided in 1964 to keep colonial borders to avoid endless disputes."],
g:[["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Cash crop","A crop grown to sell rather than to eat"],["Genocide","The deliberate destruction of a people"],["Effective occupation","Actual control of a territory, required for claims at Berlin"]],
f:["By 1914 only Ethiopia and Liberia were independent in Africa.","Ethiopia defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896.","In 1960 alone, 17 African countries gained independence."],
fun:"Ethiopia’s colors inspired the flags of many African countries.",
tl:"Ang kolonisasyon ng Aprika ay ang pananakop ng mga bansang Europeo sa halos buong Aprika mula 1880 hanggang 1914. Tanging Ethiopia at Liberia ang nanatiling malaya. Naging malaya ang karamihan ng bansang Aprikano noong 1950s at 1960s."});

ADDART({id:"uscivilwar",t:"The American Civil War",c:"History",i:"🎖️",w:"American Civil War",r:["lincoln","tubman","slavetrade","usacountry","mlk"],
p:[["A nation divided","The American Civil War was fought from 1861 to 1865 between the United States and 11 Southern states that had broken away."],["Slavery at its heart","The main cause was the conflict over slavery and its spread into new territories."],["Freedom for millions","The Union won, the country stayed united and slavery was abolished."]],
l:"The American Civil War was a conflict in the United States from 1861 to 1865 between the Union, the northern states that remained loyal to the federal government, and the Confederate States of America, formed by 11 southern states that seceded. The central cause was slavery: southern states, whose economies relied on the labor of nearly four million enslaved African Americans, feared that the federal government would restrict or end it. The war was the deadliest in American history. The Union victory preserved the nation and led to the abolition of slavery.",
s:[["Causes","By the mid-1800s, the North had growing industries and free labor, while the South depended on plantations worked by enslaved people. Bitter disputes arose over whether new western territories would allow slavery. When Abraham Lincoln, who opposed the expansion of slavery, was elected president in 1860, southern states began to secede, arguing that states had the right to leave the Union."],["The war begins","On 12 April 1861, Confederate forces attacked Fort Sumter in South Carolina. Both sides expected a short war, but it became a long and bloody struggle. The Confederacy, led by President Jefferson Davis, had skilled generals such as Robert E. Lee, while the Union had more people, factories and railways."],["Turning points","In 1863 the Union won crucial victories at Gettysburg in Pennsylvania and Vicksburg on the Mississippi River. General Ulysses S. Grant took command of Union armies, and General William T. Sherman marched through Georgia in 1864, destroying railways and supplies. On 9 April 1865, Lee surrendered to Grant at Appomattox Court House in Virginia."],["Emancipation","On 1 January 1863, Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation, declaring enslaved people in Confederate-held areas free. About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union. The 13th Amendment to the Constitution, ratified in December 1865, abolished slavery throughout the United States."]],
k:[["Years","1861–1865"],["Sides","Union and Confederacy"],["Main cause","Slavery and its expansion"],["Deaths","About 620,000–750,000"],["Slavery abolished","13th Amendment, 1865"]],
d:[["1860","Abraham Lincoln is elected president"],["12 April 1861","Confederates attack Fort Sumter"],["1 January 1863","The Emancipation Proclamation takes effect"],["July 1863","Union victories at Gettysburg and Vicksburg"],["9 April 1865","Lee surrenders at Appomattox"],["December 1865","The 13th Amendment abolishes slavery"]],
s2:[["The Gettysburg Address","On 19 November 1863, at the dedication of a cemetery at Gettysburg, Lincoln gave a speech of only about 270 words. He described the nation as conceived in liberty and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal, and called for a new birth of freedom, so that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth. It is one of the most famous speeches in history."],["The human cost","Between about 620,000 and 750,000 soldiers died, more than in any other American war, many from disease. Railways, telegraphs, rifles and ironclad warships made it one of the first industrial wars. Photographers such as Mathew Brady brought images of the battlefield dead to the public for the first time."],["Lincoln’s assassination","On 14 April 1865, just days after Lee’s surrender, Lincoln was shot by John Wilkes Booth, a Confederate sympathizer, while watching a play at Ford’s Theatre in Washington. He died the next morning, the first US president to be assassinated."],["Reconstruction","After the war, during Reconstruction from 1865 to 1877, the federal government worked to rebuild the South and protect the rights of freed people. The 14th Amendment granted citizenship and equal protection, and the 15th gave Black men the right to vote. Many Black Americans were elected to office. But violence and new laws later stripped many of these rights."],["After Reconstruction","After federal troops left the South in 1877, southern states passed Jim Crow laws enforcing racial segregation and blocking Black citizens from voting. These injustices lasted until the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, led by figures such as Martin Luther King Jr."],["Memory and debate","Americans have long debated how to remember the war. Some monuments to Confederate leaders were built decades later, during the Jim Crow era, and in recent years many have been removed. Historians agree that slavery was the central cause of the war, as Confederate leaders themselves stated at the time."]],
sp:["Harriet Tubman’s raid","Harriet Tubman, who had escaped slavery and guided many others to freedom on the Underground Railroad, served the Union army as a scout, spy and nurse. In June 1863 she helped lead the Combahee River Raid in South Carolina, guiding Union gunboats past hidden mines. The raid freed more than 700 enslaved people, making her one of the first women to help lead an armed military operation in American history."],
g:[["Secede","To formally leave a country or union"],["Union","The northern states loyal to the United States government"],["Confederacy","The southern states that seceded in 1860–1861"],["Emancipation","Being set free, especially from slavery"]],
f:["The Civil War lasted from 1861 to 1865.","The Emancipation Proclamation took effect on 1 January 1863.","The 13th Amendment abolished slavery in 1865."],
fun:"Lincoln’s Gettysburg Address was only about 270 words long.",
tl:"Ang Digmaang Sibil ng Amerika ay mula 1861 hanggang 1865 sa pagitan ng Union at ng mga estadong humiwalay sa Timog. Pangunahing sanhi nito ang pang-aalipin. Nanalo ang Union at inalis ang pang-aalipin sa Estados Unidos."});

ADDART({id:"modernchina",t:"China since 1949",c:"History",i:"🏮",w:"History of the People's Republic of China",r:["chinacountry","china","coldwar","koreanwar","trade"],
p:[["A new republic","The People’s Republic of China was founded in 1949 by the Communist Party under Mao Zedong."],["Upheavals","The Great Leap Forward and the Cultural Revolution brought famine and turmoil."],["Economic rise","Reforms from 1978 turned China into the world’s second-largest economy."]],
l:"The People’s Republic of China was proclaimed on 1 October 1949 by Mao Zedong, after the Communist Party won a long civil war against the Nationalist government, which retreated to Taiwan. Under Mao, China experienced radical campaigns, including the Great Leap Forward, which caused a devastating famine, and the Cultural Revolution. After Mao’s death in 1976, Deng Xiaoping launched economic reforms that opened China to the world and produced decades of rapid growth, lifting hundreds of millions of people out of poverty. Today China is a major global power, governed by the Communist Party.",
s:[["Founding","After decades of war with Japan and civil war, the Communist Party, led by Mao Zedong, defeated the Nationalists under Chiang Kai-shek. Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic in Beijing on 1 October 1949. The Nationalist government moved to Taiwan, where the Republic of China continued. The status of Taiwan remains a major international issue."],["The Mao years","The new government carried out land reform, taking land from landlords, and built industries with Soviet help. In 1958 Mao launched the Great Leap Forward to rapidly industrialize and collectivize farming. Unrealistic targets and policies led to a famine from 1959 to 1961 in which tens of millions of people died. In 1966 Mao launched the Cultural Revolution, in which young Red Guards attacked officials, teachers and traditional culture; it caused widespread suffering until 1976."],["Reform and opening","After Mao died in 1976, Deng Xiaoping became the leading figure. From 1978 he introduced market reforms, allowing farmers to sell surplus crops, encouraging private businesses and opening Special Economic Zones, such as Shenzhen, to foreign investment. China’s economy grew rapidly for decades, becoming the world’s factory."],["China today","China is the world’s second-largest economy and a leader in manufacturing, trade, infrastructure and technology, with high-speed railways, a space station and major tech companies. It is governed by the Chinese Communist Party, and Xi Jinping has been its leader since 2012. China’s growing power has brought cooperation and competition with other countries."]],
k:[["Founded","1 October 1949"],["Founding leader","Mao Zedong"],["Reform era began","1978, under Deng Xiaoping"],["Population","About 1.4 billion"],["World Trade Organization","Joined 2001"]],
d:[["1949","The People’s Republic of China is founded"],["1958","The Great Leap Forward begins"],["1966","The Cultural Revolution begins"],["1978","Deng Xiaoping begins economic reforms"],["1997","Hong Kong returns to Chinese rule"],["2008","Beijing hosts the Summer Olympics"]],
s2:[["Tiananmen Square, 1989","In the spring of 1989, students and workers gathered in Beijing’s Tiananmen Square and other cities to call for political reform and an end to corruption. On the night of 3 to 4 June, the government sent troops and tanks to clear the protests, killing hundreds, possibly thousands, of people. The events remain heavily censored in China."],["Lifting people out of poverty","According to the World Bank, since 1978 China has lifted around 800 million people out of extreme poverty, the largest reduction in history. Hundreds of millions of people moved from the countryside to cities to work in factories and construction. Rapid growth also brought pollution, inequality and environmental challenges."],["One-child policy","From 1980, China limited most families to one child to slow population growth. The policy was enforced, sometimes harshly, and contributed to an aging population and a gender imbalance. It was relaxed to two children in 2016 and three in 2021. In 2023 India overtook China as the world’s most populous country."],["Hong Kong and Macau","Britain returned Hong Kong to China in 1997, and Portugal returned Macau in 1999, under the principle of one country, two systems. Large protests in Hong Kong in 2019 opposed a proposed law and called for democratic reforms, and a national security law was imposed in 2020. Supporters and critics strongly disagree about these developments."],["China and its neighbors","China has territorial disputes with several neighbors, including in the South China Sea, where its claims overlap with those of the Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia, Brunei and others. In 2016 an international tribunal ruled in a case brought by the Philippines that China’s broad historical claims had no legal basis; China rejected the ruling. China is also the largest trading partner of many Asian countries, including the Philippines."],["Global role","China has become a major lender and builder of infrastructure abroad through the Belt and Road Initiative, launched in 2013. It is a permanent member of the UN Security Council. Its relationship with the United States, marked by both trade ties and rivalry, is one of the most important in the world."]],
sp:["Shenzhen","In 1980 Shenzhen, a small border town near Hong Kong, became one of China’s first Special Economic Zones, where foreign companies could invest and market rules applied. It grew at astonishing speed into a city of more than 17 million people, famous for electronics, technology companies and skyscrapers. Its transformation is often seen as a symbol of China’s economic rise."],
g:[["People’s Republic","The official name of China since 1949"],["Collectivization","Merging private farms into large collective farms"],["Special Economic Zone","An area with special rules to attract investment"],["Communist Party","The ruling party of China"]],
f:["The People’s Republic of China was founded in 1949.","Deng Xiaoping began reforms in 1978.","India overtook China in population in 2023."],
fun:"Shenzhen grew from a small town into a city of over 17 million people in about 40 years.",
tl:"Itinatag ang People's Republic of China noong 1 Oktubre 1949 ni Mao Zedong. Dumanas ito ng taggutom at kaguluhan, ngunit mula 1978, dahil sa mga reporma ni Deng Xiaoping, naging pangalawang pinakamalaking ekonomiya ito sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"middleeast",t:"The Modern Middle East",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"Modern history of the Middle East",r:["ottoman","islam","judaism","un","ofw"],
p:[["Crossroads of three continents","The Middle East links Asia, Africa and Europe and is the birthplace of Judaism, Christianity and Islam."],["New states","After the Ottoman Empire collapsed in 1918, new countries were formed, many under European control at first."],["Oil and conflict","The region has vast oil reserves and has experienced repeated wars and peace efforts."]],
l:"The modern Middle East includes countries such as Egypt, Israel, the Palestinian territories, Jordan, Lebanon, Syria, Iraq, Iran, Saudi Arabia and the Gulf states. After World War I, the Ottoman Empire’s lands were divided, mostly under British and French control, and new states gradually became independent. The discovery of oil made the region strategically important. Its modern history includes the creation of Israel and the long Israeli–Palestinian conflict, revolutions, wars, and periods of reform and peacemaking. Millions of Filipinos have worked in the Middle East.",
s:[["After the Ottomans","During World War I, Britain and France secretly agreed in 1916 to divide Ottoman lands, the Sykes–Picot Agreement. After the war, Britain received mandates over Palestine, Transjordan and Iraq, and France over Syria and Lebanon. Turkey became a republic in 1923, and Saudi Arabia was unified in 1932. Most mandates became independent states by the late 1940s."],["Oil","Oil was discovered in Persia in 1908, in Iraq in 1927 and in Saudi Arabia in 1938. The Gulf region holds a large share of the world’s oil reserves. Oil wealth transformed countries such as Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates, building modern cities and attracting workers from Asia, including the Philippines."],["Israel and Palestine","Jewish immigration to Palestine grew in the early 1900s, encouraged by the Zionist movement and later by persecution in Europe. In 1947 the UN proposed dividing Palestine into Jewish and Arab states. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, and war followed with neighboring Arab states. About 700,000 Palestinians fled or were expelled, an event Palestinians call the Nakba, meaning catastrophe. The conflict over land, security and Palestinian statehood continues."],["Wars and peace","Israel fought wars with Arab neighbors in 1948, 1956, 1967 and 1973. In the 1967 Six-Day War, Israel took control of the West Bank, Gaza, East Jerusalem, the Sinai and the Golan Heights. Egypt and Israel signed a peace treaty in 1979, and Jordan in 1994. The Oslo Accords of 1993 created the Palestinian Authority, but a final agreement has not been reached."]],
k:[["Region","Western Asia and northeastern Africa"],["Ottoman Empire ends","1922"],["Israel founded","14 May 1948"],["Iranian Revolution","1979"],["Arab Spring","2010–2012"]],
d:[["1916","The Sykes–Picot Agreement is made"],["1948","Israel declares independence; the first Arab–Israeli war"],["1967","The Six-Day War"],["1979","Egypt–Israel peace treaty; the Iranian Revolution"],["1993","The Oslo Accords are signed"],["2011","Protests of the Arab Spring spread; war begins in Syria"]],
s2:[["The Iranian Revolution","In 1979 mass protests overthrew Iran’s monarch, the Shah, and Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini established an Islamic Republic. The revolution changed Iran’s society and foreign relations. From 1980 to 1988, Iran fought a devastating war with Iraq, led by Saddam Hussein, in which hundreds of thousands died."],["The Gulf Wars","In 1990 Iraq invaded Kuwait, and in 1991 a US-led coalition drove Iraqi forces out. In 2003 the United States and allies invaded Iraq, overthrowing Saddam Hussein, citing weapons of mass destruction that were never found. The war and its aftermath brought years of violence, including the rise of the extremist group ISIS."],["The Arab Spring","Beginning in Tunisia in December 2010, protests spread across the Arab world calling for freedom, jobs and an end to corruption. Leaders were overthrown in Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen. In Syria, protests turned into a civil war that killed hundreds of thousands and displaced millions; the government of Bashar al-Assad fell in December 2024. Results across the region varied widely."],["Recent conflict in Gaza and Israel","On 7 October 2023, Hamas, the group governing Gaza, led an attack on southern Israel, killing about 1,200 people and taking around 250 hostages. Israel responded with a military campaign in Gaza that killed tens of thousands of Palestinians, according to Gaza health authorities, and caused a severe humanitarian crisis. A ceasefire took effect in 2025, though the situation has remained fragile. The war deepened suffering and division across the region."],["Filipinos in the Middle East","Millions of Filipinos have worked in Middle Eastern countries since the 1970s, as engineers, nurses, domestic workers, construction workers and in many other jobs, especially in Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait and Qatar. Their earnings support families at home. During wars and crises, the Philippine government has organized evacuations of its citizens."],["Hopes for peace","Despite conflict, there have been many efforts at peace and cooperation, from the Camp David Accords of 1978 to the Abraham Accords of 2020, in which Israel established relations with the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and others. International organizations and many people in the region continue to work toward lasting peace and a just solution for Israelis and Palestinians."]],
sp:["Three faiths, one city","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims. Its Old City contains the Western Wall, the holiest place where Jews may pray; the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, where Christians believe Jesus was crucified and buried; and the Al-Aqsa Mosque and Dome of the Rock, among Islam’s holiest sites. Both Israelis and Palestinians claim Jerusalem as their capital, and its status is one of the most sensitive issues in the conflict."],
g:[["Mandate","A territory governed by another country on behalf of the League of Nations"],["Zionism","A movement for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel"],["Nakba","The displacement of Palestinians in 1948"],["Ceasefire","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["The Middle East is the birthplace of Judaism, Christianity and Islam.","Israel was founded in 1948.","Millions of Filipinos have worked in the Middle East."],
fun:"Jerusalem’s Old City is less than one square kilometer in area but holy to three faiths.",
tl:"Ang Gitnang Silangan ay rehiyon na nag-uugnay sa Asya, Aprika at Europa at pinagmulan ng Hudaismo, Kristiyanismo at Islam. Mayaman ito sa langis at nakaranas ng maraming digmaan at pagsisikap para sa kapayapaan. Milyon-milyong Pilipino ang nagtrabaho rito."});

ADDART({id:"terrorism",t:"Terrorism and the World after 2001",c:"History",i:"🏙️",w:"Terrorism",r:["middleeast","un","hrights","coldwar","cybersecurity"],
p:[["Violence for political goals","Terrorism is the use of violence against civilians to spread fear and pursue political, religious or ideological aims."],["11 September 2001","The attacks on the United States in 2001 changed world politics and security."],["Many forms","Terrorism has come from many different groups and ideologies around the world."]],
l:"Terrorism generally means the deliberate use or threat of violence, often against civilians, to create fear and achieve political, religious or ideological goals. There is no single agreed international definition. Terrorism has a long history, but the attacks of 11 September 2001 on the United States, carried out by the group al-Qaeda, led to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, new security measures around the world and debates about how to balance safety and freedom. Terrorist groups of many kinds, including religious extremists, ethno-nationalists and far-right and far-left movements, have caused suffering in many countries.",
s:[["The 11 September attacks","On 11 September 2001, 19 al-Qaeda hijackers seized four passenger planes in the United States. Two were flown into the World Trade Center towers in New York, which collapsed, one into the Pentagon near Washington and one crashed in Pennsylvania after passengers fought back. Nearly 3,000 people were killed, from more than 90 countries."],["War in Afghanistan","Because Afghanistan’s Taliban government sheltered al-Qaeda’s leader Osama bin Laden, the United States and allies invaded Afghanistan in October 2001 and overthrew the Taliban. Bin Laden was killed by US forces in Pakistan in 2011. After 20 years, foreign forces withdrew in August 2021, and the Taliban returned to power."],["ISIS","The extremist group known as the Islamic State, or ISIS, seized large areas of Iraq and Syria in 2014 and declared a so-called caliphate. It carried out brutal killings and inspired attacks abroad. Iraqi, Kurdish, Syrian and international forces drove it from its territory by 2019, though it continues to operate in some regions."],["Many sources of terrorism","Terrorism is not linked to any single religion or region. Groups have been driven by nationalism, separatism, religious extremism, racism and political ideologies. Attacks have included the 2008 Mumbai attacks, the 2011 attacks in Norway by a far-right extremist, the 2015 Paris attacks and the 2019 Christchurch mosque attacks in New Zealand. Most victims of terrorism live in countries in Asia, Africa and the Middle East."]],
k:[["Definition","Violence against civilians for political aims (no single agreed definition)"],["11 September 2001 deaths","Nearly 3,000"],["US-led war in Afghanistan","2001–2021"],["ISIS territorial defeat","2019"],["Marawi siege","May–October 2017"]],
d:[["2001","The 11 September attacks; the war in Afghanistan begins"],["2003","The US-led invasion of Iraq"],["2004","Bombings in Madrid kill 193; SuperFerry 14 is bombed in Manila Bay"],["2011","Osama bin Laden is killed"],["2017","The Marawi siege in Mindanao"],["2021","Foreign forces withdraw from Afghanistan"]],
s2:[["Terrorism in the Philippines","The Philippines has suffered terrorist violence, especially in Mindanao and Sulu. The Abu Sayyaf group carried out kidnappings and bombings, including the bombing of SuperFerry 14 in Manila Bay in 2004, which killed 116 people, one of the deadliest terrorist attacks at sea. In 2017, militants linked to ISIS seized parts of Marawi City; a five-month battle left the city center in ruins and displaced hundreds of thousands of people."],["Peace in Mindanao","Alongside security efforts, peace agreements have sought to address the roots of conflict in Mindanao. After decades of negotiations, the government and the Moro Islamic Liberation Front signed a peace agreement, and in 2019 the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao was created, giving the region greater self-government."],["Security and freedom","After 2001, many countries introduced new security measures: airport screening, surveillance, terrorist watchlists and new laws allowing detention of suspects. Critics argued that some measures, such as detention without trial at Guantánamo Bay and harsh interrogation methods, violated human rights. Societies continue to debate how to balance security with civil liberties."],["Why people join","Researchers study why people join extremist groups. Factors can include a sense of injustice or humiliation, personal crises, the search for identity and belonging, propaganda, especially online, and recruitment by friends or family. Most people who share grievances never turn to violence."],["Preventing violent extremism","Efforts to prevent terrorism include policing and intelligence, but also education, community programs, removing extremist propaganda online, supporting victims, helping people leave extremist groups and addressing grievances through peaceful politics and development. International cooperation, including through the United Nations, is important."],["Remembering victims","Memorials honor victims of terrorism, such as the 9/11 Memorial in New York, built where the twin towers stood, with waterfalls in their footprints. The United Nations marks 21 August as the International Day of Remembrance of and Tribute to the Victims of Terrorism."]],
sp:["Flight 93","On 11 September 2001, United Airlines Flight 93 was hijacked after leaving Newark, New Jersey. Through phone calls, passengers and crew learned of the attacks in New York and Washington. They decided to fight back and stormed the cockpit. The plane crashed into a field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania, killing all aboard, but its hijackers failed to reach their target, believed to be the US Capitol or the White House. A national memorial now stands at the site."],
g:[["Terrorism","Violence against civilians to spread fear for political aims"],["Extremism","Holding extreme views, sometimes supporting violence"],["Counterterrorism","Actions taken to prevent terrorism"],["Radicalization","The process by which people adopt extremist views"]],
f:["The 11 September 2001 attacks killed nearly 3,000 people.","The Marawi siege lasted five months in 2017.","There is no single agreed international definition of terrorism."],
fun:"The 9/11 Memorial in New York has two waterfalls in the footprints of the twin towers.",
tl:"Ang terorismo ay paggamit ng karahasan laban sa mga sibilyan para maghasik ng takot at makamit ang layuning pampolitika o panrelihiyon. Binago ng pag-atake noong 11 Setyembre 2001 ang pandaigdigang politika. Nakaranas din ang Pilipinas ng terorismo, gaya ng pagkubkob sa Marawi noong 2017."});

