ADDART({id:"bangladeshcountry",t:"Bangladesh",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐅",w:"Bangladesh",r:["ganges","partition","wetlands","indiacountry","storms","myanmarcountry"],
p:[["Land of rivers","Bangladesh sits on the world’s largest river delta, formed by the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers."],["Many people","With about 173 million people, it is one of the most densely populated countries on Earth."],["Mother language","Its fight to use the Bengali language gave the world International Mother Language Day."]],
l:"Bangladesh is a country in South Asia, almost surrounded by India, with a short border with Myanmar and a coast on the Bay of Bengal. About 173 million people live there, making it the eighth most populous country in the world. Its capital is Dhaka. Bangladesh lies mostly on the vast delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers, making it fertile but vulnerable to floods and cyclones. Once East Pakistan, it won independence in 1971 after a war of liberation. It has become a major exporter of clothing and has made great progress in health and education.",
s:[["Land and water","Most of Bangladesh is low, flat delta land crossed by hundreds of rivers. Monsoon rains and melting Himalayan snow bring floods that renew the soil but can also destroy homes and crops. In the southwest, the Sundarbans, the largest mangrove forest in the world, is home to the Bengal tiger. Hills rise in the southeast."],["History","Bengal was home to ancient kingdoms and, from the 1200s, Muslim sultanates and later the Mughal Empire, becoming one of the richest regions of South Asia. Under British rule, Bengal was partitioned in 1947: its eastern part became East Pakistan, separated from West Pakistan by about 1,600 kilometers of India."],["Language and liberation","When Pakistan’s government declared Urdu the only national language, Bengali speakers protested. On 21 February 1952, police killed several student protesters in Dhaka. Tensions grew, and in 1971 the Pakistani army launched a crackdown. Bangladesh declared independence on 26 March 1971 and, with Indian help, won the war on 16 December 1971. Hundreds of thousands, by many estimates far more, were killed, and millions fled."],["Economy","Bangladesh is the world’s second-largest exporter of clothing, after China, employing millions of workers, most of them women. Its economy has grown rapidly, and poverty has fallen sharply. Remittances from Bangladeshis working abroad, rice and fish farming, and jute, the golden fiber, are also important."]],
k:[["Capital","Dhaka"],["Population","About 173 million (2024)"],["Area","About 148,000 km²"],["Language","Bengali (Bangla)"],["Currency","Taka"]],
d:[["1947","Bengal is partitioned; East Pakistan is created"],["1952","Students are killed in the Language Movement on 21 February"],["1970","The Bhola cyclone kills hundreds of thousands"],["1971","Bangladesh wins independence"],["1999","UNESCO declares International Mother Language Day"],["2006","Muhammad Yunus and Grameen Bank win the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2024","Student-led protests lead to a change of government"]],
s2:[["International Mother Language Day","To honor the students killed on 21 February 1952 while defending the Bengali language, UNESCO declared 21 February International Mother Language Day in 1999. It is observed worldwide to promote linguistic diversity and education in mother tongues. In Bangladesh, people lay flowers at the Shaheed Minar, the martyrs’ monument, in Dhaka."],["Microcredit","In 1976 the economist Muhammad Yunus began lending small amounts of money to poor women in villages to start small businesses, without requiring collateral. His Grameen Bank grew to serve millions of borrowers, and the idea of microcredit spread worldwide. Yunus and the bank won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2006."],["The Sundarbans","The Sundarbans, shared with India, is a maze of mangrove forests, tidal rivers and islands covering about 10,000 square kilometers. It protects the coast from storms and is home to Bengal tigers that swim between islands, as well as crocodiles, river dolphins and spotted deer. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Climate and disasters","Bangladesh is among the countries most vulnerable to climate change. Rising seas threaten coastal land with saltwater, and cyclones and floods affect millions. Thanks to early warning systems and thousands of cyclone shelters, deaths from cyclones have fallen dramatically since the 1970s."],["Garment workers and safety","In April 2013, the Rana Plaza building near Dhaka, which housed garment factories, collapsed, killing more than 1,100 workers. The disaster led to international agreements to improve factory safety and workers’ rights in the clothing industry."],["Rohingya refugees","Since 2017, Bangladesh has hosted about a million Rohingya refugees who fled violence in Myanmar, mostly in camps near Cox’s Bazar, one of the largest refugee settlements in the world. Cox’s Bazar is also known for one of the world’s longest natural sea beaches."]],
sp:["The 1971 Liberation War","In March 1971, after the Awami League led by Sheikh Mujibur Rahman won elections in Pakistan but was not allowed to form a government, the Pakistani army launched a brutal crackdown in East Pakistan. Bangladeshi freedom fighters, the Mukti Bahini, resisted, and about 10 million refugees fled to India. In December India entered the war, and on 16 December 1971 Pakistani forces surrendered in Dhaka. The day is celebrated as Victory Day, and Sheikh Mujibur Rahman is honored as the founding father of Bangladesh."],
g:[["Delta","Land formed where a river splits and meets the sea"],["Mangrove","A tree that grows in salty coastal water"],["Microcredit","Small loans to poor people to start businesses"],["Jute","A plant fiber used for sacks and rope"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"]],
f:["Bangladesh lies on the world’s largest river delta.","21 February is International Mother Language Day.","The Sundarbans is the largest mangrove forest in the world."],
fun:"International Mother Language Day began with Bangladesh’s struggle for the Bengali language.",
tl:"Ang Bangladesh ay bansa sa Timog Asya na nasa pinakamalaking delta ng ilog sa mundo. Isa ito sa pinakamataong bansa. Naging malaya ito noong 1971, at dahil sa pakikibaka nito para sa wikang Bengali, ipinagdiriwang ang International Mother Language Day."});

ADDART({id:"israelcountry",t:"Israel",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕍",w:"Israel",r:["middleeast","judaism","holocaust","lakes","christ","islam"],
p:[["A small, ancient land","Israel lies on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean, in a land sacred to Jews, Christians and Muslims."],["A modern state","The State of Israel was founded in 1948 as a homeland for the Jewish people."],["A land in conflict","Its history has been marked by conflict with Palestinians and neighboring states, and by efforts at peace."]],
l:"Israel is a country in the Middle East on the eastern coast of the Mediterranean Sea, with about 10 million people. It declared independence in 1948 as a Jewish state, following centuries of Jewish connection to the land, the rise of the Zionist movement and the Holocaust. About three-quarters of its citizens are Jewish and about a fifth are Arab. Israel is a parliamentary democracy with a high-technology economy. Its founding and policies are closely tied to the long Israeli–Palestinian conflict over land, security and Palestinian statehood.",
s:[["Land","Israel is small but varied, with a fertile coastal plain, hills around Jerusalem and Galilee, the Jordan Valley and the Negev Desert, which covers more than half its land. The Dead Sea, on its border with Jordan, is the lowest point on land. The Sea of Galilee is its main freshwater lake."],["Ancient roots","The Hebrew Bible tells of the ancient Israelites, and the kingdoms of Israel and Judah existed in the region about 3,000 years ago. After revolts against Rome in the first and second centuries CE, many Jews were exiled, forming communities around the world, called the diaspora. Small Jewish communities remained, and the land later came under Byzantine, Arab, Crusader, Mamluk and Ottoman rule."],["Founding","In the late 1800s, the Zionist movement called for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel, and Jewish immigration grew under Ottoman and then British rule, as did conflict with the Arab population. After the Holocaust, the UN proposed partition in 1947. Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948, followed by war with Arab states and the displacement of hundreds of thousands of Palestinians."],["Society and economy","Israel’s population includes Jews whose families came from Europe, the Middle East, North Africa, Ethiopia and elsewhere, as well as Arab citizens, Druze and others. Hebrew and Arabic are widely spoken. Israel has a strong economy in technology, medicine, agriculture and defense, and is known for many start-up companies."]],
k:[["Capital","Jerusalem (status disputed internationally)"],["Population","About 10 million (2025)"],["Area","About 22,000 km² (within pre-1967 lines)"],["Lowest point","Dead Sea shore, about 430 m below sea level"],["Currency","New shekel"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","The kingdom of Israel is established"],["70 CE","The Romans destroy the Second Temple in Jerusalem"],["1917","The Balfour Declaration supports a Jewish national home"],["1948","Israel declares independence"],["1967","The Six-Day War"],["1979","Israel and Egypt sign a peace treaty"],["2023","The Hamas attack of 7 October and the war in Gaza"]],
s2:[["Reviving Hebrew","For centuries, Hebrew was used mainly in prayer and study. In the late 1800s, Eliezer Ben-Yehuda and others worked to revive it as an everyday spoken language, creating new words for modern life. Today Hebrew is spoken by millions, one of the only examples of a language revived for daily use."],["Jerusalem","Jerusalem is holy to Jews, Christians and Muslims. Israel considers Jerusalem its capital, while Palestinians seek East Jerusalem as the capital of a future state. Most countries keep their embassies in Tel Aviv, though some have moved them to Jerusalem. Its status is among the most sensitive issues in the conflict."],["The Palestinians","The West Bank and Gaza Strip, occupied by Israel in 1967, are home to millions of Palestinians. The Palestinian Authority administers parts of the West Bank, while Hamas has governed Gaza since 2007. Most UN member states recognize a State of Palestine, though its borders and status remain unresolved. Israeli settlements in the West Bank are considered illegal under international law by most of the international community, which Israel disputes."],["Water and farming","Israel is largely dry, so it has become a world leader in water technology. Drip irrigation, developed there in the 1960s, delivers water directly to plant roots and is used worldwide. Israel desalinates seawater for much of its drinking water and recycles most of its wastewater for farming."],["Filipinos in Israel","Tens of thousands of Filipinos work in Israel, many as caregivers for elderly people, where they are widely appreciated. Filipino workers were among those killed and taken hostage in the attack of 7 October 2023, and the Philippine government has assisted its citizens during conflicts."],["Kibbutzim","A kibbutz is a community where members traditionally shared property and work, often in farming. The first was founded in 1910. Kibbutzim played a large role in early Israeli society; many have since changed, with members earning individual salaries, but the idea of communal living remains influential."]],
sp:["The Dead Sea","The Dead Sea, between Israel, the West Bank and Jordan, has a shore about 430 meters below sea level, the lowest point on land. Its water is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so people float easily, and almost nothing lives in it except some microbes. Its mineral-rich mud is used in cosmetics. The sea is shrinking by about a meter a year because water from the Jordan River is diverted for farming and drinking."],
g:[["Zionism","A movement for a Jewish homeland in the Land of Israel"],["Diaspora","A people living scattered away from their homeland"],["Kibbutz","A communal settlement in Israel"],["Occupation","Military control of a territory"],["Drip irrigation","Watering plants slowly at their roots"]],
f:["Israel was founded in 1948.","Hebrew was revived as a spoken language.","The Dead Sea is the lowest point on land."],
fun:"Israel recycles most of its wastewater to water crops.",
tl:"Ang Israel ay bansa sa silangang baybayin ng Mediterranean na itinatag noong 1948 bilang tahanan ng mga Hudyo. Banal ang lupain nito sa mga Hudyo, Kristiyano at Muslim. Kaugnay ng kasaysayan nito ang matagal na tunggalian sa mga Palestino."});

ADDART({id:"ukrainecountry",t:"Ukraine",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌻",w:"Ukraine",r:["russiacountry","ussrfall","wheat","eu","nuclear","coldwar"],
p:[["Largest in Europe","Ukraine is the largest country entirely within Europe."],["Breadbasket","Its rich black soil makes it one of the world’s great grain and sunflower oil producers."],["Defending independence","Since 2022, Ukraine has been defending itself against a full-scale Russian invasion."]],
l:"Ukraine is a country in Eastern Europe, bordering Russia, Belarus, Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania, Moldova and the Black Sea. Before 2022, about 41 million people lived in government-controlled areas; millions have since fled abroad or been displaced. Its capital is Kyiv, one of the oldest cities in Eastern Europe. Ukraine has a rich history going back to the medieval state of Kyivan Rus’. Part of the Soviet Union for most of the 20th century, it became independent in 1991. Russia annexed Crimea in 2014 and launched a full-scale invasion in February 2022.",
s:[["Land","Ukraine is mostly flat, with vast fertile plains called steppes and a famous rich black soil, chornozem. The Carpathian Mountains rise in the west, where Hoverla reaches 2,061 meters. The Dnipro River flows through the middle of the country into the Black Sea. The Crimean Peninsula juts into the Black Sea in the south."],["Kyivan Rus’","In the 800s, a state called Kyivan Rus’ grew around Kyiv, trading along rivers between Scandinavia and the Byzantine Empire. In 988 Prince Volodymyr adopted Orthodox Christianity. Ukrainians, Belarusians and Russians all trace part of their heritage to Kyivan Rus’. It was destroyed by the Mongol invasion of 1240."],["Under other powers","For centuries, Ukrainian lands were ruled by Lithuania, Poland, the Russian Empire and Austria-Hungary. In the 1600s, Cossacks formed a self-governing Hetmanate. After World War I, Ukraine briefly declared independence but became part of the Soviet Union in 1922. In 1932 and 1933, a famine caused by Soviet policies, known as the Holodomor, killed millions of Ukrainians."],["Independence and war","Ukraine declared independence on 24 August 1991, confirmed by more than 90 percent of voters in a referendum. Protests led to political changes in 2004 and 2014. In 2014 Russia annexed Crimea, a move not recognized by most countries, and backed separatists in the eastern Donbas region. On 24 February 2022, Russia launched a full-scale invasion, starting the largest war in Europe since World War II."]],
k:[["Capital","Kyiv"],["Population","About 41 million before 2022; now uncertain because of war"],["Area","About 603,500 km²"],["Highest point","Hoverla, 2,061 m"],["Currency","Hryvnia"]],
d:[["988","Prince Volodymyr adopts Christianity"],["1240","The Mongols destroy Kyiv"],["1922","Ukraine becomes part of the Soviet Union"],["1932–1933","The Holodomor famine"],["1986","The Chernobyl nuclear disaster"],["1991","Ukraine becomes independent"],["2022","Russia launches a full-scale invasion"]],
s2:[["The Holodomor","In 1932 and 1933, Soviet policies of forced collectivization and grain seizures caused a catastrophic famine in Ukraine. Millions of people starved to death. Ukraine and many other countries recognize the Holodomor, meaning death by hunger, as a genocide against Ukrainians. It is remembered each year on the fourth Saturday of November."],["Chernobyl","On 26 April 1986, a reactor at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in northern Ukraine exploded during a test, releasing radiation across Europe. The nearby city of Pripyat was evacuated, and an exclusion zone of about 2,600 square kilometers remains. It was the worst nuclear accident in history and contributed to distrust of the Soviet government."],["The war since 2022","Russia’s full-scale invasion in February 2022 was met by fierce Ukrainian resistance, which prevented the capture of Kyiv. The war has killed and wounded very large numbers of soldiers and civilians, destroyed cities and infrastructure and forced millions of Ukrainians to flee abroad. Many countries have supported Ukraine with aid and imposed sanctions on Russia, while efforts toward negotiations have continued."],["Breadbasket of the world","Ukraine is one of the world’s largest exporters of wheat, maize, barley and sunflower oil, feeding people in Asia, Africa and the Middle East. The war disrupted these exports through the Black Sea, raising food prices worldwide. The national flag’s blue and yellow are often said to represent the sky over golden wheat fields."],["Culture","Ukraine has a rich culture of folk music, embroidery called vyshyvanka, painted Easter eggs called pysanky, and dishes such as borscht, a beet soup recognized by UNESCO in 2022. The poet Taras Shevchenko, of the 1800s, is a national hero. Ukrainian bands have won the Eurovision Song Contest three times."],["Toward Europe","Ukraine has sought closer ties with the European Union. It received EU candidate status in 2022, and accession negotiations formally opened in 2024. Ukrainians see this as part of a long effort to secure their independence and democracy."]],
sp:["The black soil","About two-thirds of Ukraine is covered by chornozem, black earth, one of the most fertile soils in the world. Formed over thousands of years from grasses that grew and decayed on the steppe, it is deep, dark and rich in humus. It is said that during World War II, soldiers carried trainloads of this soil away. This soil is why Ukraine has long been called the breadbasket of Europe."],
g:[["Steppe","A large, flat, treeless grassland"],["Kyivan Rus’","A medieval state centered on Kyiv"],["Holodomor","The man-made famine of 1932–1933 in Ukraine"],["Annexation","Taking over a territory by force or decree"],["Chornozem","Fertile black soil of the steppes"]],
f:["Ukraine is the largest country entirely in Europe.","The Chernobyl disaster happened in 1986.","Ukraine became independent in 1991."],
fun:"Ukrainian borscht was recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage in 2022.",
tl:"Ang Ukraine ay pinakamalaking bansa na buong nasa Europa. Kilala ito bilang taniman ng trigo at mirasol. Naging malaya ito noong 1991, at mula 2022, ipinagtatanggol nito ang sarili laban sa malawakang pagsalakay ng Russia."});

ADDART({id:"greececountry",t:"Greece",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏛️",w:"Greece",r:["greece","gmyth","olymp","byzant","ottoman","eu"],
p:[["Cradle of the West","Greece is the birthplace of democracy, philosophy, the Olympic Games and theater."],["Thousands of islands","It has about 6,000 islands and islets, of which around 200 are inhabited."],["Sun and sea","Its beaches, ancient ruins and food attract tens of millions of visitors."]],
l:"Greece is a country in southeastern Europe at the southern end of the Balkan Peninsula, with about 10.4 million people. Its capital is Athens. Greece has thousands of islands in the Aegean and Ionian seas. Ancient Greece gave the world democracy, philosophy, mathematics, theater and the Olympic Games. After centuries of Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman rule, modern Greece won independence in the 1820s. Today it is a member of the European Union and NATO, with a large shipping industry and tourism.",
s:[["Land","Greece is mountainous, with Mount Olympus, the legendary home of the gods, rising to 2,917 meters. Its long, jagged coastline and many islands, including Crete, Rhodes, Corfu and the Cyclades, mean that no place in Greece is far from the sea. The climate is Mediterranean, with hot, dry summers."],["From ancient to Byzantine","Ancient Greek city-states such as Athens and Sparta flourished from about 800 BCE. Greek culture spread widely after Alexander the Great. Greece came under Roman rule, and later the Greek-speaking Byzantine Empire, centered on Constantinople, ruled for about a thousand years. The Ottoman Turks conquered Greece in the 1400s."],["Independence","Greeks rose against Ottoman rule in 1821. With help from Britain, France and Russia, Greece became independent, recognized in 1830. Over the following century, it gradually expanded to its current borders. Greece suffered occupation in World War II, a civil war from 1946 to 1949 and a military dictatorship from 1967 to 1974, before restoring democracy."],["Modern Greece","Greece joined the European Community, now the EU, in 1981 and adopted the euro in 2001. A severe debt crisis from 2009 led to bailouts, deep spending cuts and high unemployment, but the economy has since recovered. Tourism, shipping, farming and services are its main industries."]],
k:[["Capital","Athens"],["Population","About 10.4 million (2024)"],["Area","About 132,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Olympus, 2,917 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["c. 776 BCE","The first recorded Olympic Games"],["c. 508 BCE","Democracy begins in Athens"],["1453","Constantinople falls to the Ottomans"],["1821","The Greek War of Independence begins"],["1896","Athens hosts the first modern Olympic Games"],["1974","Democracy is restored"],["1981","Greece joins the European Community"]],
s2:[["The Acropolis","Above Athens rises the Acropolis, a rocky hill crowned by the Parthenon, a temple to the goddess Athena built in the 400s BCE. It is one of the most famous buildings in the world and a symbol of Western civilization. Greece has long called for the return of the Parthenon sculptures taken to Britain in the early 1800s and now in the British Museum."],["The Olympics come home","The ancient Olympic Games were held at Olympia from 776 BCE. In 1896 Athens hosted the first modern Olympics, and in 2004 it hosted them again. At every Olympics, the flame is lit at Olympia using the Sun’s rays and carried to the host city, and Greece leads the parade of nations at the opening ceremony."],["A seafaring nation","Greek shipowners control one of the largest merchant fleets in the world. Many of the sailors on Greek-owned ships are Filipinos, as the Philippines is one of the world’s largest suppliers of seafarers. Ports such as Piraeus, near Athens, are among the busiest in the Mediterranean."],["Greek food","Greek cuisine is based on olive oil, vegetables, grains, fish, cheese such as feta, yogurt, herbs and wine. Popular dishes include moussaka, souvlaki, Greek salad and spanakopita. The Mediterranean diet, recognized by UNESCO, is linked to good health."],["The Greek language","Greek has been written for over 3,000 years, one of the longest-recorded languages. The Greek alphabet, adapted from the Phoenician, gave rise to the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets. Many English words in science and medicine, such as biology, democracy and telephone, come from Greek."],["Islands","The Greek islands are famous for whitewashed villages, blue-domed churches and clear seas, as on Santorini and Mykonos. Crete, the largest island, was home to the ancient Minoan civilization, whose palace at Knossos is linked to the myth of the Minotaur."]],
sp:["Democracy is born","In about 508 BCE, the Athenian leader Cleisthenes introduced reforms that gave power to an assembly of citizens. All free adult male citizens could attend, speak and vote on laws and decisions, a system called demokratia, rule by the people. Women, enslaved people and foreigners were excluded, so it was far from complete. But the idea that citizens could govern themselves became one of the most influential in history."],
g:[["City-state","An independent city with its own government"],["Acropolis","A fortified hill in an ancient Greek city"],["Democracy","Government by the people"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Bailout","Financial help to prevent collapse"]],
f:["Democracy began in Athens around 508 BCE.","Greece has about 6,000 islands and islets.","Athens hosted the first modern Olympics in 1896."],
fun:"The Olympic flame for every Games is lit at Olympia in Greece using the Sun’s rays.",
tl:"Ang Gresya ay bansa sa timog-silangang Europa na pinagmulan ng demokrasya, pilosopiya at Palarong Olimpiko. Mayroon itong libu-libong isla. Maraming Pilipinong mandaragat ang nagtatrabaho sa mga barkong pag-aari ng mga Griyego."});

ADDART({id:"swedencountry",t:"Sweden",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌲",w:"Sweden",r:["viking","eu","arctic","indig","trees","bicycle"],
p:[["Land of forests and lakes","Sweden is a large Nordic country covered by forests and nearly 100,000 lakes."],["Midnight sun","In its far north, the Sun does not set for weeks in summer."],["Nobel’s homeland","Sweden is home to the Nobel Prizes, founded by the inventor Alfred Nobel."]],
l:"Sweden is a Nordic country in northern Europe on the Scandinavian Peninsula, with about 10.6 million people. Its capital is Stockholm. Most of Sweden is covered by forests and lakes, and its far north lies within the Arctic Circle. Sweden is known for its high standard of living, strong welfare system, environmental protection and companies such as Volvo, IKEA, Ericsson and Spotify. It is a constitutional monarchy and parliamentary democracy, a member of the European Union since 1995 and of NATO since 2024.",
s:[["Land","Sweden stretches about 1,600 kilometers from north to south. The north has mountains, including Kebnekaise, about 2,100 meters, the highest peak, and is home to reindeer and the Sámi people. The south has farmland and many lakes. About 70 percent of the country is forest. Winters are long and dark in the north, while summers have very long days."],["History","Swedish Vikings traveled east along the rivers of Russia and Ukraine from the 700s to the 1000s. In 1523 Gustav Vasa became king, ending the Kalmar Union with Denmark. In the 1600s, Sweden became a great power controlling much of the Baltic region, until its defeat by Russia in the Great Northern War, ending in 1721."],["Neutrality","Sweden stayed out of wars for more than 200 years, from 1814, remaining neutral in both world wars. During the Cold War, it kept a policy of non-alignment. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022, Sweden applied to join NATO and became a member in March 2024."],["Society","Sweden has a strong welfare state, with free education, universal health care, generous parental leave and pensions, funded by high taxes. It has a high level of gender equality. Swedes value lagom, meaning just the right amount, and fika, a coffee break with pastries."]],
k:[["Capital","Stockholm"],["Population","About 10.6 million (2024)"],["Area","About 450,000 km²"],["Highest point","Kebnekaise, about 2,100 m"],["Currency","Swedish krona"]],
d:[["700s–1000s","The Viking Age"],["1523","Gustav Vasa becomes king"],["1814","Sweden’s last war; a long period of peace begins"],["1901","The first Nobel Prizes are awarded"],["1995","Sweden joins the European Union"],["2018","Greta Thunberg begins her school strike for climate"],["2024","Sweden joins NATO"]],
s2:[["The Nobel Prizes","Alfred Nobel, a Swedish chemist who invented dynamite, left most of his fortune in his will to fund prizes for those who have conferred the greatest benefit to humankind. The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901. The prizes in physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and economics are presented in Stockholm each December, while the Peace Prize is awarded in Oslo, Norway."],["The Sámi","The Sámi are the Indigenous people of northern Sweden, Norway, Finland and Russia’s Kola Peninsula. Many have traditionally herded reindeer. The Sámi have their own languages, colorful clothing and a singing tradition called joik. Sweden has a Sámi Parliament, and the Sámi continue to work for their land and cultural rights."],["The right to roam","Sweden’s allemansrätten, the right of public access, allows everyone to walk, camp, swim and pick berries and mushrooms on most land, even privately owned, as long as they respect nature and privacy. This tradition reflects Swedes’ love of outdoor life."],["Swedish innovations","Swedish inventions and companies include the safety match, the three-point seatbelt, introduced by Volvo in 1959, the pacemaker, the Tetra Pak carton and the music streaming service Spotify, founded in 2006. IKEA, founded in 1943, sells flat-pack furniture around the world."],["Music","Sweden is one of the world’s biggest exporters of pop music. ABBA won the Eurovision Song Contest in 1974 with Waterloo and became one of the best-selling bands in history. Swedish songwriters and producers have written hits for many international stars."],["Climate action","Sweden was one of the first countries to introduce a carbon tax, in 1991, and gets most of its electricity from hydropower and nuclear power. In 2018 the Swedish teenager Greta Thunberg began a school strike outside parliament, inspiring a global youth climate movement."]],
sp:["The Ice Hotel","Every winter since 1989, in the village of Jukkasjärvi in northern Sweden, artists build a hotel entirely from ice and snow taken from the Torne River. Guests sleep in rooms carved with ice sculptures, on beds covered with reindeer skins, at temperatures of around minus 5°C. In spring, the hotel melts back into the river. It inspired ice hotels in other cold countries."],
g:[["Nordic","Relating to Scandinavia, Finland and Iceland"],["Welfare state","A state that provides services such as health care and pensions"],["Sámi","The Indigenous people of northern Scandinavia"],["Neutrality","Not taking sides in wars"],["Fika","A Swedish coffee break"]],
f:["The first Nobel Prizes were awarded in 1901.","Sweden joined NATO in 2024.","Volvo introduced the three-point seatbelt in 1959."],
fun:"Sweden’s Ice Hotel is rebuilt from river ice every winter.",
tl:"Ang Sweden ay bansang Nordic sa hilagang Europa na natatakpan ng gubat at lawa. Dito nagmula ang Nobel Prize. Kilala ito sa mataas na pamantayan ng pamumuhay at sa mga kompanyang gaya ng IKEA at Volvo."});

ADDART({id:"cubacountry",t:"Cuba",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎺",w:"Cuba",r:["cubancrisis","coldwar","sugar","slavetrade","phrev","usacountry"],
p:[["Pearl of the Antilles","Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean."],["A communist state","In 1959 Fidel Castro’s revolution created a communist state, which still governs Cuba."],["Music and culture","Cuba is famous for its music, dance, baseball and classic cars."]],
l:"Cuba is an island country in the Caribbean Sea, about 150 kilometers south of Florida. About 10 million people live there, and its capital is Havana. A Spanish colony for nearly four centuries, Cuba became independent in 1902 after wars of independence and the Spanish–American War of 1898, the same war that ended Spanish rule in the Philippines. In 1959 Fidel Castro led a revolution that established a communist government closely tied to the Soviet Union. Cuba is known for its music, literacy, health care, cigars and colorful old cities, but also for economic hardship and limits on political freedom.",
s:[["Land","Cuba is long and narrow, about 1,200 kilometers from east to west. It has fertile plains where sugarcane and tobacco grow, and mountains, including the Sierra Maestra, where Pico Turquino rises to 1,974 meters. Its coasts have beaches, coral reefs and many small islands."],["Spanish colony","Columbus reached Cuba in 1492. The Indigenous Taíno population was devastated by violence and disease. Spain made Cuba a center of sugar production, worked by hundreds of thousands of enslaved Africans; slavery was abolished only in 1886. Cuba became one of Spain’s most valuable colonies."],["Independence","Cubans fought Spain in the Ten Years’ War from 1868 to 1878 and again from 1895, led by figures such as the poet José Martí, who was killed in battle in 1895. In 1898 the United States entered the war against Spain. Cuba became independent in 1902, but the United States kept the right to intervene and a naval base at Guantánamo Bay."],["Revolution","In 1959 Fidel Castro, his brother Raúl and Che Guevara led rebels who overthrew the dictator Fulgencio Batista. The new government nationalized businesses, allied with the Soviet Union and established a one-party communist state. The United States imposed a trade embargo in 1962, which largely continues. Fidel Castro led Cuba until 2008 and died in 2016."]],
k:[["Capital","Havana"],["Population","About 10 million (2024)"],["Area","About 110,000 km²"],["Highest point","Pico Turquino, 1,974 m"],["Currency","Cuban peso"]],
d:[["1492","Columbus reaches Cuba"],["1868","The Ten Years’ War for independence begins"],["1898","The Spanish–American War"],["1902","Cuba becomes independent"],["1959","The Cuban Revolution triumphs"],["1962","The Cuban Missile Crisis"],["2015","Cuba and the United States restore diplomatic relations"]],
s2:[["The Cuban Missile Crisis","In October 1962, the United States discovered Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba. For 13 tense days, the world came close to nuclear war, until the Soviet Union agreed to remove the missiles in exchange for a US promise not to invade Cuba and the secret removal of US missiles from Türkiye."],["Health and education","Cuba’s government made free education and health care a priority. A national literacy campaign in 1961 sent volunteers across the countryside to teach reading and writing. Cuba has many doctors per person and has sent tens of thousands of doctors to work in other countries. Critics point to shortages of medicine and equipment."],["Economic hardship","When the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, Cuba lost its main trading partner and aid, leading to a severe crisis known as the Special Period. In the 2020s, Cuba again faced shortages of food, fuel and medicine, power blackouts and the emigration of many people. The government blames the US embargo, while critics also blame state control of the economy."],["Music and dance","Cuba is a powerhouse of music, blending African and Spanish traditions into son, rumba, mambo, cha-cha-chá and the roots of salsa. The Buena Vista Social Club recording of 1997 made older Cuban musicians famous worldwide. Cuban rumba is recognized by UNESCO."],["Old Havana","Old Havana, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, has colorful colonial buildings, plazas, forts and the Malecón seaside promenade. Classic American cars from the 1950s, kept running for decades because new cars were hard to obtain, cruise its streets and have become a symbol of the city."],["Cuba and the Philippines","Cuba and the Philippines share a history as Spanish colonies that fought for independence in the 1890s and were affected by the Spanish–American War of 1898. José Martí and José Rizal were contemporaries who both used their writing to inspire their nations. Spanish words and Catholic traditions link both cultures."]],
sp:["José Martí","José Martí, born in Havana in 1853, was a poet, journalist and revolutionary who spent much of his life in exile, writing and organizing for Cuban independence. His poems include the verses later set to music in the famous song Guantanamera. He returned to Cuba to fight in 1895 and was killed in one of the first battles. He is honored as Cuba’s national hero, and Havana’s airport bears his name."],
g:[["Embargo","An official ban on trade with a country"],["Revolution","The overthrow of a government by its people"],["Nationalize","To transfer private businesses to state ownership"],["Taíno","Indigenous people of the Caribbean"],["Son","A Cuban style of music and dance"]],
f:["Cuba is the largest island in the Caribbean.","The Cuban Revolution triumphed in 1959.","The Spanish–American War affected both Cuba and the Philippines."],
fun:"Classic 1950s American cars are still driven on Havana’s streets.",
tl:"Ang Cuba ang pinakamalaking isla sa Caribbean. Naging malaya ito noong 1902 matapos ang Digmaang Espanyol–Amerikano, ang parehong digmaan na nagwakas sa pamumuno ng Espanya sa Pilipinas. Kilala ito sa musika, sayaw at baseball."});

ADDART({id:"venezuelacountry",t:"Venezuela",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌊",w:"Venezuela",r:["waterfalls","latinind","amazon","colombiacountry","brazilcountry","inflation"],
p:[["Land of Angel Falls","Venezuela is home to Angel Falls, the world’s tallest waterfall."],["Oil giant","It has the largest proven oil reserves in the world."],["Birthplace of Bolívar","Simón Bolívar, the liberator of much of South America, was born in Caracas."]],
l:"Venezuela is a country on the northern coast of South America, with about 28 million people. Its capital is Caracas. Venezuela has spectacular landscapes, from Caribbean beaches and Andean peaks to the Orinoco River, the Amazon rainforest and the flat-topped mountains called tepuis, including the one from which Angel Falls plunges. It holds the world’s largest proven oil reserves, which made it one of Latin America’s richest countries in the 1900s. In recent years, it has suffered a severe political and economic crisis, and millions of Venezuelans have left the country.",
s:[["Land","Venezuela includes the northern end of the Andes, where Pico Bolívar rises to 4,978 meters; the Caribbean coast; the llanos, vast tropical grasslands; and the Guiana Highlands in the southeast, with their tepuis. The Orinoco River, one of South America’s largest, flows through the country to a huge delta on the Atlantic."],["History","Columbus reached Venezuela’s coast in 1498, and Spanish explorers named it Little Venice, perhaps after Indigenous houses built on stilts over water. Venezuela declared independence on 5 July 1811. Simón Bolívar, born in Caracas in 1783, led armies that freed Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia from Spain."],["Oil","Large oil deposits were found in 1914, and by the 1920s Venezuela was one of the world’s top oil exporters. Oil wealth built modern cities and highways but made the economy dependent on a single product. Venezuela was a founding member of OPEC, the organization of oil-exporting countries, in 1960."],["Crisis","From 1999, President Hugo Chávez pursued socialist policies, using oil revenue for social programs. After oil prices fell in 2014, and amid mismanagement, sanctions and corruption, the economy collapsed. Hyperinflation, shortages of food and medicine and political repression led to a mass exodus. Elections, including in 2024, were disputed, and the country’s political crisis has continued."]],
k:[["Capital","Caracas"],["Population","About 28 million (2024)"],["Area","About 916,000 km²"],["Highest point","Pico Bolívar, 4,978 m"],["Currency","Bolívar"]],
d:[["1498","Columbus reaches Venezuela’s coast"],["1783","Simón Bolívar is born in Caracas"],["1811","Venezuela declares independence"],["1914","Major oil deposits are discovered"],["1960","Venezuela helps found OPEC"],["1999","Hugo Chávez becomes president"],["2014","An economic crisis begins as oil prices fall"]],
s2:[["Angel Falls","Angel Falls, in Canaima National Park, plunges 979 meters from the tepui Auyán-tepui, making it the world’s tallest uninterrupted waterfall. The Pemon people call it Kerepakupai Merú. The surrounding tepuis inspired stories of lost worlds and the animated film Up."],["Tepuis","Tepuis are flat-topped mountains with sheer cliffs rising from the rainforest of the Guiana Highlands. They are among the oldest rock formations on Earth, about 2 billion years old. Isolated on their summits, plants and animals evolved into species found nowhere else. Mount Roraima, on the borders with Brazil and Guyana, is the most famous."],["A mass exodus","Since about 2015, more than 7 million Venezuelans have left the country, one of the largest displacement crises in the world. Most have gone to neighboring countries such as Colombia, Peru, Brazil, Ecuador and Chile, as well as Spain and the United States. Their departure has reshaped communities across Latin America."],["Beauty queens and baseball","Venezuela is famous for winning international beauty pageants, with seven Miss Universe titles. Baseball is the most popular sport, and many Venezuelan players have starred in American Major League Baseball. In music, the joropo of the llanos, played with harp, cuatro and maracas, is a national tradition."],["El Sistema","In 1975 the musician José Antonio Abreu founded El Sistema, a program providing free music education and orchestras for children, many from poor neighborhoods. It has reached hundreds of thousands of young people and inspired programs in dozens of countries. Its most famous graduate is the conductor Gustavo Dudamel."],["Wildlife","Venezuela’s ecosystems are rich in wildlife, including jaguars, giant anteaters, capybaras, the world’s largest rodent, and the scarlet ibis. The llanos flood each rainy season, attracting huge numbers of birds. Lake Maracaibo is famous for the Catatumbo lightning, flashing storms that occur on many nights of the year."]],
sp:["The everlasting storm","Over the mouth of the Catatumbo River, where it enters Lake Maracaibo, thunderstorms occur on up to about 260 nights a year, producing thousands of lightning flashes in a single night. Warm, moist air from the lake meets cool winds from the Andes, creating the perfect conditions. The Catatumbo lightning holds the record as the place with the most lightning on Earth, and sailors once used it as a natural lighthouse."],
g:[["Tepui","A flat-topped mountain with steep cliffs"],["Llanos","Tropical grasslands of Venezuela and Colombia"],["OPEC","The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries"],["Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid rise in prices"],["Exodus","A mass departure of people"]],
f:["Angel Falls is in Venezuela.","Venezuela has the world’s largest proven oil reserves.","Simón Bolívar was born in Caracas."],
fun:"Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela has more lightning than anywhere else on Earth.",
tl:"Ang Venezuela ay bansa sa hilagang baybayin ng Timog Amerika. Narito ang Angel Falls at ang pinakamalaking reserba ng langis sa mundo. Ipinanganak dito si Simón Bolívar. Dumanas ito ng malubhang krisis, at milyon-milyong Venezuelan ang umalis."});

ADDART({id:"algeriacountry",t:"Algeria",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏜️",w:"Algeria",r:["sahara","desert","africacolon","decol","islam","francecountry"],
p:[["Africa’s largest country","Algeria is the largest country in Africa by area."],["Mostly desert","More than four-fifths of its land is part of the Sahara."],["Hard-won independence","Algeria won independence from France in 1962 after a long and bloody war."]],
l:"Algeria is a country in North Africa, the largest on the continent by area, with about 47 million people. Its capital is Algiers, on the Mediterranean coast. Most Algerians live in the fertile north, while the vast south is part of the Sahara Desert. Algeria has a rich heritage of Amazigh, also called Berber, Roman, Arab, Ottoman and French influences. After 132 years of French rule, it won independence in 1962 following a war of liberation. It is a major producer of oil and natural gas, supplying much of Europe.",
s:[["Land","Northern Algeria has a Mediterranean coast, fertile plains and the Atlas Mountains. South of the mountains lies the Sahara, covering more than 80 percent of the country, with sand seas called ergs, rocky plateaus and the Hoggar Mountains, where Mount Tahat rises to 2,908 meters. Oases support towns and date palms."],["Ancient history","The Amazigh peoples have lived in North Africa for thousands of years. Ancient kingdoms such as Numidia rose in the region, which later became part of the Roman Empire; ruins such as Timgad and Djemila survive. Arab armies arrived in the 600s, bringing Islam and Arabic. From 1516, Algiers was part of the Ottoman Empire."],["French rule","France invaded Algeria in 1830 and made it part of France, encouraging hundreds of thousands of European settlers. Algerian Muslims had few rights. Resistance began immediately, led by figures such as Emir Abdelkader, and continued in various forms throughout the colonial period."],["The war of independence","On 1 November 1954, the National Liberation Front, FLN, launched a war for independence. The war was extremely violent, involving guerrilla fighting, bombings, torture and the deaths of hundreds of thousands of people. Algeria became independent on 5 July 1962, and most European settlers left."]],
k:[["Capital","Algiers"],["Population","About 47 million (2024)"],["Area","About 2.38 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Tahat, 2,908 m"],["Currency","Algerian dinar"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE","The kingdom of Numidia flourishes"],["600s","Arab armies bring Islam to the region"],["1516","Algiers comes under Ottoman rule"],["1830","France invades Algeria"],["1954","The war of independence begins"],["1962","Algeria becomes independent"],["2016","Tamazight becomes an official language"]],
s2:[["Tassili n’Ajjer","In the Sahara of southeastern Algeria, the Tassili n’Ajjer plateau contains thousands of ancient rock paintings and engravings, some over 10,000 years old. They show elephants, giraffes, hippos and herds of cattle, revealing that the Sahara was once green and full of life. The site is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The Amazigh","The Amazigh, or Berbers, are the Indigenous peoples of North Africa. Groups such as the Kabyle in northern Algeria and the Tuareg of the Sahara have their own languages and traditions. In 2016 Tamazight, the Amazigh language, became an official language of Algeria alongside Arabic. Yennayer, the Amazigh New Year, is a public holiday."],["The 1990s","In 1991, when an Islamist party was poised to win elections, the army cancelled the vote. A brutal conflict followed between the government and armed Islamist groups, in which an estimated 150,000 or more people died during the 1990s. A national reconciliation process ended most of the violence in the early 2000s."],["Oil and gas","Algeria is one of Africa’s largest producers of oil and natural gas, which provide most of its export income. Pipelines under the Mediterranean carry gas to Italy and Spain. The government is seeking to diversify the economy into farming, manufacturing and solar energy, which the Sahara could provide in huge amounts."],["The Casbah of Algiers","The Casbah, the old city of Algiers, is a maze of narrow streets, stairways, mosques and Ottoman-era houses climbing a hillside above the sea. It played an important role during the war of independence and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Football and famous Algerians","Football is hugely popular, and Algeria’s team won the Africa Cup of Nations in 1990 and 2019. The writer Albert Camus, a Nobel laureate, was born in Algeria. The French football star Zinedine Zidane is of Algerian descent. Raï music from Oran has become popular worldwide."]],
sp:["Rock art of a green Sahara","Thousands of years ago, the Sahara was far wetter than today, with lakes, rivers and grasslands. The rock art of Tassili n’Ajjer records this lost world: hunters chasing antelope, cattle herders with their animals, swimming figures and mysterious round-headed beings. As the climate dried about 5,000 years ago, the images began to show camels instead of cattle, recording one of the greatest environmental changes in human history."],
g:[["Amazigh","Indigenous people of North Africa, also called Berbers"],["Erg","A large sea of sand dunes"],["Oasis","A place in a desert with water"],["FLN","The National Liberation Front, which led Algeria’s independence war"],["Casbah","The old fortified quarter of a North African city"]],
f:["Algeria is the largest country in Africa.","More than 80 percent of Algeria is Sahara Desert.","Algeria became independent in 1962."],
fun:"Ancient rock paintings in Algeria show hippos and giraffes in what is now the Sahara.",
tl:"Ang Algeria ang pinakamalaking bansa sa Aprika. Mahigit apat na ikalima nito ay bahagi ng Sahara. Naging malaya ito mula sa Pransiya noong 1962 matapos ang mahaba at madugong digmaan."});

;
/* data/rivers.js */
// Planet Earth articles: great rivers — the Amazon, the Yangtze, the Ganges, the Mississippi and the Danube.
addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{amazonriver:[-60,-3.1,"Brazil",30,"Amazon River"],yangtze:[112,30.5,"China",30,"Yangtze River"],ganges:[83,25.3,"India",30,"Ganges River"],mississippi:[-90.5,35,"United States of America",30,"Mississippi River"],danube:[19,46,"Hungary",30,"Danube River"]})});

ADDART({id:"amazonriver",t:"The Amazon River",c:"Geography",i:"🌊",w:"Amazon River",r:["amazon","brazilcountry","perucountry","nile","wcycle"],
p:[["The mightiest river","The Amazon carries more water than any other river on Earth."],["Through the rainforest","It flows across South America through the world’s largest tropical rainforest."],["No bridges","Not a single bridge crosses the main Amazon River."]],
l:"The Amazon River flows about 6,400 kilometers across northern South America, from the Andes of Peru to the Atlantic Ocean in Brazil. It is either the longest or second-longest river in the world, depending on how it is measured, and by far the largest by volume, carrying about a fifth of all the fresh water that rivers pour into the oceans. Its basin, covering about 7 million square kilometers, contains the Amazon rainforest and an astonishing variety of life, and is home to millions of people, including many Indigenous nations.",
s:[["From the Andes to the Atlantic","The Amazon begins as streams high in the Andes of Peru. Joined by more than 1,000 tributaries from Peru, Ecuador, Colombia, Bolivia, Venezuela and Brazil, it grows into a giant. In Brazil, the upper river is called the Solimões until it meets the Rio Negro near Manaus. At its mouth, it can be more than 300 kilometers wide, including its delta islands."],["A river of records","The Amazon discharges an average of about 200,000 cubic meters of water per second into the Atlantic, more than the next several largest rivers combined. Its fresh water pushes far out to sea, and sailors once drank fresh water from the ocean out of sight of land. In the rainy season, the river rises many meters, flooding vast forests."],["Life in the river","The Amazon has more fish species than any other river system, perhaps 3,000 or more, including piranhas, electric eels and the giant arapaima, one of the largest freshwater fish. Pink river dolphins, called botos, giant otters, manatees and caimans also live in its waters."],["People of the river","Indigenous peoples have lived along the Amazon for thousands of years, fishing, farming and managing the forest. Today cities such as Manaus, Belém and Iquitos stand on its banks. Boats are the main form of transport, since few roads cross the rainforest, which is one reason no bridge spans the main river."]],
k:[["Length","About 6,400 km"],["Average discharge","About 200,000 m³ per second"],["Basin area","About 7 million km²"],["Mouth","Atlantic Ocean, Brazil"],["Source","Andes Mountains, Peru"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 BCE","People live along the Amazon"],["1500","Vicente Yáñez Pinzón reaches the Amazon’s mouth"],["1542","Francisco de Orellana travels down the river"],["1669","Manaus is founded as a Portuguese fort"],["1890s","The rubber boom brings wealth to Manaus"],["1971","A source in the Peruvian Andes is identified by an expedition"]],
s2:[["The Meeting of the Waters","Near Manaus, the dark, tea-colored Rio Negro meets the sandy, pale Solimões. Because they differ in temperature, speed and density, the two rivers flow side by side without mixing for several kilometers, creating a striking two-colored river. Boats take visitors to see this Meeting of the Waters."],["Naming the river","In 1542 the Spanish explorer Francisco de Orellana led the first European voyage down the river. His chronicler described battles with fierce women warriors, whom the Spanish compared to the Amazons of Greek mythology, giving the river its name. Historians think the warriors may have been men with long hair or women fighting alongside men."],["The rubber boom","In the late 1800s, the world demanded rubber for bicycle and car tires, and the Amazon’s rubber trees made Manaus enormously rich; its grand opera house, the Teatro Amazonas, opened in 1896. But rubber tappers, many of them Indigenous people, were often treated brutally. The boom collapsed after rubber plantations were established in Southeast Asia."],["The pororoca","Near the Amazon’s mouth, during certain tides, the ocean surges up the river in a powerful wave called the pororoca, which can be several meters high and travel for many kilometers inland with a roaring sound. Surfers sometimes ride it for long distances."],["Longest river?","The Nile has traditionally been called the world’s longest river, at about 6,650 kilometers, with the Amazon second. But some researchers argue that, measured from its most distant source in Peru and including certain channels at its mouth, the Amazon is longer. The answer depends on where a river is said to begin and end."],["Threats","Deforestation, dams, mining, pollution from mercury used in gold mining and overfishing threaten the Amazon. Severe droughts in 2023 and 2024 dropped river levels to record lows, stranding communities and killing river dolphins. Protecting the river and forest is vital for local people and the global climate."]],
sp:["Pink river dolphins","The boto, or Amazon river dolphin, is the largest river dolphin in the world and is often pink, especially older males. Unlike ocean dolphins, it has a flexible neck that lets it turn its head to swim among flooded tree trunks. In local legends, botos can transform into handsome men who come ashore at night. Botos are endangered, threatened by fishing nets, dams, pollution and droughts."],
g:[["Tributary","A river that flows into a larger river"],["Discharge","The volume of water a river carries"],["Basin","The land drained by a river and its tributaries"],["Tidal bore","A wave that travels up a river with the tide"]],
f:["The Amazon carries more water than any other river.","No bridge crosses the main Amazon.","The Amazon is home to pink river dolphins."],
fun:"Sailors once found fresh water in the ocean far from land because of the Amazon’s huge flow.",
tl:"Ang Ilog Amazon ang may pinakamaraming tubig sa lahat ng ilog sa mundo. Dumadaloy ito sa Timog Amerika sa pinakamalaking tropikal na gubat. Walang tulay na tumatawid sa pangunahing ilog nito."});

ADDART({id:"yangtze",t:"The Yangtze River",c:"Geography",i:"🚢",w:"Yangtze",r:["chinacountry","china","hydropower","lakes","rice"],
p:[["China’s longest river","The Yangtze is the longest river in Asia and the third-longest in the world."],["Heart of China","About a third of China’s people live in its basin."],["Three Gorges","It is home to the Three Gorges Dam, the world’s largest power station."]],
l:"The Yangtze, called the Chang Jiang, or Long River, in Chinese, flows about 6,300 kilometers from the Tibetan Plateau to the East China Sea at Shanghai. It is the longest river in Asia and the third-longest in the world. Its basin is home to hundreds of millions of people and produces much of China’s rice, fish and industry. The river has shaped Chinese history and culture for thousands of years, serving as a highway for trade and a source of both life and devastating floods.",
s:[["Course","The Yangtze rises from glaciers on the Tibetan Plateau, over 5,000 meters high, and rushes through deep gorges in Yunnan. It flows east through the Sichuan Basin and the city of Chongqing, cuts through the Three Gorges, crosses the plains around Wuhan and Nanjing, and reaches the sea at Shanghai."],["Lifeline of China","The Yangtze basin covers about a fifth of China’s land and is home to about a third of its population. Its fertile plains are major rice-growing regions, and its waters support fisheries and shipping. Ocean-going ships can travel far upriver, making it one of the busiest inland waterways in the world."],["History","Early civilizations grew along the Yangtze, where rice was first domesticated thousands of years ago. The river was a boundary between northern and southern China in many periods and appears often in Chinese poetry and painting. Great cities such as Nanjing, a former capital, and Wuhan grew along its banks."],["Floods","The Yangtze has caused some of the deadliest floods in history. In 1931 flooding across central China killed huge numbers of people, perhaps millions, through drowning, disease and famine. Major floods also occurred in 1954 and 1998. Dams, dikes and flood management have greatly reduced the risks."]],
k:[["Length","About 6,300 km"],["Chinese name","Chang Jiang (Long River)"],["Source","Tibetan Plateau"],["Mouth","East China Sea, at Shanghai"],["Largest dam","Three Gorges Dam"]],
d:[["c. 7000 BCE","Rice farming develops in the Yangtze region"],["1931","Catastrophic floods strike central China"],["1957","The first bridge across the lower Yangtze opens at Wuhan"],["1998","Major Yangtze floods"],["2006","The baiji river dolphin is declared functionally extinct"],["2020","A ten-year fishing ban begins on the Yangtze"]],
s2:[["The Three Gorges","The Three Gorges are spectacular canyons between Chongqing and Yichang, with towering cliffs celebrated by poets for centuries. The Three Gorges Dam, completed in stages and fully operating by 2012, created a reservoir about 600 kilometers long. It generates huge amounts of electricity and helps control floods, but flooded towns and historic sites and displaced more than a million people."],["The baiji","The baiji, or Yangtze river dolphin, was a pale gray dolphin with a long beak that lived only in the Yangtze. Pollution, ship traffic, dams and fishing nets drove it to decline, and after a survey in 2006 found none, scientists declared it functionally extinct, the first dolphin species driven to extinction by humans."],["Protecting the river","The Yangtze finless porpoise, a relative of the baiji, is critically endangered. In 2020 China began a ten-year ban on commercial fishing across much of the Yangtze to help fish and porpoises recover. Efforts to reduce pollution and restore wetlands are also under way."],["Bridges and cities","For most of history, the Yangtze was crossed only by boat. The first bridge over its lower reaches opened at Wuhan in 1957. Today more than 100 bridges cross the river, along with tunnels. Shanghai, at its mouth, is one of the world’s largest cities and busiest ports."],["The river in culture","The Yangtze appears throughout Chinese literature. The Tang dynasty poet Li Bai wrote of sailing through the Three Gorges, where the cries of monkeys echoed from the cliffs. The river was the setting for the famous Battle of Red Cliffs in 208 CE, retold in the classic novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms."],["Yangtze and Yellow","China has two great rivers: the Yangtze in the south and the Yellow River, or Huang He, in the north, often called the cradle of Chinese civilization. The South–North Water Transfer Project carries water from the Yangtze basin to the drier north, including Beijing, through canals and tunnels."]],
sp:["Swimming the Yangtze","In 1966, at age 72, Mao Zedong swam in the Yangtze at Wuhan, an event widely publicized to show his strength at the start of the Cultural Revolution. Each year, Wuhan holds a mass swim across the river in his memory, drawing thousands of participants. The Yangtze’s currents are strong, and swimmers must be skilled and supported by safety boats."],
g:[["Gorge","A narrow valley with steep rocky walls"],["Basin","The land drained by a river"],["Functionally extinct","Too few left for a species to survive"],["Reservoir","A lake created by a dam"]],
f:["The Yangtze is the longest river in Asia.","About a third of China’s people live in the Yangtze basin.","The baiji dolphin was declared functionally extinct in 2006."],
fun:"The Yangtze’s Chinese name, Chang Jiang, simply means Long River.",
tl:"Ang Ilog Yangtze ang pinakamahabang ilog sa Asya at pangatlo sa mundo. Mga isang-katlo ng mamamayan ng Tsina ang nakatira sa paligid nito. Narito ang Three Gorges Dam, ang pinakamalaking planta ng kuryente sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"ganges",t:"The Ganges River",c:"Geography",i:"🪔",w:"Ganges",r:["hindu","indiacountry","bangladeshcountry","himal","wetlands"],
p:[["Holy waters","The Ganges, or Ganga, is the most sacred river in Hinduism."],["From the Himalayas","It flows from a Himalayan glacier across northern India to the Bay of Bengal."],["Life for millions","Hundreds of millions of people depend on the Ganges and its basin."]],
l:"The Ganges, known in India as the Ganga, flows about 2,500 kilometers from the Himalayas across the plains of northern India and through Bangladesh to the Bay of Bengal. Revered as a goddess in Hinduism, it is believed to purify those who bathe in its waters, and holy cities such as Varanasi and Haridwar line its banks. Its basin is one of the most densely populated regions on Earth, home to hundreds of millions of people who rely on the river for water, farming and fishing. Together with the Brahmaputra, it forms the world’s largest delta.",
s:[["Course","The Ganges begins at the Gangotri Glacier in the Indian Himalayas, at an ice cave called Gaumukh, the cow’s mouth. It flows through mountain valleys, enters the plains at Haridwar and runs east past Kanpur, Prayagraj, Varanasi and Patna. In Bangladesh it is called the Padma, and it joins the Brahmaputra and Meghna to form a vast delta."],["Sacred river","In Hindu tradition, the goddess Ganga descended from heaven to Earth, her fall broken by the god Shiva’s hair. Hindus bathe in the river to cleanse sins, scatter the ashes of the dead in its waters and carry its water home for rituals. Evening ceremonies called aarti, with lamps and chanting, are held on its banks."],["The Ganges plain","The Indo-Gangetic Plain, watered by the Ganges and its tributaries, has some of the most fertile soil in the world, built from silt carried down from the Himalayas. Rice, wheat, sugarcane and lentils are grown there. Ancient kingdoms and empires, such as the Maurya and Gupta, flourished in the Ganges valley."],["The delta","The Ganges–Brahmaputra delta, mostly in Bangladesh and the Indian state of West Bengal, is the largest delta in the world. It includes the Sundarbans mangrove forest, home of the Bengal tiger. The delta is fertile and densely populated but vulnerable to floods, cyclones and rising seas."]],
k:[["Length","About 2,500 km"],["Source","Gangotri Glacier, Himalayas"],["Mouth","Bay of Bengal"],["Countries","India and Bangladesh"],["Holy city","Varanasi"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Vedic culture spreads into the Ganges plain"],["c. 320 BCE","The Maurya Empire rises in the Ganges valley"],["1986","India launches the Ganga Action Plan"],["2008","The Ganges is declared India’s national river"],["2014","India launches the Namami Gange program"],["2025","The Maha Kumbh Mela draws vast crowds to Prayagraj"]],
s2:[["Varanasi","Varanasi, on the banks of the Ganges, is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world and the holiest city of Hinduism. Pilgrims descend stone steps called ghats to bathe at dawn, and cremations take place at riverside ghats day and night, as many Hindus believe that dying in Varanasi brings release from the cycle of rebirth."],["The Kumbh Mela","The Kumbh Mela is a Hindu festival held in rotation at four riverside sites, the largest at Prayagraj, where the Ganges meets the Yamuna. Pilgrims bathe at auspicious times to wash away sins. The Maha Kumbh Mela of 2025 was reported to draw hundreds of millions of visitors over six weeks, making it one of the largest gatherings in human history."],["Pollution and cleanup","The Ganges is badly polluted by untreated sewage, industrial waste and runoff, harming the health of people who bathe in and drink from it. India has launched several cleanup programs, including the Namami Gange program in 2014, building sewage treatment plants and cleaning riverbanks."],["The Ganges river dolphin","The Ganges river dolphin is nearly blind and finds its way and food using echolocation in the muddy water. It is India’s national aquatic animal and is endangered by pollution, dams and fishing nets. It is sometimes called the susu because of the sound it makes when breathing."],["Glaciers and water","The Ganges depends partly on meltwater from Himalayan glaciers, especially in the dry season. Scientists are concerned that warming temperatures are shrinking these glaciers, which could affect water supplies for hundreds of millions of people in the long term."],["Sharing the river","India and Bangladesh share the Ganges. India’s Farakka Barrage, completed in 1975, diverts water to the Hooghly River to keep the port of Kolkata open, reducing flow into Bangladesh in the dry season. A treaty signed in 1996 set rules for sharing the river’s water."]],
sp:["The descent of the Ganga","According to Hindu legend, King Bhagiratha prayed for the heavenly river Ganga to come down to Earth to free the souls of his ancestors. Ganga agreed, but her fall would have shattered the Earth, so the god Shiva caught her in his tangled hair and let her flow gently down in streams. A huge rock carving at Mamallapuram in southern India, from the 600s, is believed by many to show this story, with a natural cleft in the rock representing the river."],
g:[["Ghat","Steps leading down to a river in India"],["Delta","Land formed where a river splits and meets the sea"],["Pilgrim","A person who travels to a sacred place"],["Echolocation","Finding objects by listening to echoes of sounds"]],
f:["The Ganges is the most sacred river in Hinduism.","It begins at the Gangotri Glacier.","The Ganges–Brahmaputra delta is the world’s largest delta."],
fun:"The Maha Kumbh Mela of 2025 was one of the largest gatherings of people in history.",
tl:"Ang Ilog Ganges, o Ganga, ang pinakabanal na ilog sa Hinduismo. Nagmumula ito sa Himalayas at dumadaloy sa hilagang India hanggang Bay of Bengal. Daan-daang milyong tao ang umaasa rito."});

ADDART({id:"mississippi",t:"The Mississippi River",c:"Geography",i:"🛶",w:"Mississippi River",r:["usacountry","uscivilwar","wetlands","storms","jazz"],
p:[["America’s great river","The Mississippi is the chief river of North America’s largest river system."],["Draining a continent","With its tributaries, it drains about 40 percent of the contiguous United States."],["River of stories","It inspired Mark Twain’s books and the birth of blues and jazz."]],
l:"The Mississippi River flows about 3,700 kilometers from Lake Itasca in Minnesota to the Gulf of Mexico below New Orleans. Together with the Missouri River, its longest tributary, it forms one of the world’s longest river systems. Its basin drains all or parts of 31 US states and two Canadian provinces, about 40 percent of the contiguous United States. The river has been vital for Indigenous peoples, explorers, farmers and trade for centuries, and it carries huge amounts of grain and goods on barges today.",
s:[["Course","The Mississippi begins as a small stream flowing out of Lake Itasca in northern Minnesota, so narrow that people can step across it. It grows as it flows south, joined by the Missouri near St. Louis and the Ohio at Cairo, Illinois. In its lower course, it winds in great loops through a broad floodplain to its delta in Louisiana."],["Indigenous peoples","The name Mississippi comes from an Ojibwe word meaning great river. Indigenous peoples lived along the river for thousands of years. Around 1050 to 1350 CE, the Mississippian culture built Cahokia, near present-day St. Louis, a city with huge earthen mounds and perhaps tens of thousands of people, the largest settlement north of Mexico at the time."],["Exploration and trade","The Spanish explorer Hernando de Soto reached the river in 1541, and the Frenchman La Salle traveled to its mouth in 1682, claiming the region for France. New Orleans was founded in 1718. After the United States bought the Louisiana Territory in 1803, the river became a great highway for farm goods, carried first by flatboats and later by steamboats."],["Floods and engineering","The Mississippi has flooded many times. The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 covered about 70,000 square kilometers and displaced hundreds of thousands of people. Since then, engineers have built levees, dams, locks and floodways to control the river and keep it navigable."]],
k:[["Length","About 3,700 km"],["Source","Lake Itasca, Minnesota"],["Mouth","Gulf of Mexico"],["Basin","About 40% of the contiguous United States"],["Longest tributary","Missouri River"]],
d:[["c. 1050","Cahokia rises near the river"],["1541","Hernando de Soto reaches the Mississippi"],["1682","La Salle reaches the river’s mouth"],["1803","The United States buys the Louisiana Territory"],["1863","Union forces capture Vicksburg"],["1927","The Great Mississippi Flood"]],
s2:[["Mark Twain’s river","Samuel Clemens, who wrote as Mark Twain, grew up in Hannibal, Missouri, on the Mississippi and worked as a steamboat pilot. His pen name comes from a riverboat call meaning two fathoms deep, safe water. His books Life on the Mississippi and Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, in which Huck and Jim, a man escaping slavery, float down the river on a raft, made the river famous."],["Music of the river","The Mississippi Delta region gave birth to the blues, sung by African American musicians in the early 1900s. Downriver, New Orleans was the birthplace of jazz. Musicians traveled up the river to cities such as Memphis, St. Louis and Chicago, spreading these styles that shaped modern popular music."],["A vital waterway","Barges pushed by towboats carry grain, soybeans, coal, oil and other goods along the Mississippi. Much of America’s grain exports travel down the river to ports near New Orleans. Locks and dams on the upper river allow boats to move between different water levels."],["The Civil War","During the American Civil War, control of the Mississippi was a key goal. The Union captured New Orleans in 1862 and the fortress city of Vicksburg in July 1863 after a long siege, splitting the Confederacy in two."],["The disappearing delta","Louisiana’s coastal wetlands, built by sediment from the Mississippi, are disappearing as levees stop floods from spreading new sediment, and canals and rising seas cause erosion. The state loses large areas of land every year. These wetlands help protect New Orleans from hurricanes, so restoration projects aim to divert sediment back into them."],["The dead zone","Fertilizer washed from farms across the Mississippi basin flows into the Gulf of Mexico, causing algae to bloom. When the algae die and decay, they use up oxygen, creating a dead zone each summer where fish and other sea life cannot survive. Farmers and governments are working to reduce fertilizer runoff."]],
sp:["Cahokia","Around 1100 CE, Cahokia, near the Mississippi in present-day Illinois, was a thriving city of the Mississippian culture, with perhaps 10,000 to 20,000 people. Its largest earthwork, Monks Mound, covers about 6 hectares and stands about 30 meters high, one of the largest earthen structures in the Americas. The city declined by about 1350. Cahokia Mounds is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Levee","An embankment built to prevent river flooding"],["Lock","A chamber that raises or lowers boats between water levels"],["Barge","A flat-bottomed boat for carrying goods"],["Floodplain","Flat land beside a river that floods"]],
f:["Mississippi means great river in Ojibwe.","Mark Twain was a Mississippi steamboat pilot.","The Mississippi basin drains about 40 percent of the contiguous US."],
fun:"At its source in Minnesota, the Mississippi is narrow enough to step across.",
tl:"Ang Ilog Mississippi ang pangunahing ilog ng Hilagang Amerika. Kasama ang mga sanga nito, dinadaluyan nito ang mga 40 porsiyento ng Estados Unidos. Naging inspirasyon ito ni Mark Twain at ng musikang blues at jazz."});

ADDART({id:"danube",t:"The Danube River",c:"Geography",i:"🎻",w:"Danube",r:["alps","rome","eu","wetlands","ottoman"],
p:[["River of ten countries","The Danube flows through or along ten countries, more than any other river."],["Four capitals","Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest and Belgrade all stand on its banks."],["A famous waltz","It inspired one of the most famous waltzes ever written."]],
l:"The Danube is Europe’s second-longest river, after the Volga, flowing about 2,850 kilometers from the Black Forest in Germany to the Black Sea. It passes through or forms the border of ten countries: Germany, Austria, Slovakia, Hungary, Croatia, Serbia, Romania, Bulgaria, Moldova and Ukraine, more than any other river in the world. Four capital cities lie on its banks. For thousands of years, the Danube has been a highway for trade and a frontier between empires, and its delta is one of Europe’s greatest wetlands.",
s:[["Course","The Danube begins in the Black Forest of southwestern Germany, where two streams meet at Donaueschingen. It flows east through Germany and Austria, past Vienna and Bratislava, turns south through Hungary at Budapest, passes Belgrade in Serbia and cuts through the Iron Gates gorge between Serbia and Romania before reaching its delta on the Black Sea."],["A frontier of empires","For centuries, the Danube formed the northern frontier of the Roman Empire, guarded by forts and towns, and parts of this frontier are now World Heritage Sites. Later, the river saw the expansion of the Ottoman Empire, which besieged Vienna in 1529 and 1683, and it became a key route for the Habsburg Empire."],["Cities of the Danube","Vienna, the capital of Austria, is famous for music and palaces. Bratislava, Slovakia’s capital, lies near the borders with Austria and Hungary. Budapest, formed by uniting Buda and Pest on either bank in 1873, is known for its Parliament building and bridges. Belgrade stands where the Sava River joins the Danube."],["A working river","The Danube is an important waterway for shipping goods such as grain, ore and fuel. Canals link it to the Rhine, allowing boats to travel between the North Sea and the Black Sea. River cruises are also popular. Hydroelectric dams, such as the Iron Gates dam, produce electricity."]],
k:[["Length","About 2,850 km"],["Source","Black Forest, Germany"],["Mouth","Black Sea"],["Countries","10"],["Capitals on its banks","Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest, Belgrade"]],
d:[["c. 100 CE","The Danube forms part of the Roman frontier"],["1529","The Ottomans besiege Vienna"],["1867","Johann Strauss II composes The Blue Danube"],["1873","Buda and Pest unite to form Budapest"],["1972","The Iron Gates dam is completed"],["1991","The Danube Delta becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["The Blue Danube","In 1867 the Austrian composer Johann Strauss II wrote the waltz On the Beautiful Blue Danube, which became one of the most famous pieces of classical music in the world. It is played every New Year’s Day by the Vienna Philharmonic. It was also used memorably in the 1968 science fiction film 2001: A Space Odyssey."],["The Danube Delta","Where the Danube meets the Black Sea, mostly in Romania with part in Ukraine, it forms a delta of channels, lakes, reed beds and islands covering about 4,000 square kilometers. It is one of the largest wetlands in Europe and home to more than 300 bird species, including great colonies of pelicans."],["The Iron Gates","The Iron Gates is a dramatic gorge where the Danube cuts through the Carpathian Mountains between Serbia and Romania. Once dangerous for boats because of rapids and rocks, it was tamed by a huge dam completed in 1972. A giant carved face of the ancient Dacian king Decebalus overlooks the river."],["Bridges and history","The Danube has been crossed by famous bridges, such as Budapest’s Chain Bridge, opened in 1849. In ancient times, the Roman emperor Trajan built a great bridge across the lower Danube around 105 CE to invade Dacia, now Romania; its ruins survive."],["Protecting the river","The Danube faces pollution from industry, farming and cities, and dams have changed its flow and blocked migrating fish such as sturgeon. The countries along the river cooperate through the International Commission for the Protection of the Danube River, and 29 June is celebrated as Danube Day."],["Is it really blue?","Despite the famous waltz, the Danube usually looks brown, gray or green, colored by sediment and algae. Its color changes with the light, weather and season. Many writers have joked that the river is blue only to those in love."]],
sp:["A river of many names","Because it flows through so many countries, the Danube has many names: Donau in German, Dunaj in Slovak, Duna in Hungarian, Dunav in Serbian, Croatian and Bulgarian, Dunărea in Romanian and Dunai in Ukrainian. All come from the ancient name Danuvius, which may come from an old word for river. The river links peoples speaking Germanic, Slavic, Hungarian and Romance languages."],
g:[["Gorge","A narrow valley with steep walls"],["Delta","Land formed at a river’s mouth"],["Frontier","A border region between territories"],["Waltz","A dance in triple time, popular in Vienna"]],
f:["The Danube flows through or along ten countries.","Four capital cities stand on the Danube.","The Danube Delta is one of Europe’s largest wetlands."],
fun:"The Blue Danube waltz is played every New Year’s Day in Vienna.",
tl:"Ang Ilog Danube ang pangalawang pinakamahabang ilog sa Europa. Dumadaloy ito sa o sa hangganan ng sampung bansa, higit sa alinmang ilog. Apat na kabisera ang nasa pampang nito."});

;
/* data/earth5.js */
// Planet Earth articles, part 5: rocks and minerals, fossils, fossil fuels, maps, and time zones.
ADDART({id:"rocks",t:"Rocks and Minerals",c:"Geography",i:"🪨",w:"Rock (geology)",r:["volc","tect","crystals","metals","fossils"],
p:[["The stuff of Earth","Rocks are solid materials made of minerals, forming Earth’s crust."],["Three main kinds","Rocks are igneous, sedimentary or metamorphic, depending on how they formed."],["Always changing","Over millions of years, rocks change from one kind into another."]],
l:"Rocks are natural solid materials made of one or more minerals, and they make up Earth’s crust and much of its interior. Minerals are naturally occurring solids with a definite chemical makeup and an orderly crystal structure, such as quartz, feldspar and calcite. Geologists group rocks into three main types: igneous rocks, formed from cooled molten rock; sedimentary rocks, formed from layers of sediment; and metamorphic rocks, transformed by heat and pressure. Through the slow rock cycle, Earth constantly recycles its rocks.",
s:[["Igneous rocks","Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools and hardens. When it cools slowly underground, large crystals grow, forming rocks such as granite. When lava cools quickly at the surface, it forms fine-grained rocks such as basalt, which makes up most of the ocean floor, or glassy obsidian. Pumice is so full of gas bubbles that it can float on water."],["Sedimentary rocks","Sedimentary rocks form when sand, mud, pebbles, shells and other particles settle in layers, usually under water, and are squeezed and cemented together over long periods. Sandstone, shale and limestone are examples. Limestone often forms from the shells of sea creatures. Sedimentary rocks often contain fossils."],["Metamorphic rocks","Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are changed by heat and pressure deep underground, without melting. Limestone becomes marble, shale becomes slate and granite can become gneiss. These rocks often show bands or layers created as minerals rearranged under pressure."],["The rock cycle","Rocks are constantly recycled. Igneous rocks are worn down by wind, water and ice into sediment, which forms sedimentary rock. Buried rocks can be transformed into metamorphic rock or melted into magma, which rises and cools as new igneous rock. Plate tectonics drives this cycle, which takes millions of years."]],
k:[["Main rock types","Igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic"],["Known minerals","About 6,000"],["Most common in the crust","Feldspars"],["Oldest Earth minerals","Zircon crystals, about 4.4 billion years old"],["Hardness scale","Mohs scale"]],
d:[["c. 4.4 billion years ago","The oldest known zircon crystals form"],["c. 4 billion years ago","The Acasta Gneiss, among the oldest rocks, forms in Canada"],["1556","Georgius Agricola publishes a major book on mining and minerals"],["1785","James Hutton presents his theory of the rock cycle and deep time"],["1812","Friedrich Mohs creates his hardness scale"],["1830s","Charles Lyell popularizes the idea of slow geological change"]],
s2:[["James Hutton and deep time","In 1785 the Scottish geologist James Hutton argued that rocks form through slow processes, such as erosion, deposition and uplift, that still operate today. At Siccar Point in Scotland, he saw tilted layers of rock covered by flat layers, showing that vast ages must have passed. He wrote that he found no vestige of a beginning, no prospect of an end."],["Minerals and gems","About 6,000 minerals are known. Quartz and feldspars are the most common in the crust. Some rare minerals, such as diamond, ruby, sapphire and emerald, are prized as gemstones. Minerals are identified by properties such as color, hardness, luster, streak and the way they break."],["Rocks in daily life","People have used rocks for tools, buildings and art since prehistoric times. Granite and marble are used in buildings and monuments, limestone is used to make cement and concrete, clay is fired into bricks and pottery, and minerals provide metals, salt, glass and fertilizer."],["Rocks of the Philippines","The Philippines, built by volcanoes and plate collisions, has a great variety of rocks. Romblon is famous for marble. The Chocolate Hills of Bohol are mounds of limestone shaped by rain and erosion. Volcanic rocks, such as the pumice and ash from Mount Pinatubo’s 1991 eruption, cover large areas of Luzon."],["Rocks from space","Meteorites are rocks from space, and astronauts brought back about 382 kilograms of Moon rocks during the Apollo missions. Studying these rocks has helped scientists learn the age of the Solar System and the history of the Moon."],["Collecting rocks","Rock collecting is a popular hobby. Collectors look for interesting minerals, crystals and fossils, using hand lenses, hammers and field guides. Many countries have rules protecting important geological sites, so collectors should always follow local laws and take only what is allowed."]],
sp:["Uluru","Uluru, a huge sandstone rock rising about 348 meters from the flat desert of central Australia, glows red at sunrise and sunset because of iron minerals in its surface. It is sacred to the Anangu, the Aboriginal people of the area. Formed from sediments laid down about 550 million years ago, it was later tilted upright, and most of the rock lies hidden underground. Climbing Uluru was banned in 2019 out of respect for Anangu wishes."],
g:[["Mineral","A natural solid with a definite chemical makeup and crystal structure"],["Igneous rock","Rock formed from cooled magma or lava"],["Sedimentary rock","Rock formed from layers of sediment"],["Metamorphic rock","Rock changed by heat and pressure"]],
f:["Marble is limestone changed by heat and pressure.","Pumice can float on water.","The oldest Earth minerals are about 4.4 billion years old."],
fun:"Pumice, a volcanic rock full of bubbles, can float on water.",
tl:"Ang bato ay matigas na bagay na binubuo ng mga mineral. Tatlo ang pangunahing uri nito: igneous, sedimentary at metamorphic. Sa loob ng milyon-milyong taon, nagbabago ang bato mula sa isang uri patungo sa iba."});

ADDART({id:"fossils",t:"Fossils",c:"Geography",i:"🦕",w:"Fossil",r:["dino","evo","rocks","humanorigins","extinction"],
p:[["Clues from the past","Fossils are preserved remains or traces of ancient living things."],["Written in stone","Most fossils form in sedimentary rocks, layer by layer over millions of years."],["Reading Earth’s history","Fossils show how life has changed over billions of years."]],
l:"Fossils are the preserved remains, impressions or traces of organisms that lived long ago, usually more than about 10,000 years. They include bones, shells, teeth, leaves, footprints, burrows and even ancient insects trapped in amber. Most fossils form when organisms are quickly buried in sediment that later becomes rock. By studying fossils, a science called paleontology, scientists have reconstructed the history of life on Earth, from microbes billions of years old to dinosaurs, early mammals and human ancestors.",
s:[["How fossils form","When a plant or animal dies, it usually decays or is eaten. But if it is quickly buried in mud, sand or volcanic ash, it may be preserved. Over time, minerals in groundwater can replace the original material, turning bone or wood into stone, a process called petrification. Hard parts, such as bones, teeth and shells, are most likely to become fossils."],["Kinds of fossils","Body fossils are the remains of the organism itself. Trace fossils, such as footprints, nests and burrows, record behavior. Molds are impressions left in rock, and casts form when molds fill with minerals. Some organisms are preserved almost whole, such as insects in amber, mammoths frozen in ice and animals trapped in tar."],["Dating fossils","Scientists date fossils by their position in rock layers, since lower layers are usually older, and by radiometric dating of volcanic layers above and below them. Index fossils, species that lived for a short time over a wide area, help match rock layers in different places."],["What fossils tell us","Fossils show that life has changed greatly over time, with new species appearing and others becoming extinct. They reveal ancient climates, environments and connections between continents. Transitional fossils, such as Archaeopteryx, which had both dinosaur and bird features, show how major groups evolved."]],
k:[["Science of fossils","Paleontology"],["Oldest fossils","Stromatolites, about 3.5 billion years old"],["Famous transitional fossil","Archaeopteryx (found 1861)"],["Pioneer fossil hunter","Mary Anning"],["Typical age","More than 10,000 years"]],
d:[["c. 3.5 billion years ago","Stromatolites form in Australia"],["1811","Mary Anning’s family finds an ichthyosaur at Lyme Regis"],["1861","Archaeopteryx is discovered in Germany"],["1974","Lucy, a 3.2-million-year-old human ancestor, is found"],["1990","Sue, a nearly complete Tyrannosaurus rex, is found"],["1996","Feathered dinosaur fossils are announced from China"]],
s2:[["Mary Anning","Mary Anning, born in 1799 in Lyme Regis on England’s southern coast, searched the crumbling cliffs for fossils to sell. As a young girl, she and her brother found the first complete ichthyosaur skeleton known to science, and she later discovered plesiosaurs and a pterosaur. As a woman from a poor family, she received little credit in her lifetime, but she is now honored as one of the great fossil hunters."],["Feathered dinosaurs","In 1996 scientists described Sinosauropteryx from Liaoning, China, a small dinosaur with fuzzy, feather-like filaments. Many feathered dinosaurs have since been found there, confirming that birds evolved from dinosaurs. Some fossils even preserve pigment structures that reveal the colors of their feathers."],["Stromatolites","Stromatolites are layered mounds built by colonies of microbes called cyanobacteria. Fossil stromatolites about 3.5 billion years old in Western Australia are among the oldest evidence of life. Living stromatolites still grow at Shark Bay in Australia, giving a glimpse of early life on Earth."],["Fossils in the Philippines","In Kalinga, northern Luzon, scientists found the butchered skeleton of an extinct rhinoceros, about 709,000 years old, along with stone tools, showing that early humans lived on Luzon far earlier than once thought. Fossils of extinct elephants called stegodons and giant tortoises have also been found in the Philippines."],["Sue the T. rex","In 1990 the fossil hunter Sue Hendrickson discovered a Tyrannosaurus rex skeleton in South Dakota, about 90 percent complete, one of the most complete ever found. Named Sue, it was sold at auction for over 8 million dollars in 1997 and is displayed at the Field Museum in Chicago."],["Fossil fuels","Coal, oil and natural gas are called fossil fuels because they formed from the remains of ancient plants and microscopic organisms buried and transformed over millions of years. Coal often contains fossil leaves and tree trunks from ancient swamp forests."]],
sp:["Insects in amber","Amber is fossilized tree resin. When sticky resin oozed from ancient trees, it sometimes trapped insects, spiders, flowers and even small lizards and feathers, which were preserved in incredible detail as the resin hardened over millions of years. Amber from Myanmar, about 99 million years old, contains a piece of a feathered dinosaur tail. Amber inspired the story of Jurassic Park, though scientists have not been able to recover dinosaur DNA."],
g:[["Fossil","Preserved remains or traces of ancient life"],["Paleontology","The study of fossils and ancient life"],["Petrification","Turning organic material into stone"],["Trace fossil","Evidence of activity, such as footprints"]],
f:["The oldest fossils are about 3.5 billion years old.","Archaeopteryx shows features of both dinosaurs and birds.","A 709,000-year-old butchered rhino was found in Kalinga."],
fun:"A piece of a feathered dinosaur tail was found trapped in amber.",
tl:"Ang fossil ay labi o bakas ng sinaunang buhay na napreserba sa bato. Ipinapakita nito kung paano nagbago ang buhay sa Earth. Sa Kalinga, natagpuan ang buto ng rhinoceros na mga 709,000 taon na ang edad."});

ADDART({id:"fossilfuels",t:"Fossil Fuels",c:"Geography",i:"🛢️",w:"Fossil fuel",r:["climchange","renew","elec","cars","plastics"],
p:[["Energy from ancient life","Coal, oil and natural gas formed from the remains of living things over millions of years."],["Powering the world","Fossil fuels still supply most of the world’s energy."],["A warming planet","Burning them releases carbon dioxide, the main cause of climate change."]],
l:"Fossil fuels are coal, oil, also called petroleum, and natural gas, formed over millions of years from the buried remains of ancient plants and microscopic sea organisms. Since the Industrial Revolution, they have powered factories, cars, ships, planes and power plants and are used to make plastics, fertilizers and many chemicals. They still provide about four-fifths of the world’s energy. But burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide and other pollutants, causing climate change and air pollution, so countries are working to shift to cleaner energy.",
s:[["How they formed","Coal formed mainly from plants in ancient swamp forests, especially about 300 million years ago, which were buried, compressed and heated over time. Oil and natural gas formed mostly from tiny marine organisms, such as plankton, buried on the seafloor and transformed by heat and pressure into liquid and gas trapped in rocks."],["Coal","Coal powered the steam engines of the Industrial Revolution and is still burned to generate a large share of the world’s electricity, especially in China and India. Burning coal releases the most carbon dioxide per unit of energy of any fossil fuel and also produces soot and harmful gases."],["Oil","Oil is refined into gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, heating oil and materials for plastics and chemicals. The modern oil industry began in the mid-1800s, with wells in Baku, now Azerbaijan, and Pennsylvania in the United States. Major producers include the United States, Saudi Arabia and Russia."],["Natural gas","Natural gas, mostly methane, is used for heating, cooking, electricity and industry. It burns more cleanly than coal, but methane that leaks into the air is a powerful greenhouse gas. Gas is transported by pipelines and, as a super-cooled liquid, by special ships."]],
k:[["Types","Coal, oil, natural gas"],["Share of world energy","About four-fifths"],["First modern oil well (US)","Drake Well, Pennsylvania, 1859"],["Main greenhouse gas released","Carbon dioxide"],["Philippine gas field","Malampaya"]],
d:[["c. 300 million years ago","Great coal-forming swamp forests grow"],["1700s","Coal powers the Industrial Revolution"],["1859","Edwin Drake drills a famous oil well in Pennsylvania"],["1973","An oil crisis causes prices to soar"],["2015","The Paris Agreement on climate change is adopted"],["2023","Countries agree at COP28 to transition away from fossil fuels"]],
s2:[["Fossil fuels and climate","Burning fossil fuels is the largest source of the carbon dioxide that is warming the planet. Since the 1800s, carbon dioxide in the air has risen by about half. Scientists say that limiting warming requires rapidly reducing fossil fuel use and reaching net-zero emissions around the middle of this century."],["Air pollution","Burning coal, oil and gas also releases fine particles, nitrogen oxides and sulfur dioxide, which harm lungs and hearts. Air pollution from fossil fuels contributes to millions of early deaths each year, especially in crowded cities with heavy traffic and coal-fired power plants."],["Oil and power","Because oil is so valuable, it has shaped world politics. Oil wealth has transformed countries in the Middle East and elsewhere, but has also been linked to wars, corruption and economic dependence. In 1973 an oil embargo by Arab producers caused a global energy crisis."],["Fossil fuels in the Philippines","The Philippines imports most of its oil and coal. Coal-fired plants generate a large share of its electricity. The Malampaya gas field off Palawan, producing since 2001, has supplied a significant portion of Luzon’s power. The country is expanding solar, wind, geothermal and hydropower to reduce dependence on imported fuels."],["Oil spills","Oil spills from tankers, pipelines and offshore wells can devastate seas and coasts. In 2010 the Deepwater Horizon disaster in the Gulf of Mexico released millions of barrels of oil. In 2023 the sinking of the tanker MT Princess Empress off Oriental Mindoro spilled industrial oil that damaged coral reefs, mangroves and fishing communities."],["The energy transition","Many countries are shifting to renewable energy such as solar and wind, electric vehicles and more efficient buildings. Fossil fuels will still be used for some time, especially for industry and aviation, but their share is expected to fall. The transition aims to cut emissions while keeping energy affordable and reliable."]],
sp:["Ancient sunlight","The energy in fossil fuels originally came from the Sun. Hundreds of millions of years ago, plants and plankton captured sunlight through photosynthesis and stored its energy in their bodies. When they were buried and transformed into coal, oil and gas, that energy was locked away. When we burn fossil fuels today, we release ancient sunlight, along with the carbon those organisms took from the air long ago."],
g:[["Fossil fuel","A fuel formed from ancient organisms"],["Petroleum","Crude oil"],["Methane","The main gas in natural gas"],["Greenhouse gas","A gas that traps heat in the atmosphere"]],
f:["Fossil fuels supply about four-fifths of the world’s energy.","Coal formed from ancient swamp plants.","Burning fossil fuels is the main cause of climate change."],
fun:"The energy in fossil fuels is ancient sunlight captured by plants millions of years ago.",
tl:"Ang fossil fuel, gaya ng karbon, langis at natural gas, ay nabuo mula sa labi ng sinaunang buhay sa loob ng milyon-milyong taon. Sila pa rin ang pangunahing pinagkukunan ng enerhiya sa mundo, ngunit ang pagsunog sa kanila ang pangunahing sanhi ng pagbabago ng klima."});

ADDART({id:"maps",t:"Maps, Latitude and Longitude",c:"Geography",i:"🗺️",w:"Map",r:["gps","continents","explor","timezones","earthplanet"],
p:[["Pictures of places","A map is a drawing of an area that shows where things are."],["A global grid","Lines of latitude and longitude let us pinpoint any place on Earth."],["From clay to GPS","Maps have evolved from clay tablets to satellite images on phones."]],
l:"A map is a simplified representation of an area, showing the locations of places and features such as cities, roads, rivers, mountains and borders. People have made maps for thousands of years to find their way, record land and understand the world. Modern maps use a grid of latitude and longitude to give every place on Earth a precise address. Because Earth is round, every flat map distorts it in some way. Today satellites, GPS and computers create maps of extraordinary detail, available on smartphones.",
s:[["Latitude","Lines of latitude, or parallels, run east and west around the globe, measuring distance north or south of the equator, which is 0 degrees. The North Pole is 90 degrees north and the South Pole 90 degrees south. Manila lies at about 14.6 degrees north. The Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn and the Arctic and Antarctic Circles are special lines of latitude."],["Longitude","Lines of longitude, or meridians, run from pole to pole, measuring distance east or west of the prime meridian, 0 degrees, which passes through Greenwich in London. Longitude goes up to 180 degrees east and west. Manila is at about 121 degrees east. Together, latitude and longitude give coordinates for any place."],["Parts of a map","Maps usually include a title, a scale showing how distances on the map relate to real distances, a legend or key explaining symbols and colors, and a compass rose or north arrow. Contour lines on topographic maps show height, with closely spaced lines indicating steep slopes."],["Map projections","Turning a round Earth into a flat map always causes distortion, like flattening an orange peel. The Mercator projection of 1569 keeps directions accurate for sailors but makes places near the poles look far too big; Greenland looks as large as Africa, although Africa is about 14 times bigger. Other projections balance size and shape differently."]],
k:[["Latitude","Distance north or south of the equator"],["Longitude","Distance east or west of the prime meridian"],["Prime meridian","Greenwich, London (agreed 1884)"],["Famous projection","Mercator (1569)"],["Manila’s coordinates","About 14.6° N, 121° E"]],
d:[["c. 600 BCE","The Babylonian Map of the World is made on clay"],["c. 150 CE","Ptolemy writes his Geography with a grid of coordinates"],["1154","Al-Idrisi completes a world map for King Roger II of Sicily"],["1569","Gerardus Mercator publishes his projection"],["1734","The Murillo Velarde map of the Philippines is published"],["2005","Google Maps is launched"]],
s2:[["Ancient maps","The Babylonian Map of the World, carved on a clay tablet around 600 BCE, shows Babylon at the center of a circular world surrounded by ocean. The Greek scholar Ptolemy, working in Alexandria around 150 CE, described how to map the world using latitude and longitude, listing the coordinates of thousands of places."],["Al-Idrisi’s map","In 1154 the Arab geographer Muhammad al-Idrisi, working at the court of King Roger II of Sicily, completed the Tabula Rogeriana, one of the most accurate world maps of the Middle Ages. Based on travelers’ reports, it showed Europe, Asia and North Africa, with south at the top."],["The longitude problem","Sailors could find latitude from the Sun or stars, but finding longitude required knowing the exact time at a reference place. Errors caused shipwrecks. In the 1700s, the English clockmaker John Harrison built marine chronometers, clocks accurate enough at sea to calculate longitude; his H4 passed a sea trial in 1761."],["The Murillo Velarde map","In 1734 the Jesuit priest Pedro Murillo Velarde published a detailed map of the Philippines, engraved by the Filipino artist Nicolás de la Cruz Bagay, with illustrations by Francisco Suárez. Called the mother of all Philippine maps, it shows the islands and surrounding seas, including Scarborough Shoal, and was used as evidence in the Philippines’ South China Sea arbitration case."],["Maps in your pocket","Today digital maps use satellite images, aerial photographs and GPS. Apps can show your location, find routes, estimate travel times and show live traffic. Volunteers around the world contribute to OpenStreetMap, a free, editable map of the world started in 2004, which is used in disaster relief."],["Mapping for disasters","Maps help save lives during disasters. Hazard maps show areas at risk of floods, landslides, storm surges and volcanic eruptions. In the Philippines, agencies such as PHIVOLCS and PAGASA, along with Project NOAH, produce hazard maps that guide evacuations and planning."]],
sp:["The mother of all Philippine maps","The 1734 Murillo Velarde map is so detailed that it is considered the first scientific map of the Philippines. Its borders are decorated with scenes of Filipino life, showing people of different groups, plants, animals and city views. In 2014 a businessman bought a rare copy at auction in London and donated it to the Philippine government. It was presented as evidence in the arbitration case that the Philippines won in 2016 regarding the South China Sea."],
g:[["Latitude","Distance north or south of the equator, in degrees"],["Longitude","Distance east or west of the prime meridian, in degrees"],["Projection","A method of showing the round Earth on a flat map"],["Scale","The relation between map distance and real distance"]],
f:["The equator is 0 degrees latitude.","The prime meridian passes through Greenwich, London.","Africa is about 14 times larger than Greenland."],
fun:"On the Mercator map, Greenland looks as big as Africa, but Africa is about 14 times larger.",
tl:"Ang mapa ay larawan ng isang lugar na nagpapakita kung nasaan ang mga bagay. Gamit ang latitude at longitude, matutukoy ang anumang lugar sa Earth. Ang mapa ni Murillo Velarde noong 1734 ay tinaguriang ina ng lahat ng mapa ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"timezones",t:"Time Zones",c:"Geography",i:"🕰️",w:"Time zone",r:["timephys","maps","earthplanet","rail","samoacountry"],
p:[["A world of clocks","Because Earth rotates, it is day in some places and night in others at the same moment."],["Twenty-four zones","The world is divided into time zones, roughly 15 degrees of longitude wide."],["Philippine time","The Philippines uses Philippine Standard Time, eight hours ahead of UTC."]],
l:"A time zone is a region that uses the same standard time. Because Earth rotates once every 24 hours, the Sun rises at different times in different places. To keep clocks in step with daylight while avoiding confusion, the world is divided into time zones, each roughly 15 degrees of longitude wide, one hour apart. Time zones are measured from Coordinated Universal Time, UTC. The Philippines uses Philippine Standard Time, UTC plus 8 hours, the same as China, Singapore, Malaysia and Western Australia.",
s:[["Why time zones?","Before the 1800s, towns kept their own local time based on the Sun, so noon was when the Sun was highest. Towns just a few kilometers apart had slightly different times. This caused little trouble until railways and telegraphs made fast travel and communication possible, and train schedules became confusing."],["Railway time","In 1847 most British railways adopted a single standard time, Greenwich Mean Time. In 1883 railways in the United States and Canada introduced standard time zones, on a day nicknamed the day of two noons. The Canadian engineer Sandford Fleming proposed a worldwide system of time zones."],["The prime meridian","In 1884 the International Meridian Conference in Washington chose the meridian through Greenwich, London, as the prime meridian, 0 degrees longitude, and the starting point for world time. Today world time is based on UTC, kept by extremely accurate atomic clocks."],["The International Date Line","Halfway around the world from Greenwich, near 180 degrees longitude, lies the International Date Line. Crossing it westward, the calendar jumps forward a day; crossing eastward, it goes back a day. The line zigzags to avoid splitting countries, and some Pacific nations have moved it to suit their trade."]],
k:[["Width of a zone","About 15° of longitude"],["World standard","Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)"],["Philippine Standard Time","UTC+8"],["Prime meridian adopted","1884"],["Country with most time zones","France (including overseas territories)"]],
d:[["1847","British railways adopt Greenwich time"],["1883","North American railways adopt standard time zones"],["1884","The International Meridian Conference chooses Greenwich"],["1916","Germany introduces daylight saving time"],["1972","Coordinated Universal Time comes into use"],["2013","The Philippines passes a law on Philippine Standard Time"]],
s2:[["The Philippines skips a day","For more than three centuries, the Philippines was linked to Spain through Mexico by the Manila galleons sailing across the Pacific, so it kept the same calendar date as the Americas, a day behind its Asian neighbors. To align with Asian trade partners, the Philippines skipped Tuesday, 31 December 1844, jumping straight from Monday, 30 December, to Wednesday, 1 January 1845."],["Odd time zones","Not all time zones are whole hours. India uses UTC plus 5 hours 30 minutes, and Nepal uses UTC plus 5 hours 45 minutes. China, despite spanning about five time zones geographically, uses a single time, Beijing Time, so in western China the Sun may rise as late as 10 in the morning in winter."],["Daylight saving time","Some countries move their clocks forward an hour in summer to make better use of evening daylight, a practice called daylight saving time, first adopted widely by Germany in 1916. Most countries near the equator, including the Philippines, do not use it, because day length changes little through the year."],["Juan Time and PST","Philippine Standard Time is kept by PAGASA using atomic clocks. A 2013 law requires government offices and broadcasters to synchronize their clocks with it, partly to discourage the habit of lateness jokingly called Filipino time or Juan Time. Accurate time is important for transport, finance and communication."],["Jet lag","Flying quickly across many time zones can cause jet lag, as the body’s internal clock stays on the old time. Travelers may feel sleepy during the day and awake at night. Getting sunlight at the right times and adjusting sleep gradually can help the body adapt."],["Who sees the new year first?","Because of the date line, the first places to welcome each new year are Pacific islands such as Kiribati’s Line Islands, at UTC plus 14, followed by Samoa, Tonga and New Zealand. The last are American Samoa and remote islands at UTC minus 11 and minus 12, more than a full day later."]],
sp:["The day of two noons","On 18 November 1883, railways across the United States and Canada switched to four standard time zones. In many cities, clocks were reset at noon, so people experienced noon twice, once by the old local time and once by the new standard time. The change was organized by the railways, not the government; the United States did not make time zones law until 1918."],
g:[["Time zone","A region that uses the same standard time"],["UTC","Coordinated Universal Time, the world’s time standard"],["International Date Line","The line where the calendar date changes"],["Daylight saving time","Moving clocks forward in summer"]],
f:["The world has about 24 main time zones.","The Philippines uses UTC+8.","The Philippines skipped 31 December 1844."],
fun:"The Philippines skipped 31 December 1844 to match the calendar of its Asian neighbors.",
tl:"Ang time zone ay rehiyong gumagamit ng iisang oras. Dahil umiikot ang Earth, magkakaiba ang oras sa iba't ibang lugar. Ginagamit ng Pilipinas ang Philippine Standard Time, walong oras na mas maaga kaysa UTC. Nilaktawan ng Pilipinas ang 31 Disyembre 1844."});

;
/* data/env.js */
// Environment articles: pollution, deforestation, plastic waste and conservation.
ADDART({id:"pollution",t:"Pollution",c:"Geography",i:"🏭",w:"Pollution",r:["fossilfuels","plasticwaste","climchange","sanitation","lungs"],
p:[["Harmful substances","Pollution is the release of harmful substances or energy into the air, water or land."],["Many kinds","There is air pollution, water pollution, soil pollution, noise pollution and light pollution."],["Can be reduced","Laws, cleaner technology and individual choices have reduced many kinds of pollution."]],
l:"Pollution is the introduction of harmful substances or forms of energy into the environment, damaging the health of people, animals and ecosystems. Air pollution from vehicles, factories, power plants and burning waste is one of the world’s largest health risks, linked to millions of early deaths each year. Water pollution from sewage, chemicals and farm runoff contaminates rivers, lakes and seas. Other forms include soil, noise and light pollution. Many countries have greatly reduced some pollutants through laws and cleaner technologies, showing that pollution can be tackled.",
s:[["Air pollution","Air pollution includes tiny particles, smoke, soot, ground-level ozone, nitrogen oxides and sulfur dioxide. It comes from vehicles, power plants, factories, cooking with wood or charcoal and burning crops or rubbish. The World Health Organization estimates that outdoor and household air pollution together cause millions of premature deaths each year, from heart disease, strokes, lung disease and cancer."],["Water pollution","Rivers, lakes and seas are polluted by untreated sewage, industrial chemicals, oil, plastic and fertilizers and pesticides washed from farms. Polluted water spreads diseases such as cholera and kills fish and other wildlife. Billions of people still lack safely managed sanitation, so sewage often flows into waterways."],["Soil, noise and light","Soil can be contaminated by industrial waste, mining, pesticides and leaking landfills, affecting crops and groundwater. Noise from traffic, airplanes and construction can disturb sleep and health. Artificial light at night, called light pollution, hides the stars and confuses animals such as migrating birds and baby sea turtles."],["Fighting pollution","Laws limiting emissions, catalytic converters on cars, cleaner fuels, sewage treatment plants and filters on factory chimneys have dramatically cleaned the air and water in many places. The worldwide phase-out of leaded gasoline, completed in 2021, removed a toxic metal that harmed children’s brains."]],
k:[["Main types","Air, water, soil, noise, light"],["Deaths from air pollution","Millions each year (WHO)"],["Leaded gasoline phased out","2021"],["Treaty on mercury","Minamata Convention, 2013"],["Famous smog disaster","London, 1952"]],
d:[["1952","The Great Smog of London kills thousands"],["1956","Minamata disease is identified in Japan"],["1962","Rachel Carson publishes Silent Spring"],["1970","The first Earth Day is held"],["2008","The Philippine Supreme Court orders the cleanup of Manila Bay"],["2021","Leaded gasoline is phased out worldwide"]],
s2:[["The Great Smog of London","In December 1952, cold, still weather trapped smoke from coal fires and factories over London for five days, creating a thick, yellow-black smog. People could barely see their own feet. Thousands died, and later estimates suggest around 12,000 deaths. The disaster led to Britain’s Clean Air Act of 1956."],["Minamata disease","In the 1950s, people in Minamata, Japan, began suffering severe nerve damage, and cats behaved strangely and died. The cause was mercury released by a chemical factory into the bay, which built up in the fish people ate. Thousands of people were affected. The Minamata Convention, a global treaty to reduce mercury pollution, was named in their memory."],["Silent Spring","In 1962 the American biologist Rachel Carson published Silent Spring, describing how pesticides such as DDT were harming birds and other wildlife and could harm people. The book helped launch the modern environmental movement and led to bans on DDT for farm use in many countries."],["Pollution in the Philippines","Metro Manila faces air pollution from traffic, especially older diesel vehicles, and many rivers, including the Pasig, have been heavily polluted by sewage and waste. In 2008 the Supreme Court ordered government agencies to clean up Manila Bay. Rehabilitation programs, new sewage systems and community efforts have helped some waterways begin to recover."],["Light pollution","Light pollution has made the Milky Way invisible to a large share of humanity, including most people in many cities. It also affects wildlife: newly hatched sea turtles head toward bright lights instead of the sea, and migrating birds crash into lit buildings. Dark-sky parks protect places where the stars can still be seen."],["What people can do","Individuals can help reduce pollution by walking, cycling or using public transport, saving energy, avoiding burning rubbish, disposing of chemicals and batteries properly, reducing plastic waste and supporting clean-air and clean-water policies. Communities can monitor air quality and organize cleanups."]],
sp:["The first Earth Day","On 22 April 1970, about 20 million Americans took part in the first Earth Day, with rallies, teach-ins and cleanups to demand action on pollution. Its success helped lead to new environmental laws and agencies. Earth Day became international in 1990 and is now celebrated each year on 22 April by hundreds of millions of people in nearly every country, including the Philippines."],
g:[["Pollutant","A substance that harms the environment"],["Smog","A mixture of smoke and fog or air pollution"],["Runoff","Water that flows over land into rivers, often carrying pollutants"],["Sewage","Waste water from homes and buildings"]],
f:["Air pollution is one of the world’s biggest health risks.","Leaded gasoline was phased out worldwide in 2021.","Earth Day is celebrated on 22 April."],
fun:"Light pollution hides the Milky Way from most people living in big cities.",
tl:"Ang polusyon ay paglalabas ng mapanganib na bagay sa hangin, tubig o lupa. May polusyon sa hangin, tubig, lupa, ingay at liwanag. Nabawasan ang maraming uri nito dahil sa mga batas at mas malinis na teknolohiya."});

ADDART({id:"deforestation",t:"Deforestation",c:"Geography",i:"🪵",w:"Deforestation",r:["trees","amazon","climchange","extinction","maathai"],
p:[["Losing forests","Deforestation is the permanent clearing of forests for other uses, such as farming."],["Millions of hectares","The world loses millions of hectares of forest each year, mostly in the tropics."],["Why it matters","Forests shelter wildlife, store carbon, protect soil and water and support people."]],
l:"Deforestation is the clearing of forests to convert the land to other uses, such as farms, pastures, plantations, mines, roads and towns. Forests cover about 31 percent of Earth’s land, but they have been shrinking for thousands of years, and the loss has accelerated in the tropics. Deforestation destroys habitats, drives species toward extinction, releases carbon dioxide that warms the climate and can cause floods, landslides and soil erosion. Many countries and communities are working to protect forests and restore them.",
s:[["Causes","Most tropical deforestation is driven by agriculture: cattle ranching, soybeans, oil palm, cocoa, coffee and small-scale farming. Logging, mining, roads, dams, fires and growing cities also play roles. Demand for products such as beef, palm oil and timber in distant countries can drive forest loss far away."],["Effects on wildlife","Tropical forests hold more than half of the world’s land species. When forests are cut, animals such as orangutans, tigers, jaguars and countless insects and plants lose their homes. Fragmented forests leave isolated populations that may not survive."],["Effects on climate","Trees store carbon in their wood, leaves and roots. When forests are burned or cleared, much of this carbon is released as carbon dioxide. Deforestation and other land-use changes account for a significant share of global greenhouse gas emissions. Forests also create rainfall and cool the land."],["Effects on people","Hundreds of millions of people depend on forests for food, fuel, medicine and income, including many Indigenous peoples. Deforestation can bring conflict over land. Without trees to hold soil and absorb rain, hillsides are more prone to landslides and floods."]],
k:[["Forest cover","About 31% of land"],["Annual forest loss","Around 10 million hectares (2015–2020)"],["Main driver","Agriculture"],["Green Belt Movement trees","More than 51 million"],["Philippine program","National Greening Program, 2011"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 years ago","Farming begins to clear forests"],["1977","Wangari Maathai founds the Green Belt Movement"],["1991","Floods in Ormoc, linked to deforestation, kill thousands"],["2004","Wangari Maathai wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2011","The Philippines launches the National Greening Program"],["2021","Over 140 countries pledge to halt forest loss by 2030"]],
s2:[["The Amazon","The Amazon rainforest, the world’s largest, has lost a significant share of its original area, mainly to cattle pastures and soybean farms. Scientists warn that if too much is lost, parts of the forest could dry out and turn into savanna, a tipping point. Brazil reduced deforestation sharply in the 2000s and again in recent years through enforcement and protected areas."],["Forests of the Philippines","Much of the Philippines was once covered by forest, but logging, farming and settlement cleared large areas in the 1900s, leaving a fraction of the original cover. In 1991 flash floods in Ormoc, Leyte, during a tropical storm killed thousands of people, a disaster linked to the loss of forests on surrounding slopes. The National Greening Program and community forestry aim to restore forests."],["Wangari Maathai","In 1977 the Kenyan biologist Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement, which helped women plant trees to restore land, provide firewood and improve their lives. The movement has planted more than 51 million trees. In 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize."],["Palm oil","Oil palm plantations in Indonesia and Malaysia have replaced large areas of rainforest, threatening orangutans and other wildlife. Palm oil is found in many foods and products. Certification schemes and laws in importing countries aim to ensure that palm oil is produced without destroying forests."],["Protecting forests","Ways to protect forests include creating protected areas, recognizing Indigenous land rights, which studies link to lower deforestation, enforcing laws against illegal logging, paying communities to conserve forests and using satellites to monitor forest loss in near real time."],["Planting and restoring","Reforestation replants trees where forests were lost. Successful projects use native tree species and involve local communities, who care for seedlings as they grow. Letting forests regrow naturally can also be effective. Planting the wrong trees in the wrong places, however, can harm grasslands and water supplies."]],
sp:["Watching forests from space","Since 2000, scientists have used satellite images to map forest loss across the entire planet. Global Forest Watch, launched in 2014, lets anyone see where trees have been cleared, often within days, and sends alerts to governments, rangers and communities. Indigenous patrols in the Amazon and Southeast Asia use these alerts on their phones to find and stop illegal logging."],
g:[["Deforestation","Permanent clearing of forests"],["Reforestation","Replanting trees where forests were lost"],["Habitat","The natural home of a plant or animal"],["Tipping point","A point beyond which a system changes dramatically"]],
f:["Forests cover about 31 percent of Earth’s land.","Agriculture is the main driver of deforestation.","Wangari Maathai’s movement planted over 51 million trees."],
fun:"Satellites can detect forest clearing within days, anywhere in the world.",
tl:"Ang deforestation ay ang pagputol ng mga gubat para gamitin ang lupa sa ibang bagay, gaya ng pagsasaka. Milyon-milyong ektarya ng gubat ang nawawala bawat taon. Mahalaga ang gubat sa mga hayop, sa klima at sa tao."});

ADDART({id:"plasticwaste",t:"Plastic Waste",c:"Geography",i:"🥤",w:"Plastic pollution",r:["plastics","pollution","pacific","seaturtle","trench"],
p:[["A modern problem","Hundreds of millions of tonnes of plastic are made every year, and much becomes waste."],["Lasting for centuries","Plastic does not rot; it breaks into tiny pieces called microplastics."],["In the ocean","Plastic waste reaches rivers and seas, harming marine life."]],
l:"Plastic waste is one of the most visible environmental problems of our time. The world produces more than 400 million tonnes of plastic each year, much of it for packaging used only once. Only a small share, less than a tenth historically, has been recycled; the rest is burned, buried in landfills or leaks into the environment. Plastic does not biodegrade but slowly breaks into microplastics that are now found everywhere, from mountain tops to the deepest ocean trenches and even inside human bodies.",
s:[["The rise of plastic","Plastic production grew rapidly after the 1950s because plastic is cheap, light, strong and versatile. It is used in packaging, clothing, cars, medical equipment and countless products. Global production has risen from about 2 million tonnes in 1950 to more than 400 million tonnes today."],["Where plastic goes","Much plastic waste is not collected or managed properly, especially where waste systems are weak. It blows into rivers and is carried to the sea. Studies estimate that millions of tonnes of plastic enter the oceans each year. Rivers in Asia, Africa and Latin America, often flowing through crowded cities, carry much of it."],["Harm to wildlife","Sea turtles mistake plastic bags for jellyfish, seabirds feed plastic pieces to their chicks and whales and dolphins become entangled in fishing nets. Many animals die with stomachs full of plastic. Plastic also carries toxic chemicals and can transport invasive species across oceans."],["Microplastics","Microplastics are pieces smaller than 5 millimeters, from broken-down larger items, synthetic clothing fibers and tire dust. They have been found in seawater, soil, drinking water, food, air, Arctic ice and human blood. Scientists are still studying their effects on health."]],
k:[["Annual production","More than 400 million tonnes"],["Share ever recycled","Less than 10%"],["Microplastic size","Smaller than 5 mm"],["First national plastic bag ban","Bangladesh, 2002"],["Philippine law","Extended Producer Responsibility Act, 2022"]],
d:[["1907","Bakelite, the first fully synthetic plastic, is invented"],["1950s","Mass production of plastic begins"],["1997","The Great Pacific Garbage Patch is noticed"],["2002","Bangladesh bans thin plastic bags"],["2022","The Philippines passes its Extended Producer Responsibility Act"],["2022","Countries begin negotiating a global plastics treaty"]],
s2:[["The Great Pacific Garbage Patch","In 1997 the sailor Charles Moore noticed vast amounts of plastic floating in the North Pacific between Hawaii and California. This area, where ocean currents collect floating debris, became known as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch. It is not a solid island but a huge region with high concentrations of plastic, much of it tiny pieces and fishing gear."],["Plastic in the Philippines","Studies have found that rivers in the Philippines, including the Pasig River, are among the largest sources of plastic flowing into the oceans, partly because of dense populations near rivers and the widespread use of single-use sachets. The Philippines has responded with local bans on plastic bags, waste segregation laws and the 2022 Extended Producer Responsibility Act, requiring large companies to recover their plastic packaging."],["Sachets","In many Asian countries, products such as shampoo, coffee and toothpaste are sold in small sachets, which make goods affordable for people on low daily incomes. But sachets are made of layered plastic that is very hard to recycle, so billions end up as waste. Companies are testing refill stations and recyclable packaging."],["Reduce, reuse, recycle","The best way to cut plastic waste is to use less, such as bringing reusable bags, bottles and containers. Reuse comes next, then recycling. Many plastics are hard to recycle, so reducing production and designing packaging for reuse are increasingly seen as key solutions."],["A global treaty","In 2022 the United Nations Environment Assembly agreed to negotiate a legally binding global treaty to end plastic pollution. Negotiating rounds were held in several countries, but as of 2025 countries had not agreed on a final text, disagreeing especially over whether to limit plastic production."],["Ocean cleanups","Volunteers around the world clean beaches and rivers, collecting and recording the waste they find. The International Coastal Cleanup, held each September, involves hundreds of thousands of people, including many in the Philippines. Devices that catch plastic in rivers before it reaches the sea are also being tested."]],
sp:["Plastic at the bottom of the sea","In 2019 the explorer Victor Vescovo dived nearly 11 kilometers to the bottom of the Mariana Trench, the deepest point in the ocean, and reported seeing what appeared to be a plastic bag. Scientists have also found microplastics in the stomachs of tiny animals living in the deepest trenches. These discoveries show that plastic pollution has reached even the most remote places on Earth."],
g:[["Microplastic","A plastic particle smaller than 5 mm"],["Single-use plastic","Plastic designed to be used once and thrown away"],["Biodegrade","To break down naturally by living organisms"],["Sachet","A small sealed plastic packet"]],
f:["Less than 10 percent of plastic has ever been recycled.","Microplastics have been found in human blood.","The Philippines passed an Extended Producer Responsibility Act in 2022."],
fun:"Plastic has been found at the bottom of the Mariana Trench, the deepest place in the ocean.",
tl:"Ang basurang plastik ay malaking problema ngayon. Daan-daang milyong tonelada ng plastik ang ginagawa bawat taon, at kaunti lamang ang nare-recycle. Hindi ito nabubulok kundi nagiging microplastic na matatagpuan na kahit saan."});

ADDART({id:"conservation",t:"Conservation",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌱",w:"Conservation biology",r:["extinction","eco","phbio","tubbataha","eagle"],
p:[["Protecting nature","Conservation is the protection of wildlife, habitats and natural resources for the future."],["Parks and reserves","Protected areas on land and sea help species survive."],["Everyone can help","Governments, scientists, communities and individuals all play a part."]],
l:"Conservation is the effort to protect and restore nature, including plants, animals, ecosystems and natural resources such as water, soil and forests. It aims to prevent extinctions, keep ecosystems healthy and ensure that people can benefit from nature in the future. Methods include creating protected areas, controlling hunting and trade, restoring habitats, breeding endangered species and working with local communities. In 2022 nearly 200 countries agreed to protect 30 percent of the world’s land and oceans by 2030.",
s:[["Protected areas","National parks, wildlife reserves and marine protected areas set aside land and water where nature is protected. Yellowstone, founded in 1872, was the first national park. Today protected areas cover about 17 percent of land and about 8 percent of the ocean. In the Philippines, they include Tubbataha Reefs, Mount Apo and the Puerto Princesa Underground River."],["Protecting species","Laws protect endangered species from hunting and collecting. The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species, CITES, which came into force in 1975, regulates trade in wildlife and wildlife products. Breeding programs in zoos and conservation centers have saved species such as the Arabian oryx and California condor from extinction."],["Working with communities","Conservation works best when local people benefit and take part. Community-managed forests, fisheries and marine sanctuaries, ecotourism and Indigenous land rights have proven effective in many places. Indigenous peoples manage large areas of land that are rich in biodiversity."],["Restoration","Restoration repairs damaged ecosystems by replanting forests and mangroves, restoring wetlands, removing invasive species and reintroducing animals. Removing dams has allowed fish to return to rivers, and wolves reintroduced to Yellowstone helped its ecosystem recover."]],
k:[["First national park","Yellowstone, 1872"],["Wildlife trade treaty","CITES, in force 1975"],["Global target","Protect 30% of land and sea by 2030"],["Land protected","About 17%"],["Ocean protected","About 8%"]],
d:[["1872","Yellowstone becomes the first national park"],["1948","The International Union for Conservation of Nature is founded"],["1961","The World Wildlife Fund is founded"],["1975","CITES comes into force"],["1982","Apo Island’s community marine sanctuary is set up"],["2022","Countries agree to protect 30% of land and sea by 2030"]],
s2:[["Apo Island","In 1982, with help from marine biologist Angel Alcala of Silliman University, the fishing community of Apo Island, off Negros Oriental, set aside part of its reef as a no-fishing sanctuary. Fish populations inside grew, and fish spilled over into nearby fishing areas, increasing catches. Apo Island became a model for thousands of community-managed marine protected areas across the Philippines and the world."],["Saving the Philippine eagle","The Philippine eagle, one of the world’s largest and rarest eagles, is critically endangered by forest loss and hunting. The Philippine Eagle Foundation in Davao breeds eagles in captivity and works with Indigenous communities to protect forest nests. In 1992 it hatched Pag-asa, the first Philippine eagle bred in captivity."],["The panda logo","The World Wildlife Fund, now called the World Wide Fund for Nature in many countries, was founded in 1961 and chose the giant panda as its logo. Pandas themselves have become a conservation success: in 2016 they were moved from endangered to vulnerable after their numbers increased thanks to forest protection."],["Seed banks","Seed banks store seeds to protect plant diversity. The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, built inside a mountain in the Arctic and opened in 2008, holds more than a million seed samples from around the world as a backup for humanity’s crops. The International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños also keeps a large rice gene bank."],["Conservation challenges","Conservation faces challenges such as limited funding, poaching, illegal logging, conflicts between people and wildlife and climate change, which may push species out of protected areas. Balancing the needs of people and nature requires careful planning and cooperation."],["What you can do","People can support conservation by learning about local wildlife, avoiding products made from endangered species, reducing waste and energy use, planting native trees, joining cleanups and citizen science projects, and visiting nature responsibly."]],
sp:["The return of the Arabian oryx","The Arabian oryx, a white antelope with long straight horns, was hunted to extinction in the wild by 1972. A few survived in zoos, and a breeding program called Operation Oryx raised a herd. From 1982, oryx were released back into the deserts of Oman and later other countries. In 2011 it became the first animal to move from extinct in the wild back to vulnerable on the IUCN Red List."],
g:[["Conservation","Protection and wise use of nature"],["Protected area","Land or sea set aside to protect nature"],["Marine sanctuary","A sea area where fishing is restricted"],["Reintroduction","Returning a species to the wild"]],
f:["Yellowstone was the first national park.","Apo Island’s sanctuary became a model for the world.","Countries aim to protect 30% of land and sea by 2030."],
fun:"The Arabian oryx went from extinct in the wild back to vulnerable thanks to conservation.",
tl:"Ang konserbasyon ay pagprotekta sa mga hayop, halaman at likas na yaman para sa hinaharap. Kabilang dito ang mga protektadong lugar gaya ng Tubbataha at Apo Island. Lahat ay maaaring tumulong."});

;
/* data/animals9.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 9: fishes, corals, monkeys, horses and dogs.
ADDART({id:"fishes",t:"Fishes",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Fish",r:["shark","wshark","seahorse","fish","coral"],
p:[["Life in water","Fishes are cold-blooded animals with backbones that live in water and breathe with gills."],["Most diverse vertebrates","There are more species of fish than of all other vertebrates combined."],["From tiny to giant","Fishes range from gobies about a centimeter long to the whale shark, the largest fish."]],
l:"Fishes are aquatic vertebrates, animals with backbones, that typically breathe through gills, swim with fins and have scales. With more than 35,000 known species, they make up about half of all vertebrate species. Fishes live in almost every watery habitat, from mountain streams and tropical reefs to the freezing waters of Antarctica and the deepest ocean trenches. They were the first vertebrates, appearing more than 500 million years ago, and are a vital source of food for billions of people.",
s:[["Kinds of fishes","Scientists group fishes into three main kinds. Jawless fishes, such as lampreys and hagfish, are the most ancient. Cartilaginous fishes, such as sharks, rays and chimaeras, have skeletons of flexible cartilage. Bony fishes, by far the largest group, have skeletons of bone and include almost all familiar fish, from tuna to goldfish."],["How fishes breathe","Water flows into a fish’s mouth and over its gills, feathery organs full of blood vessels that absorb oxygen dissolved in the water. Some fishes can also breathe air: lungfish have lungs, and some catfish and gouramis gulp air at the surface."],["Senses and swimming","Fishes swim by moving their bodies and tails, steering with their fins. Many bony fishes have a gas-filled swim bladder that helps them float at the right depth. Along their sides runs the lateral line, a sense organ that detects movement and vibrations in the water, helping schools of fish move together."],["Fishes and people","Fish provide an important source of protein for billions of people. In the Philippines, fish such as bangus, or milkfish, tilapia, galunggong and tuna are everyday foods. Overfishing, pollution and habitat loss threaten many fish populations, making sustainable fishing and aquaculture important."]],
k:[["Known species","More than 35,000"],["Largest fish","Whale shark"],["Among the smallest","Philippine dwarf goby, about 1 cm"],["Breathing organs","Gills"],["First appeared","More than 500 million years ago"]],
d:[["c. 530 million years ago","The first fish-like vertebrates appear"],["c. 375 million years ago","Tiktaalik, a fish with limb-like fins, lives"],["1938","A living coelacanth is discovered off South Africa"],["1998","A second coelacanth species is found in Indonesia"],["2000s","Deep-sea snailfish are filmed in ocean trenches"],["2023","A snailfish is filmed at over 8,300 m"]],
s2:[["Tiny fishes of the Philippines","The Philippines is home to some of the world’s smallest fishes. The dwarf pygmy goby, Pandaka pygmaea, is only about a centimeter long when fully grown. The sinarapan, found in Lake Buhi in Camarines Sur, is among the smallest commercially harvested fish, so tiny that thousands are needed to make a meal."],["Bangus","The milkfish, called bangus in the Philippines, is often regarded as the national fish. It has been farmed in brackish-water ponds in Southeast Asia for centuries. Bangus is famous for its many bones, and deboned bangus is a popular product. Dagupan in Pangasinan celebrates a bangus festival."],["Amazing adaptations","Flying fish leap from the water and glide on wing-like fins for up to 200 meters to escape predators. Electric eels can produce shocks of over 800 volts. Anglerfish lure prey in the dark with glowing lights, and some Antarctic fishes have natural antifreeze in their blood."],["Clownfish and anemones","Clownfish live among the stinging tentacles of sea anemones, protected by a special mucus on their skin. The anemone protects the clownfish from predators, and the clownfish cleans the anemone and may scare away animals that eat it. This partnership is an example of mutualism. The Philippines’ reefs are home to several clownfish species."],["Salmon journeys","Salmon hatch in rivers, swim to the sea to grow and then return, sometimes thousands of kilometers, to the same stream where they were born to spawn, finding it partly by smell. Their journeys bring nutrients from the ocean to forests, as bears and eagles eat them."],["From fins to legs","About 375 million years ago, some lobe-finned fishes began to venture into shallow water and onto land. Tiktaalik, discovered in the Canadian Arctic in 2004, had fins with wrist-like bones and could prop itself up. All land vertebrates, including humans, descend from such fishes."]],
sp:["The living fossil","The coelacanth was known only from fossils and thought to have died out about 66 million years ago. In December 1938, a museum curator in South Africa, Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer, spotted a strange blue fish with fleshy, limb-like fins in a fisherman’s catch. It was a living coelacanth. A second species was found in Indonesia in 1998. Coelacanths live in deep, dark waters and can live for about 100 years."],
g:[["Vertebrate","An animal with a backbone"],["Gill","An organ for breathing oxygen from water"],["Swim bladder","A gas-filled organ that helps fish float"],["Lateral line","A sense organ that detects movement in water"]],
f:["There are more than 35,000 species of fish.","Bangus is often called the national fish of the Philippines.","The coelacanth was rediscovered in 1938."],
fun:"The Philippine dwarf goby is one of the smallest fish in the world, about a centimeter long.",
tl:"Ang isda ay hayop na may gulugod na nabubuhay sa tubig at humihinga gamit ang hasang. Mas marami ang uri ng isda kaysa sa lahat ng iba pang hayop na may gulugod. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang bangus at ang napakaliit na sinarapan."});

ADDART({id:"coral",t:"Coral Reefs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪸",w:"Coral reef",r:["reef","tubbataha","fishes","climchange","jelly"],
p:[["Animals, not plants","Corals are tiny animals related to jellyfish that build reefs from limestone."],["Rainforests of the sea","Coral reefs cover less than 1 percent of the ocean floor but support about a quarter of marine species."],["Richest reefs","The Philippines lies in the Coral Triangle, the world’s richest area of coral reefs."]],
l:"Coral reefs are underwater structures built by colonies of tiny animals called coral polyps, which secrete skeletons of calcium carbonate, or limestone. Over thousands of years, these skeletons build up into massive reefs. Reef-building corals live in warm, clear, shallow tropical waters and depend on tiny algae living inside their tissues. Coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems on Earth, home to fish, turtles, sharks and countless other creatures. They protect coasts and support fishing and tourism, but are seriously threatened by warming seas.",
s:[["What corals are","A coral polyp is a small, soft animal with a mouth surrounded by stinging tentacles, related to jellyfish and sea anemones. Hard corals build limestone skeletons, and thousands of polyps live together in a colony. Soft corals, such as sea fans, do not build reefs. Some corals feed by catching plankton with their tentacles at night."],["Partners with algae","Reef-building corals have tiny algae called zooxanthellae living inside them. The algae use sunlight to make food through photosynthesis, sharing it with the coral, while the coral provides shelter and nutrients. The algae also give corals much of their color. This partnership is why reefs grow in clear, sunlit water."],["Types of reefs","Fringing reefs grow close to shore. Barrier reefs are separated from the coast by a lagoon, like the Great Barrier Reef of Australia. Atolls are rings of coral around a lagoon, often formed on the rim of a sunken volcano, as Charles Darwin proposed in 1842."],["Why reefs matter","Coral reefs support about a quarter of all marine species, provide food and income for hundreds of millions of people and protect coastlines by breaking waves during storms. They attract divers and tourists and are sources of new medicines."]],
k:[["Builders","Coral polyps"],["Share of ocean floor","Less than 1%"],["Share of marine species","About 25%"],["Largest reef system","Great Barrier Reef, Australia"],["Richest region","The Coral Triangle"]],
d:[["1842","Darwin publishes his theory of how atolls form"],["1981","The Great Barrier Reef becomes a World Heritage Site"],["1993","Tubbataha Reefs becomes a World Heritage Site"],["1998","The first recorded global coral bleaching event"],["2009","Six countries launch the Coral Triangle Initiative"],["2024","Scientists confirm a fourth global bleaching event"]],
s2:[["The Coral Triangle","The Coral Triangle, spanning the Philippines, Indonesia, Malaysia, Papua New Guinea, Timor-Leste and the Solomon Islands, contains about three-quarters of the world’s coral species and more than 2,000 species of reef fish. In 2009 these six countries launched the Coral Triangle Initiative to protect its reefs, fisheries and food security."],["The center of the center","Scientists have called the Verde Island Passage, between Batangas and Mindoro in the Philippines, the center of the center of marine shore fish biodiversity, because it has an exceptionally high number of species in a small area. Anilao in Batangas is a famous diving destination for its colorful marine life."],["Coral bleaching","When water becomes too warm, corals expel their algae and turn white, a process called bleaching. If the heat lasts too long, the corals starve and die. Global bleaching events struck in 1998, 2010, 2014 to 2017 and from 2023, when scientists confirmed a fourth global event affecting reefs in dozens of countries."],["Other threats","Reefs are also harmed by pollution, sediment from deforestation, overfishing, destructive fishing using dynamite or cyanide, coastal development, careless anchoring and diving, and ocean acidification, as the sea absorbs carbon dioxide and makes it harder for corals to build skeletons."],["Saving reefs","Marine protected areas, such as Tubbataha Reefs and Apo Island in the Philippines, allow reefs and fish to recover. Scientists grow coral fragments in nurseries and plant them on damaged reefs, and some are studying heat-tolerant corals. Reducing greenhouse gas emissions is the most important step for reefs’ long-term survival."],["Spawning","On a few nights each year, often after a full moon, many corals on a reef release eggs and sperm at the same time in a mass spawning event that looks like an underwater snowstorm. This timing increases the chances that eggs will be fertilized. The larvae drift before settling to start new colonies."]],
sp:["An underwater snowstorm","On certain nights in spring or summer, after the full moon, corals across a reef release billions of tiny pink, white and orange bundles of eggs and sperm at once. Divers who witness it describe it as an upside-down snowstorm. Scientists still do not fully understand how corals coordinate this timing, though moonlight, water temperature and the length of the day all seem to play a part."],
g:[["Polyp","A small coral animal with tentacles"],["Zooxanthellae","Algae living inside coral tissues"],["Bleaching","Corals turning white after losing their algae"],["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral reef around a lagoon"]],
f:["Corals are animals, not plants.","Coral reefs support about a quarter of marine species.","The Philippines lies in the Coral Triangle."],
fun:"Scientists call the Verde Island Passage the center of the center of marine fish diversity.",
tl:"Ang coral reef ay itinatayo ng maliliit na hayop na tinatawag na coral polyp. Bagama't sakop lamang nito ang wala pang 1 porsiyento ng sahig ng dagat, tirahan ito ng mga isang-kapat ng uri ng buhay sa dagat. Nasa Coral Triangle ang Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"monkeys",t:"Monkeys",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐒",w:"Monkey",r:["gorilla","tarsier","amazon","borneo","humanorigins"],
p:[["Clever primates","Monkeys are intelligent, social primates found in tropical forests, savannas and mountains."],["Old World and New World","Monkeys are divided into Old World monkeys of Africa and Asia and New World monkeys of the Americas."],["Not apes","Monkeys usually have tails; apes, such as gorillas and chimpanzees, do not."]],
l:"Monkeys are primates, the group of mammals that also includes apes, lemurs and humans. There are more than 260 species of monkeys, living mainly in tropical regions of Africa, Asia and Central and South America. Most live in trees and in social groups, and many are highly intelligent, using tools and communicating with calls and gestures. Monkeys range from the tiny pygmy marmoset, weighing about 100 grams, to the mandrill, the largest. Many monkey species are threatened by deforestation, hunting and the pet trade.",
s:[["Old World monkeys","Old World monkeys live in Africa and Asia. They include macaques, baboons, mandrills, colobus monkeys and langurs. Their nostrils are close together and point downward, and their tails, if they have them, cannot grip. Many have cheek pouches for storing food and tough sitting pads on their bottoms."],["New World monkeys","New World monkeys live in Central and South America. They include capuchins, howler monkeys, spider monkeys, squirrel monkeys, marmosets and tamarins. They have wide, flat noses with outward-facing nostrils, and many have prehensile tails that can grip branches like a fifth hand."],["Monkeys or apes?","Apes, which include gibbons, orangutans, gorillas, chimpanzees and bonobos, are a separate group of primates. Apes have no tails, are generally larger and have more flexible shoulders, allowing them to swing from branch to branch. Humans are more closely related to apes than to monkeys."],["Social life","Most monkeys live in groups, from small families to troops of hundreds. Group members groom each other, picking through fur to remove dirt and parasites, which builds friendships. Monkeys communicate with calls, facial expressions and body language; vervet monkeys have different alarm calls for eagles, leopards and snakes."]],
k:[["Species","More than 260"],["Smallest","Pygmy marmoset, about 100 g"],["Largest","Mandrill"],["Loudest","Howler monkey"],["Philippine monkey","Long-tailed macaque"]],
d:[["c. 40 million years ago","Ancestors of New World monkeys reach South America from Africa"],["c. 25–30 million years ago","The lines leading to Old World monkeys and apes split"],["1949","A rhesus monkey named Albert II reaches space"],["1960s","Japanese scientists study macaques washing sweet potatoes"],["2000s","New monkey species continue to be discovered in the Amazon"],["2018","The first primates are cloned from body cells in China"]],
s2:[["Monkeys of the Philippines","The Philippine long-tailed macaque, a subspecies of the crab-eating macaque, is the only monkey native to the Philippines, found on many islands, including Luzon, Mindanao and Palawan. It lives in forests and mangroves and sometimes raids crops. The Philippine tarsier, though a primate, is not a monkey."],["Snow monkeys","Japanese macaques live farther north than any other monkey, in mountains where winter snow can be deep. At Jigokudani, in the Japanese Alps, some troops soak in natural hot springs to keep warm, a famous sight. Japanese macaques have been studied for decades, revealing learned traditions passed among troop members."],["The sweet potato washers","In the 1950s, on the Japanese island of Kōjima, scientists gave macaques sweet potatoes on the beach. A young female named Imo began washing the sand off in water, and the habit spread to others in her troop, who later began dipping potatoes in the sea, perhaps for the salty taste. It became a famous example of culture among animals."],["Tool users","Capuchin monkeys in Brazil use stones as hammers and anvils to crack open hard nuts, and archaeologists have found that they have done so for at least 3,000 years. Long-tailed macaques in Thailand use stones to open shellfish. These behaviors show remarkable intelligence and learning."],["Howler monkeys","Howler monkeys of Central and South America are among the loudest land animals; their deep, roaring calls, made using an enlarged throat bone, can be heard several kilometers away through the forest. Troops howl at dawn and dusk to announce their territory."],["Threats","About half of all primate species are threatened with extinction because of deforestation, hunting for bushmeat and the illegal pet trade. Monkeys kept as pets often suffer and can spread diseases. Protecting forests and enforcing wildlife laws are key to their survival."]],
sp:["The proboscis monkey","The proboscis monkey lives only on the island of Borneo, in mangroves and riverside forests. Adult males have huge, drooping noses that may help amplify their honking calls and attract females. Proboscis monkeys are excellent swimmers, with partly webbed feet, and sometimes leap from trees into rivers. They eat mostly leaves and unripe fruit, which they digest with a special stomach, giving them round pot bellies."],
g:[["Primate","A mammal group including monkeys, apes and humans"],["Prehensile tail","A tail that can grip"],["Troop","A group of monkeys"],["Grooming","Cleaning another animal’s fur"]],
f:["Monkeys usually have tails, but apes do not.","The pygmy marmoset is the smallest monkey.","The long-tailed macaque is the only monkey native to the Philippines."],
fun:"Some Japanese macaques bathe in hot springs to stay warm in winter.",
tl:"Ang unggoy ay matalino at sosyal na primate na matatagpuan sa mga gubat ng Aprika, Asya at Amerika. Karaniwang may buntot ang unggoy, hindi tulad ng mga ape. Ang long-tailed macaque ang tanging katutubong unggoy sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"horses",t:"Horses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐎",w:"Horse",r:["domestication","mongol","evo","grasslands","olymp"],
p:[["Partners of people","Horses have carried people, pulled loads and worked farms for thousands of years."],["Built for speed","Horses can gallop at around 50 to 70 kilometers per hour."],["Changing history","Horses transformed travel, farming, trade and warfare across the world."]],
l:"The horse is a large hoofed mammal that has been domesticated for thousands of years. Horses transformed human history, allowing people to travel farther and faster, carry goods, plow fields and fight wars on horseback. Today there are tens of millions of horses worldwide, used for riding, sport, farm work and companionship. Horses are herd animals, intelligent and sensitive, and communicate with body language and sounds. The only truly wild horse surviving today is Przewalski’s horse of Central Asia.",
s:[["Evolution","The ancestors of horses were small, forest-dwelling animals, such as Eohippus, about the size of a fox, which lived about 55 million years ago and had several toes on each foot. As grasslands spread, horses evolved larger bodies, longer legs and single hooves for running, and teeth for grinding tough grasses."],["Domestication","Genetic studies published in 2021 suggest that modern domestic horses descend from horses first domesticated on the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas around 4,200 years ago. They spread rapidly across Asia and Europe along with spoked-wheel chariots. Horses had been hunted, and some kept, earlier."],["Body and senses","Horses have the largest eyes of any land mammal and can see almost all the way around them. They can sleep standing up, thanks to a system of tendons and ligaments that locks their legs. Their hooves are made of keratin, like our fingernails, and are often protected with horseshoes."],["Breeds and uses","There are hundreds of horse breeds, from small, sturdy Shetland ponies to giant draft horses such as Shires and fast Thoroughbred racehorses. Arabian horses, from the Arabian Peninsula, are famous for their endurance and beauty. Horses are used in racing, show jumping, dressage, polo, rodeos and therapy."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Equus caballus"],["Domesticated","About 4,200 years ago (steppe)"],["Top speed","About 70 km/h"],["Only wild horse","Przewalski’s horse"],["Height measured in","Hands (1 hand = 10.16 cm)"]],
d:[["c. 55 million years ago","Eohippus, an early horse ancestor, lives"],["c. 2200 BCE","Horses are domesticated on the western Eurasian steppe"],["1206","Genghis Khan’s mounted armies begin building the Mongol Empire"],["c. 1500s","Spanish colonizers bring horses to the Americas"],["1912","Equestrian events are added to the modern Olympics"],["1990s","Przewalski’s horses are reintroduced to Mongolia"]],
s2:[["Horses and history","Mounted warriors and chariots changed warfare, allowing empires to expand. The Mongols, whose soldiers could ride for days with several horses each, built the largest land empire in history. Horses carried messengers, pulled stagecoaches and plows, and powered much of transport until cars and tractors replaced them in the 1900s."],["Horses in the Americas","Horses evolved in North America but died out there around 10,000 years ago. Spanish colonizers brought them back in the 1500s. Many Indigenous peoples of the Great Plains, such as the Comanche and Lakota, quickly became skilled riders, transforming their ways of hunting bison and traveling."],["Przewalski’s horse","Przewalski’s horse, a stocky wild horse of the Central Asian steppes, became extinct in the wild by the 1960s. A few survived in zoos, and breeding programs saved the species. Since the 1990s, herds have been released into Mongolia, Kazakhstan and China, where they again roam free."],["Horses in the Philippines","Horses were brought to the Philippines during the Spanish era. Horse-drawn carriages called kalesa and tartanilla still carry passengers in places such as Vigan and Cebu. Small, hardy native horses were once widely used for farm work and transport in the countryside."],["Wild horses today","Many horses that roam free, such as the mustangs of the American West and the brumbies of Australia, are feral: descended from domestic horses that escaped. Managing their numbers is often debated, as they can damage fragile habitats."],["How horses communicate","Horses communicate with their ears, eyes, tails and posture, as well as with neighs, whinnies, snorts and nickers. Pinned-back ears signal anger, while relaxed ears and a lowered head show calm. Horses form strong bonds within herds and with people who care for them."]],
sp:["Measuring in hands","Horses are traditionally measured in hands, from the ground to the top of the shoulders, called the withers. One hand equals 4 inches, about 10.16 centimeters, based on the width of a human hand. A horse that is 15.2 hands is 15 hands and 2 inches tall. Animals under 14.2 hands are usually called ponies. The world’s smallest horses are just a few hands tall, while large draft horses can exceed 18 hands."],
g:[["Domestication","Taming and breeding animals for human use"],["Hoof","The hard foot of a horse"],["Feral","Living wild after escaping from domestication"],["Withers","The ridge between a horse’s shoulder blades"]],
f:["Horses can sleep standing up.","Przewalski’s horse is the only truly wild horse.","Horses are measured in hands."],
fun:"Horses have the largest eyes of any land mammal.",
tl:"Ang kabayo ay malaking hayop na may kuko na pinaamo ng tao libu-libong taon na ang nakararaan. Binago nito ang paglalakbay, pagsasaka at digmaan. Sa Pilipinas, ginagamit pa rin ang kalesa sa Vigan."});

ADDART({id:"dogs",t:"Dogs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐕",w:"Dog",r:["wolf","domestication","cats","immune","smelltaste"],
p:[["Our oldest friends","Dogs were the first animals domesticated by humans, descended from wolves."],["Hundreds of breeds","People have bred dogs into hundreds of breeds, from tiny Chihuahuas to giant Great Danes."],["Super sniffers","A dog’s sense of smell is many thousands of times stronger than ours."]],
l:"The dog is a domesticated mammal descended from wolves and the first animal tamed by humans, at least 15,000 years ago and possibly much earlier. Dogs have lived alongside people as hunting partners, guards, herders, helpers and beloved companions. There are hundreds of millions of dogs in the world, and people have bred hundreds of breeds of many shapes and sizes. Dogs are loyal, social and intelligent, able to read human gestures and emotions, and they work as guide dogs, search-and-rescue dogs and detection dogs.",
s:[["From wolf to dog","Dogs descend from an ancient population of gray wolves. Wolves that were less fearful of people may have scavenged near human camps and gradually became tame. Over generations, they changed in appearance and behavior, becoming dogs. Scientists debate exactly when and where this happened, with estimates ranging from about 15,000 to more than 20,000 years ago."],["Breeds","Over thousands of years, people bred dogs for particular jobs, such as herding, hunting, guarding and pulling sleds, creating a huge variety of breeds. Major kennel organizations recognize several hundred breeds. Many dogs worldwide, however, are mixed-breed or village dogs, such as the aspin, the native dog of the Philippines."],["Senses","Dogs’ sense of smell is extraordinary, with hundreds of millions of scent receptors compared with about 6 million in humans. They can detect tiny traces of odors, which is why they are trained to find drugs, explosives, missing people and even some diseases. Dogs also hear higher-pitched sounds than people."],["Dogs and people","Dogs understand pointing, follow human gaze and respond to tone of voice and facial expressions. Studies show that when dogs and their owners gaze at each other, both release oxytocin, a hormone linked to bonding. Owning a dog can encourage exercise and reduce loneliness."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Canis familiaris"],["Ancestor","Gray wolf"],["Domesticated","At least 15,000 years ago"],["Recognized breeds","Several hundred"],["Philippine native dog","Aspin"]],
d:[["c. 15,000 years ago","Dogs are domesticated from wolves"],["c. 14,000 years ago","A dog is buried with humans at Bonn-Oberkassel, Germany"],["1873","The first kennel club is founded in Britain"],["1925","Sled dogs carry medicine to Nome, Alaska"],["1957","Laika becomes the first animal to orbit Earth"],["2007","The Philippines passes its Anti-Rabies Act"]],
s2:[["Kabang the hero dog","In 2011, in Zamboanga City, a dog named Kabang leaped in front of a speeding motorcycle to save two girls, her owner’s daughter and niece. She survived but lost her snout and upper jaw. Her story spread around the world, and donations paid for her treatment in the United States. She became a symbol of loyalty and bravery and died in 2021."],["Working dogs","Dogs work in many roles: guide dogs lead people who are blind, hearing dogs alert people who are deaf, herding dogs manage sheep, police and military dogs track suspects and sniff for explosives, and therapy dogs comfort patients. Search-and-rescue dogs have found survivors after earthquakes and collapsed buildings."],["Laika","On 3 November 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 2, carrying a dog named Laika, a stray from the streets of Moscow. She became the first animal to orbit Earth but died within hours from overheating, as there was no plan to return her. Her flight helped prepare for human spaceflight and is remembered with sadness."],["Rabies","Rabies is a deadly viral disease spread mainly through bites from infected dogs. It kills tens of thousands of people each year worldwide, mostly in Asia and Africa, including in the Philippines. Vaccinating dogs and getting prompt treatment after a bite can prevent it. The Philippines’ Anti-Rabies Act of 2007 requires dog vaccination and registration."],["The serum run","In January 1925, an outbreak of diphtheria threatened the town of Nome in Alaska, cut off by winter. Teams of sled dogs relayed medicine about 1,085 kilometers through blizzards in about five and a half days. The lead dogs, Balto and Togo, became famous, and the event inspired the Iditarod sled race."],["Caring for dogs","Responsible owners give dogs good food, clean water, exercise, training, veterinary care and vaccinations, and spay or neuter them to prevent unwanted puppies. Animal welfare groups rescue stray dogs and encourage adoption. Dogs need love and attention, as they are deeply social animals."]],
sp:["Hachikō, the loyal dog","Hachikō, an Akita dog in Tokyo, used to meet his owner, Professor Hidesaburō Ueno, at Shibuya Station every evening. In 1925 the professor died suddenly at work and never returned. For nearly ten years, Hachikō went to the station every day at the time the train arrived, waiting for him. He became a national symbol of loyalty in Japan, and a bronze statue of him stands outside Shibuya Station, a popular meeting place."],
g:[["Domesticated","Tamed and bred by humans"],["Breed","A group of animals with similar traits bred by people"],["Aspin","A native mixed-breed dog of the Philippines"],["Rabies","A deadly viral disease spread by animal bites"]],
f:["Dogs descend from wolves.","Dogs have hundreds of millions of scent receptors.","Laika was the first animal to orbit Earth."],
fun:"Hachikō waited at a Tokyo train station for his owner every day for nearly ten years.",
tl:"Ang aso ang unang hayop na pinaamo ng tao, nagmula sa mga lobo. Daan-daan ang lahi ng aso. Napakalakas ng pang-amoy nila. Ang aspin ang katutubong aso ng Pilipinas, at kilala si Kabang ng Zamboanga sa kanyang katapangan."});

;
/* data/animals10.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 10: cats, rodents, domestication, animal behavior and food chains.
ADDART({id:"cats",t:"Cats",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐈",w:"Cat",r:["dogs","domestication","lion","tiger","egypt"],
p:[["Small hunters","The domestic cat is a small carnivore that has lived alongside people for about 10,000 years."],["Loved worldwide","Cats are among the world’s most popular pets."],["Wild relatives","Domestic cats belong to the cat family, along with lions, tigers and leopards."]],
l:"The domestic cat is a small, carnivorous mammal that has lived with humans for thousands of years. Cats probably began living near people in the Near East about 10,000 years ago, when early farmers stored grain that attracted mice, and wildcats came to hunt them. Today hundreds of millions of cats live around the world as pets, farm cats and strays. Cats are agile hunters with sharp senses, admired for their independence, grace and playful personalities. They belong to the cat family, Felidae, which includes about 40 species.",
s:[["Origins","Domestic cats descend from the African wildcat of the Near East and North Africa. Unlike dogs, cats largely domesticated themselves, choosing to live near people for the abundant prey around farms. A cat buried with a person in Cyprus about 9,500 years ago is among the earliest evidence of the close bond between cats and humans."],["Built to hunt","Cats have sharp retractable claws, keen hearing that can detect the high squeaks of mice, whiskers that sense air movements and eyes that see well in dim light. A reflective layer behind the retina makes their eyes shine at night. They hunt by stalking and pouncing."],["Behavior","Cats communicate by meowing, purring, hissing and body language, such as tail position and slow blinks. Adult cats rarely meow to each other; meowing is mostly directed at people. Cats groom themselves for hours and sleep for about 12 to 16 hours a day."],["Breeds","There are dozens of recognized cat breeds, such as the Persian, Siamese, Maine Coon and Sphynx, though most cats are mixed-breed. In the Philippines, the native street cat is affectionately called puspin, from pusang Pinoy. The Maine Coon is one of the largest domestic breeds."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Felis catus"],["Ancestor","African wildcat"],["Domestication began","About 10,000 years ago, Near East"],["Sleep","About 12–16 hours a day"],["Philippine street cat","Puspin"]],
d:[["c. 7500 BCE","A cat is buried with a person in Cyprus"],["c. 2000 BCE","Cats are common in ancient Egyptian homes"],["c. 950 BCE","The cat goddess Bastet is widely worshipped in Egypt"],["1871","The first cat show is held in London"],["1963","Félicette becomes the first cat in space"],["2004","Scientists report the Cyprus cat burial"]],
s2:[["Cats in ancient Egypt","Ancient Egyptians valued cats for protecting grain stores from rodents and snakes. They were associated with the goddess Bastet, and killing a cat could be punished severely. Many cats were mummified and buried in special cemeteries; archaeologists have found hundreds of thousands of cat mummies."],["Why cats purr","Cats purr when they are content, but also when they are stressed, injured or giving birth. The sound, made by rapid movements of muscles in the voice box, has frequencies of about 25 to 150 vibrations per second. Some scientists suggest purring may help cats heal and calm themselves."],["Landing on their feet","When cats fall, they twist their bodies in midair to land on their feet, a skill called the righting reflex, which develops when kittens are a few weeks old. Their flexible spines and lack of a fixed collarbone help. Still, falls from high windows can seriously injure them."],["Cats and wildlife","Pet and stray cats that roam outdoors kill huge numbers of birds, small mammals and reptiles. On islands, introduced cats have driven some species to extinction. Keeping cats indoors at night or in enclosures and neutering strays help protect wildlife."],["Cats in culture","Cats appear in myths, stories and art worldwide. In Japan, the maneki-neko, a beckoning cat figurine, is believed to bring good luck to shops. Cats starred in early internet videos and remain among the most popular subjects online."],["Caring for cats","Cats need balanced food, fresh water, a clean litter box, scratching posts, play and veterinary care, including vaccinations. Spaying and neutering prevent unwanted kittens. Community programs in many cities, including in the Philippines, trap, neuter and return stray cats."]],
sp:["Félicette, the space cat","On 18 October 1963, France launched a rocket carrying a cat named Félicette, a stray from Paris, on a 15-minute flight about 157 kilometers high, beyond the edge of space. Electrodes recorded her brain activity. She returned safely by parachute, becoming the first and only cat to fly to space. In 2019 a bronze statue of her was unveiled in Strasbourg."],
g:[["Carnivore","An animal that eats mainly meat"],["Retractable claws","Claws that can be pulled in"],["Felidae","The cat family"],["Righting reflex","A cat’s ability to land on its feet"]],
f:["Cats have lived with people for about 10,000 years.","Cats sleep about 12 to 16 hours a day.","Félicette was the first cat in space."],
fun:"Adult cats rarely meow to each other; they meow mostly to people.",
tl:"Ang pusa ay maliit na hayop na kumakain ng karne at mga 10,000 taon nang namumuhay kasama ng tao. Isa ito sa pinakapopular na alagang hayop. Ang puspin ang katutubong pusa ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"rodents",t:"Rodents",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐀",w:"Rodent",r:["phbio","microbe","blackdeath","eco","cats"],
p:[["The biggest mammal group","Rodents make up about 40 percent of all mammal species."],["Ever-growing teeth","Their front teeth never stop growing, so they must gnaw to keep them short."],["From mice to capybaras","Rodents include mice, rats, squirrels, beavers, porcupines and capybaras."]],
l:"Rodents are the largest group of mammals, with about 2,500 species, roughly two out of every five mammal species. They include mice, rats, squirrels, hamsters, guinea pigs, beavers, porcupines, chipmunks and capybaras. All rodents have a pair of sharp front teeth, or incisors, in each jaw that grow throughout their lives. Rodents live on every continent except Antarctica and play important roles in ecosystems as seed spreaders and prey. Some spread diseases and damage crops, while others are pets and vital in medical research.",
s:[["Teeth for gnawing","The name rodent comes from the Latin rodere, to gnaw. Rodents’ incisors have hard enamel on the front and softer dentine behind, so gnawing wears them into sharp chisel edges. Because they never stop growing, rodents must gnaw constantly; otherwise, their teeth would grow too long."],["Variety","The smallest rodents, such as pygmy jerboas, weigh just a few grams, while the capybara of South America, the largest, can weigh more than 60 kilograms. Rodents live underground, in trees, in deserts, in rivers and in cities. Flying squirrels glide between trees, and beavers build dams."],["Rodents in ecosystems","Rodents eat seeds, nuts, roots, insects and plants. Squirrels and other rodents bury seeds and nuts and forget some, helping trees grow. Rodents are food for owls, snakes, cats, foxes and many other predators, making them key links in food chains."],["Rodents and people","Rats and mice can damage crops and stored food and spread diseases, such as leptospirosis, spread through water contaminated by rat urine, which is common in the Philippines after floods. Historically, fleas on rats helped spread plague. Mice and rats are also widely used in medical research, and hamsters, guinea pigs and gerbils are popular pets."]],
k:[["Species","About 2,500"],["Share of mammal species","About 40%"],["Largest","Capybara"],["Smallest","Pygmy jerboas, a few grams"],["Key feature","Ever-growing incisors"]],
d:[["c. 56 million years ago","Rodents spread widely after the age of dinosaurs"],["1300s","Rats and fleas help spread the Black Death"],["1900s","Laboratory mice and rats become key to medical research"],["2011","Seven new species of forest mice are described from Luzon"],["2010s","Many more native rodent species are described from Luzon’s mountains"],["2020s","Research on naked mole-rats sheds light on aging"]],
s2:[["Cloud rats of the Philippines","The Philippines, especially Luzon, has an extraordinary variety of native rodents found nowhere else, including the giant cloud rats, furry tree-dwelling rodents that can weigh more than 2 kilograms. Scientists, including teams led by Lawrence Heaney and Filipino researchers, have discovered many new species on Luzon’s mountains in recent decades."],["Naked mole-rats","Naked mole-rats of East Africa live underground in colonies ruled by a single breeding queen, like bees or ants. They are nearly hairless, can survive with very little oxygen, rarely get cancer and can live more than 30 years, extraordinarily long for a rodent. Scientists study them to learn about aging and disease."],["Beavers, nature’s engineers","Beavers cut down trees with their teeth and build dams of branches and mud across streams, creating ponds where they build lodges. Their dams create wetlands that benefit fish, birds and other wildlife and store water. Beavers are being reintroduced in parts of Europe to restore wetlands."],["Rats are clever","Rats are intelligent and social. They can learn mazes, recognize other rats and even laugh with high-pitched chirps when tickled, as scientists discovered. Trained giant African pouched rats sniff out landmines and detect tuberculosis in samples."],["Rodents and disease","Rodents can carry diseases such as leptospirosis, hantavirus, Lassa fever and plague. Good sanitation, safe food storage, keeping homes free of rubbish and avoiding floodwater with cuts on the skin help reduce the risk. After floods in the Philippines, health authorities warn about leptospirosis and provide preventive medicine."],["Pet rodents","Hamsters, guinea pigs, gerbils, rats and mice are popular pets because they are small and easy to care for. Guinea pigs were domesticated in the Andes thousands of years ago, where they are still raised for food. Pet rodents need proper cages, food, chew toys and gentle handling."]],
sp:["The capybara","The capybara of South America is the largest rodent in the world, as big as a medium-sized dog. It lives in groups near rivers, lakes and marshes, and it is an excellent swimmer, with partly webbed feet; it can stay underwater for several minutes to escape predators. Capybaras are famous for their calm nature, and other animals, from birds to monkeys, often sit on them. They have become popular in internet videos."],
g:[["Rodent","A mammal with ever-growing front teeth for gnawing"],["Incisor","A front tooth for cutting"],["Leptospirosis","A disease spread through water contaminated by animal urine"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["Rodents make up about 40 percent of mammal species.","Rodents’ front teeth never stop growing.","The capybara is the largest rodent."],
fun:"Rats make high-pitched laughing sounds when tickled.",
tl:"Ang rodent ang pinakamalaking grupo ng mammal, kabilang ang daga, ardilya at capybara. Hindi tumitigil sa paglaki ang kanilang mga ngipin sa harap. Sa Luzon, may mga natatanging rodent gaya ng cloud rat."});

ADDART({id:"domestication",t:"Domestication of Plants and Animals",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐄",w:"Domestication",r:["agri","dogs","horses","rice","wheat"],
p:[["Taming the wild","Domestication is the process by which people turned wild plants and animals into farm crops, livestock and pets."],["A turning point","Domestication allowed farming, villages and the growth of civilizations."],["Many centers","Plants and animals were domesticated independently in several regions of the world."]],
l:"Domestication is the long process by which humans selected and bred wild plants and animals over many generations until they became crops, livestock and pets that depend on people. Dogs were the first animals domesticated, by hunter-gatherers more than 15,000 years ago. From about 11,000 years ago, people in several regions began domesticating plants such as wheat, rice and maize, and animals such as sheep, goats, cattle and pigs. Domestication made farming possible and transformed human societies, diets and the landscape.",
s:[["How domestication works","Early farmers saved seeds from plants with useful traits, such as bigger grains or seeds that stayed on the stalk, and planted them again. Over many generations, crops changed. Herders kept and bred the calmest, most useful animals. Domesticated species gradually became different from their wild ancestors."],["Centers of domestication","Domestication happened independently in several places. The Fertile Crescent of the Middle East gave wheat, barley, lentils, sheep, goats and cattle. China gave rice, millet and pigs. Mesoamerica gave maize, beans and squash. The Andes gave potatoes, llamas and guinea pigs. New Guinea gave taro and bananas."],["Domesticated animals","Important domesticated animals include dogs, sheep, goats, cattle, pigs, chickens, horses, donkeys, camels, water buffalo, llamas, ducks and silkworms. Cats largely domesticated themselves by living near farms. Animals provided meat, milk, wool, leather, transport and power for plowing."],["Changes in domesticated species","Domesticated plants and animals often look very different from their wild ancestors. Maize descends from teosinte, a wild grass with tiny cobs only a few centimeters long. Domesticated animals tend to be tamer and often have floppy ears, curly tails or patchy coats, changes scientists call the domestication syndrome."]],
k:[["First domesticated animal","Dog, more than 15,000 years ago"],["Early plant domestication","About 11,000 years ago"],["Main centers","Fertile Crescent, China, Mesoamerica, Andes, New Guinea"],["Maize ancestor","Teosinte"],["Chicken ancestor","Red junglefowl of Southeast Asia"]],
d:[["c. 15,000 years ago","Dogs are domesticated"],["c. 9000 BCE","Wheat, barley, sheep and goats are domesticated in the Fertile Crescent"],["c. 7000 BCE","Rice is domesticated in China"],["c. 7000 BCE","Maize is domesticated from teosinte in Mexico"],["c. 1500 BCE","Chickens become common domestic birds in Southeast Asia"],["1959","The Soviet fox domestication experiment begins"]],
s2:[["The silver fox experiment","In 1959 the Soviet scientist Dmitry Belyaev began breeding silver foxes in Siberia, choosing only the tamest in each generation. Within decades, the foxes wagged their tails, sought human attention and developed floppy ears and patchy coats, like dogs. The experiment showed how selecting for tameness can bring many other changes."],["Chickens","Chickens descend from the red junglefowl of Southeast Asia. A 2022 study suggested that chickens were domesticated around 3,500 years ago, when red junglefowl were attracted to fields of rice and millet. Today chickens are the most numerous birds on Earth, with tens of billions alive at any time."],["The carabao","The carabao, a type of water buffalo, is the national animal of the Philippines. Domesticated in Asia thousands of years ago, water buffalo have long pulled plows in muddy rice paddies and carried loads. The Philippine Carabao Center, created in 1992, works to improve carabao breeding for milk and meat."],["The aurochs","Domestic cattle descend from the aurochs, a huge wild ox that once roamed Europe, Asia and North Africa. Aurochs appear in Stone Age cave paintings. Hunting and habitat loss drove them to extinction; the last known aurochs died in Poland in 1627."],["Domestication and disease","Living close to animals exposed people to new diseases. Many human diseases, such as measles, influenza and tuberculosis, are thought to have come originally from domesticated animals. When Europeans reached the Americas, diseases from their livestock-rich world devastated Indigenous peoples."],["Modern breeding","Today plant and animal breeders use genetics to develop crops that resist pests and drought and animals that produce more milk or meat. Seed banks preserve the wild relatives of crops, whose genes may help farmers adapt to changing climates."]],
sp:["From teosinte to maize","About 9,000 years ago, farmers in southern Mexico began growing teosinte, a wild grass with small ears holding only a handful of hard seeds. By choosing plants with larger ears and softer kernels, generation after generation, they transformed it into maize, or corn, with large cobs packed with hundreds of kernels. Maize spread throughout the Americas and, after 1492, around the world. It is now one of the most widely grown crops on Earth."],
g:[["Domestication","Turning wild species into ones bred and raised by people"],["Livestock","Farm animals raised for food or work"],["Selective breeding","Choosing parents with desired traits"],["Fertile Crescent","A region of the Middle East where farming began"]],
f:["Dogs were the first domesticated animals.","Maize descends from a wild grass called teosinte.","The carabao is the national animal of the Philippines."],
fun:"Chickens descend from the red junglefowl of Southeast Asia.",
tl:"Ang domestikasyon ay ang matagal na proseso ng pagpapaamo ng mga halaman at hayop para maging pananim, alagang hayop at alaga sa bahay. Ang aso ang unang pinaamo. Ang kalabaw ang pambansang hayop ng Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"animalbehavior",t:"Animal Behavior",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐝",w:"Ethology",r:["birdmigr","bee","ants","gorilla","evo"],
p:[["Why animals do what they do","Animal behavior is the study of how and why animals act as they do."],["Born with it or learned","Some behaviors are instincts; others are learned by experience and from others."],["Surprising intelligence","Many animals use tools, solve problems and communicate in complex ways."]],
l:"Animal behavior is everything animals do: finding food, avoiding predators, choosing mates, raising young, communicating and living in groups. The scientific study of animal behavior, especially in natural settings, is called ethology. Some behaviors are instinctive, present from birth, while others are learned through experience or by watching others. Studies of animal behavior have revealed remarkable intelligence and social lives among creatures from bees to elephants, and help us understand our own behavior.",
s:[["Instinct and learning","Instincts are behaviors animals perform without being taught, such as a spider spinning a web or a newly hatched sea turtle heading for the sea. Learned behaviors develop through experience: young birds learn songs from adults, chimpanzees learn to crack nuts by watching their mothers and dogs learn commands."],["Communication","Animals communicate with sounds, colors, movements and chemicals. Honeybees dance to tell others where flowers are. Ants leave scent trails. Humpback whales sing long, changing songs. Vervet monkeys use different alarm calls for different predators. Fireflies flash light signals to find mates."],["Living in groups","Many animals live in groups for protection, hunting or raising young. Wolves hunt in packs, elephants live in families led by the oldest female, called the matriarch, and fish form schools. Ants, bees and termites live in colonies where individuals have different roles."],["Courtship","Animals use elaborate displays to attract mates. Peacocks spread their tail feathers, birds-of-paradise perform dances, bowerbirds build and decorate structures and frogs call loudly at night. These displays signal health and fitness to potential mates."]],
k:[["Science of behavior","Ethology"],["Nobel Prize winners (1973)","Lorenz, Tinbergen and von Frisch"],["Famous discovery","Honeybee waggle dance"],["Tool use observed","Chimpanzees, crows, octopuses and others"],["Mirror test passed","Great apes, dolphins, elephants, magpies"]],
d:[["1872","Darwin publishes a book on emotional expression in humans and animals"],["1930s","Konrad Lorenz studies imprinting in geese"],["1940s","Karl von Frisch decodes the honeybee dance"],["1960","Jane Goodall observes chimpanzees using tools"],["1973","Lorenz, Tinbergen and von Frisch share the Nobel Prize"],["2008","Magpies pass the mirror self-recognition test"]],
s2:[["Imprinting","The Austrian scientist Konrad Lorenz found that newly hatched goslings follow the first moving object they see, usually their mother, a process called imprinting. When goslings saw Lorenz first, they followed him everywhere as if he were their parent. Imprinting shows how certain learning happens during a critical early period."],["The waggle dance","The Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch discovered that honeybees returning from good flowers perform a waggle dance in the hive. The direction of the dance shows the direction of the food relative to the Sun, and its length shows the distance. He shared the 1973 Nobel Prize with Lorenz and the Dutch scientist Niko Tinbergen."],["Jane Goodall and chimpanzees","In 1960, at Gombe in Tanzania, the British scientist Jane Goodall observed chimpanzees stripping leaves from twigs and using them to fish termites from their mounds. At the time, toolmaking was thought to be unique to humans. Her decades of research revealed chimpanzees’ complex social lives, emotions and personalities."],["Clever animals","New Caledonian crows bend wires into hooks to retrieve food, and some crows solve puzzles requiring several steps. Octopuses open jars and escape tanks. Dolphins call each other by signature whistles that work like names. Elephants appear to mourn their dead."],["The mirror test","In the mirror test, an animal is marked with a spot it can only see in a mirror. If it touches the mark on its own body, it may recognize itself. Great apes, dolphins, elephants and magpies have passed, and in 2019 researchers reported that a small fish, the cleaner wrasse, seemed to pass too, sparking debate about what the test shows."],["Animal behavior and conservation","Understanding behavior helps protect animals. Knowing migration routes, breeding seasons and feeding habits guides the design of protected areas and fishing rules. Studying how animals react to people helps reduce conflicts, for example keeping elephants away from crops."]],
sp:["Elephants never forget","Elephants live in close family groups led by the matriarch, usually the oldest female, whose memory of water holes and dangers helps the family survive droughts. Elephants communicate with low rumbles that can travel several kilometers through the ground, and they comfort upset family members by touching them with their trunks. They also show great interest in the bones of dead elephants, touching them gently, which some scientists interpret as a form of mourning."],
g:[["Ethology","The study of animal behavior in natural settings"],["Instinct","A behavior an animal does without learning"],["Imprinting","Rapid early learning of a parent’s identity"],["Matriarch","The female leader of a group"]],
f:["Honeybees dance to share the location of food.","Jane Goodall saw chimpanzees making tools in 1960.","Konrad Lorenz was followed by goslings that imprinted on him."],
fun:"Dolphins use signature whistles that work like names.",
tl:"Ang pag-uugali ng hayop ay pag-aaral kung paano at bakit kumikilos ang mga hayop. May mga likas na ugali at may natututunan. Maraming hayop ang gumagamit ng kasangkapan at nakikipag-usap sa masalimuot na paraan."});

ADDART({id:"foodchains",t:"Food Chains and Food Webs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🔗",w:"Food chain",r:["eco","photo","conservation","eagle","coral"],
p:[["Who eats whom","A food chain shows how energy passes from one living thing to another as they eat and are eaten."],["Starting with the Sun","Most food chains begin with plants or algae that capture sunlight."],["Webs of life","In nature, many food chains link together to form complex food webs."]],
l:"A food chain is a sequence showing how energy and nutrients pass from one organism to another in an ecosystem. It usually starts with producers, such as plants and algae, which make their own food from sunlight. Herbivores eat the producers, carnivores eat the herbivores and decomposers break down dead matter, returning nutrients to the soil. Because most animals eat and are eaten by many different species, food chains connect into food webs. Understanding them shows how removing or adding one species can affect a whole ecosystem.",
s:[["Producers","Producers make their own food. Plants, algae and some bacteria use sunlight, water and carbon dioxide to make sugars through photosynthesis. On land, grasses and trees are key producers; in the ocean, tiny floating algae called phytoplankton produce about half of the oxygen on Earth and form the base of marine food chains."],["Consumers","Consumers eat other organisms. Primary consumers, or herbivores, such as grasshoppers, deer and zooplankton, eat producers. Secondary consumers eat herbivores, and tertiary consumers eat other carnivores. Animals at the top, such as eagles, sharks and tigers, are called apex predators."],["Decomposers","Decomposers, such as bacteria, fungi and earthworms, break down dead plants and animals and waste, releasing nutrients back into the soil and water for producers to use again. Without them, dead matter would pile up and nutrients would run out."],["Energy pyramids","At each step in a food chain, much energy is lost as heat and used for living, so only about 10 percent passes to the next level. This is why there are many more plants than herbivores, and many more herbivores than top predators, and why food chains rarely have more than four or five links."]],
k:[["Start of most chains","Producers using sunlight"],["Energy passed to next level","About 10%"],["Top predators","Apex predators"],["Recyclers","Decomposers"],["Oxygen from phytoplankton","About half of Earth’s oxygen"]],
d:[["1927","Charles Elton describes food chains and pyramids of numbers"],["1942","Raymond Lindeman describes energy flow between feeding levels"],["1962","Rachel Carson warns about pesticides building up in food chains"],["1966","Robert Paine shows the role of keystone species"],["1977","Chemosynthetic food chains are discovered at deep-sea vents"],["1995","Wolves return to Yellowstone, revealing a trophic cascade"]],
s2:[["Keystone species","In 1966 the ecologist Robert Paine removed a predatory starfish from a stretch of rocky shore in Washington state. Without it, mussels took over and many other species disappeared. Species like this starfish, which have a large effect on their ecosystem relative to their numbers, are called keystone species."],["Trophic cascades","When top predators disappear or return, effects can ripple down the food web, called a trophic cascade. When wolves returned to Yellowstone in 1995, elk changed their behavior, allowing willows and aspens to regrow along streams, which helped beavers and birds. Sea otters eating sea urchins protect kelp forests in the same way."],["Poison in the chain","Some chemicals, such as the pesticide DDT and mercury, build up in animals’ bodies and become more concentrated at each level of the food chain, a process called biomagnification. Top predators such as eagles, ospreys and large fish end up with the highest levels. DDT thinned eagle eggshells, nearly wiping out bald eagles before it was banned."],["Life without sunlight","At hydrothermal vents on the deep seafloor, discovered to host life in 1977, bacteria make food from chemicals such as hydrogen sulfide rather than sunlight, a process called chemosynthesis. These bacteria support food webs of giant tube worms, clams, crabs and fish in total darkness."],["A Philippine food web","In a Philippine rainforest, fruiting trees feed insects, birds, bats and monkeys; snakes and civets eat smaller animals; and the Philippine eagle, an apex predator, hunts monkeys, flying lemurs and large birds. Fungi and termites recycle fallen leaves and wood. Losing forests removes the eagle’s prey and threatens the whole web."],["People in food webs","Humans are part of food webs too. Overfishing large predators such as tuna and sharks can change ocean food webs, sometimes allowing jellyfish to increase. Understanding food webs helps manage fisheries, control pests naturally and protect ecosystems."]],
sp:["The ocean’s invisible forests","Phytoplankton are microscopic algae and bacteria drifting in sunlit surface waters. Though too small to see individually, they are so numerous that they produce roughly half of the world’s oxygen and absorb large amounts of carbon dioxide. They are eaten by zooplankton, which feed small fish, which feed larger fish, seabirds, seals and whales. Even the blue whale, the largest animal ever, eats tiny krill that feed on phytoplankton."],
g:[["Producer","An organism that makes its own food"],["Consumer","An organism that eats other organisms"],["Decomposer","An organism that breaks down dead matter"],["Apex predator","A predator at the top of a food chain"]],
f:["Most food chains begin with sunlight.","Only about 10 percent of energy passes to the next level.","Phytoplankton produce about half of Earth’s oxygen."],
fun:"The blue whale, the largest animal ever, feeds on tiny krill.",
tl:"Ang food chain ay nagpapakita kung paano lumilipat ang enerhiya mula sa isang buhay na bagay patungo sa iba sa pagkain at pagiging pagkain. Nagsisimula ito sa halaman na gumagamit ng sikat ng araw. Nag-uugnay ang maraming food chain para bumuo ng food web."});

