// Religion articles, part 2: Sikhism, Shinto, Jainism and Indigenous religions.
ADDART({id:"sikhism",t:"Sikhism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🙏",w:"Sikhism",r:["relig","hindu","islam","indiacountry","festivals"],
p:[["One God, equal people","Sikhism is a religion founded in Punjab in the 1400s that teaches belief in one God and the equality of all people."],["Ten Gurus","It was shaped by ten Gurus, beginning with Guru Nanak."],["Service and sharing","Sikhs are known for seva, selfless service, and langar, free community kitchens open to all."]],
l:"Sikhism is a monotheistic religion founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab region of South Asia in the late 1400s. It has about 25 to 30 million followers, called Sikhs, making it one of the world’s larger religions; most live in the Indian state of Punjab, with large communities in Canada, the United Kingdom, the United States and elsewhere. Sikhism teaches devotion to one God, honest work, sharing with others, equality of all people regardless of caste or gender, and service to humanity. Its scripture, the Guru Granth Sahib, is revered as the eternal Guru.",
s:[["Guru Nanak","Guru Nanak, born in 1469 near Lahore in present-day Pakistan, taught that there is one God, beyond Hindu and Muslim divisions, and that people can reach God through devotion, meditation on God’s name and living truthfully. He traveled widely, singing hymns, and founded a community at Kartarpur."],["The ten Gurus","Nine Gurus followed Guru Nanak. The fifth, Guru Arjan, compiled the scripture and built the Harmandir Sahib, the Golden Temple, in Amritsar. The tenth, Guru Gobind Singh, founded the Khalsa, a community of initiated Sikhs, in 1699, and declared that after him the scripture itself would be the Guru."],["Beliefs","Sikhs believe in one God, Ik Onkar, the creator present in all. They reject caste distinctions and teach equality of men and women. Three central principles are naam japna, remembering God; kirat karni, earning an honest living; and vand chakna, sharing with others. Sikhs believe in karma and rebirth, and liberation through God’s grace."],["The Five Ks","Initiated Sikhs of the Khalsa keep five articles of faith: kesh, uncut hair, usually covered with a turban; kara, a steel bracelet; kanga, a wooden comb; kachera, cotton undershorts; and kirpan, a small sword symbolizing the duty to protect the weak. The turban is a sign of Sikh identity, honor and commitment."]],
k:[["Founder","Guru Nanak (1469–1539)"],["Followers","About 25–30 million"],["Scripture","Guru Granth Sahib"],["Holiest site","Harmandir Sahib (Golden Temple), Amritsar"],["Place of worship","Gurdwara"]],
d:[["1469","Guru Nanak is born"],["1604","The Adi Granth scripture is installed in the Golden Temple"],["1699","Guru Gobind Singh founds the Khalsa"],["1708","The scripture becomes the eternal Guru"],["1799","Maharaja Ranjit Singh establishes the Sikh Empire"],["2019","The Kartarpur Corridor opens between India and Pakistan"]],
s2:[["Langar","Every gurdwara has a langar, a free community kitchen where anyone, regardless of religion, caste or wealth, can sit together on the floor and share a vegetarian meal. The langar at the Golden Temple in Amritsar, run largely by volunteers, serves around 100,000 people a day, one of the largest free kitchens in the world."],["The Golden Temple","The Harmandir Sahib in Amritsar, covered in gold leaf and surrounded by a sacred pool, is the holiest site in Sikhism. It has four doors, open to people from all four directions, symbolizing openness to all. Hymns from the scripture are sung there almost continuously from early morning to night."],["The Sikh Empire","In 1799 Maharaja Ranjit Singh captured Lahore and built a powerful Sikh Empire in Punjab, known for its religious tolerance. After his death, the British conquered it in two wars, annexing Punjab in 1849. Many Sikhs later served in the British Indian Army in both world wars."],["Sikhs around the world","Large Sikh communities live in Canada, the United Kingdom, the United States, Australia, Malaysia and elsewhere. There is also a Sikh community in the Philippines, with gurdwaras in Manila and other cities, mostly descendants of Punjabi migrants who came in the 1900s."],["Seva","Seva, selfless service, is central to Sikh life. Sikhs volunteer in gurdwaras and communities, and Sikh charities provide food and aid during disasters around the world, from earthquakes to floods to refugee crises."],["Festivals","Major Sikh festivals include Vaisakhi, in April, which celebrates the founding of the Khalsa and the harvest, and the birthdays of the Gurus, especially Guru Nanak. Bandi Chhor Divas, celebrated around Diwali, marks the release of the sixth Guru from prison."]],
sp:["The Kartarpur Corridor","Guru Nanak spent his final years at Kartarpur, now in Pakistan, just across the border from India. After the partition of 1947, Indian Sikhs could only look across at the gurdwara there through binoculars. In November 2019, for the 550th anniversary of Guru Nanak’s birth, India and Pakistan opened the Kartarpur Corridor, allowing Indian pilgrims to visit without a visa. It was a rare symbol of cooperation between the two countries."],
g:[["Guru","A spiritual teacher"],["Gurdwara","A Sikh place of worship"],["Langar","A free community kitchen"],["Khalsa","The community of initiated Sikhs"]],
f:["Sikhism was founded by Guru Nanak.","The Golden Temple’s kitchen serves about 100,000 people a day.","The Guru Granth Sahib is the eternal Guru."],
fun:"The langar at the Golden Temple serves free meals to about 100,000 people every day.",
tl:"Ang Sikhismo ay relihiyong itinatag ni Guru Nanak sa Punjab noong 1400s. Itinuturo nito ang paniniwala sa iisang Diyos at pagkakapantay-pantay ng lahat ng tao. Kilala ang mga Sikh sa libreng kusina na tinatawag na langar."});

ADDART({id:"shinto",t:"Shinto",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"⛩️",w:"Shinto",r:["japan","japancountry","buddh","festivals","fuji"],
p:[["The way of the kami","Shinto is the traditional religion of Japan, centered on the worship of kami, sacred spirits."],["Shrines and gates","Its shrines, marked by torii gates, are found throughout Japan."],["Living tradition","Many Japanese take part in Shinto festivals and rituals alongside Buddhist practices."]],
l:"Shinto, meaning the way of the kami, is the indigenous religion of Japan. It centers on the worship of kami, sacred spirits or divine presences found in nature, such as mountains, rivers, trees and rocks, as well as in ancestors and important people. Shinto has no single founder or central scripture but is expressed through rituals, festivals and tens of thousands of shrines across Japan. Most Japanese people take part in Shinto customs, such as visiting shrines at New Year, often alongside Buddhist practices.",
s:[["Kami","Kami are sacred beings or forces. They include the sun goddess Amaterasu, from whom the imperial family traditionally claims descent, the spirits of mountains such as Fuji, waterfalls and ancient trees, and the spirits of ancestors. Shinto encourages gratitude, respect and harmony with nature and the kami."],["Shrines","Shinto shrines, called jinja, are homes for kami. Visitors pass through a torii gate, which marks the entrance to sacred space, purify their hands and mouth with water, ring a bell, bow, clap twice and pray. There are about 80,000 shrines in Japan, from huge complexes to tiny roadside shrines."],["Purity","Purification is central to Shinto. Rituals remove impurity caused by death, illness or wrongdoing. Priests wave a wand of paper streamers over people and objects to purify them, and some worshippers stand under waterfalls in a purification practice called misogi."],["Myths","Shinto myths are recorded in Japan’s oldest books, the Kojiki of 712 and the Nihon Shoki of 720. They tell how the gods Izanagi and Izanami created the Japanese islands, and how the sun goddess Amaterasu hid in a cave, plunging the world into darkness, until the other gods lured her out."]],
k:[["Meaning","The way of the kami"],["Sacred spirits","Kami"],["Number of shrines","About 80,000"],["Oldest texts","Kojiki (712), Nihon Shoki (720)"],["Symbol","Torii gate"]],
d:[["c. 300s–500s","Early shrine worship develops"],["712","The Kojiki is compiled"],["720","The Nihon Shoki is compiled"],["1868","The Meiji government promotes State Shinto"],["1945","State Shinto is abolished after World War II"],["2013","Ise Grand Shrine is rebuilt for the 62nd time"]],
s2:[["Shinto and Buddhism","Buddhism arrived in Japan in the 500s, and for centuries Shinto and Buddhism blended, with kami seen as forms of Buddhas. Many Japanese today participate in both: a common saying is that people are born Shinto, through shrine blessings for babies, marry Christian or Shinto and die Buddhist, with Buddhist funerals."],["State Shinto","From 1868, the Meiji government separated Shinto from Buddhism and promoted State Shinto, emphasizing loyalty to the emperor as divine. It was used to support nationalism and militarism until 1945. After World War II, the emperor renounced his divine status, and Shinto was separated from the state."],["Ise Grand Shrine","The Ise Grand Shrine, dedicated to Amaterasu, is the most sacred Shinto site. Every 20 years, its main buildings are rebuilt on an adjacent site using traditional techniques, a ritual carried out for about 1,300 years. The 62nd rebuilding was completed in 2013, and the next is planned for 2033."],["Fushimi Inari","Fushimi Inari Taisha in Kyoto, dedicated to Inari, the kami of rice and prosperity, is famous for thousands of bright orange torii gates lining paths up a mountain. Businesses donate gates in hope of success. Statues of foxes, Inari’s messengers, guard the shrine."],["Festivals","Shinto festivals, matsuri, are held throughout the year to honor kami, pray for harvests and celebrate communities, with parades of portable shrines called mikoshi, music, dancing and food stalls. At New Year, millions visit shrines for hatsumōde, the first prayer of the year."],["Shinto in daily life","Shinto customs appear in daily life: blessing new buildings and cars, shrine visits for children at ages three, five and seven, and household shrines called kamidana. Sumo wrestling has Shinto roots, with rituals such as throwing salt to purify the ring."]],
sp:["Rebuilding forever","At the Ise Grand Shrine, the main sanctuaries are torn down and rebuilt exactly as before every 20 years, a ceremony called Shikinen Sengū. Carpenters use cypress wood and traditional methods without nails, passing their skills to the next generation. The old materials are reused in other shrines. The practice reflects Shinto ideas of renewal and purity, and it means the shrine is always both ancient and new."],
g:[["Kami","A sacred spirit in Shinto"],["Jinja","A Shinto shrine"],["Torii","A gate marking a shrine’s sacred space"],["Matsuri","A Shinto festival"]],
f:["Shinto means the way of the kami.","Japan has about 80,000 Shinto shrines.","Ise Grand Shrine is rebuilt every 20 years."],
fun:"The Ise Grand Shrine has been rebuilt every 20 years for about 1,300 years.",
tl:"Ang Shinto ang tradisyonal na relihiyon ng Hapon, nakasentro sa pagsamba sa mga kami o banal na espiritu sa kalikasan. Makikilala ang mga dambana nito sa torii gate. Maraming Hapones ang sumasali sa mga pista nito kasabay ng Budismo."});

ADDART({id:"jainism",t:"Jainism",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🤲",w:"Jainism",r:["indianphil","hindu","buddh","gandhi","indiacountry"],
p:[["The path of non-violence","Jainism is an ancient Indian religion whose central principle is ahimsa, non-violence toward all living beings."],["Spiritual victors","Jains follow the teachings of 24 Tirthankaras, the last of whom was Mahavira."],["Careful living","Jains try to avoid harming even the smallest creatures."]],
l:"Jainism is an ancient religion of India that teaches a path to spiritual liberation through non-violence, truthfulness, self-discipline and non-attachment. Jains follow the teachings of 24 Tirthankaras, ford-makers who crossed the river of rebirth, the last being Mahavira, a contemporary of the Buddha in the 500s BCE. Jainism has about 4 to 5 million followers, mostly in India, and has influenced Indian thought far beyond its numbers, including Mahatma Gandhi’s philosophy of non-violence.",
s:[["Mahavira","Mahavira, born a prince in what is now Bihar, India, gave up his wealth at about age 30 to live as a wandering ascetic. After years of meditation and fasting, Jains believe he achieved kevala jnana, perfect knowledge. He taught for about 30 years and attracted many followers. Jains regard him as the 24th Tirthankara, following Parshvanatha."],["Ahimsa","Ahimsa, non-violence, is the heart of Jainism. Jains believe that all living beings, including animals, insects and even plants, have souls. They are strict vegetarians, and many avoid root vegetables, whose harvesting kills small organisms. Monks may sweep the ground before them and wear cloths over their mouths to avoid harming tiny creatures."],["Many-sided truth","Jainism teaches anekantavada, the many-sidedness of reality: no single viewpoint captures the whole truth. A famous story tells of blind men touching an elephant, each describing it differently. This encourages tolerance and humility toward others’ views."],["Vows","Jains take vows of non-violence, truthfulness, not stealing, chastity and non-attachment to possessions. Monks and nuns follow these vows strictly, owning almost nothing. Lay Jains follow them in a more moderate way, and many are known for honesty in business and generous charity."]],
k:[["Central principle","Ahimsa (non-violence)"],["Tirthankaras","24"],["Last Tirthankara","Mahavira"],["Followers","About 4–5 million"],["Main traditions","Digambara and Shvetambara"]],
d:[["c. 800s BCE","Parshvanatha, the 23rd Tirthankara, is believed to have lived"],["c. 500s BCE","Mahavira teaches"],["c. 300s BCE","The Mauryan emperor Chandragupta reportedly becomes a Jain monk"],["c. 400s CE","Jain scriptures are written down at Valabhi"],["981","The giant statue of Bahubali is carved at Shravanabelagola"],["1400s","The Ranakpur Jain temple is built"]],
s2:[["Two traditions","Jainism has two main traditions. Digambara, sky-clad, monks wear no clothes, renouncing all possessions, and believe women must be reborn as men to achieve liberation. Shvetambara, white-clad, monks and nuns wear white robes, and believe women can achieve liberation. They also differ on which scriptures are authoritative."],["Temples","Jains have built magnificent temples. The Dilwara Temples on Mount Abu and the Ranakpur Temple in Rajasthan are famous for intricate marble carvings; Ranakpur has 1,444 carved pillars, no two alike. The hill of Shatrunjaya at Palitana has hundreds of temples and is a major pilgrimage site."],["Bahubali","At Shravanabelagola in Karnataka stands a 17-meter statue of Bahubali, a revered figure, carved from a single block of granite in 981. Every 12 years, during the Mahamastakabhisheka ceremony, the statue is bathed with milk, saffron and flowers poured from above, attracting huge crowds."],["Fasting","Fasting is an important spiritual practice for Jains, to purify the soul and strengthen self-control. During the festival of Paryushana, many Jains fast and seek forgiveness, saying micchami dukkadam, may all my wrongs be forgiven, to friends and family."],["Influence on Gandhi","Mahatma Gandhi grew up in Gujarat, where Jainism was influential, and was deeply influenced by a Jain teacher, Shrimad Rajchandra. Jain ideas of non-violence and truth helped shape Gandhi’s philosophy of nonviolent resistance."],["Jains today","Most Jains live in India, especially in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra and Karnataka, with communities in the United Kingdom, the United States, Kenya and elsewhere. Jains are known for their contributions to business, education and charity, including animal hospitals and bird sanctuaries."]],
sp:["The blind men and the elephant","An old parable, used by Jains to explain many-sided truth, tells of several blind men who touch an elephant for the first time. One feels its trunk and says an elephant is like a snake. Another touches its leg and says it is like a tree. Another feels its ear and says it is like a fan. Each is partly right, but none sees the whole. The story teaches that people may grasp only part of the truth and should respect other viewpoints."],
g:[["Ahimsa","Non-violence toward all living beings"],["Tirthankara","A spiritual teacher who has achieved liberation"],["Ascetic","A person who gives up comforts for spiritual reasons"],["Anekantavada","The doctrine of many-sided truth"]],
f:["Mahavira was the 24th Tirthankara.","Jains practice strict non-violence.","Ranakpur Temple has 1,444 carved pillars."],
fun:"Some Jain monks sweep the ground in front of them to avoid stepping on insects.",
tl:"Ang Jainismo ay sinaunang relihiyon ng India na nakasentro sa ahimsa, ang hindi pananakit sa lahat ng buhay na nilalang. Sinusunod nito ang 24 na Tirthankara, at si Mahavira ang huli sa kanila."});

ADDART({id:"indigrelig",t:"Indigenous Religions",c:"Religion and Philosophy",i:"🪶",w:"Indigenous religion",r:["indig","phfolk","relig","australiacountry","banaue"],
p:[["Ancient traditions","Indigenous religions are the traditional spiritual beliefs of Indigenous peoples around the world."],["Land and spirits","Many honor the land, nature spirits and ancestors."],["Living faiths","These traditions continue today, often alongside other religions."]],
l:"Indigenous religions are the traditional spiritual beliefs and practices of Indigenous peoples, passed down through generations, often orally. They are extremely diverse, from the Dreaming of Aboriginal Australians and the traditions of Native American nations to African traditional religions, Siberian shamanism and the anito beliefs of the Philippines. Many share a close relationship with the land, respect for nature spirits and ancestors, and rituals led by spiritual specialists. Despite colonization and suppression, many Indigenous religions survive and are being revitalized.",
s:[["Common features","Although very diverse, many Indigenous religions see the natural world as alive with spirits, honor ancestors who continue to influence the living, connect identity to particular lands and sacred places, and pass knowledge through stories, songs, dances and ceremonies rather than written scriptures."],["Spiritual specialists","Many traditions have spiritual specialists, such as shamans, healers, diviners or priests, who communicate with spirits, heal the sick, lead ceremonies and keep sacred knowledge. In the Philippines, these leaders were often women called babaylan in the Visayas and catalonan in Tagalog areas."],["Philippine traditions","Before Islam and Christianity, peoples across the Philippines honored anito, ancestral spirits and nature spirits, and diwata, powerful deities or spirits. The Tagalog supreme god was Bathala. In the Cordillera, the Ifugao and other peoples perform rituals for rice planting and harvest, and the Hudhud chants of the Ifugao are recognized by UNESCO."],["Colonization and survival","During colonization, Indigenous religions were often suppressed, and sacred objects destroyed. Many peoples adopted Christianity or Islam while keeping elements of their older beliefs. Today, Indigenous communities are working to preserve and revive their traditions, languages and sacred sites."]],
k:[["Common elements","Nature spirits, ancestors, sacred land, oral tradition"],["Philippine spirits","Anito and diwata"],["Philippine spiritual leaders","Babaylan, catalonan"],["UNESCO-listed tradition","Hudhud chants of the Ifugao"],["UN declaration","UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, 2007"]],
d:[["Tens of thousands of years ago","Aboriginal Australian traditions develop"],["1500s","Colonization brings suppression of many traditions"],["1978","The US passes the American Indian Religious Freedom Act"],["1997","The Philippines passes the Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act"],["2007","The UN adopts the Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples"],["2019","Climbing Uluru is banned out of respect for Anangu beliefs"]],
s2:[["The Dreaming","Aboriginal Australians speak of the Dreaming, the time when ancestral beings shaped the land, created life and established laws. The Dreaming is not only in the past; it continues to connect people, land and ancestors. Songlines, paths across the land marked by songs and stories, map sacred sites and guide travel."],["African traditional religions","African traditional religions are diverse, often including belief in a supreme creator, lesser deities or spirits and honored ancestors. The Yoruba religion of West Africa honors orishas, divine beings. Carried across the Atlantic by enslaved Africans, it influenced religions such as Santería in Cuba and Candomblé in Brazil."],["Native American traditions","Native American nations have many spiritual traditions, often involving ceremonies such as the Sun Dance, sweat lodges and vision quests, and a deep connection to the land and animals. For a long time, the United States suppressed some ceremonies; the American Indian Religious Freedom Act of 1978 recognized their right to practice."],["Shamanism","In Siberia, Mongolia, Korea and elsewhere, shamans enter trance states through drumming, chanting or dance to communicate with spirits, heal and guide their communities. Korean shamanic rituals, called gut, are still performed. The word shaman comes from the Evenki language of Siberia."],["The babaylan","In pre-colonial Philippine societies, the babaylan were respected spiritual leaders, healers and keepers of knowledge, mostly women and sometimes men who took on feminine roles. They led rituals, cured illness with herbs and prayers and mediated with spirits. Some led resistance against Spanish colonizers. Today the babaylan is a symbol of Filipino heritage and women’s leadership."],["Sacred sites","Many Indigenous peoples hold mountains, forests, rivers and rocks as sacred. Protecting these sites from mining, development and tourism is an important issue. In the Philippines, the Indigenous Peoples’ Rights Act of 1997 recognizes ancestral domains, and in Australia, climbing Uluru was banned in 2019 at the request of the Anangu people."]],
sp:["The Hudhud chants","The Ifugao people of northern Luzon chant the Hudhud, long epic narratives sung during rice sowing and harvest, funeral wakes and other rituals. A lead chanter, often an elderly woman, sings the story while others answer in chorus. The chants tell of heroes, customs and the importance of rice farming in the terraces. UNESCO recognized the Hudhud as a masterpiece of intangible heritage in 2001, and efforts are under way to teach it to young Ifugao."],
g:[["Animism","The belief that natural things have spirits"],["Shaman","A spiritual specialist who communicates with spirits"],["Anito","Ancestral and nature spirits in Philippine belief"],["Babaylan","A pre-colonial Filipino spiritual leader and healer"]],
f:["Indigenous religions are often passed down orally.","The Tagalog supreme god was called Bathala.","The Hudhud chants were recognized by UNESCO in 2001."],
fun:"The word shaman comes from the Evenki language of Siberia.",
tl:"Ang mga katutubong relihiyon ay tradisyonal na paniniwala ng mga katutubong mamamayan sa buong mundo. Iginagalang nila ang lupa, espiritu ng kalikasan at mga ninuno. Sa Pilipinas, kabilang dito ang anito, diwata at ang babaylan."});

;
/* data/fields.js */
// Fields of study: psychology, archaeology and anthropology.
ADDART({id:"psychology",t:"Psychology",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🧠",w:"Psychology",r:["brain","mental","memory","emotions","scimeth"],
p:[["The science of the mind","Psychology is the scientific study of the mind and behavior."],["Many branches","Psychologists study learning, memory, emotions, development, relationships and mental health."],["Helping people","Psychology helps people understand themselves and supports mental health, education and work."]],
l:"Psychology is the scientific study of the mind and behavior: how people think, feel, learn, remember, perceive, develop and interact with others. It became a separate science in the late 1800s and now includes many specialties, from studying how children develop to treating mental health conditions. Psychologists use experiments, observations, surveys and brain imaging to understand human behavior. Psychology is applied in clinics, schools, businesses, sports, courts and many other settings.",
s:[["Beginnings","Philosophers in ancient Greece, India and China asked questions about the mind. In 1879 the German scientist Wilhelm Wundt opened the first psychology laboratory in Leipzig, using experiments to study perception and reaction times. In 1890 the American William James published The Principles of Psychology."],["Schools of thought","Sigmund Freud, an Austrian doctor, developed psychoanalysis, emphasizing unconscious desires and childhood experiences. Behaviorists such as Ivan Pavlov, John Watson and B. F. Skinner studied how behavior is learned through conditioning. From the 1950s, cognitive psychology studied mental processes such as memory and problem-solving, while humanistic psychologists such as Abraham Maslow emphasized personal growth."],["Branches","Clinical psychologists help people with mental health conditions. Developmental psychologists study how people change from infancy to old age. Social psychologists study how people influence one another. Cognitive psychologists study thinking and memory. Educational, industrial and sports psychologists apply psychology to learning, work and performance."],["Research methods","Psychologists design experiments to test ideas, comparing groups under different conditions. They also use questionnaires, interviews, observation and brain scans. Because human behavior is complex, studies must be carefully designed, and results need to be repeated by other researchers to be trusted."]],
k:[["Definition","Scientific study of mind and behavior"],["First lab","Wilhelm Wundt, Leipzig, 1879"],["Famous theorists","Freud, Pavlov, Skinner, Piaget, Maslow"],["Philippine approach","Sikolohiyang Pilipino"],["Philippine law","Psychology Act of 2009"]],
d:[["1879","Wundt opens the first psychology laboratory"],["1890","William James publishes The Principles of Psychology"],["1900","Freud publishes The Interpretation of Dreams"],["1904","Pavlov wins the Nobel Prize for work on digestion that led to studies of conditioning"],["1943","Maslow proposes his hierarchy of needs"],["1970s","Virgilio Enriquez develops Sikolohiyang Pilipino"]],
s2:[["Pavlov’s dogs","The Russian scientist Ivan Pavlov noticed that dogs began to drool not only when they saw food but when they heard sounds linked with feeding. By repeatedly pairing a sound with food, he trained dogs to drool at the sound alone. This process, called classical conditioning, showed how learning can link events."],["Piaget and children","The Swiss psychologist Jean Piaget studied how children think, proposing that they pass through stages of mental development, from infants exploring with their senses to teenagers capable of abstract reasoning. His ideas influenced education, though later research has shown that children are often more capable than he thought."],["Maslow’s hierarchy","In 1943 the American psychologist Abraham Maslow proposed that people have a hierarchy of needs, often shown as a pyramid: physical needs such as food and sleep at the base, then safety, love and belonging, esteem and, at the top, self-actualization, fulfilling one’s potential. The idea is popular, though research suggests needs are not always met in strict order."],["Sikolohiyang Pilipino","In the 1970s, the Filipino psychologist Virgilio Enriquez founded Sikolohiyang Pilipino, Filipino psychology, arguing that psychology should be rooted in Filipino culture and language rather than simply imported from the West. It highlights concepts such as kapwa, a shared identity with others, and pakikiramdam, sensitivity to others’ feelings."],["Famous experiments","In the 1960s, Stanley Milgram’s experiments found that many people would obey an authority figure’s instructions to give what they believed were painful electric shocks to others. Such studies revealed important truths about human behavior but raised ethical concerns, leading to stricter rules protecting research participants."],["The replication crisis","In the 2010s, researchers tried to repeat many well-known psychology studies and found that a significant share could not be reproduced. This replication crisis led to reforms, such as registering study plans in advance, sharing data and using larger samples, making psychology more reliable."]],
sp:["The marshmallow test","In the late 1960s and early 1970s, psychologist Walter Mischel offered young children a choice: eat one marshmallow now, or wait alone for about 15 minutes and receive two. Some children ate it immediately, while others covered their eyes or sang to resist. Follow-up studies suggested that children who waited did better later in life, but later research found that family background explained much of the difference, showing how findings can be refined over time."],
g:[["Psychology","The scientific study of the mind and behavior"],["Conditioning","Learning by linking events or consequences"],["Cognitive","Relating to thinking, memory and understanding"],["Kapwa","A Filipino concept of shared identity with others"]],
f:["Wundt opened the first psychology lab in 1879.","Pavlov showed that dogs could learn to drool at a sound.","Virgilio Enriquez founded Sikolohiyang Pilipino."],
fun:"Children in the marshmallow test sang and covered their eyes to resist eating the treat.",
tl:"Ang sikolohiya ay siyentipikong pag-aaral ng isip at pag-uugali. Pinag-aaralan nito ang pagkatuto, alaala, damdamin at kalusugan ng isip. Itinatag ni Virgilio Enriquez ang Sikolohiyang Pilipino na nakaugat sa kulturang Pilipino."});

ADDART({id:"archaeology",t:"Archaeology",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⛏️",w:"Archaeology",r:["stoneage","humanorigins","egypt","pyramids","seaempires"],
p:[["Digging into the past","Archaeology is the study of the human past through the objects and remains people left behind."],["Clues in the ground","Archaeologists study tools, pottery, bones, buildings and even ancient seeds and DNA."],["Rewriting history","Archaeological discoveries constantly change our understanding of history."]],
l:"Archaeology is the study of human history and prehistory through the excavation and analysis of material remains, such as tools, pottery, buildings, bones, art and the traces of everyday life. It covers everything from the earliest stone tools, millions of years old, to modern times. Archaeologists use careful digging, scientific dating, laboratory analysis and new technologies such as satellite images, laser scanning and ancient DNA. Their discoveries reveal how people lived, what they ate, what they believed and how societies changed.",
s:[["Finding sites","Archaeological sites are found by surveying the land, studying old maps and records, noticing unusual features such as mounds and crop marks, and using technologies such as ground-penetrating radar, aerial photography, satellite imagery and lidar, which can reveal ruins hidden under forests. Many discoveries are made by chance, during construction or farming."],["Excavation","Excavation is slow, careful work. Archaeologists dig in layers, recording the exact position of every find, because context, where an object is found and what it is near, tells as much as the object itself. Deeper layers are usually older, a principle called stratigraphy."],["Dating the past","Radiocarbon dating, developed in 1949, measures carbon-14 in once-living materials up to about 50,000 years old. Tree rings, called dendrochronology, give precise dates for wood. Other methods date volcanic layers, pottery and even burnt flint. Styles of objects also help date sites."],["Science in archaeology","Scientists analyze bones to learn about ancient diets and diseases, seeds and pollen to reconstruct farming and environments, and ancient DNA to trace migrations and family relationships. Chemical analysis can reveal where materials such as metals and stones came from, showing ancient trade routes."]],
k:[["Definition","Study of the past through material remains"],["Key principle","Stratigraphy"],["Radiocarbon dating","Developed 1949"],["Famous find","Tutankhamun’s tomb (1922)"],["Philippine treasure","Butuan balangay boats"]],
d:[["1748","Excavations begin at Pompeii"],["1799","The Rosetta Stone is found in Egypt"],["1922","Howard Carter discovers Tutankhamun’s tomb"],["1962","Robert Fox excavates the Tabon Caves in Palawan"],["1974","The Terracotta Army is discovered in China"],["1976","The first Butuan balangay boat is excavated"]],
s2:[["Tutankhamun","In November 1922, the British archaeologist Howard Carter, working with Egyptian workers and funded by Lord Carnarvon, discovered the nearly intact tomb of the young pharaoh Tutankhamun in Egypt’s Valley of the Kings. It contained over 5,000 objects, including his famous gold mask. The discovery caused a worldwide sensation."],["The Rosetta Stone","In 1799 French soldiers in Egypt found a stone inscribed with the same decree in three scripts: Egyptian hieroglyphs, Demotic and ancient Greek. Using the Greek text, the French scholar Jean-François Champollion deciphered hieroglyphs in 1822, unlocking the writing of ancient Egypt. The stone is now in the British Museum."],["Archaeology in the Philippines","Philippine archaeology has revealed the Tabon Caves of Palawan, with remains tens of thousands of years old, excavated by Robert Fox and the National Museum from 1962; the Manunggul Jar; the Laguna Copperplate Inscription; Homo luzonensis from Callao Cave; and the balangay boats of Butuan, wooden boats from about 320 CE onward, among the oldest watercraft found in Southeast Asia."],["Pompeii","In 79 CE the eruption of Mount Vesuvius buried the Roman town of Pompeii under ash, preserving houses, shops, paintings and even loaves of bread. Excavations began in 1748. Archaeologists filled hollows left by bodies with plaster, creating casts that show victims in their final moments. Pompeii gives a unique snapshot of Roman life."],["Looting and ethics","Looters dig up and sell ancient objects, destroying their context and erasing history. International laws aim to stop this trade. Many countries and communities are asking museums to return objects taken during colonial times, such as the Benin Bronzes. Archaeologists today work closely with local and Indigenous communities."],["Underwater archaeology","Archaeologists also explore shipwrecks and submerged sites. Off the Philippines, the wreck of the San Diego, a Spanish galleon sunk in battle with the Dutch in 1600 near Fortune Island, Batangas, was excavated in the 1990s, yielding thousands of artifacts, including Chinese porcelain and weapons."]],
sp:["The balangay boats of Butuan","In 1976 people digging in Butuan, Agusan del Norte, found the remains of wooden boats buried in mud. Archaeologists from the National Museum identified them as balangay, plank-built boats joined with wooden pegs, the oldest dated to about 320 CE. Several more were later found. They show the advanced shipbuilding skills of early Filipinos, who traded across Southeast Asia. In 2009 a team sailed a replica balangay around Southeast Asia, and the balangay was declared a national boat."],
g:[["Archaeology","The study of the past through material remains"],["Excavation","Carefully digging up a site"],["Stratigraphy","The study of layers in the ground"],["Artifact","An object made or used by people"]],
f:["Tutankhamun’s tomb was found in 1922.","The Rosetta Stone helped decode hieroglyphs.","The oldest Butuan balangay dates to about 320 CE."],
fun:"A replica balangay boat sailed around Southeast Asia in 2009.",
tl:"Ang arkeolohiya ay pag-aaral ng nakaraan ng tao sa pamamagitan ng mga bagay at labi na naiwan nila. Pinag-aaralan nito ang kasangkapan, palayok at buto. Sa Pilipinas, mahalagang tuklas ang mga bangkang balangay ng Butuan."});

ADDART({id:"anthropology",t:"Anthropology",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🌏",w:"Anthropology",r:["humanorigins","archaeology","langevo","indig","relig"],
p:[["The study of humanity","Anthropology studies human beings: our cultures, societies, languages, biology and history."],["Living among people","Anthropologists often live with communities for long periods to understand their ways of life."],["Understanding differences","Anthropology helps people appreciate the variety of human cultures."]],
l:"Anthropology is the study of humanity in all its aspects: human evolution and biology, cultures and societies past and present, and languages. It asks what makes us human and why human ways of life differ so much around the world. Anthropologists study everything from family life, religion and politics to food, art and technology, in villages, cities and online communities. Their method of long-term fieldwork, living among the people they study, has deeply influenced how we understand other cultures.",
s:[["Branches","Anthropology is often divided into four fields. Cultural or social anthropology studies how people live, their beliefs, customs and social organization. Biological anthropology studies human evolution, genetics and variation. Linguistic anthropology studies language in social life. Archaeology, in some countries considered part of anthropology, studies the past through material remains."],["Fieldwork","Anthropologists conduct fieldwork, often living in a community for a year or more, learning the language and taking part in daily life, a method called participant observation. The Polish-British anthropologist Bronisław Malinowski, who lived in the Trobriand Islands from 1915 to 1918, helped establish this approach."],["Cultural relativism","The German-American anthropologist Franz Boas argued that cultures should be understood on their own terms rather than ranked as more or less civilized, an approach called cultural relativism. He challenged the racist theories common in his time, showing that differences between groups were shaped by culture and environment, not biology."],["What anthropologists study","Anthropologists study kinship and family, religion and ritual, economic exchange, politics and power, gender, health and healing, migration, globalization and how communities adapt to change. Today they also study scientists, corporations, digital communities and cities."]],
k:[["Definition","The study of humanity"],["Main fields","Cultural, biological, linguistic, archaeological"],["Key method","Participant observation"],["Key thinkers","Boas, Malinowski, Mead, Lévi-Strauss"],["Father of Philippine anthropology","H. Otley Beyer"]],
d:[["1871","Edward Tylor defines culture in Primitive Culture"],["1904","Filipinos are displayed at the St. Louis World’s Fair"],["1915","Malinowski begins fieldwork in the Trobriand Islands"],["1928","Margaret Mead publishes Coming of Age in Samoa"],["1955","Claude Lévi-Strauss publishes Tristes Tropiques"],["1960s","F. Landa Jocano conducts studies of Philippine society"]],
s2:[["Margaret Mead","The American anthropologist Margaret Mead studied adolescence in Samoa and gender roles in Papua New Guinea. Her 1928 book Coming of Age in Samoa argued that the stresses of adolescence are shaped by culture, not just biology. Later critics questioned some of her findings, but she brought anthropology to a wide public."],["Anthropology and colonialism","Early anthropology developed alongside colonialism, and some research helped colonial governments control colonized peoples. At the 1904 St. Louis World’s Fair in the United States, more than a thousand Filipinos from various ethnic groups were displayed in reconstructed villages, presented in ways that reinforced racist ideas. Modern anthropology reflects critically on this history."],["Anthropology in the Philippines","H. Otley Beyer, an American who came to the Philippines in 1905 and taught at the University of the Philippines, is often called the father of Philippine anthropology. Filipino anthropologists such as F. Landa Jocano studied Filipino family life, folk beliefs and regional cultures, and many today work with Indigenous communities on issues such as land rights and cultural preservation."],["Lévi-Strauss and structuralism","The French anthropologist Claude Lévi-Strauss argued that myths, kinship systems and customs around the world share underlying structures of human thought, such as pairs of opposites like raw and cooked. His approach, called structuralism, influenced many fields in the mid-1900s."],["Applied anthropology","Anthropologists work in health, development, business, design and disaster response. During the Ebola epidemic in West Africa in 2014, anthropologists helped health workers understand local burial customs and design safer practices that communities would accept."],["Ethnocentrism","Ethnocentrism is judging other cultures by the standards of one’s own, often assuming one’s own ways are best. Anthropology encourages people to understand why others live as they do, while still recognizing universal human rights. Learning about other cultures can also help people see their own customs in a new light."]],
sp:["The Kula ring","In the Trobriand Islands of Papua New Guinea, Malinowski studied the Kula ring, a remarkable system of exchange in which people traveled long distances by canoe to trade shell necklaces in one direction around a circle of islands and shell armbands in the other. The objects had no practical use, but exchanging them created lasting relationships, prestige and trust between communities. Malinowski’s study showed that economic exchange is about relationships, not just goods."],
g:[["Anthropology","The study of humanity"],["Fieldwork","Research carried out among the people being studied"],["Participant observation","Learning by taking part in a community’s life"],["Cultural relativism","Understanding a culture on its own terms"]],
f:["Malinowski lived in the Trobriand Islands from 1915 to 1918.","Franz Boas challenged racist theories.","Filipinos were displayed at the 1904 St. Louis World’s Fair."],
fun:"In the Kula ring, shell necklaces and armbands travel in opposite directions around a circle of islands.",
tl:"Ang antropolohiya ay pag-aaral ng sangkatauhan: kultura, lipunan, wika at biyolohiya. Madalas tumira ang mga antropologo sa mga komunidad nang matagal para maunawaan ang kanilang pamumuhay. Si H. Otley Beyer ang tinaguriang ama ng antropolohiyang Pilipino."});

;
/* data/life.js */
// Everyday life articles: family, education, clothing and fashion, toys and games.
ADDART({id:"family",t:"Family",c:"Economics and Society",i:"👨‍👩‍👧",w:"Family",r:["ofw","festivals","aging","pregnancy","anthropology"],
p:[["The first community","A family is a group of people connected by birth, marriage, adoption or close bonds who care for one another."],["Many forms","Families take many forms, from small nuclear families to large extended families."],["Changing over time","Family life has changed greatly as societies have changed."]],
l:"A family is a group of people related by birth, marriage, adoption or deep commitment, who usually share a home, resources and responsibilities for one another. Families raise children, care for the sick and elderly, pass on language, values and traditions, and provide love and support. Family structures vary widely across cultures and have changed over time. In the Philippines, as in many countries, close extended families, respect for elders and strong family loyalty are deeply valued.",
s:[["Kinds of families","A nuclear family consists of parents and their children. An extended family includes grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins, sometimes living together. Other families include single-parent families, blended families formed when parents remarry, families with adopted or foster children and families headed by grandparents."],["Kinship","Anthropologists study kinship, the ways societies define family relationships. Some cultures trace descent through the father’s line, some through the mother’s and some through both. Kinship terms differ: in some languages, cousins are called brothers and sisters, and there are special words for older and younger siblings."],["Filipino families","Filipino families are often close-knit, with strong ties among extended relatives and godparents, called ninong and ninang. Younger people show respect by taking an elder’s hand to their forehead, a gesture called mano, and use terms such as kuya for older brother and ate for older sister. Family gatherings mark birthdays, holidays and life events."],["Changing families","Around the world, families have become smaller as people marry later and have fewer children. More women work outside the home, and more people live alone or in different family arrangements. Migration also separates families; millions of Filipino families have a parent or sibling working abroad."]],
k:[["Main forms","Nuclear and extended families"],["Filipino gesture of respect","Mano"],["Godparents","Ninong and ninang"],["International Day of Families","15 May"],["Global fertility rate","About 2.2 children per woman (2024)"]],
d:[["c. 10,000 years ago","Settled farming changes family and household life"],["1948","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights calls the family the natural unit of society"],["1987","The Family Code of the Philippines is enacted"],["1989","The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child is adopted"],["1993","The UN proclaims the International Day of Families"],["2020s","The global average number of children per woman falls toward replacement level"]],
s2:[["Families and migration","Millions of Filipinos work overseas to support their families, sending remittances that pay for education, housing and health care. This brings economic benefits but also challenges, as children may grow up apart from a parent. Families stay connected through video calls, balikbayan boxes of gifts and visits home."],["Respect for elders","In many Asian, African and Latin American cultures, elders hold a respected place in the family and are often cared for at home by their children. In the Philippines, it is common for grandparents to live with or near their grandchildren and help raise them. Laws in some countries encourage families to care for older relatives."],["Marriage customs","Marriage customs vary widely, from arranged marriages to love matches, from small ceremonies to grand weddings lasting days. Filipino weddings often include sponsors called ninong and ninang, the veil and cord ceremony and the throwing of rice for prosperity. The legal age and rules for marriage differ by country."],["Divorce and the Philippines","Laws on ending marriages vary. The Philippines is one of very few places without a general divorce law for most citizens; married couples can seek annulment or legal separation, while Muslim Filipinos may divorce under the Code of Muslim Personal Laws. Proposals to introduce divorce have been debated for years, with strong views on both sides."],["Children’s rights","The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child, adopted in 1989 and ratified by almost every country, recognizes children’s rights to protection, education, health care and to be heard. It emphasizes the importance of families in children’s well-being."],["Family and society","Families are often called the basic unit of society. Governments support families through programs such as parental leave, child care, family allowances and cash transfer programs. The Philippines’ Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, 4Ps, gives cash grants to poor families who keep children in school and attend health checkups."]],
sp:["Balikbayan boxes","Filipinos working abroad often send large cardboard boxes, called balikbayan boxes, filled with gifts for their families: canned food, chocolates, clothes, toiletries and toys. The tradition grew in the 1980s, as more Filipinos worked overseas. Families eagerly await the boxes, which are filled over months. They have become a symbol of the love and sacrifice of overseas Filipino workers for their families back home."],
g:[["Nuclear family","Parents and their children"],["Extended family","A family including grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins"],["Kinship","Family relationships recognized by a society"],["Mano","A Filipino gesture of respect to elders"]],
f:["The Family Code of the Philippines was enacted in 1987.","The International Day of Families is on 15 May.","Families around the world are getting smaller."],
fun:"Balikbayan boxes sent by Filipinos abroad can take months to fill with gifts.",
tl:"Ang pamilya ay grupo ng mga taong magkakaugnay sa dugo, kasal o pag-ampon na nagmamalasakit sa isa't isa. Iba-iba ang anyo nito. Sa Pilipinas, mahalaga ang malapit na ugnayan ng pinalawak na pamilya at paggalang sa nakatatanda."});

ADDART({id:"education",t:"Education and Schools",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🏫",w:"Education",r:["malala","alphabet","langevo","family","print"],
p:[["Learning for life","Education is the process of learning knowledge, skills and values, at home, in school and throughout life."],["Schools around the world","Most children today attend school, though millions still cannot."],["A human right","Education is recognized as a basic human right."]],
l:"Education is the process of learning and teaching knowledge, skills, values and ways of thinking. It happens in families and communities, in schools and universities, and throughout life. Formal schooling began thousands of years ago with schools for scribes and priests and has expanded until today most of the world’s children attend school. Education is recognized as a human right because it opens opportunities, improves health and income and helps people take part in society. Yet millions of children are still out of school, and many in school do not learn basic skills.",
s:[["Early schools","Some of the first schools trained scribes in ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt over 4,000 years ago, teaching writing on clay tablets and papyrus. In ancient Greece, Plato founded the Academy around 387 BCE. In China, Confucian education prepared students for government examinations. Religious institutions in many cultures taught reading and sacred texts."],["Universities","Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco, founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, is often cited as the oldest existing educational institution to grant degrees. Nalanda in India was a great center of learning. Medieval Europe founded universities such as Bologna and Oxford. The University of Santo Tomas in Manila, founded in 1611, is the oldest existing university in Asia."],["Schooling for all","In the 1700s and 1800s, countries such as Prussia began requiring all children to attend school. Mass public education spread in the 1900s. Global literacy has risen from a small minority of adults in 1800 to the large majority today."],["How people learn","Educators have developed many approaches. The Italian doctor Maria Montessori designed classrooms where children learn through hands-on activities at their own pace. Others emphasize discussion, projects, play or direct teaching. Research on how the brain learns continues to shape education."]],
k:[["Oldest degree-granting institution","Al-Qarawiyyin, Fez (859)"],["Oldest university in Asia","University of Santo Tomas, Manila (1611)"],["Global goal","Quality education for all by 2030"],["Philippine reform","K to 12 program (2013)"],["World Teachers’ Day","5 October"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","Scribal schools operate in Mesopotamia"],["c. 387 BCE","Plato founds the Academy in Athens"],["859","Al-Qarawiyyin is founded in Fez"],["1611","The University of Santo Tomas is founded in Manila"],["1901","American teachers called Thomasites arrive in the Philippines"],["2013","The Philippines adopts the K to 12 basic education program"]],
s2:[["Education in the Philippines","During the Spanish era, education was mostly run by religious orders. In 1901 about 500 American teachers, called Thomasites after the ship that brought many of them, arrived to set up public schools teaching in English. The University of the Philippines was founded in 1908. In 2013 the K to 12 law added kindergarten and two years of senior high school, extending basic education to 13 years."],["Children out of school","According to UNESCO, around 250 million children and young people worldwide are out of school, most in Africa and South Asia, because of poverty, conflict, discrimination, child labor, early marriage or lack of schools. Girls face particular barriers in some regions. Malala Yousafzai became a global advocate for girls’ education after surviving an attack for speaking out."],["Learning, not just schooling","Being in school does not always mean learning. Studies show that many children finish primary school unable to read a simple text. Better-trained teachers, learning in children’s mother tongue in the early years, good books and attention to struggling students help improve learning."],["Technology and education","Computers, the internet and educational apps have expanded access to knowledge. During the COVID-19 pandemic, schools in many countries closed, and students learned online or through printed modules and television, as in the Philippines. The experience showed both the potential and the limits of remote learning."],["Lifelong learning","Education does not end with school. Adults learn new skills for work, take courses, read, travel and learn from each other. Libraries, community centers, online courses and vocational training support lifelong learning."],["Teachers","Teachers are central to education. World Teachers’ Day is celebrated on 5 October. Filipino teachers are respected at home and many teach abroad, in countries such as the United States, Thailand and Vietnam."]],
sp:["Fatima al-Fihri","In 859 Fatima al-Fihri, a young woman whose family had moved from Kairouan in present-day Tunisia to Fez in Morocco, used her inheritance to build a mosque and center of learning, al-Qarawiyyin. It grew into a famous university where scholars studied religion, law, mathematics, astronomy and medicine. It still operates today, and it is often cited, including by Guinness World Records, as the oldest existing educational institution in the world to grant degrees."],
g:[["Literacy","The ability to read and write"],["Curriculum","The subjects taught in a school"],["Vocational training","Education for specific jobs or trades"],["Thomasites","American teachers sent to the Philippines in the early 1900s"]],
f:["Al-Qarawiyyin was founded by Fatima al-Fihri in 859.","The University of Santo Tomas is the oldest university in Asia.","World Teachers’ Day is on 5 October."],
fun:"The University of Santo Tomas in Manila, founded in 1611, is older than Harvard.",
tl:"Ang edukasyon ay proseso ng pagkatuto ng kaalaman, kasanayan at pagpapahalaga, sa tahanan, paaralan at sa buong buhay. Kinikilala ito bilang karapatang pantao. Ang Unibersidad ng Santo Tomas, itinatag noong 1611, ang pinakamatandang unibersidad sa Asya."});

ADDART({id:"clothing",t:"Clothing and Fashion",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"👘",w:"Clothing",r:["silk","bangladeshcountry","phfest","photography","plasticwaste"],
p:[["More than covering","Clothing protects the body, but also expresses identity, culture and style."],["Ancient invention","People have worn clothes for at least tens of thousands of years."],["Traditional and modern","From the barong Tagalog to jeans, clothing reflects history and creativity."]],
l:"Clothing is what people wear to protect their bodies from weather and injury and to express identity, culture, status, beliefs and personal style. Humans began wearing clothes tens of thousands of years ago, sewing animal skins with bone needles and later weaving plant fibers, wool and silk into cloth. Every culture has developed its own traditional dress, and fashion, the changing styles of clothing, is today a global industry worth trillions of dollars, employing millions of people but also raising concerns about workers’ conditions and the environment.",
s:[["Ancient clothing","Studies of body lice, which live in clothing, suggest humans began wearing clothes perhaps 100,000 or more years ago. Bone needles, some tens of thousands of years old, show that people sewed fitted clothes. Flax fibers about 30,000 years old found in a cave in Georgia may have been used for textiles."],["Fibers and fabrics","Cotton was grown in India, Pakistan and Peru thousands of years ago. China produced silk from silkworm cocoons and kept the method secret for centuries. Wool came from sheep, and linen from flax. In the 1900s, synthetic fibers such as nylon and polyester were invented."],["Traditional dress","Many cultures have distinctive traditional clothing, such as the Japanese kimono, the Indian sari, the Korean hanbok, the Scottish kilt, the West African kente cloth and the Mexican huipil. In the Philippines, the barong Tagalog for men and the baro’t saya and terno for women are national dress, while Indigenous peoples have their own woven textiles."],["Fashion","Fashion refers to popular styles that change over time. Paris became a center of fashion in the 1600s and 1700s, and the haute couture industry grew there in the 1800s. Today fashion capitals include Paris, Milan, New York, London and Tokyo, and designers from around the world, including the Philippines, show their work internationally."]],
k:[["Earliest clothes","Perhaps 100,000+ years ago"],["Philippine national dress","Barong Tagalog, baro’t saya, terno"],["Piña fabric","Woven from pineapple leaf fibers"],["Blue jeans patented","1873"],["Fashion capitals","Paris, Milan, New York, London, Tokyo"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Silk is produced in China"],["1846","Elias Howe patents a practical sewing machine"],["1873","Levi Strauss and Jacob Davis patent riveted jeans"],["1935","Nylon is invented"],["1975","The barong Tagalog is promoted as national attire"],["2013","The Rana Plaza disaster draws attention to garment workers"]],
s2:[["The barong Tagalog","The barong Tagalog is an embroidered, lightweight shirt worn untucked, often made from sheer fabrics such as piña, woven from pineapple leaf fibers, or jusi. It developed during the Spanish era and became a symbol of Filipino identity. It is worn at weddings, formal events and official occasions, and is often called the national dress for men."],["The terno","The terno is a formal Filipino dress with distinctive butterfly sleeves, standing high and stiff on the shoulders. It evolved from the baro’t saya in the early 1900s. Designers such as Ramon Valera shaped its modern form, and it is worn at state occasions and formal events."],["Woven traditions","Indigenous Filipino weavers create striking textiles. The T’boli of South Cotabato weave t’nalak, abaca cloth with patterns said to come from dreams, and the weaver Lang Dulay was recognized as a National Living Treasure. Other traditions include the inabel of Ilocos, the Yakan weaving of Basilan and the malong of Mindanao."],["Blue jeans","In 1873 the merchant Levi Strauss and the tailor Jacob Davis patented work trousers made of tough denim with copper rivets at the stress points, designed for miners and laborers in the American West. Jeans later became popular with young people in the 1950s and are now worn worldwide."],["Fast fashion","Fast fashion brands produce cheap, trendy clothes quickly, encouraging people to buy more and discard clothes sooner. This has environmental costs, including water use, pollution from dyes, microplastic fibers and textile waste, and is linked to low wages and unsafe conditions in some factories. Buying fewer, longer-lasting clothes, repairing and secondhand shopping help reduce these impacts."],["Clothing and identity","Clothing communicates identity: uniforms show roles, religious dress expresses faith, and styles can signal belonging to a group or generation. Clothing also reflects climate; people in hot countries often wear light, loose clothing, while people in cold regions wear layers and furs."]],
sp:["Piña, fabric from pineapples","Piña cloth is a delicate, translucent fabric woven from the fibers of pineapple leaves, a Philippine tradition centered in Aklan. Workers scrape the leaves to extract fine fibers, which are knotted together and woven by hand on looms. Weaving a piece can take weeks. Piña was prized in the 1800s and exported to Europe. Today it is used for barong Tagalog and special garments, and the craft is being revived by weavers and designers."],
g:[["Textile","A woven or knitted fabric"],["Fiber","A thread-like strand used to make cloth"],["Haute couture","High-end, custom-made fashion"],["Fast fashion","Cheap, quickly produced trendy clothing"]],
f:["The barong Tagalog is often made from piña fabric.","Jeans were patented in 1873.","Silk was first produced in China."],
fun:"Piña cloth is woven from the fibers of pineapple leaves.",
tl:"Ang damit ay nagpoprotekta sa katawan at nagpapahayag ng pagkakakilanlan at kultura. Matagal nang nagsusuot ng damit ang tao. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang barong Tagalog na kadalasang gawa sa piña at ang terno na may butterfly sleeves."});

ADDART({id:"toys",t:"Toys and Games",c:"Sports",i:"🪀",w:"Toy",r:["chess","videogames","phsport","family","education"],
p:[["Play is ancient","People have played with toys and games for thousands of years."],["Learning through play","Play helps children grow, learn skills and make friends."],["From sungka to LEGO","Toys and games range from traditional games to modern inventions."]],
l:"Toys are objects made for play, and games are activities with rules played for fun or competition. Archaeologists have found toys and board games thousands of years old, from dolls and spinning tops to the board games of ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia. Play is important for children’s development, helping them learn physical, social and thinking skills. Every culture has traditional games, such as the Filipino patintero and sungka, while modern toys such as LEGO bricks and the Rubik’s Cube have become popular worldwide.",
s:[["Ancient toys","Children in ancient times played with dolls made of clay, wood or cloth, balls, spinning tops, rattles, marbles and toy animals on wheels. Toys have been found in ancient Egyptian tombs and the cities of the Indus Valley. The yo-yo was known in ancient Greece around 500 BCE."],["Ancient board games","Senet, played in Egypt from about 3100 BCE, is among the oldest known board games. The Royal Game of Ur was played in Mesopotamia around 2600 BCE. Mancala games, played by moving seeds or stones through pits on a board, have ancient roots in Africa and Asia; the Philippine version is sungka. Go, from China, is over 2,500 years old."],["Why play matters","Play helps children develop strength and coordination, imagination, problem-solving, language and social skills such as sharing, taking turns and handling winning and losing. The UN Convention on the Rights of the Child recognizes every child’s right to play, and the UN first marked an International Day of Play on 11 June 2024."],["Modern toys","Mass production in the 1800s and 1900s made toys widely available. The teddy bear appeared in 1902, LEGO’s interlocking plastic bricks were patented in 1958 and the Rubik’s Cube was invented in 1974. Today electronic toys and video games are popular, alongside traditional play."]],
k:[["Oldest board games","Senet and the Royal Game of Ur"],["Philippine mancala","Sungka"],["Yo-yo popularized in the US by","Pedro Flores, a Filipino"],["Rubik’s Cube invented","1974"],["International Day of Play","11 June"]],
d:[["c. 3100 BCE","Senet is played in Egypt"],["c. 2600 BCE","The Royal Game of Ur is played"],["c. 500 BCE","Yo-yos are known in ancient Greece"],["1902","The teddy bear is created"],["1928","Pedro Flores begins making yo-yos in California"],["1974","Ernő Rubik invents his cube"]],
s2:[["Larong Pinoy","Traditional Filipino games, called larong Pinoy, are played outdoors with simple materials. Patintero is a tag game where players try to cross lines guarded by opponents. Tumbang preso involves knocking down a can with slippers. Piko is a hopping game like hopscotch. Others include luksong tinik, sipa, keeping a rattan ball or weighted washer in the air with the feet; trumpo, spinning tops, and holen, marbles."],["Pedro Flores and the yo-yo","The yo-yo has ancient roots, and the toy was popular in the Philippines. In 1928 Pedro Flores, a Filipino immigrant in Santa Barbara, California, began manufacturing yo-yos, using the Filipino name and a looped string that let the yo-yo spin, or sleep, at the end of the string. His company sparked a yo-yo craze in America, and his business was later bought by Donald Duncan."],["LEGO","The Danish carpenter Ole Kirk Christiansen began making wooden toys in the 1930s and named his company LEGO, from the Danish leg godt, play well. In 1958 the company patented its interlocking plastic brick. Today billions of LEGO pieces are made each year, and LEGO is used in education, robotics and art."],["The Rubik’s Cube","In 1974 the Hungarian architecture professor Ernő Rubik invented a puzzle cube with colored squares that can be twisted and turned. It has more than 43 quintillion possible arrangements. It became one of the best-selling toys in history, and speedcubers now solve it in just a few seconds."],["Toys and the environment","Many toys are made of plastic and quickly discarded. Some companies are developing toys from recycled or plant-based materials, and families can reduce waste by sharing, swapping, repairing and donating toys. Simple toys and games, like many traditional Filipino games, need few materials at all."],["Toys and culture","Toys reflect their times and cultures: dolls dressed in local clothing, toy jeepneys in the Philippines, kites in many Asian countries and puzzles and models of modern technology. Museums in many countries, including toy museums, collect toys as part of cultural heritage."]],
sp:["The Royal Game of Ur","In the 1920s, the archaeologist Leonard Woolley excavated royal tombs at Ur in present-day Iraq and found beautiful game boards decorated with shell and colored stone, about 4,500 years old. For a long time, no one knew the rules. Then a clay tablet from about 177 BCE, written by a Babylonian scribe, was found to describe how to play. It was a race game, using dice, a bit like backgammon. Today people play the Royal Game of Ur again using the reconstructed rules."],
g:[["Toy","An object made for play"],["Board game","A game played by moving pieces on a board"],["Mancala","A family of games played by moving seeds through pits"],["Larong Pinoy","Traditional Filipino games"]],
f:["Senet is one of the oldest board games.","Pedro Flores helped make the yo-yo popular in America.","The Rubik’s Cube has over 43 quintillion arrangements."],
fun:"A Filipino immigrant, Pedro Flores, started the yo-yo craze in the United States.",
tl:"Libu-libong taon nang naglalaro ang tao gamit ang mga laruan at laro. Tumutulong ang paglalaro sa paglaki at pagkatuto ng mga bata. Kabilang sa larong Pinoy ang patintero, tumbang preso at sungka, at isang Pilipino ang nagpasikat ng yo-yo sa Amerika."});

;
/* data/orgs.js */
// International organizations: the World Health Organization, NATO and the African Union.
ADDART({id:"who",t:"The World Health Organization",c:"Economics and Society",i:"⚕️",w:"World Health Organization",r:["un","vacc","pandemic","malaria","tb"],
p:[["Health for all","The World Health Organization, WHO, is the United Nations agency for international public health."],["A global coordinator","It sets health standards, tracks diseases and helps countries respond to health emergencies."],["Office in Manila","WHO’s regional office for the Western Pacific is based in Manila."]],
l:"The World Health Organization is a specialized agency of the United Nations, founded in 1948, responsible for international public health. Based in Geneva, Switzerland, with almost every country as a member, WHO sets health guidelines and standards, monitors disease outbreaks, coordinates responses to health emergencies, supports vaccination and disease-control programs, and helps countries strengthen their health systems. Its greatest achievement is the eradication of smallpox. WHO has also faced criticism, particularly over its handling of some outbreaks, and depends on funding from member states and donors.",
s:[["Founding","After World War II, countries agreed to create a single global health agency. WHO’s constitution came into force on 7 April 1948, a date now celebrated as World Health Day. Its constitution defines health as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease."],["What WHO does","WHO sets international standards for medicines and treatments, publishes guidelines, classifies diseases, monitors outbreaks through a global network, declares international health emergencies and coordinates responses. It supports campaigns against diseases such as polio, malaria, tuberculosis and HIV, and promotes vaccination, clean water and healthy lifestyles."],["Structure","WHO is governed by the World Health Assembly, where all member states meet each year in Geneva. It is led by a Director-General and has six regional offices, including the Regional Office for the Western Pacific in Manila, which covers countries from China and Japan to Australia and the Pacific islands."],["Funding","WHO is funded by required contributions from member states and voluntary donations from governments, foundations and other organizations. Critics have noted that much of its budget comes from voluntary contributions earmarked for specific programs, limiting its flexibility. In January 2025 the United States, historically its largest donor, announced its withdrawal, which took effect in 2026."]],
k:[["Founded","7 April 1948"],["Headquarters","Geneva, Switzerland"],["Members","Over 190 member states"],["Western Pacific office","Manila, Philippines"],["Greatest achievement","Eradication of smallpox (1980)"]],
d:[["1948","WHO is founded"],["1951","WHO’s Western Pacific regional office opens in Manila"],["1980","Smallpox is declared eradicated"],["1988","The Global Polio Eradication Initiative begins"],["2020","WHO declares COVID-19 a pandemic"],["2025","Member states adopt the Pandemic Agreement"]],
s2:[["Eradicating smallpox","Smallpox killed hundreds of millions of people over centuries. In 1967 WHO launched an intensified campaign to eradicate it, using vaccination and a strategy of finding every case and vaccinating everyone around it. The last natural case occurred in Somalia in 1977, and in 1980 WHO declared smallpox eradicated, the first human disease ever eliminated."],["Fighting polio","The Global Polio Eradication Initiative, launched in 1988, has reduced polio cases by more than 99 percent. Wild poliovirus remains only in a few areas of Pakistan and Afghanistan. The Philippines was declared polio-free in 2000 but faced an outbreak in 2019, which was later ended through vaccination campaigns."],["COVID-19","In January 2020, WHO declared the new coronavirus outbreak a public health emergency of international concern and, on 11 March 2020, characterized it as a pandemic. It coordinated research, advice and the COVAX program to share vaccines with poorer countries. It was also criticized for some early communications and for its reliance on information from member states. It ended the emergency declaration in May 2023."],["Tobacco control","WHO’s Framework Convention on Tobacco Control, adopted in 2003, was the first international treaty negotiated under WHO. It commits countries to measures such as warning labels, advertising bans and tobacco taxes. It has been ratified by most countries, including the Philippines."],["The Pandemic Agreement","After COVID-19, WHO member states negotiated an agreement to better prevent, prepare for and respond to future pandemics, including sharing information, vaccines and medicines more fairly. The World Health Assembly adopted the Pandemic Agreement in May 2025, though details on some issues were left for further negotiation."],["Criticism and reform","WHO has been praised for its global role but also criticized, for example over its slow response to the Ebola outbreak in West Africa in 2014, its bureaucracy and its dependence on powerful donors. It has undertaken reforms, including creating a health emergencies program in 2016."]],
sp:["The last case of smallpox","On 26 October 1977, a hospital cook named Ali Maow Maalin in Merca, Somalia, developed the rash of smallpox. He became the last person to catch the disease naturally. WHO teams traced and vaccinated everyone he had contact with, and no further cases followed. Maalin recovered and later worked as a vaccinator in the campaign against polio. After two more years of checking, WHO declared smallpox eradicated on 8 May 1980."],
g:[["Public health","Protecting and improving the health of populations"],["Eradication","Completely eliminating a disease worldwide"],["Pandemic","A disease outbreak spreading across many countries"],["Specialized agency","An independent UN organization for a specific field"]],
f:["WHO was founded on 7 April 1948.","Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.","WHO’s Western Pacific office is in Manila."],
fun:"World Health Day, 7 April, marks the founding of WHO.",
tl:"Ang World Health Organization ay ahensiya ng United Nations para sa pandaigdigang kalusugan. Itinatag ito noong 1948. Nasa Maynila ang tanggapan nito para sa Kanlurang Pasipiko. Ang pinakamalaking tagumpay nito ang pagpuksa sa bulutong."});

ADDART({id:"nato",t:"NATO",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🛡️",w:"NATO",r:["coldwar","ww2","un","ukrainecountry","swedencountry"],
p:[["An alliance for defense","NATO, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, is a military alliance of countries in Europe and North America."],["All for one","Its key principle is that an attack on one member is an attack on all."],["Growing membership","Founded with 12 members in 1949, it now has 32."]],
l:"The North Atlantic Treaty Organization, NATO, is a political and military alliance founded in 1949 by the United States, Canada and ten Western European countries. It was created at the start of the Cold War to deter Soviet expansion in Europe. Its central commitment, in Article 5 of its treaty, is that an armed attack against one member is considered an attack against all. After the Cold War, NATO expanded to include many Central and Eastern European countries and took on new missions. It has 32 members, the newest being Finland in 2023 and Sweden in 2024.",
s:[["Founding","After World War II, Western European countries feared Soviet power as communist governments took control in Eastern Europe. On 4 April 1949, twelve countries signed the North Atlantic Treaty in Washington: Belgium, Canada, Denmark, France, Iceland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway, Portugal, the United Kingdom and the United States."],["Article 5","Article 5 of the treaty states that an armed attack against one member shall be considered an attack against them all, and each will assist as it deems necessary. It has been invoked only once: after the 11 September 2001 attacks on the United States."],["The Cold War","During the Cold War, NATO faced the Soviet Union and its allies, who formed the Warsaw Pact in 1955. The two blocs stationed large armies and nuclear weapons in Europe but never fought each other directly. The Warsaw Pact dissolved in 1991 as the Cold War ended."],["After the Cold War","After 1991, NATO took on new roles, including peacekeeping in Bosnia and Kosovo in the 1990s, leading the international security mission in Afghanistan from 2003 to 2014 and operations against piracy. Many former Warsaw Pact countries and former Soviet republics joined NATO."]],
k:[["Founded","4 April 1949"],["Headquarters","Brussels, Belgium"],["Members","32"],["Key principle","Article 5: collective defense"],["Newest members","Finland (2023), Sweden (2024)"]],
d:[["1949","The North Atlantic Treaty is signed"],["1955","West Germany joins; the Warsaw Pact is formed"],["1999","Poland, Hungary and Czechia join"],["2001","Article 5 is invoked after the 11 September attacks"],["2004","Seven Central and Eastern European countries join"],["2024","Sweden becomes NATO’s 32nd member"]],
s2:[["Expansion and debate","NATO’s expansion eastward after the Cold War has been widely debated. Supporters say countries freely chose to join to protect their independence and democracy. Russia’s government has strongly opposed expansion, viewing it as a threat. Some analysts argue expansion contributed to tensions with Russia, while others argue it protected members from Russian aggression."],["Russia’s invasion of Ukraine","Ukraine is not a NATO member, but after Russia’s full-scale invasion in 2022, NATO members provided Ukraine with weapons, training and aid and strengthened forces on NATO’s eastern flank. Finland and Sweden, long neutral, applied to join and became members in 2023 and 2024."],["Defense spending","In 2014 NATO members pledged to aim to spend at least 2 percent of their economic output on defense. Many did not meet this goal for years, a source of tension within the alliance. In 2025 members agreed on a higher target of 5 percent by 2035, including spending on related security needs."],["Supporters and critics","Supporters see NATO as a successful alliance that kept peace in Europe and deters aggression. Critics argue that it is too costly, that it can draw countries into conflicts, or that its actions, such as the 1999 bombing of Yugoslavia and the 2011 intervention in Libya, caused harm. Debate over its role continues."],["NATO and Asia","NATO is focused on Europe and North America, but it has partnerships with countries such as Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand. The Philippines is not a NATO member; its main defense alliance is the 1951 Mutual Defense Treaty with the United States."],["How NATO decides","NATO makes decisions by consensus, meaning all members must agree. Its civilian leader is the Secretary General, and its military forces are commanded by the Supreme Allied Commander Europe. Members contribute troops and equipment to NATO missions."]],
sp:["Article 5 after 9/11","NATO was created mainly to defend Europe from a Soviet attack. But the only time Article 5 has been invoked was after the 11 September 2001 terrorist attacks on the United States. The next day, NATO allies declared the attacks an attack on all members. NATO aircraft patrolled American skies, and allied forces later served in Afghanistan. Many soldiers from European and Canadian forces died there."],
g:[["Alliance","An agreement between countries to cooperate, especially for defense"],["Collective defense","The principle that members defend each other"],["Warsaw Pact","The Soviet-led alliance of the Cold War"],["Consensus","Agreement by all members"]],
f:["NATO was founded in 1949.","Article 5 was invoked only after the 11 September attacks.","Sweden became NATO’s 32nd member in 2024."],
fun:"NATO’s collective defense clause has been invoked only once, after an attack on the United States.",
tl:"Ang NATO ay alyansang militar ng mga bansa sa Europa at Hilagang Amerika na itinatag noong 1949. Ang pangunahing prinsipyo nito ay ang pag-atake sa isang miyembro ay pag-atake sa lahat. Mayroon itong 32 miyembro ngayon."});

ADDART({id:"africanunion",t:"The African Union",c:"Economics and Society",i:"🌍",w:"African Union",r:["africacolon","decol","nkrumah","ethiopiacountry","un"],
p:[["Uniting a continent","The African Union is an organization of 55 African countries working for unity, peace and development."],["From the OAU","It replaced the Organisation of African Unity, founded in 1963."],["Big ambitions","Its goals include a continent-wide free trade area and its long-term plan, Agenda 2063."]],
l:"The African Union is a continental organization of 55 member states, covering all the countries of Africa. Launched in 2002 as the successor to the Organisation of African Unity, which was founded in 1963, it is headquartered in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. The AU aims to promote unity and cooperation among African countries, defend their sovereignty, promote peace and security, democracy, human rights and economic development, and give Africa a stronger voice in world affairs. In 2023 it became a permanent member of the G20.",
s:[["The Organisation of African Unity","On 25 May 1963, leaders of 32 newly independent African countries met in Addis Ababa and founded the Organisation of African Unity, OAU. It supported liberation movements fighting colonial rule and apartheid and defended the independence of African states. 25 May is celebrated as Africa Day."],["A new union","By the 1990s, many felt the OAU had been too weak to stop wars and human rights abuses. In 2002, in Durban, South Africa, the African Union was launched with stronger institutions and a mandate to intervene in member states in grave circumstances, such as genocide and war crimes."],["Institutions","The AU’s institutions include the Assembly of heads of state, the AU Commission, the Peace and Security Council, the Pan-African Parliament based in South Africa and the African Court on Human and Peoples’ Rights. Its headquarters in Addis Ababa was built with Chinese funding and opened in 2012."],["Agenda 2063","In 2013, on the 50th anniversary of the OAU, the AU adopted Agenda 2063, a 50-year plan for an integrated, prosperous and peaceful Africa. Goals include economic growth, infrastructure, education, gender equality, good governance and a single African passport and market."]],
k:[["Founded","2002 (OAU founded 1963)"],["Headquarters","Addis Ababa, Ethiopia"],["Members","55"],["Africa Day","25 May"],["Long-term plan","Agenda 2063"]],
d:[["1963","The Organisation of African Unity is founded"],["1984","Morocco leaves the OAU over Western Sahara"],["2002","The African Union is launched"],["2017","Morocco rejoins the African Union"],["2021","Trading under the African Continental Free Trade Area begins"],["2023","The AU becomes a permanent member of the G20"]],
s2:[["The African Continental Free Trade Area","The African Continental Free Trade Area, AfCFTA, agreed in 2018, aims to create a single market across Africa, reducing tariffs and trade barriers among its members. Trading under the agreement began in 2021. By number of participating countries, it is the largest free trade area in the world, potentially covering more than 1.4 billion people."],["Peace and security","The AU has sent peacekeeping missions to countries including Burundi, Sudan’s Darfur region and Somalia, where its mission has fought the militant group al-Shabaab since 2007. Its Peace and Security Council responds to conflicts and crises across the continent."],["Coups and democracy","The AU suspends member states whose governments are overthrown in military coups, a stance meant to promote constitutional government. In recent years, it has suspended countries including Mali, Guinea, Burkina Faso, Niger and Gabon after coups. Critics say its responses are sometimes inconsistent."],["Pan-Africanism","The AU grew from Pan-Africanism, a movement for unity and solidarity among African peoples and the African diaspora. Leaders such as Kwame Nkrumah of Ghana dreamed of a united Africa. Nkrumah declared that Africa must unite, and his ideas inspired the founding of the OAU."],["A voice in the world","The AU represents African positions in international forums on issues such as climate change, trade, debt and reform of the UN Security Council. Joining the G20 in 2023 as a permanent member gave Africa a greater voice in global economic discussions."],["Challenges","The AU faces challenges such as limited funding, much of it from outside donors, conflicts in several regions, differences among member states and difficulties enforcing its decisions. Supporters argue that it has made progress in promoting cooperation and African solutions to African problems."]],
sp:["Africa Day","On 25 May 1963, in Addis Ababa, Emperor Haile Selassie of Ethiopia hosted leaders of 32 African nations as they signed the charter of the Organisation of African Unity. Many of them had led their countries to independence only months or a few years before. The day is now celebrated as Africa Day, with cultural events, concerts and discussions about the continent’s future in Africa and around the world."],
g:[["Pan-Africanism","A movement for unity among African peoples"],["Free trade area","A region where countries trade with few tariffs"],["Coup","A sudden, illegal seizure of government power"],["Diaspora","People living outside their ancestral homeland"]],
f:["The African Union has 55 members.","Africa Day is celebrated on 25 May.","The AU joined the G20 in 2023."],
fun:"By number of countries, the African Continental Free Trade Area is the largest free trade area in the world.",
tl:"Ang African Union ay samahan ng 55 bansang Aprikano para sa pagkakaisa, kapayapaan at kaunlaran. Pinalitan nito ang Organisation of African Unity na itinatag noong 1963. Nasa Addis Ababa ang punong-tanggapan nito."});

;
/* data/mathcode.js */
// Science and Numbers articles: coding, binary numbers, graphs and coordinates, trigonometry, shapes and symmetry.
ADDART({id:"coding",t:"Computer Programming",c:"Technology",i:"👩‍💻",w:"Computer programming",r:["comp","algorithm","lovelace","ai","binary"],
p:[["Telling computers what to do","Programming, or coding, is writing instructions that computers follow."],["Many languages","Programmers use languages such as Python, JavaScript, C and Java."],["Everywhere","Apps, games, websites, cars, phones and satellites all run on code."]],
l:"Computer programming, often called coding, is the process of writing instructions, called code, that tell a computer what to do. Programs are written in programming languages, which are translated into the binary instructions a computer’s processor understands. Programming powers everything from smartphone apps, websites and video games to banking systems, medical devices, cars and spacecraft. Coding involves breaking problems into steps, testing solutions and fixing errors, called bugs. Millions of people around the world, including many in the Philippines, work as programmers.",
s:[["Early programmers","In 1843 Ada Lovelace wrote what is considered the first computer program, an algorithm for Charles Babbage’s planned Analytical Engine. In the 1940s, the first electronic computers were programmed by setting switches and plugging cables; the women who programmed the ENIAC computer in 1946 were pioneers of the field."],["Programming languages","Early computers were programmed in machine code, strings of numbers. Higher-level languages made programming easier: FORTRAN in 1957 for science, COBOL in 1959 for business, C in 1972, Python in 1991 and JavaScript, created in 1995 in about ten days, which runs much of the web. Different languages suit different tasks."],["Basic ideas","Most programs use a few basic ideas: variables, which store information; conditions, which make decisions, such as if the score is over 100, show a message; loops, which repeat actions; and functions, reusable blocks of instructions. Programs are built by combining these pieces."],["Bugs and debugging","Errors in code are called bugs, and finding and fixing them is debugging. In 1947, operators of the Harvard Mark II computer found a moth trapped in a relay and taped it in their logbook as the first actual case of bug being found. Testing and careful design help reduce bugs, which can cause serious problems."]],
k:[["First program","Ada Lovelace, 1843"],["Popular languages","Python, JavaScript, Java, C, C++"],["Python created","1991"],["Beginner language","Scratch (2007)"],["Term for errors","Bugs"]],
d:[["1843","Ada Lovelace writes the first algorithm for a machine"],["1946","Six women program the ENIAC computer"],["1952","Grace Hopper creates an early compiler"],["1957","FORTRAN is released"],["1991","Python and Linux are first released"],["2007","MIT releases Scratch for young learners"]],
s2:[["Grace Hopper","The American computer scientist and naval officer Grace Hopper developed one of the first compilers in 1952, a program that translates human-readable instructions into machine code. She helped create COBOL, designed to be readable like English, which is still used in banks and governments. She popularized the term debugging."],["Learning to code","Many young people learn coding with visual tools such as Scratch, created at MIT in 2007, where they drag blocks to create games and animations. Python is popular for beginners because its code is easy to read. Events such as the Hour of Code encourage students worldwide to try programming."],["Open source","Open-source software is code that anyone can view, use and improve. The Linux operating system, started by Linus Torvalds in 1991, powers most web servers, Android phones and supercomputers. Volunteers and companies around the world collaborate on open-source projects."],["Coding in the Philippines","The Philippines has a large information technology and business process outsourcing industry, employing over a million people, including software developers. Filipino programmers build apps, websites and systems for companies worldwide, and coding is increasingly taught in schools and bootcamps."],["Programming and artificial intelligence","Programmers write the code that trains and runs artificial intelligence systems. Today AI tools can also help write code, suggest fixes and explain programs, changing how programmers work. Understanding how code works remains important for checking that programs are correct and safe."],["Careers in programming","Programmers work as software engineers, web developers, game developers, data scientists and cybersecurity specialists, among other roles. Programming also helps in science, art, music and business. Problem-solving, patience and teamwork are as important as knowing a language."]],
sp:["The first computer bug","On 9 September 1947, engineers working on the Harvard Mark II computer in the United States traced a problem to a moth trapped in one of the machine’s relays. They removed it and taped it into their logbook with the note first actual case of bug being found. Engineers had already used bug to mean a technical fault, but the story, often told by Grace Hopper, made the word famous. The logbook page, with the moth, is now in the Smithsonian Institution."],
g:[["Code","Instructions written for a computer"],["Programming language","A language for writing computer instructions"],["Bug","An error in a program"],["Loop","An instruction that repeats actions"]],
f:["Ada Lovelace wrote the first computer algorithm.","JavaScript was created in about ten days.","A moth found in a computer in 1947 became a famous bug."],
fun:"A real moth found inside a computer in 1947 is kept in a museum.",
tl:"Ang programming o coding ay pagsulat ng mga utos na sinusunod ng kompyuter. Gumagamit ang mga programmer ng mga wika gaya ng Python at JavaScript. Pinatatakbo ng code ang mga app, laro, website at marami pang iba."});

ADDART({id:"binary",t:"Binary Numbers",c:"Mathematics",i:"🔢",w:"Binary number",r:["comp","logic","coding","zero","chip"],
p:[["Just two digits","Binary is a number system that uses only two digits: 0 and 1."],["The language of computers","Computers store and process all information as binary."],["Small units","A single binary digit is a bit; eight bits make a byte."]],
l:"The binary number system, or base 2, uses only two digits, 0 and 1, instead of the ten digits of our everyday decimal system. Each position in a binary number represents a power of two. Binary is ideal for computers because electronic circuits can easily represent two states, such as on and off. Everything a computer handles, numbers, letters, pictures, sounds and videos, is ultimately stored as long strings of 0s and 1s, called bits.",
s:[["How binary works","In decimal, each place is worth ten times the one to its right: ones, tens, hundreds. In binary, each place is worth twice as much: ones, twos, fours, eights, sixteens and so on. So the binary number 1011 equals 8 + 0 + 2 + 1, which is 11 in decimal. Counting in binary goes 0, 1, 10, 11, 100, 101, 110, 111, 1000."],["Bits and bytes","A bit, short for binary digit, is a single 0 or 1. Eight bits make a byte, which can represent 256 different values, enough for a letter, a number or a symbol. A kilobyte is about a thousand bytes, a megabyte about a million, a gigabyte about a billion and a terabyte about a trillion."],["Binary in computers","Inside computer chips, billions of tiny transistors act as switches that are either on or off, representing 1 or 0. Using logic gates, they add, compare and process binary numbers at incredible speeds. Hard drives, flash memory and optical discs also store data as patterns of two states."],["Representing everything","Text is stored by giving each character a number: in the ASCII code of 1963, the letter A is 65, or 1000001 in binary. Unicode, introduced in 1991, includes over 150,000 characters from the world’s writing systems, plus emoji. Images are stored as numbers for the color of each pixel, and sounds as numbers measuring sound waves."]],
k:[["Base","2"],["Digits","0 and 1"],["Bit","One binary digit"],["Byte","8 bits"],["Values in a byte","256"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","The I Ching uses patterns of broken and solid lines"],["1703","Gottfried Leibniz publishes his account of binary arithmetic"],["1854","George Boole develops binary logic"],["1937","Claude Shannon shows that circuits can perform binary logic"],["1963","The ASCII character code is published"],["1991","The first version of Unicode is published"]],
s2:[["Leibniz and the I Ching","The German mathematician Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz developed binary arithmetic and published it in 1703. He was fascinated to learn that the ancient Chinese book the I Ching used 64 hexagrams made of broken and solid lines, which he saw as matching binary numbers from 0 to 63. He believed binary reflected deep truths about creation."],["Why not decimal computers?","Some early computers used decimal, but binary proved simpler and more reliable. It is much easier to build circuits that distinguish between two states than ten. Small errors in voltage are less likely to confuse on with off than to confuse, say, a 6 with a 7."],["Converting binary","To convert decimal to binary, divide repeatedly by 2 and write down the remainders from bottom to top: 13 divided by 2 is 6 remainder 1, 6 divided by 2 is 3 remainder 0, 3 divided by 2 is 1 remainder 1 and 1 divided by 2 is 0 remainder 1, giving 1101. Programmers also use hexadecimal, base 16, as a shorter way to write binary."],["Binary in space","In 1974 scientists used the Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico to beam a message toward a star cluster about 25,000 light-years away. The Arecibo message contained 1,679 binary digits which, arranged in a grid, form pictures of numbers, DNA, a human figure and the Solar System."],["Two-state systems everywhere","Binary ideas appear beyond computers: Morse code uses short and long signals, Braille uses raised and flat dots in patterns of six and barcodes use dark and light bars. QR codes store information in patterns of black and white squares."],["Binary humor","A popular joke among programmers goes: there are 10 kinds of people in the world, those who understand binary and those who don’t. In binary, 10 means two."]],
sp:["Counting on your fingers in binary","Using binary, you can count much higher than ten on your fingers. Let each finger stand for a binary place: your right thumb is 1, index finger 2, middle finger 4, ring finger 8 and little finger 16, and your left hand continues with 32, 64, 128, 256 and 512. Raised fingers count as 1, folded as 0. With ten fingers, you can count from 0 all the way to 1,023."],
g:[["Binary","A number system using only 0 and 1"],["Bit","A single binary digit"],["Byte","A group of eight bits"],["Hexadecimal","A base-16 number system"]],
f:["Binary uses only 0 and 1.","A byte has eight bits.","Leibniz published binary arithmetic in 1703."],
fun:"Using binary, you can count to 1,023 on your ten fingers.",
tl:"Ang binary ay sistema ng numero na gumagamit lamang ng 0 at 1. Ito ang wika ng mga kompyuter, na nag-iimbak ng lahat ng impormasyon bilang mga 0 at 1. Ang isang bit ay isang digit, at walong bit ay isang byte."});

ADDART({id:"graphs",t:"Graphs and Coordinates",c:"Mathematics",i:"📈",w:"Cartesian coordinate system",r:["alg","stats","maps","calc","nightingale"],
p:[["Pictures of numbers","Graphs turn numbers and relationships into pictures that are easy to understand."],["A grid of points","The coordinate system lets us describe any point using two numbers."],["Everywhere in daily life","Graphs appear in news, science, business, weather reports and sports."]],
l:"A graph is a visual way of showing data or mathematical relationships. The coordinate system, developed by René Descartes and Pierre de Fermat in the 1600s, uses two number lines, the x-axis and y-axis, crossing at right angles, so that any point on a flat surface can be described by a pair of numbers. This linked algebra and geometry, allowing equations to be drawn as lines and curves. Today graphs and charts, such as line graphs, bar charts and pie charts, help people understand data in science, business, news and everyday life.",
s:[["Coordinates","On a coordinate plane, the horizontal number line is the x-axis and the vertical one is the y-axis. They meet at the origin, the point (0, 0). A point is described by an ordered pair, such as (3, 2), meaning 3 units right and 2 units up. The axes divide the plane into four quadrants."],["Graphing equations","Coordinates let us draw equations. The equation y = 2x makes a straight line through the origin, rising 2 units for every 1 unit across; this steepness is called the slope. The equation y = x² makes a U-shaped curve called a parabola, the path a thrown ball follows."],["Kinds of charts","Line graphs show how something changes over time, such as temperature through the year. Bar charts compare amounts, such as rainfall in different months. Pie charts show parts of a whole. Scatter plots show whether two things are related, such as height and shoe size."],["Reading graphs carefully","Graphs can mislead. A vertical axis that does not start at zero can make small differences look huge. Charts may leave out important data or use confusing scales. Checking the axes, labels, units and source helps readers understand what a graph really shows."]],
k:[["Inventors of coordinates","René Descartes and Pierre de Fermat"],["Axes","Horizontal x-axis, vertical y-axis"],["Center point","The origin (0, 0)"],["Bar and line charts invented","William Playfair, 1786"],["Pie chart invented","William Playfair, 1801"]],
d:[["1637","Descartes publishes La Géométrie"],["1786","William Playfair publishes the first bar and line charts"],["1801","Playfair introduces the pie chart"],["1858","Florence Nightingale publishes her rose diagram"],["1869","Charles Minard draws his famous map of Napoleon’s march"],["1977","John Tukey promotes exploring data with graphs"]],
s2:[["Descartes and the fly","According to a popular legend, René Descartes, lying in bed, watched a fly crawling on the ceiling and realized he could describe its position by its distance from two walls. Whether or not the story is true, his 1637 work La Géométrie showed how to describe points with numbers, founding analytic geometry. The coordinate plane is often called the Cartesian plane after him."],["William Playfair","The Scottish engineer William Playfair invented the line graph and bar chart in 1786 and the pie chart in 1801 to show economic data such as trade and national debt. He believed that pictures of data could be understood at a glance, far more easily than tables of numbers."],["Florence Nightingale’s diagram","During the Crimean War, the nurse Florence Nightingale collected data showing that far more soldiers died from preventable diseases than from battle wounds. In 1858 she presented it in a colorful diagram like a pie chart with wedges of different lengths, called a rose diagram, which helped persuade officials to improve sanitation."],["Coordinates in daily life","Coordinates are used in maps, with latitude and longitude, in video games to position characters, in computer screens where each pixel has coordinates, in seating charts and in spreadsheets, where cells are named by column and row, such as B3."],["Three dimensions","Adding a third axis, the z-axis, allows coordinates to describe points in space, (x, y, z). Three-dimensional coordinates are used in architecture, engineering, 3D printing, computer animation and to track aircraft and satellites."],["Data in the news","News organizations use charts to explain elections, prices, health data and climate change. During the COVID-19 pandemic, graphs of case numbers became familiar worldwide. Learning to read and question graphs is an important part of understanding the news."]],
sp:["The famous map of Napoleon’s march","In 1869 the French engineer Charles Minard drew a chart showing Napoleon’s army marching to Moscow in 1812 and retreating. A thick band shows the army’s size, starting with about 422,000 soldiers and shrinking to about 10,000 by the end, with a temperature line below showing the bitter cold during the retreat. Combining map, numbers, time and temperature, it has been called one of the best statistical graphics ever drawn."],
g:[["Coordinate","A number that describes a position"],["Axis","A reference line on a graph"],["Origin","The point where the axes meet, (0, 0)"],["Slope","The steepness of a line"]],
f:["Descartes published La Géométrie in 1637.","William Playfair invented the bar chart.","Florence Nightingale used diagrams to improve hospitals."],
fun:"Legend says Descartes thought of coordinates while watching a fly on his ceiling.",
tl:"Ang graph ay paraan ng pagpapakita ng datos o ugnayang matematika sa larawan. Ginagamit ng coordinate system ang x-axis at y-axis para matukoy ang anumang punto gamit ang dalawang numero. Nakikita ang mga graph sa balita, agham at negosyo."});

ADDART({id:"trig",t:"Trigonometry",c:"Mathematics",i:"📐",w:"Trigonometry",r:["euclid","pi","maps","goldage","everest"],
p:[["Measuring triangles","Trigonometry is the branch of mathematics that studies the relationships between the angles and sides of triangles."],["Ancient and useful","It was developed by astronomers in ancient Greece, India and the Islamic world."],["From mountains to music","It is used to measure heights and distances, navigate and describe waves."]],
l:"Trigonometry is the branch of mathematics dealing with the relationships between the angles and sides of triangles, especially right triangles. Its main tools are the sine, cosine and tangent functions. Developed by ancient astronomers to calculate the positions of stars and planets, it became essential for surveying, navigation, architecture and engineering. Today trigonometry is used in physics, computer graphics, music, GPS and many other fields, because its functions describe waves and repeating patterns.",
s:[["Sine, cosine and tangent","In a right triangle, the side opposite the right angle is the hypotenuse. For one of the other angles, the sine is the length of the opposite side divided by the hypotenuse, the cosine is the adjacent side divided by the hypotenuse and the tangent is the opposite side divided by the adjacent side. Students often remember this as SOH-CAH-TOA."],["Measuring the unreachable","Trigonometry lets us measure distances that cannot be reached directly. By measuring a distance along the ground and the angle to the top of a tree, building or mountain, we can calculate its height. Surveyors use this method, called triangulation, to make maps."],["History","The Greek astronomer Hipparchus, of the 100s BCE, is often called the founder of trigonometry; he made tables of chords. Indian mathematicians, such as Aryabhata around 499 CE, developed the sine function. Scholars in the Islamic world, including al-Battani and Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, developed trigonometry as a separate field."],["Waves and cycles","Sine and cosine produce smooth, repeating wave shapes. They describe anything that goes back and forth in cycles: sound waves, light waves, ocean tides, alternating current electricity, the swinging of a pendulum and the seasons. Engineers and scientists use trigonometry to analyze and design these systems."]],
k:[["Main functions","Sine, cosine, tangent"],["Memory aid","SOH-CAH-TOA"],["Founder","Hipparchus (2nd century BCE)"],["Sine developed by","Indian mathematicians, such as Aryabhata"],["Key use","Triangulation in surveying"]],
d:[["c. 240 BCE","Eratosthenes estimates Earth’s size using angles"],["c. 150 BCE","Hipparchus makes the first trigonometric tables"],["499 CE","Aryabhata describes sine"],["c. 900","Al-Battani improves trigonometric methods"],["1200s","Nasir al-Din al-Tusi treats trigonometry as its own field"],["1856","The Great Trigonometrical Survey calculates the height of Everest"]],
s2:[["How sine got its name","The Indian word for half-chord was jya. Arab scholars wrote it as jiba, which in Arabic script, without vowels, looked like jaib, meaning pocket or fold. When translated into Latin in the 1100s, it became sinus, meaning fold or bay, which became sine in English. The name is the result of a centuries-old mix-up."],["Measuring Everest","From 1802 the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India measured the subcontinent with great precision using triangulation. In 1852 a mathematician named Radhanath Sikdar calculated that a distant Himalayan peak was the highest in the world. In 1856 its height was announced as 29,002 feet, about 8,840 meters. It was named after the survey’s former leader, George Everest."],["Measuring the Earth","Around 240 BCE, the Greek scholar Eratosthenes noticed that at noon on midsummer day, the Sun shone straight down a well in Syene, Egypt, while in Alexandria it cast a shadow at an angle of about 7 degrees. Using this angle and the distance between the cities, he estimated Earth’s circumference with remarkable accuracy."],["Trigonometry in technology","Trigonometry is used in GPS to calculate positions from satellites, in computer graphics and video games to rotate and move objects, in architecture and engineering to design roofs, bridges and ramps, and in astronomy to measure the distances to nearby stars."],["The unit circle","Mathematicians extend trigonometry beyond triangles using the unit circle, a circle with a radius of 1 centered at the origin. As a point moves around the circle, its x-coordinate is the cosine of the angle and its y-coordinate is the sine. This shows why sine and cosine repeat every 360 degrees."],["Music and sound","Every musical sound can be described as a combination of sine waves of different frequencies. The French mathematician Joseph Fourier showed in the early 1800s that complex waves can be broken into simple sine waves. This idea is used in digital music, phones and audio compression."]],
sp:["Radhanath Sikdar","Radhanath Sikdar, born in Kolkata in 1813, was a gifted Indian mathematician who joined the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India. Working on observations of distant Himalayan peaks, known only by numbers, he calculated in 1852 that Peak XV was the highest mountain in the world. The survey announced the result in 1856 and later named the peak Mount Everest. Sikdar’s careful calculations, done by hand with logarithm tables and trigonometry, made the discovery possible."],
g:[["Hypotenuse","The longest side of a right triangle"],["Sine","The ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse"],["Triangulation","Measuring distances using triangles"],["Right triangle","A triangle with one 90-degree angle"]],
f:["SOH-CAH-TOA helps remember sine, cosine and tangent.","Hipparchus is called the founder of trigonometry.","Trigonometry helped measure the height of Mount Everest."],
fun:"The word sine came from a mistranslation of an Arabic word meaning pocket.",
tl:"Ang trigonometriya ay sangay ng matematika na nag-aaral ng ugnayan ng mga anggulo at gilid ng tatsulok. Ginagamit ito sa pagsukat ng taas at layo, nabigasyon at paglalarawan ng mga alon."});

ADDART({id:"shapes",t:"Shapes and Symmetry",c:"Mathematics",i:"🔷",w:"Shape",r:["euclid","pi","fib","crystals","sculpture"],
p:[["The geometry around us","Shapes are everywhere, from honeycombs and snowflakes to buildings and art."],["Flat and solid","There are two-dimensional shapes like triangles and three-dimensional solids like cubes and spheres."],["Balance and patterns","Symmetry and repeating patterns appear throughout nature and human design."]],
l:"Shapes are the forms of objects, studied in the branch of mathematics called geometry. Two-dimensional shapes, such as triangles, squares, circles and hexagons, lie flat, while three-dimensional solids, such as cubes, pyramids, cylinders and spheres, take up space. Mathematicians study their properties, such as angles, sides, area and volume, and their symmetry, the balanced way a shape repeats. Shapes and symmetry appear throughout nature, art, architecture and engineering, from the hexagons of honeycombs to the patterns of Filipino weaving.",
s:[["Polygons","A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides. Triangles have three sides, quadrilaterals four, pentagons five, hexagons six and octagons eight. A regular polygon has all sides and angles equal. The angles inside a triangle always add up to 180 degrees, and inside any quadrilateral to 360 degrees."],["Circles","A circle is a set of points all the same distance, the radius, from a center. The distance around a circle, its circumference, divided by its diameter always equals pi, about 3.14159. Circles are used in wheels, gears, plates and countless designs because they roll smoothly and spread force evenly."],["Solids","Three-dimensional shapes include cubes, prisms, pyramids, cylinders, cones and spheres. The five Platonic solids are the only solids whose faces are all identical regular polygons: the tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron and icosahedron. Ancient Greek thinkers linked them to the elements."],["Symmetry","A shape has reflection symmetry if one half is a mirror image of the other, like a butterfly. It has rotational symmetry if it looks the same after being turned, like a starfish or a snowflake. Symmetry is found in living things, crystals, flowers and art, and is linked to beauty and balance."]],
k:[["Angles in a triangle","180°"],["Platonic solids","5"],["Honeycomb shape","Hexagon"],["Snowflake symmetry","Six-fold"],["Fractal term coined","Benoit Mandelbrot, 1975"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","Euclid writes the Elements on geometry"],["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes studies spheres and cylinders"],["1600s","Kepler studies snowflakes and the packing of spheres"],["1950s","M. C. Escher creates famous tessellation art"],["1975","Benoit Mandelbrot coins the word fractal"],["1999","Thomas Hales proves the honeycomb conjecture"]],
s2:[["Why bees use hexagons","Honeybees build combs of hexagonal cells. Hexagons fit together without gaps and use the least wax to enclose a given area, compared with other shapes that tile a flat surface. Mathematicians long suspected this, and in 1999 the American mathematician Thomas Hales proved this honeycomb conjecture."],["Tessellations","A tessellation is a pattern of shapes that fit together without gaps or overlaps, like floor tiles. Only three regular polygons can tessellate on their own: triangles, squares and hexagons. The Dutch artist M. C. Escher created tessellations of birds, fish and lizards. Islamic art features intricate geometric tessellations."],["Strong triangles","Triangles are the strongest simple shape because they cannot change shape without changing the length of a side. Engineers use triangles in bridges, roof trusses, towers and bicycle frames. The Eiffel Tower is built from thousands of triangular iron frameworks."],["Fractals","Fractals are shapes that repeat their pattern at smaller and smaller scales. Ferns, broccoli, coastlines, lightning and blood vessels have fractal patterns. The mathematician Benoit Mandelbrot coined the word fractal in 1975 and studied the famous Mandelbrot set."],["Shapes in Filipino art","Geometric shapes are central to many Filipino crafts: the diamond and zigzag patterns of Ilocano inabel weaving, the intricate designs of the Yakan and T’boli, the okir carvings of the Maranao, and the star shape of the Christmas parol, a symbol of hope."],["Shapes in nature","Nature is full of shapes: spherical raindrops and planets, spiral shells and galaxies, hexagonal basalt columns and honeycombs, six-pointed snowflakes and the symmetrical patterns of flowers and starfish. Studying these shapes helps scientists understand the forces that create them."]],
sp:["The Platonic solids","Only five solid shapes can be built using identical regular polygons with the same number meeting at every corner: the tetrahedron, made of 4 triangles; the cube, 6 squares; the octahedron, 8 triangles; the dodecahedron, 12 pentagons; and the icosahedron, 20 triangles. The Greek philosopher Plato linked them to fire, earth, air, the cosmos and water. Euclid proved that there can be no others. Many tabletop game dice are made in these shapes."],
g:[["Polygon","A flat shape with straight sides"],["Symmetry","A balanced, repeating arrangement"],["Tessellation","A pattern of shapes fitting together without gaps"],["Fractal","A shape that repeats its pattern at smaller scales"]],
f:["The angles in a triangle add up to 180 degrees.","There are only five Platonic solids.","Bees build hexagonal cells to save wax."],
fun:"Many tabletop game dice are shaped like the five Platonic solids.",
tl:"Ang mga hugis ay nasa lahat ng dako, mula sa pukyutan at snowflake hanggang sa mga gusali at sining. May mga hugis na patag gaya ng tatsulok at may mga solid gaya ng kubo. Makikita rin ang simetriya sa kalikasan at sa paghahabing Pilipino."});

;
/* data/inventions.js */
// Technology articles: paper, the compass, gunpowder, skyscrapers, tunnels, medical imaging and nuclear weapons.
ADDART({id:"paper",t:"Paper",c:"Technology",i:"📜",w:"Paper",r:["print","china","calligraphy","trees","money"],
p:[["A Chinese invention","Paper was invented in China about 2,000 years ago."],["Spreading knowledge","Cheap paper made books, letters, newspapers and money widely available."],["Still essential","Despite computers, the world uses hundreds of millions of tonnes of paper each year."]],
l:"Paper is a thin material made from plant fibers that have been separated, soaked, pressed and dried into sheets. It was invented in China, traditionally credited to the court official Cai Lun in 105 CE, though earlier forms existed. Paper spread across Asia, the Islamic world and Europe, replacing materials such as bamboo strips, silk, papyrus and parchment. Together with printing, it transformed the spread of knowledge. Paper is one of the Four Great Inventions of ancient China, along with printing, the compass and gunpowder.",
s:[["Invention in China","Archaeologists have found pieces of paper in China from the 2nd century BCE, used for wrapping. In 105 CE the official Cai Lun presented the emperor with an improved method of making paper from tree bark, hemp, old rags and fishing nets. Paper soon replaced heavy bamboo strips and expensive silk for writing."],["How paper is made","To make paper, plant fibers are beaten and soaked in water to form a pulp. A fine screen is dipped into the pulp and lifted, catching a thin layer of fibers. The water drains away, and the layer is pressed and dried into a sheet. Modern paper mills do this on huge machines that produce continuous rolls."],["Spread to the world","Papermaking spread to Korea and Japan by the 600s. Muslim lands learned papermaking in the 700s; Samarkand and Baghdad became centers, and paper helped the Islamic Golden Age flourish. Papermaking reached Spain by the 1100s and spread through Europe, where it later supplied Gutenberg’s printing press."],["Wood pulp paper","Early paper was made from rags and plants. In the 1840s, inventors developed ways to make paper from wood pulp, which made paper much cheaper and allowed mass production of newspapers, books and packaging. Today most paper is made from wood fibers and recycled paper."]],
k:[["Invented in","China"],["Traditionally credited to","Cai Lun, 105 CE"],["Main raw material today","Wood pulp and recycled paper"],["Four Great Inventions","Paper, printing, compass, gunpowder"],["Japanese paper on UNESCO list","Washi (2014)"]],
d:[["c. 100s BCE","Early paper is used in China"],["105 CE","Cai Lun presents improved papermaking to the emperor"],["c. 610","Papermaking reaches Japan"],["794","A paper mill operates in Baghdad"],["c. 1150","Papermaking reaches Spain"],["1840s","Paper made from wood pulp is developed"]],
s2:[["Paper money","China was also the first country to use paper money. Merchants in Sichuan used promissory notes, and in the early 1000s the Song government issued official paper currency called jiaozi. When Marco Polo visited China in the 1200s, he was amazed that paper could be used as money."],["Washi and origami","Japanese washi paper, handmade from the fibers of shrubs such as kozo, is strong, light and long-lasting. It is used for art, calligraphy, lanterns and sliding doors. Origami, the art of paper folding, developed in Japan. In 2014 UNESCO recognized washi papermaking."],["Paper before paper","Before paper, people wrote on clay tablets, papyrus, made from reeds in Egypt, parchment and vellum, made from animal skins, palm leaves in South and Southeast Asia, and bamboo and silk in China. In Mesoamerica, the Maya and Aztecs made bark paper called amate."],["Paper and the environment","Paper production uses large amounts of wood, water and energy. Sustainable forestry, recycling and using alternative fibers reduce its impact. Paper is one of the most recycled materials; fibers can be recycled several times before they become too short."],["Paper in the Philippines","In the Philippines, early writing was done on bamboo, leaves and bark. Paper arrived with traders and the Spanish, and the first books printed in the Philippines, such as the Doctrina Christiana of 1593, used Chinese-style printing techniques and paper. Paper crafts, such as paper lanterns called parol, are part of Filipino festivals."],["The digital age","Many people predicted a paperless office with computers, but paper use remained high for decades. Today digital documents have reduced some uses of paper, such as letters, while packaging made from paper has grown with online shopping."]],
sp:["Cai Lun","Cai Lun was a court official of the Han dynasty who, according to Chinese records, presented the emperor in 105 CE with a method of making paper from mulberry bark, hemp, rags and old fishing nets. The emperor praised his work, and Cai Lun became famous. Archaeological finds show that simpler paper existed earlier, but his improvements helped make paper practical for writing. He is honored in China as a patron of papermaking."],
g:[["Pulp","Mashed, wet plant fibers used to make paper"],["Papyrus","A writing material made from reeds in ancient Egypt"],["Parchment","Writing material made from animal skin"],["Washi","Traditional Japanese handmade paper"]],
f:["Paper was invented in China.","China issued the first paper money.","Paper can be recycled several times."],
fun:"Marco Polo was amazed that people in China used paper as money.",
tl:"Naimbento ang papel sa Tsina mga 2,000 taon na ang nakararaan. Dahil sa murang papel, lumaganap ang mga aklat, liham, pahayagan at salapi. Isa ito sa Apat na Dakilang Imbensiyon ng sinaunang Tsina."});

ADDART({id:"compass",t:"The Compass",c:"Technology",i:"🧭",w:"Compass",r:["magnet","explor","maps","china","zhenghe"],
p:[["Finding north","A compass is an instrument with a magnetic needle that points toward Earth’s magnetic north."],["A Chinese invention","The magnetic compass was developed in China and used for navigation by about 1100."],["Opening the oceans","The compass helped sailors cross oceans and explore the world."]],
l:"A compass is an instrument for finding direction. A magnetic compass has a needle made of magnetized metal that turns to align with Earth’s magnetic field, pointing roughly north and south. The compass was developed in China, first for divination and then, by around 1100, for navigation at sea. It spread to the Islamic world and Europe, where it became essential for the great voyages of exploration. Today compasses are still used by hikers and sailors, and electronic compasses are built into smartphones.",
s:[["How it works","Earth acts like a giant magnet, with a magnetic field running roughly between its north and south poles. A compass needle is a small magnet that lines up with this field. The end that points north is called the north-seeking pole. Compasses do not point exactly to the geographic North Pole, but to the magnetic north pole, which is in a different place."],["Chinese origins","Ancient Chinese people knew that lodestone, a naturally magnetic rock, attracts iron. A device called a south-pointer, a lodestone spoon on a bronze plate, may have been used for divination and planning buildings. In 1088 the scholar Shen Kuo described a magnetized needle compass and noted that it did not point exactly north."],["Compasses at sea","Chinese texts from the early 1100s describe sailors using compasses in cloudy weather when they could not see the Sun or stars. The compass reached Europe and the Islamic world by the late 1100s or 1200s. It allowed sailors to keep a steady course across open seas, helping the voyages of Zheng He, Columbus, Magellan and others."],["Magnetic declination","The difference between magnetic north and true north is called magnetic declination, and it varies from place to place and changes over time. Navigators must correct for it. The magnetic north pole moves; in recent decades it has drifted from northern Canada toward Siberia."]],
k:[["Invented in","China"],["Needle compass described","Shen Kuo, 1088"],["Used at sea","By about 1100"],["Points to","Magnetic north"],["Modern version","Electronic magnetometer in phones"]],
d:[["c. 200s BCE","Chinese texts describe lodestone attracting iron"],["1088","Shen Kuo describes the magnetic needle compass"],["c. 1119","A Chinese text describes compasses used by sailors"],["c. 1190","Alexander Neckam describes the compass in Europe"],["1600","William Gilbert explains that Earth is a giant magnet"],["1906","The gyrocompass is invented"]],
s2:[["William Gilbert","In 1600 the English physician William Gilbert published On the Magnet, showing through experiments with a magnetized sphere that Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet. This explained why compasses point north and was one of the first great works of experimental science."],["The compass rose","On maps and compasses, a compass rose shows the directions: north, east, south and west, and in-between directions such as northeast. Medieval charts used for sailing showed elaborate compass roses with lines radiating across the sea, helping sailors plot courses."],["The gyrocompass","Steel ships and electrical equipment can disturb magnetic compasses. In 1906 the German inventor Hermann Anschütz-Kaempfe developed the gyrocompass, which uses a spinning wheel that aligns with Earth’s rotation to find true north. Large ships and aircraft use gyrocompasses."],["Animals with compasses","Many animals sense Earth’s magnetic field. Migrating birds, sea turtles, salmon and some insects use it to find their way over long distances. Scientists think birds may detect magnetism through special molecules in their eyes, allowing them to see the magnetic field in some way."],["Compasses today","Hikers and orienteers use compasses with maps to navigate. Smartphones contain tiny electronic compasses called magnetometers, used by map apps to show which way you are facing. GPS, however, has replaced the compass for many navigation tasks."],["The magnetic field flips","Over geological time, Earth’s magnetic field has reversed many times, with magnetic north and south swapping places. Evidence is preserved in volcanic rocks on the seafloor. The last full reversal happened about 780,000 years ago. Scientists cannot predict when the next will occur."]],
sp:["Shen Kuo’s discovery","Shen Kuo was a Chinese polymath of the Song dynasty, a statesman, astronomer, engineer and writer. In his Dream Pool Essays of 1088, he described how magicians rubbed a needle with lodestone so that it pointed south, and how to hang it by a single silk thread. He also observed that the needle pointed slightly east of true south, the first known description of magnetic declination, centuries before it was noted in Europe."],
g:[["Lodestone","A naturally magnetic rock"],["Magnetic north","The direction a compass needle points"],["Declination","The angle between magnetic north and true north"],["Gyrocompass","A compass using a spinning wheel to find true north"]],
f:["The compass was invented in China.","Shen Kuo described the needle compass in 1088.","Earth’s magnetic field has reversed many times."],
fun:"Your smartphone contains a tiny electronic compass.",
tl:"Ang kompas ay kasangkapang may magnetikong karayom na tumuturo sa hilaga. Naimbento ito sa Tsina at ginamit sa paglalayag noong mga 1100. Tumulong ito sa mga mandaragat na tumawid ng karagatan."});

ADDART({id:"gunpowder",t:"Gunpowder",c:"Technology",i:"🎆",w:"Gunpowder",r:["china","mongol","ottoman","medieurope","alchemy"],
p:[["An accidental discovery","Gunpowder was discovered in China by alchemists seeking an elixir of life."],["Changing warfare","It led to cannons and guns, transforming warfare around the world."],["Lights in the sky","Gunpowder also gave us fireworks, used in celebrations everywhere."]],
l:"Gunpowder, also called black powder, is an explosive mixture of saltpeter, charcoal and sulfur. It was discovered in China in the 800s, probably by alchemists searching for an elixir of immortality. The Chinese used it for fireworks, fire arrows and early bombs and guns. Knowledge of gunpowder spread across Asia and to the Islamic world and Europe, where cannons and firearms transformed warfare, making castle walls and armored knights less effective. Gunpowder is one of the Four Great Inventions of ancient China.",
s:[["Discovery","Chinese alchemists experimenting with saltpeter, sulfur and charcoal found that the mixture could burn fiercely. A Daoist text from the 800s warned that some people who mixed these substances had their beards singed and houses burned down. The first known written formulas for gunpowder appear in a Chinese military book, the Wujing Zongyao, compiled in 1044."],["Early weapons","The Chinese used gunpowder in fire arrows, flamethrowers called fire lances, bombs thrown by catapults and rockets. By the 1200s, they had developed early guns, metal tubes that fired projectiles. The Mongols used gunpowder weapons in their conquests, helping spread knowledge westward."],["Gunpowder in Europe","Gunpowder reached Europe in the 1200s; the English scholar Roger Bacon described a recipe around 1267. Cannons appeared in Europe in the early 1300s. Powerful cannons could break down castle walls, as when the Ottomans used giant cannons to breach the walls of Constantinople in 1453."],["Gunpowder empires","The Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal empires, sometimes called the gunpowder empires, used cannons and firearms to build large states in the 1500s and 1600s. Firearms also played a major role in European colonial expansion around the world."]],
k:[["Ingredients","Saltpeter, charcoal, sulfur"],["Discovered","China, 800s"],["First written formula","Wujing Zongyao, 1044"],["Reached Europe","1200s"],["Replaced in mining by","Dynamite (1867)"]],
d:[["c. 850","Chinese texts mention the burning mixture"],["1044","The Wujing Zongyao gives gunpowder formulas"],["c. 1267","Roger Bacon describes gunpowder in Europe"],["1300s","Cannons spread in Europe and Asia"],["1453","Ottoman cannons breach the walls of Constantinople"],["1867","Alfred Nobel patents dynamite"]],
s2:[["Fireworks","Fireworks began in China, where bamboo stalks thrown into fire exploded with a bang, and later gunpowder-filled tubes produced bright displays, believed to scare away evil spirits. Liuyang in Hunan is still a world center of fireworks production. Fireworks are now used at New Year, national days and festivals worldwide."],["Lantaka and Panday Pira","In the Philippines, before and during the Spanish era, Muslim and other communities used bronze swivel cannons called lantaka on ships and forts. Panday Pira, a metalsmith of the 1500s, is often honored as the first Filipino cannon maker, casting cannons for Rajah Sulayman’s Manila and later for the Spanish."],["Firecrackers and safety","In the Philippines, firecrackers and fireworks are a popular part of New Year celebrations to welcome good luck, but they cause many injuries each year, especially to children. Laws and local ordinances restrict their use, and communities are encouraged to hold supervised public displays instead."],["From gunpowder to dynamite","Gunpowder was used for centuries in mining, quarrying and building roads and canals. In 1867 the Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel patented dynamite, which was more powerful and safer to handle. Nobel used his fortune to found the Nobel Prizes."],["Rockets","Chinese fire arrows were early rockets, propelled by burning gunpowder. Rocket weapons spread to India, where the Kingdom of Mysore used iron-cased rockets against the British in the late 1700s. These inspired British rockets and, centuries later, the development of modern rocketry."],["The end of castles","Before gunpowder, tall stone walls protected castles and cities. Cannons could knock them down, so builders developed new forts with low, thick, angled walls, called star forts. The walls of Intramuros in Manila, with their thick stone bastions, were built to withstand cannon fire."]],
sp:["An elixir that exploded","Chinese alchemists of the Tang dynasty spent years mixing substances, hoping to create an elixir that would grant eternal life. Ironically, one of their most important discoveries was a mixture that could cause death and destruction. A book from the mid-800s warned alchemists not to mix saltpeter, sulfur and charcoal, because the mixture flared up and burned their hands, faces and houses. That dangerous mixture was gunpowder."],
g:[["Saltpeter","Potassium nitrate, a key ingredient of gunpowder"],["Cannon","A large gun that fires heavy projectiles"],["Lantaka","A bronze swivel cannon used in the Philippines and Southeast Asia"],["Star fort","A fortress with angled walls designed against cannons"]],
f:["Gunpowder was discovered in China.","The first written formulas date from 1044.","Ottoman cannons breached Constantinople’s walls in 1453."],
fun:"Gunpowder was discovered by alchemists trying to make an elixir of eternal life.",
tl:"Natuklasan ang pulbura sa Tsina ng mga alkemistang naghahanap ng elixir ng buhay. Binago nito ang digmaan dahil sa kanyon at baril. Nagbigay rin ito ng paputok na ginagamit sa mga pagdiriwang."});

ADDART({id:"skyscrapers",t:"Skyscrapers",c:"Technology",i:"🏙️",w:"Skyscraper",r:["architecture","urban","malaysiacountry","bridges","quake"],
p:[["Reaching for the sky","Skyscrapers are very tall buildings with many floors."],["Steel and elevators","Steel frames and safe elevators made skyscrapers possible in the late 1800s."],["Record breakers","The tallest building, the Burj Khalifa in Dubai, is 828 meters tall."]],
l:"A skyscraper is a very tall, continuously habitable building with many floors, usually over 150 meters or 40 stories. Skyscrapers became possible in the late 1800s thanks to steel frames, which carry the building’s weight instead of thick walls, and safe passenger elevators. They began in Chicago and New York and spread around the world. Today the tallest skyscrapers are in Asia and the Middle East, led by the Burj Khalifa in Dubai, at 828 meters. Skyscrapers let cities house many people and businesses on small areas of land.",
s:[["The first skyscrapers","The ten-story Home Insurance Building in Chicago, completed in 1885, is often called the first skyscraper because it used a metal frame to support its weight. After a great fire in 1871, Chicago rebuilt with tall buildings, and New York soon followed. Architects such as Louis Sullivan designed skyscrapers with the idea that form follows function."],["Elevators","Tall buildings needed safe elevators. In 1854 Elisha Otis demonstrated his safety elevator in New York by having the rope cut while he stood on the platform; a brake stopped it from falling. Electric elevators followed, and today high-speed elevators can travel more than 70 kilometers per hour."],["Engineering","Skyscrapers must resist gravity, wind and earthquakes. Engineers use steel and reinforced concrete cores, foundations reaching deep into the ground and shapes that reduce wind forces. Some buildings have tuned mass dampers, giant weights that sway to counter the building’s movement."],["Race to the top","The Empire State Building in New York, at 381 meters, was the tallest building from 1931 to 1970. Later record holders included the World Trade Center, the Sears Tower in Chicago, the Petronas Towers in Kuala Lumpur, Taipei 101 and, since 2010, the Burj Khalifa. Merdeka 118 in Kuala Lumpur became the second-tallest in 2023."]],
k:[["Tallest building","Burj Khalifa, Dubai, 828 m (2010)"],["Often called first skyscraper","Home Insurance Building, Chicago (1885)"],["Safety elevator","Elisha Otis, 1854"],["Second tallest","Merdeka 118, Kuala Lumpur"],["Tallest 1931–1970","Empire State Building"]],
d:[["1854","Elisha Otis demonstrates the safety elevator"],["1885","The Home Insurance Building is completed in Chicago"],["1931","The Empire State Building opens"],["1998","The Petronas Towers are completed"],["2004","Taipei 101 becomes the world’s tallest building"],["2010","The Burj Khalifa opens in Dubai"]],
s2:[["The Burj Khalifa","The Burj Khalifa in Dubai, United Arab Emirates, opened in 2010 and stands 828 meters tall, with more than 160 floors. Its Y-shaped plan, inspired partly by a desert flower, helps it resist strong winds. Thousands of workers from South Asia and other countries built it. On clear days, its spire can be seen from about 95 kilometers away."],["Taipei 101","Taipei 101 in Taiwan, completed in 2004, was the world’s tallest building until 2010. Because Taiwan has typhoons and earthquakes, the tower contains a giant steel ball weighing 660 tonnes, suspended near the top, which sways to reduce the building’s movement. Visitors can see this tuned mass damper."],["Skyscrapers in the Philippines","Metro Manila’s skylines in Makati, Bonifacio Global City and Ortigas are filled with high-rise office and residential towers. Philippine engineers design them to withstand strong typhoons and earthquakes, following the National Building Code and seismic standards."],["Green skyscrapers","Modern skyscrapers aim to use less energy, with efficient glass, natural ventilation, solar panels and rainwater recycling. Some, like Singapore’s buildings covered in plants and Milan’s Bosco Verticale, with hundreds of trees on its balconies, bring greenery into dense cities."],["Living high","Tall residential towers house many people in cities where land is scarce, such as Hong Kong, Singapore and Manila. Supporters say they make efficient use of land and transport; critics point to costs, energy use and a lack of community spaces. Good design includes parks, shops and public spaces at their base."],["Fire safety","Skyscrapers need strong fire protection, since people may be far from the ground. They use fireproof materials, sprinklers, protected stairways and refuge floors. Disasters such as the Grenfell Tower fire in London in 2017, where flammable cladding spread flames, led to stricter rules."]],
sp:["Otis’s daring demonstration","In 1854 at the Crystal Palace exhibition in New York, the inventor Elisha Otis stood on an open elevator platform raised high above a crowd. He then ordered the only rope holding it to be cut. The crowd gasped, but the platform dropped only a few centimeters before his safety brake, spring-loaded teeth gripping the guide rails, stopped it. All safe, gentlemen, he reportedly said. His invention made tall buildings practical."],
g:[["Skyscraper","A very tall building with many floors"],["Steel frame","A skeleton of steel beams supporting a building"],["Tuned mass damper","A heavy weight that reduces a building’s swaying"],["Spire","A tall pointed structure on top of a building"]],
f:["The Burj Khalifa is 828 meters tall.","Elisha Otis invented the safety elevator brake.","Taipei 101 has a 660-tonne damper ball."],
fun:"Taipei 101 has a giant steel ball inside that sways to keep the building steady.",
tl:"Ang skyscraper ay napakataas na gusali na may maraming palapag. Naging posible ito dahil sa balangkas na bakal at ligtas na elevator. Ang Burj Khalifa sa Dubai, 828 metro ang taas, ang pinakamataas na gusali sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"tunnels",t:"Tunnels",c:"Technology",i:"🚇",w:"Tunnel",r:["rail","bridges","canals","rocks","istanbul"],
p:[["Passages underground","Tunnels are underground passages for trains, cars, water, people and utilities."],["Engineering marvels","Some tunnels run for tens of kilometers through mountains or under the sea."],["Ancient and modern","People have dug tunnels for thousands of years, from ancient aqueducts to modern subways."]],
l:"A tunnel is an underground or underwater passage, built for transport, water supply, sewers, mining or utilities. People have dug tunnels since ancient times, using hand tools and fire. Modern tunnels are built with explosives, giant tunnel boring machines and sunken tube sections. Famous tunnels include the Channel Tunnel between England and France, the Gotthard Base Tunnel through the Alps, the longest railway tunnel in the world, and subway systems beneath many cities. The Philippines is building its first subway in Metro Manila.",
s:[["Ancient tunnels","In the 500s BCE, on the Greek island of Samos, the engineer Eupalinos dug a tunnel over a kilometer long through a mountain to carry water, with teams digging from both ends and meeting in the middle with remarkable accuracy. Ancient Persians dug qanats, underground channels carrying water to dry lands."],["How tunnels are built","In hard rock, tunnels are often blasted with explosives. In softer ground, tunnel boring machines, giant rotating cutting heads, dig while installing concrete linings behind them. Underwater tunnels can be built by sinking prefabricated tube sections into a trench on the seabed. Engineers must deal with water, unstable rock and ventilation."],["The tunneling shield","In 1825 the engineer Marc Brunel, with his son Isambard Kingdom Brunel, began building a tunnel under the River Thames in London, using a tunneling shield that protected workers as they dug. Completed in 1843, it was the first tunnel known to have been built under a navigable river. The shield idea is the basis of modern tunnel boring machines."],["Subways","The world’s first underground railway, the Metropolitan Railway, opened in London in 1863. Today many cities, including Tokyo, Seoul, Shanghai, New York, Paris and Mexico City, have extensive subway systems. In the Philippines, construction of the Metro Manila Subway began in 2019."]],
k:[["Longest railway tunnel","Gotthard Base Tunnel, about 57 km (2016)"],["Longest undersea section","Channel Tunnel, about 38 km under the sea"],["First underground railway","London, 1863"],["Ancient example","Tunnel of Eupalinos, Samos"],["Philippine project","Metro Manila Subway"]],
d:[["c. 550 BCE","The Tunnel of Eupalinos is dug on Samos"],["1843","The Thames Tunnel opens in London"],["1863","The first underground railway opens in London"],["1988","The Seikan Tunnel opens in Japan"],["1994","The Channel Tunnel opens"],["2016","The Gotthard Base Tunnel opens"]],
s2:[["The Channel Tunnel","The Channel Tunnel, opened in 1994, links England and France beneath the English Channel. About 50 kilometers long, with about 38 kilometers under the sea, it has the longest undersea section of any tunnel. High-speed trains carry passengers and vehicles between London and Paris in a few hours. When British and French workers met underground in 1990, they shook hands through the gap."],["The Gotthard Base Tunnel","The Gotthard Base Tunnel through the Swiss Alps, opened in 2016, is about 57 kilometers long, the longest railway tunnel in the world. It lies up to 2,300 meters beneath the mountains. Trains can now cross the Alps faster and with less climbing, moving freight from roads to rail."],["The Seikan Tunnel","The Seikan Tunnel in Japan, opened in 1988, connects the islands of Honshu and Hokkaido under the Tsugaru Strait. At about 54 kilometers, it was for many years the world’s longest railway tunnel. Building it took decades because of difficult ground and flooding."],["The Metro Manila Subway","The Metro Manila Subway, the Philippines’ first underground railway, is being built to connect Valenzuela to Bicutan and the airport area, about 33 kilometers long, with assistance from Japan. Tunnel boring machines began digging in the early 2020s. It is expected to reduce traffic and travel times in the crowded capital."],["Water and sewer tunnels","Many of the world’s longest tunnels carry water. The Delaware Aqueduct, which supplies New York City, is about 137 kilometers long. Tunnels also carry sewage and storm water; Tokyo has a giant underground flood-control system with huge tanks that protect the city from heavy rain."],["Dangers and safety","Tunnel building was once very dangerous, with risks of collapse, flooding, explosions and bad air. Modern tunnels have ventilation systems, emergency exits, fire protection and monitoring. Tunnel workers traditionally honor Saint Barbara, their patron saint, for protection."]],
sp:["Digging from both ends","Around 550 BCE, the engineer Eupalinos of Megara directed the digging of a 1,036-meter tunnel through Mount Kastro on the Greek island of Samos to bring water to the city. Two teams dug from opposite sides of the mountain using only hand tools. When they met in the middle, their tunnels were off by only a few meters, a stunning achievement of ancient geometry and surveying. The tunnel can still be visited today."],
g:[["Tunnel boring machine","A machine that digs tunnels with a rotating cutting head"],["Subway","An underground railway in a city"],["Qanat","An ancient underground water channel"],["Ventilation","Bringing fresh air into an enclosed space"]],
f:["The Gotthard Base Tunnel is about 57 km long.","London opened the first underground railway in 1863.","The Philippines is building the Metro Manila Subway."],
fun:"Workers digging the Channel Tunnel from England and France shook hands underground when they met in 1990.",
tl:"Ang tunnel ay daanan sa ilalim ng lupa o tubig para sa tren, sasakyan, tubig at iba pa. May mga tunnel na umaabot nang sampu-sampung kilometro sa ilalim ng bundok o dagat. Itinatayo ngayon ang Metro Manila Subway."});

ADDART({id:"medimaging",t:"Medical Imaging",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩻",w:"Medical imaging",r:["surgery","curie","cancer","bones","pregnancy"],
p:[["Seeing inside the body","Medical imaging lets doctors see inside the body without surgery."],["X-rays to MRI","Methods include X-rays, ultrasound, CT scans and MRI."],["Saving lives","Imaging helps doctors diagnose diseases and injuries and plan treatment."]],
l:"Medical imaging is the use of technology to create pictures of the inside of the body to diagnose and treat illnesses and injuries. It began in 1895 with the discovery of X-rays, which revealed bones for the first time without surgery. Today doctors use many methods, including X-rays, ultrasound, computed tomography, or CT, magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI, and nuclear medicine scans. Each shows different things and has different benefits and risks. Imaging has transformed medicine, helping detect broken bones, cancers, heart disease and much more.",
s:[["X-rays","On 8 November 1895, the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen discovered mysterious rays that could pass through flesh but were blocked by bone. He made an image of his wife’s hand, showing her bones and wedding ring. X-rays were quickly used to find broken bones and swallowed objects. Röntgen won the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901."],["Ultrasound","Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves that bounce off tissues to create moving images. It is safe, as it uses no radiation, and is widely used to check babies during pregnancy and examine organs such as the heart, liver and kidneys. It was developed for medicine in the 1950s."],["CT scans","Computed tomography, CT, takes many X-ray images from different angles and uses a computer to combine them into cross-sectional slices of the body. The British engineer Godfrey Hounsfield built the first CT scanner, which scanned its first patient in 1971. He shared the 1979 Nobel Prize with Allan Cormack."],["MRI","Magnetic resonance imaging uses powerful magnets and radio waves to create detailed images, especially of soft tissues such as the brain, spinal cord, muscles and joints, without radiation. The first human MRI scans were made in the 1970s. Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield won the 2003 Nobel Prize for their contributions."]],
k:[["X-rays discovered","Wilhelm Röntgen, 1895"],["First CT scan of a patient","1971"],["MRI Nobel Prize","2003"],["Safe in pregnancy","Ultrasound"],["Uses no radiation","Ultrasound and MRI"]],
d:[["1895","Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1901","Röntgen wins the first Nobel Prize in Physics"],["1914–1918","Marie Curie organizes mobile X-ray units in World War I"],["1958","Ultrasound in pregnancy is described"],["1971","The first CT scan of a patient"],["1977","The first MRI scan of a human body"]],
s2:[["The little Curies","During World War I, Marie Curie realized that X-rays could help surgeons find bullets and shrapnel in wounded soldiers. She organized about 20 mobile X-ray vehicles, nicknamed petites Curies, little Curies, and trained women as X-ray operators. She drove some vehicles to the front herself. Over a million soldiers were examined."],["Radiation safety","Early X-ray workers did not know that radiation could harm the body, and many suffered burns and cancer. Today doses are kept as low as possible, and staff use lead shields. X-rays and CT scans use ionizing radiation, so doctors weigh benefits and risks, while ultrasound and MRI do not use it."],["Nuclear medicine","In nuclear medicine, patients receive a small amount of a radioactive substance that collects in certain tissues, and special cameras detect the radiation. Positron emission tomography, PET, can show how organs and tissues are working, such as detecting active cancer cells or brain activity."],["Imaging and artificial intelligence","Artificial intelligence systems are being trained to analyze medical images, helping detect signs of diseases such as breast cancer, lung disease and eye conditions, sometimes as accurately as specialists. Such tools are used to assist, not replace, doctors, and must be carefully tested."],["Imaging around the world","Access to medical imaging varies greatly. Many hospitals in low-income countries lack CT and MRI scanners or specialists to read images. Portable ultrasound devices that connect to smartphones and mobile X-ray units are helping bring imaging to remote areas, including island communities in the Philippines."],["Mammograms","A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast used to detect breast cancer early, when it is easier to treat. Health organizations recommend screening for women in certain age groups. Early detection through imaging has helped improve survival rates for many cancers."]],
sp:["Röntgen’s mysterious rays","In November 1895, Wilhelm Röntgen was experimenting with electricity passed through a glass tube in his darkened laboratory in Würzburg, Germany, when he noticed a nearby screen glowing. He realized that invisible rays from the tube were passing through cardboard. He called them X-rays, X meaning unknown. When he photographed his wife Anna Bertha’s hand, she reportedly exclaimed that she had seen her own death. Within months, X-rays were being used in hospitals around the world."],
g:[["X-ray","Radiation that passes through soft tissue but is blocked by bone"],["CT scan","Computer-combined X-ray slices of the body"],["MRI","Imaging using magnets and radio waves"],["Ultrasound","Imaging using high-frequency sound waves"]],
f:["Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895.","Ultrasound uses sound waves, not radiation.","Marie Curie organized mobile X-ray units in World War I."],
fun:"X-rays got their name because X stood for unknown.",
tl:"Ang medical imaging ay paggamit ng teknolohiya para makita ang loob ng katawan nang walang operasyon. Kabilang dito ang X-ray, ultrasound, CT scan at MRI. Natuklasan ang X-ray noong 1895."});

ADDART({id:"nukeweapons",t:"Nuclear Weapons",c:"Technology",i:"☢️",w:"Nuclear weapon",r:["atomicbomb","coldwar","cubancrisis","nuclear","radioact"],
p:[["The most destructive weapons","Nuclear weapons release enormous energy from splitting or fusing atoms."],["Used twice in war","They were used only twice in war, on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in 1945."],["Efforts to control them","Treaties aim to stop their spread, ban testing and eventually eliminate them."]],
l:"Nuclear weapons are explosive devices that release enormous amounts of energy from nuclear reactions: fission, the splitting of heavy atoms such as uranium or plutonium, or fusion, the joining of light atoms. A single weapon can destroy a city. They were first developed by the United States during World War II and used against Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945. During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union built tens of thousands. Today nine countries are believed to have about 12,000 nuclear weapons, and international treaties seek to prevent their spread and use.",
s:[["The Manhattan Project","Fearing that Nazi Germany might build an atomic bomb, the United States, with Britain and Canada, launched the Manhattan Project in 1942, led by General Leslie Groves and the physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer. The first nuclear test, code-named Trinity, exploded in the New Mexico desert on 16 July 1945."],["Hiroshima and Nagasaki","On 6 August 1945, the United States dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima, and on 9 August another on Nagasaki. The explosions, fires and radiation killed an estimated 150,000 to 246,000 people by the end of 1945, most of them civilians. Japan surrendered on 15 August. Survivors, called hibakusha, suffered lasting injuries and illnesses."],["The arms race","The Soviet Union tested its first atomic bomb in 1949. Both superpowers then developed far more powerful hydrogen bombs, using fusion. The Soviet Tsar Bomba, tested in 1961, was the largest explosion ever caused by humans. By the mid-1980s, the world had about 70,000 nuclear warheads."],["Effects","A nuclear explosion produces a blinding flash, intense heat that can cause fires across a city, a blast wave that flattens buildings and radiation that causes illness and death. Radioactive fallout can spread far. Scientists warn that a large nuclear war could cause a nuclear winter, cooling the climate and causing global famine."]],
k:[["First test","Trinity, 16 July 1945"],["Used in war","Hiroshima and Nagasaki, 1945"],["Countries with nuclear weapons","9"],["Estimated warheads (2025)","About 12,000"],["Key treaty","Non-Proliferation Treaty (1968)"]],
d:[["1945","The first nuclear test; Hiroshima and Nagasaki are bombed"],["1949","The Soviet Union tests its first atomic bomb"],["1962","The Cuban Missile Crisis"],["1968","The Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty is signed"],["1995","Southeast Asian nations sign the Bangkok Treaty creating a nuclear-free zone"],["2017","The Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons is adopted"]],
s2:[["Who has nuclear weapons","The United States and Russia hold about 90 percent of the world’s nuclear weapons. The United Kingdom, France and China also have them and, with the US and Russia, are recognized as nuclear-weapon states under the Non-Proliferation Treaty. India, Pakistan and North Korea have tested nuclear weapons, and Israel is widely believed to have them, though it has not confirmed this."],["Treaties","The Partial Test Ban Treaty of 1963 banned tests in the atmosphere, space and underwater. The Non-Proliferation Treaty of 1968 aims to stop the spread of nuclear weapons, promote disarmament and allow peaceful nuclear energy. The Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty of 1996 bans all test explosions but has not yet entered into force."],["Banning nuclear weapons","The Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons, adopted in 2017 and in force since 2021, bans nuclear weapons for countries that join. The Philippines is a party. None of the nuclear-armed states has joined; they argue that nuclear deterrence keeps the peace. The International Campaign to Abolish Nuclear Weapons won the 2017 Nobel Peace Prize."],["Nuclear-free zones","Several regions have agreed to remain free of nuclear weapons, including Latin America and the Caribbean, the South Pacific, Africa, Central Asia and Southeast Asia. The Treaty of Bangkok of 1995, signed by all ten ASEAN members at the time, including the Philippines, created the Southeast Asian Nuclear-Weapon-Free Zone."],["Deterrence debate","Supporters of nuclear deterrence argue that the threat of devastating retaliation has prevented war between major powers since 1945. Critics argue that the risk of accidents, miscalculation or use is unacceptable, pointing to near misses during the Cold War, and that the only safe number of nuclear weapons is zero."],["The voices of survivors","Hibakusha, survivors of Hiroshima and Nagasaki, have spent decades sharing their stories to warn the world. Their organization, Nihon Hidankyo, won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2024 for its efforts to achieve a world free of nuclear weapons and to show that they must never be used again."]],
sp:["The Doomsday Clock","Since 1947 the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists, founded by scientists who had worked on the Manhattan Project, has published the Doomsday Clock, a symbolic clock showing how close humanity is to a global catastrophe, represented as midnight. It was set at 7 minutes to midnight in 1947, moved to 17 minutes after the Cold War ended, and in recent years has been set closer to midnight than ever, at less than two minutes, reflecting nuclear risks, climate change and other threats."],
g:[["Fission","Splitting heavy atoms to release energy"],["Fusion","Joining light atoms to release energy"],["Deterrence","Preventing attack by threatening retaliation"],["Fallout","Radioactive particles spread after a nuclear explosion"]],
f:["Nuclear weapons were used in war only in 1945.","Nine countries have nuclear weapons.","The Philippines is a party to the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons."],
fun:"The Doomsday Clock has been set closer to midnight in recent years than ever before.",
tl:"Ang sandatang nukleyar ay naglalabas ng napakalaking enerhiya mula sa paghati o pagsasanib ng mga atom. Dalawang beses lamang itong ginamit sa digmaan, sa Hiroshima at Nagasaki noong 1945. May mga kasunduan para pigilan ang paglaganap nito."});

;
/* data/arts5.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 5: Baroque, Romanticism, modern art, Islamic art, Chinese art and African art.
ADDART({id:"baroque",t:"Baroque Art and Music",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎼",w:"Baroque",r:["renais","michel","musicw","opera","sculpture"],
p:[["Drama and grandeur","The Baroque was a style of art, architecture and music in Europe from about 1600 to 1750."],["Light and motion","Baroque artists used dramatic light, rich colors and movement to stir emotions."],["Built for earthquakes","The Baroque churches of the Philippines are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."]],
l:"The Baroque was a period and style in European art, architecture and music lasting roughly from 1600 to 1750. Following the Renaissance, Baroque artists sought to amaze and move viewers with drama, movement, strong contrasts of light and shadow, rich decoration and emotion. The style was promoted by the Catholic Church during the Counter-Reformation and by powerful monarchs. Great Baroque figures include the painters Caravaggio, Rubens, Rembrandt and Velázquez, the sculptor Bernini and the composers Vivaldi, Bach and Handel. The style spread to the Americas and the Philippines.",
s:[["Painting","Baroque painters used chiaroscuro, strong contrasts of light and dark, to create drama. Caravaggio painted religious scenes with ordinary people lit by sharp beams of light. Peter Paul Rubens painted energetic, colorful scenes. In the Dutch Republic, Rembrandt painted deeply human portraits, and Johannes Vermeer painted quiet scenes of daily life. In Spain, Diego Velázquez painted Las Meninas in 1656."],["Sculpture and architecture","Gian Lorenzo Bernini filled Rome with dramatic sculptures and fountains and designed the great colonnade of St Peter’s Square. Baroque buildings feature curved walls, domes, columns, gilded decoration and ceiling paintings that seem to open to the sky. The Palace of Versailles, built for King Louis XIV of France, showed royal power on a grand scale."],["Music","Baroque music developed opera, the concerto, the sonata and the oratorio. Claudio Monteverdi composed some of the first great operas. Antonio Vivaldi wrote The Four Seasons, published in 1725. Johann Sebastian Bach wrote masterpieces for organ, choir and orchestra, and George Frideric Handel composed Messiah, first performed in Dublin in 1742."],["Why Baroque?","The word baroque may come from a Portuguese word for an irregularly shaped pearl and was first used as an insult, meaning strange or overdone. Later critics recognized the period as one of the great ages of European art. Baroque art reflected an age of religious conflict, exploration, science and absolute monarchy."]],
k:[["Period","About 1600–1750"],["Painters","Caravaggio, Rubens, Rembrandt, Velázquez, Vermeer"],["Sculptor","Gian Lorenzo Bernini"],["Composers","Monteverdi, Vivaldi, Bach, Handel"],["Philippine heritage","Baroque Churches of the Philippines (UNESCO, 1993)"]],
d:[["1600","Caravaggio’s dramatic religious paintings astonish Rome"],["1607","Monteverdi’s opera L’Orfeo is performed"],["1642","Rembrandt paints The Night Watch"],["1656","Velázquez paints Las Meninas"],["1725","Vivaldi publishes The Four Seasons"],["1742","Handel’s Messiah premieres in Dublin"]],
s2:[["Earthquake Baroque","Spanish missionaries brought the Baroque style to the Philippines, but Filipino builders adapted it to a land of earthquakes and typhoons, creating massive churches with thick walls and huge buttresses, sometimes called Earthquake Baroque. Four churches, San Agustín in Manila, Paoay in Ilocos Norte, Santa María in Ilocos Sur and Miagao in Iloilo, were declared UNESCO World Heritage Sites in 1993."],["Miagao Church","The Church of Santo Tomás de Villanueva in Miagao, Iloilo, completed in 1797, is built of yellowish stone. Its facade is carved with local plants, such as coconut, papaya and guava trees, and a figure of Saint Christopher carrying the Christ Child, dressed in local clothing. It shows how Filipino artisans blended European styles with local life."],["Rembrandt","Rembrandt van Rijn, working in Amsterdam, painted portraits, biblical scenes and nearly 100 self-portraits that show him aging over four decades. His huge group portrait known as The Night Watch, of 1642, shows a militia company bursting into action, lit dramatically. It hangs in the Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam."],["Bach","Johann Sebastian Bach, born in Germany in 1685, worked as an organist and choir director, composing more than 1,000 works, including the Brandenburg Concertos, the Mass in B minor and hundreds of cantatas. Little known outside Germany in his lifetime, he is now regarded as one of the greatest composers of all time."],["Baroque in the Americas","The Baroque spread to Spanish and Portuguese colonies in the Americas, where Indigenous and African artists created richly decorated churches. In Mexico, Peru and Brazil, gilded altars and carved facades combined European forms with local motifs, such as tropical fruits and Indigenous symbols."],["Baroque music today","Baroque music is still widely performed. Some ensembles play on period instruments, such as harpsichords and gut-string violins, to recreate how the music originally sounded. Pieces such as Pachelbel’s Canon and Vivaldi’s Four Seasons are among the most popular classical music in the world."]],
sp:["Bernini’s Ecstasy of Saint Teresa","In a chapel in Rome, Gian Lorenzo Bernini created a marble sculpture, completed in 1652, showing the Spanish mystic Saint Teresa of Ávila in a moment of spiritual rapture, as an angel prepares to pierce her heart with a golden arrow. Hidden windows let light fall on the figures, and gilded rays stream down behind them. Marble seems to become soft cloth and floating clouds. Bernini even carved members of the chapel’s patron family watching from theater boxes on the side walls, turning the chapel into a stage."],
g:[["Baroque","A dramatic European style of about 1600–1750"],["Chiaroscuro","Strong contrast of light and dark in art"],["Counter-Reformation","The Catholic Church’s response to the Protestant Reformation"],["Oratorio","A large musical work for voices and orchestra, often religious"]],
f:["The Baroque period lasted from about 1600 to 1750.","Four Philippine Baroque churches are UNESCO sites.","Vivaldi wrote The Four Seasons."],
fun:"The facade of Miagao Church in Iloilo is carved with coconut, papaya and guava trees.",
tl:"Ang Baroque ay istilo ng sining, arkitektura at musika sa Europa mula mga 1600 hanggang 1750. Gumamit ito ng madramang liwanag at galaw. Ang mga simbahang Baroque ng Pilipinas, gaya ng Miagao at Paoay, ay UNESCO World Heritage Sites."});

ADDART({id:"romanticism",t:"Romanticism",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🌄",w:"Romanticism",r:["enlightenment","poetry","beethoven","nationalism","jluna"],
p:[["Feeling and imagination","Romanticism was a movement in art, literature and music that celebrated emotion, imagination and nature."],["A reaction","It arose in the late 1700s, partly as a reaction to the Enlightenment’s focus on reason and to industrialization."],["Heroes and nations","Romantic artists admired individual genius, the sublime power of nature and national traditions."]],
l:"Romanticism was an artistic, literary, musical and intellectual movement that began in Europe in the late 1700s and flourished in the first half of the 1800s. Romantics valued emotion, intuition, imagination and individual experience over strict rules and pure reason. They found inspiration in the beauty and terror of nature, the past, folklore and the struggles of individuals and nations for freedom. Romanticism influenced poets such as Wordsworth and Byron, painters such as Turner and Delacroix, composers such as Chopin and Wagner, and writers worldwide, including in the Philippines.",
s:[["Origins","Romanticism emerged partly in reaction to the Enlightenment’s emphasis on reason and order and to the growth of industrial cities. German writers of the Sturm und Drang, storm and stress, movement, such as Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, celebrated passion. The French Revolution inspired hopes of freedom and change."],["Poetry","In 1798 William Wordsworth and Samuel Taylor Coleridge published Lyrical Ballads, using everyday language to describe nature and ordinary life. Later English Romantic poets included Lord Byron, Percy Bysshe Shelley and John Keats. Romantic poets often wrote about love, longing, nature and freedom."],["Painting","Romantic painters depicted dramatic landscapes, storms and historical scenes. The German Caspar David Friedrich painted lone figures before vast natural scenes, as in Wanderer above the Sea of Fog. The English painter J. M. W. Turner captured light and storms. In France, Eugène Delacroix painted Liberty Leading the People in 1830."],["Music","Romantic composers expressed intense emotions and often drew on national folk music. Beethoven bridged the classical and Romantic eras. Composers such as Franz Schubert, Frédéric Chopin, Franz Liszt, Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky and Richard Wagner wrote passionate songs, piano pieces, symphonies and operas."]],
k:[["Period","Late 1700s to mid-1800s"],["Key values","Emotion, imagination, nature, individualism"],["Poets","Wordsworth, Coleridge, Byron, Shelley, Keats"],["Painters","Friedrich, Turner, Delacroix, Goya"],["Composers","Schubert, Chopin, Liszt, Wagner"]],
d:[["1774","Goethe publishes The Sorrows of Young Werther"],["1798","Wordsworth and Coleridge publish Lyrical Ballads"],["1818","Mary Shelley publishes Frankenstein"],["1830","Delacroix paints Liberty Leading the People"],["1838","Francisco Balagtas publishes Florante at Laura"],["1884","Juan Luna’s Spoliarium wins gold in Madrid"]],
s2:[["Frankenstein","In 1818 Mary Shelley, then a young woman of 20, published Frankenstein, about a scientist who creates a living being and is horrified by it. Written during a stormy summer in Switzerland when friends challenged each other to write ghost stories, it combined Romantic themes of nature, ambition and loneliness and is often called the first science fiction novel."],["The sublime","Romantics were fascinated by the sublime: experiences of awe and even fear before overwhelming things, such as towering mountains, stormy seas and vast skies. Paintings of shipwrecks, volcanoes and avalanches expressed both the beauty and the terrifying power of nature."],["Romanticism and nationalism","Romantics celebrated folk songs, fairy tales, legends and national histories. The Brothers Grimm collected German folktales, and composers wove folk melodies into their works. These interests helped inspire nationalist movements across Europe and later in colonized countries."],["Romanticism in the Philippines","Romantic ideals reached the Philippines in the 1800s. Francisco Balagtas’s Florante at Laura of 1838, with its tale of love and tyranny in an imaginary kingdom, is seen as a Romantic work. Juan Luna’s huge painting Spoliarium, showing dead gladiators dragged from a Roman arena, won a gold medal in Madrid in 1884 and was seen as a symbol of Filipino suffering under colonial rule."],["Goya","The Spanish painter Francisco Goya, whose work bridged the late 1700s and early 1800s, depicted the horrors of war in The Third of May 1808, showing French soldiers executing Spanish civilians. His later dark paintings and prints explored fear, cruelty and madness, influencing Romantic and later modern art."],["Legacy","Romanticism shaped modern ideas about creativity, the artist as an inspired genius, the value of personal feelings and the importance of nature. Its influence can be seen in later literature, film, music and the environmental movement."]],
sp:["Wanderer above the Sea of Fog","Caspar David Friedrich’s painting of about 1818 shows a man in a dark coat, seen from behind, standing on a rocky peak and gazing over a sea of mist and distant mountains. Because we cannot see his face, viewers imagine themselves in his place, contemplating the vastness of nature. The painting has become one of the most famous images of Romanticism, symbolizing reflection, solitude and the search for meaning."],
g:[["Romanticism","A movement valuing emotion, imagination and nature"],["Sublime","A feeling of awe before something vast or powerful"],["Sturm und Drang","A German movement emphasizing passion"],["Folklore","Traditional stories, songs and customs of a people"]],
f:["Lyrical Ballads was published in 1798.","Mary Shelley wrote Frankenstein at age 20.","Juan Luna’s Spoliarium won gold in Madrid in 1884."],
fun:"Frankenstein began as an entry in a ghost-story contest among friends.",
tl:"Ang Romantisismo ay kilusan sa sining, panitikan at musika na nagdiwang sa damdamin, imahinasyon at kalikasan. Nagsimula ito noong huling bahagi ng 1700s. Itinuturing na akdang Romantiko ang Florante at Laura ni Balagtas."});

ADDART({id:"modernart",t:"Modern Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🖼️",w:"Modern art",r:["impress","vangogh","picasso","kahlo","photography"],
p:[["Breaking the rules","Modern art refers to bold new styles from the late 1800s to the 1970s that broke with traditional ways of painting."],["Many movements","Cubism, Surrealism, Abstract art and Pop Art are among its famous movements."],["New ways of seeing","Modern artists explored color, shape, dreams and ideas rather than simply copying reality."]],
l:"Modern art is a broad term for the art produced from about the 1860s to the 1970s, when artists rejected many traditional rules of realistic representation and experimented with new ways of seeing and expressing. Movements followed one another rapidly: Impressionism, Post-Impressionism, Fauvism, Expressionism, Cubism, Futurism, Dada, Surrealism, Abstract Expressionism and Pop Art. Artists explored pure color, geometric forms, dreams, emotions and everyday objects. Modern art spread worldwide and inspired artists in Asia, Africa and Latin America, including the Philippine modernists.",
s:[["Color and expression","After Impressionism, Post-Impressionists such as Vincent van Gogh, Paul Cézanne and Paul Gauguin used color and form expressively. In 1905 Henri Matisse and others, called Fauves, wild beasts, painted with bold, unnatural colors. Expressionists such as Edvard Munch, who painted The Scream, used distorted forms to express emotions."],["Cubism","Around 1907 Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque developed Cubism, breaking objects into geometric shapes and showing them from several viewpoints at once. Picasso’s Les Demoiselles d’Avignon of 1907, influenced partly by African masks, shocked viewers. Cubism changed how artists thought about space and representation."],["Abstraction","Some artists abandoned recognizable subjects altogether. Wassily Kandinsky painted some of the first abstract works around 1910 to 1913, using color and line like music. Piet Mondrian painted grids of black lines and primary colors. After World War II, American Abstract Expressionists such as Jackson Pollock dripped and splashed paint across huge canvases."],["Dada, Surrealism and Pop","During World War I, Dada artists mocked traditional art; Marcel Duchamp exhibited a urinal titled Fountain in 1917. Surrealists such as Salvador Dalí and René Magritte painted dreamlike images. In the 1950s and 1960s, Pop artists such as Andy Warhol used images from advertising and popular culture, such as soup cans and celebrities."]],
k:[["Period","About 1860s–1970s"],["Major movements","Cubism, Expressionism, Surrealism, Abstract art, Pop Art"],["Key artists","Picasso, Matisse, Kandinsky, Dalí, Pollock, Warhol"],["Museum of Modern Art founded","New York, 1929"],["Father of Philippine modern art","Victorio Edades"]],
d:[["1893","Edvard Munch paints The Scream"],["1905","The Fauves exhibit in Paris"],["1907","Picasso paints Les Demoiselles d’Avignon"],["1917","Duchamp submits Fountain"],["1928","Victorio Edades holds a landmark modernist exhibition in Manila"],["1962","Andy Warhol exhibits Campbell’s Soup Cans"]],
s2:[["Philippine modernism","In 1928 the painter Victorio Edades, who had studied in the United States, held an exhibition in Manila of bold, modernist works, challenging the popular romantic style of Fernando Amorsolo, known for sunlit rural scenes. Edades, often called the father of Philippine modern art, and artists such as Carlos Francisco and Galo Ocampo, known as the Triumvirate, led the movement."],["The Thirteen Moderns","In the 1930s and 1940s, a group known as the Thirteen Moderns, including Vicente Manansala, Hernando Ocampo, Anita Magsaysay-Ho and Cesar Legaspi, developed Filipino modern art. Manansala blended Cubism with Filipino subjects, such as jeepneys and market vendors, in a style called transparent Cubism."],["Frida Kahlo and Mexican art","In Mexico, Frida Kahlo painted vivid self-portraits drawing on Mexican folk art and her own pain and identity, while Diego Rivera and other muralists painted huge public murals about Mexican history and workers. Their work showed how modern art could express national identity."],["Modern art in Asia","Artists across Asia combined modern styles with their own traditions. In India, Amrita Sher-Gil and the Progressive Artists’ Group, including M. F. Husain, created modern Indian art. In Japan, the Gutai group of the 1950s experimented with performance and action. In China, artists blended ink painting with Western techniques."],["Museums","The Museum of Modern Art in New York, founded in 1929, the Centre Pompidou in Paris and Tate Modern in London, opened in 2000 in a former power station, are among the world’s great modern art museums. In the Philippines, the National Museum of Fine Arts displays works by Filipino modernists."],["Is it art?","Modern art often provoked controversy. Critics mocked Impressionists, Cubists and abstract painters, and some people still ask whether a drip painting or a soup can is really art. Supporters argue that modern art opened new ways of seeing and questioning the world, and that art need not simply copy reality."]],
sp:["Duchamp’s Fountain","In 1917 Marcel Duchamp bought an ordinary porcelain urinal, turned it on its side, signed it R. Mutt and submitted it to an art exhibition in New York under the title Fountain. The organizers refused to display it. Duchamp’s point was that an artist’s choice and idea could make something art. The original was lost, but Fountain was later voted in a 2004 survey of art experts as the most influential modern artwork of the 20th century."],
g:[["Cubism","Art showing objects broken into geometric shapes from many viewpoints"],["Abstract art","Art that does not represent recognizable objects"],["Surrealism","Art inspired by dreams and the unconscious"],["Pop Art","Art based on popular culture and advertising"]],
f:["Picasso and Braque developed Cubism.","Munch painted The Scream in 1893.","Victorio Edades is called the father of Philippine modern art."],
fun:"Duchamp’s Fountain, a signed urinal, was voted one of the most influential artworks of the 20th century.",
tl:"Ang modernong sining ay mga bagong istilo mula huling bahagi ng 1800s hanggang 1970s na lumabag sa tradisyonal na paraan ng pagpipinta. Kabilang dito ang Cubism, Surrealism at Pop Art. Si Victorio Edades ang tinaguriang ama ng modernong sining sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"islamicart",t:"Islamic Art and Architecture",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🕌",w:"Islamic art",r:["islam","calligraphy","goldage","tajmahal","istanbul"],
p:[["Beauty in pattern","Islamic art is famous for geometric patterns, flowing plant designs and beautiful calligraphy."],["Great buildings","Mosques, palaces and tombs from Spain to India are among the world’s architectural masterpieces."],["A shared tradition","Islamic art developed across many lands and cultures over more than 1,400 years."]],
l:"Islamic art refers to the art and architecture created in lands where Islam was the main religion or culture, from Spain and North Africa to the Middle East, Central Asia, South Asia and Southeast Asia, from the 600s to the present. It includes mosques, palaces, tombs, gardens, manuscripts, ceramics, textiles, carpets, metalwork and glass. Islamic art is especially known for intricate geometric patterns, arabesques of flowing plant forms and calligraphy, the art of beautiful writing. Masterpieces include the Dome of the Rock, the Alhambra, the mosques of Istanbul and Isfahan and the Taj Mahal.",
s:[["Geometry","Islamic artists created intricate geometric patterns of stars, polygons and interlacing lines that seem to repeat infinitely, symbolizing the order and unity of creation. These patterns decorate tiles, wood, plaster, metal and stone. Some medieval patterns use complex mathematics that Western mathematicians described only centuries later."],["Calligraphy and arabesque","Because of the importance of the Qur’an, calligraphy is the most honored art form, decorating buildings, books and objects. Arabesques, flowing designs of vines, leaves and flowers, cover surfaces with rhythmic patterns. In religious settings, Islamic art generally avoids images of people and animals, though figurative art flourished in palaces and illustrated books."],["Architecture","Mosques typically have a prayer hall, a mihrab, a niche showing the direction of Mecca, and often a dome, minarets and a courtyard with fountains for washing before prayer. Muqarnas, honeycomb-like vaulting, create stunning ceilings. Gardens divided into four parts by water channels represented paradise."],["Arts of daily life","Islamic lands produced magnificent carpets, especially in Persia, Anatolia and Central Asia; lustrous ceramics and tiles, such as the Iznik tiles of the Ottoman Empire; enameled glass lamps; inlaid metalwork; and illuminated manuscripts. Persian and Mughal miniature paintings illustrated poems, histories and court life."]],
k:[["Key features","Geometry, arabesque, calligraphy"],["Early masterpiece","Dome of the Rock, Jerusalem (691–692)"],["Famous palace","Alhambra, Granada, Spain"],["Famous tomb","Taj Mahal, India"],["Honeycomb vaulting","Muqarnas"]],
d:[["691–692","The Dome of the Rock is completed in Jerusalem"],["785","Construction of the Great Mosque of Córdoba begins"],["1200s–1300s","The Alhambra is built in Granada"],["1557","The Süleymaniye Mosque is completed in Istanbul"],["1611–1629","The Shah Mosque is built in Isfahan"],["1653","The Taj Mahal is completed"]],
s2:[["The Dome of the Rock","Completed in 691 or 692 in Jerusalem, the Dome of the Rock is one of the oldest surviving Islamic monuments. Its golden dome rises over an octagonal building decorated with mosaics and tiles. It stands over a rock sacred to Muslims and Jews, linked in Islamic tradition to the Prophet Muhammad’s night journey."],["The Alhambra","The Alhambra, a palace and fortress complex in Granada, Spain, was built mainly by the Nasrid rulers in the 1200s and 1300s. Its courtyards, such as the Court of the Lions, are surrounded by delicate arches, carved stucco, colorful tiles and muqarnas ceilings, with fountains and pools reflecting the buildings. Poems are carved into its walls."],["Isfahan","Isfahan in Iran was the capital of the Safavid dynasty. Shah Abbas I created a vast square surrounded by the Shah Mosque, covered in blue and turquoise tiles, the Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque and a palace. A Persian saying called Isfahan half the world because of its splendor."],["Mimar Sinan","Mimar Sinan, chief architect of the Ottoman Empire in the 1500s, designed hundreds of buildings, including mosques, bridges, baths and schools. His masterpieces include the Süleymaniye Mosque in Istanbul and the Selimiye Mosque in Edirne, whose great domes rival that of Hagia Sophia."],["Islamic art in the Philippines","In the southern Philippines, Muslim communities developed distinctive art. The Maranao are famous for okir, flowing carved designs on wood and metal, and the colorful torogan, the house of a datu. Mosques range from historic ones, such as the Sheikh Karimul Makhdum Mosque in Simunul, Tawi-Tawi, traditionally dated to 1380 as the oldest in the Philippines, to modern ones like the Grand Mosque of Cotabato."],["Modern Islamic architecture","Contemporary architects continue Islamic traditions in new forms. The Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque in Abu Dhabi, completed in 2007, has 82 domes and one of the world’s largest hand-knotted carpets. The Museum of Islamic Art in Doha, designed by I. M. Pei, opened in 2008."]],
sp:["The oldest mosque in the Philippines","On the island of Simunul in Tawi-Tawi stands the Sheikh Karimul Makhdum Mosque, traditionally said to have been founded in 1380 by an Arab missionary, Karimul Makhdum, who helped spread Islam in the southern Philippines. Though rebuilt over the centuries, some original wooden pillars are said to survive. It is regarded as the oldest mosque in the Philippines and has been declared a National Historical Landmark."],
g:[["Arabesque","A flowing design of plant forms"],["Mihrab","A niche in a mosque showing the direction of Mecca"],["Minaret","A tall tower of a mosque"],["Muqarnas","Honeycomb-like decorative vaulting"]],
f:["The Dome of the Rock was completed around 691–692.","The Alhambra is in Granada, Spain.","The Sheikh Karimul Makhdum Mosque is the oldest in the Philippines."],
fun:"A Persian saying calls the city of Isfahan half the world because of its beauty.",
tl:"Kilala ang sining Islamiko sa mga disenyong heometriko, arabesque at kaligrapiya. Kabilang sa mga obra maestra ang Alhambra at Taj Mahal. Sa Pilipinas, makikita ang okir ng Maranao at ang pinakamatandang moske sa Simunul, Tawi-Tawi."});

ADDART({id:"chineseart",t:"Chinese Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🏞️",w:"Chinese art",r:["calligraphy","china","sculpture","chinesephil","paper"],
p:[["Thousands of years","Chinese art has a continuous history of more than 5,000 years."],["Ink and brush","Landscape painting with ink and brush is one of its most celebrated forms."],["Porcelain and jade","China is famous for porcelain, jade carving, bronze vessels and silk."]],
l:"Chinese art is one of the world’s oldest continuous artistic traditions, stretching back more than 5,000 years. It includes painting, calligraphy, ceramics, jade carving, bronze casting, sculpture, lacquerware, silk textiles and architecture. Chinese painters developed landscape painting, shan shui, mountains and water, as a way of expressing harmony between people and nature. Chinese porcelain was so prized around the world that china became an English word for fine dishes. Chinese art strongly influenced Korea, Japan, Vietnam and beyond.",
s:[["Ancient arts","Neolithic peoples in China made painted pottery and carved jade discs and tubes as early as about 3000 BCE. During the Shang and Zhou dynasties, from about 1600 BCE, artisans cast magnificent bronze ritual vessels decorated with patterns and mythical animals. The Terracotta Army of the first emperor, from about 210 BCE, shows great skill in sculpture."],["Painting","Chinese painters used brush and ink on silk or paper, often in long hanging or handscrolls. From the Song dynasty, landscape painting reached great heights; Fan Kuan’s Travelers among Mountains and Streams, from about 1000, shows a towering mountain over tiny travelers. Paintings often included poems and calligraphy, combining the three perfections."],["Porcelain","Chinese potters developed porcelain, a hard, white, translucent ceramic, perfected over centuries. Jingdezhen became the porcelain capital. Blue-and-white porcelain of the Ming dynasty, painted with cobalt blue, was exported across Asia, the Middle East and Europe. Archaeologists have found Chinese porcelain at many sites in the Philippines, evidence of centuries of trade."],["Jade and silk","Jade, a hard stone associated with purity and immortality, has been carved in China for thousands of years into ornaments, ritual objects and sculptures. Silk weaving and embroidery produced beautiful robes and banners, traded along the Silk Road."]],
k:[["History","More than 5,000 years"],["Famous painting form","Shan shui (landscape)"],["Porcelain capital","Jingdezhen"],["Three perfections","Painting, poetry and calligraphy"],["Famous scroll","Along the River During the Qingming Festival"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Jade objects are carved by the Liangzhu culture"],["c. 1200 BCE","Shang dynasty bronze vessels are cast"],["c. 210 BCE","The Terracotta Army is buried"],["c. 1000","Fan Kuan paints Travelers among Mountains and Streams"],["1100s","Zhang Zeduan paints Along the River During the Qingming Festival"],["1300s–1600s","Ming blue-and-white porcelain is exported worldwide"]],
s2:[["Along the River During the Qingming Festival","This handscroll, attributed to Zhang Zeduan of the Song dynasty in the 1100s, is over 5 meters long and shows life in a bustling city, with hundreds of people, boats, bridges, shops and animals in remarkable detail. It is one of the most famous Chinese paintings and has been copied many times."],["Chinese gardens","Chinese gardens, such as the classical gardens of Suzhou, are designed like three-dimensional landscape paintings, with rocks, water, plants and pavilions arranged to create changing views. They express ideas of harmony with nature and are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."],["Porcelain in the Philippines","For centuries, Chinese junks brought porcelain, silk and other goods to Philippine trading ports. Chinese ceramics have been found in burial sites across the islands, such as in Calatagan, Batangas. Manila later became a hub of the galleon trade, shipping Chinese porcelain and silk to Mexico and beyond."],["Qi Baishi","Qi Baishi, who lived from 1864 to 1957, was a self-taught painter who began as a carpenter. He painted shrimp, crabs, flowers and insects with lively, simple brushstrokes, combining traditional ink painting with a fresh, playful style. His works are among the most valuable Chinese paintings ever sold."],["Contemporary Chinese art","Contemporary Chinese artists work in many forms, from ink painting to installation and video. Some, such as Xu Bing and Cai Guo-Qiang, known for gunpowder drawings and fireworks, gained international fame. Ai Weiwei became known both for his art and for his criticism of the Chinese government."],["Influence abroad","Chinese art influenced Korea, Japan and Vietnam, which developed their own traditions of ink painting, ceramics and calligraphy. In Europe, a fashion for Chinese-style designs, called chinoiserie, swept through palaces and homes in the 1700s, and Europeans long tried to discover the secret of porcelain."]],
sp:["The secret of porcelain","For centuries, Europeans imported Chinese porcelain at great expense but could not make it themselves. They called it white gold. In the early 1700s, the alchemist Johann Friedrich Böttger, working for the ruler of Saxony, finally discovered how to make true hard-paste porcelain using kaolin clay, the key ingredient used in China. The Meissen porcelain factory was founded in 1710. Until then, porcelain was so linked to China that the English simply called it china."],
g:[["Shan shui","Chinese landscape painting of mountains and water"],["Porcelain","A hard, white, translucent ceramic"],["Handscroll","A long horizontal painting viewed by unrolling"],["Chinoiserie","European imitation of Chinese styles"]],
f:["Chinese art has a history of over 5,000 years.","Jingdezhen is China’s porcelain capital.","Chinese porcelain has been found at many sites in the Philippines."],
fun:"Porcelain was so linked to China that the English simply called it china.",
tl:"Ang sining Tsino ay may mahigit 5,000 taong kasaysayan. Tanyag ang pagpipinta ng tanawin gamit ang tinta at pinsel, pati ang porselana, jade at seda. Natagpuan ang porselanang Tsino sa maraming lugar sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"africanart",t:"African Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎭",w:"African art",r:["caveart","sculpture","africacolon","nigeriacountry","ghanacountry"],
p:[["Rich and diverse","African art includes the sculptures, masks, textiles and paintings of a continent of thousands of cultures."],["Ancient roots","Some of the oldest art in the world comes from Africa."],["Global influence","African art inspired modern artists such as Picasso and Matisse."]],
l:"African art is the art of the peoples of Africa, one of the world’s most varied and ancient artistic traditions. It includes rock paintings tens of thousands of years old, the terracotta sculptures of Nok, the bronze heads of Ife and Benin, carved masks and figures used in ceremonies, colorful textiles such as kente, beadwork, pottery, architecture and contemporary art. Much traditional African art was made for religious, social and political purposes, linking people with ancestors, spirits and community. African art has influenced artists worldwide.",
s:[["Ancient art","Engraved pieces of ochre from Blombos Cave in South Africa, about 75,000 years old, are among the oldest known examples of symbolic art. Rock art in the Sahara and southern Africa shows animals, hunters and dancers. The Nok culture of Nigeria, from about 500 BCE, produced striking terracotta heads with elaborate hairstyles."],["Ife and Benin","Artists of Ife, a Yoruba city in present-day Nigeria, cast remarkably lifelike bronze and terracotta heads between the 1100s and 1400s. The Kingdom of Benin produced thousands of brass plaques and sculptures recording kings and court life. These works demonstrate sophisticated metal-casting skills."],["Masks and sculpture","Across West and Central Africa, carved wooden masks and figures are used in ceremonies, dances, initiations, harvest festivals and funerals. A mask comes alive when worn by a dancer with costume, music and movement. Figures may represent ancestors, spirits or ideals of beauty and character."],["Textiles and design","African textiles include the kente cloth of the Asante and Ewe of Ghana, woven in narrow strips with symbolic patterns; bogolanfini, mud cloth from Mali; adire, indigo-dyed cloth of the Yoruba; and kanga cloths of East Africa. Ndebele women in southern Africa paint their houses with bold geometric designs."]],
k:[["Oldest symbolic art","Blombos Cave ochre, about 75,000 years old"],["Ancient sculpture","Nok terracottas (from about 500 BCE)"],["Famous bronzes","Ife heads and Benin Bronzes"],["Famous textile","Kente cloth (Ghana)"],["Contemporary artist","El Anatsui"]],
d:[["c. 75,000 years ago","Engraved ochre is made at Blombos Cave"],["c. 500 BCE","The Nok culture makes terracotta sculptures"],["1100s–1400s","Ife artists cast naturalistic heads"],["1897","British forces loot the Benin Bronzes"],["1907","African masks influence Picasso’s Cubism"],["2022","Germany begins returning Benin Bronzes to Nigeria"]],
s2:[["Influence on modern art","In the early 1900s, European artists such as Pablo Picasso, Henri Matisse and others saw African masks and sculptures in Paris and were inspired by their bold forms and abstraction. This influence shaped Cubism and other modern movements, though at the time African artworks were often displayed without credit to their makers or understanding of their meanings."],["Returning treasures","During colonial times, many African artworks were taken by force, such as the Benin Bronzes looted by British troops in 1897. African countries have long called for their return. Since the late 2010s, museums in Germany, France, the United Kingdom and the United States have begun returning some objects."],["El Anatsui","The Ghanaian artist El Anatsui, working in Nigeria, creates huge shimmering sculptures that hang like tapestries, made from thousands of aluminum bottle caps and pieces of metal sewn together with copper wire. His works, which refer to history, trade and consumption, are displayed in major museums worldwide."],["Great Zimbabwe","The ruins of Great Zimbabwe, a stone city built between the 1100s and 1400s, include massive walls built without mortar. Carved soapstone birds found there became a national symbol and appear on Zimbabwe’s flag."],["Ethiopian art","Ethiopia has a unique tradition of Christian art, including illuminated manuscripts, painted icons and rock-cut churches, such as those of Lalibela, carved from solid rock in the 1100s and 1200s. Ethiopian painting often shows figures with large, expressive eyes."],["Contemporary African art","African artists today work in painting, photography, sculpture, film, fashion and digital art, exploring identity, history, cities and politics. The Zeitz Museum of Contemporary Art Africa opened in Cape Town in 2017, and art fairs and biennials in Dakar, Lagos and Marrakech showcase African artists."]],
sp:["The Ife heads","In 1938 workers digging the foundations of a house in Ife, Nigeria, found a group of bronze heads of astonishing beauty and realism, with fine facial lines and calm expressions. Some European experts at first refused to believe Africans had made them, wrongly suggesting outside influence. Scientific study confirmed they were made by Ife artists between about the 1100s and 1400s, using the lost-wax technique. They are now recognized as masterpieces of world art."],
g:[["Terracotta","Fired clay used for sculpture"],["Kente","A colorful woven cloth of Ghana"],["Mask","A face covering used in ceremonies and dances"],["Restitution","Returning objects to their rightful owners"]],
f:["Nok terracottas date from about 500 BCE.","African masks influenced Picasso.","El Anatsui makes sculptures from bottle caps."],
fun:"Great Zimbabwe’s carved soapstone bird appears on the flag of Zimbabwe.",
tl:"Ang sining Aprikano ay binubuo ng mga eskultura, maskara, tela at pinta ng libu-libong kultura sa Aprika. Ilan sa pinakamatandang sining sa mundo ay mula rito. Naging inspirasyon ito ng mga modernong pintor gaya ni Picasso."});

;
/* data/arts6.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 6: rock and pop music, K-pop, Indian classical music and dance, Bollywood and Asian theatre.
ADDART({id:"rockpop",t:"Rock and Pop Music",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎸",w:"Rock music",r:["beatles","blues","guitar","opm","jazz"],
p:[["Music of the young","Rock and roll exploded in the 1950s and became the sound of a generation."],["Pop for everyone","Pop music, short for popular music, aims for catchy songs that appeal to wide audiences."],["Filipino bands","Filipino bands such as the Eraserheads built a strong local rock tradition."]],
l:"Rock and pop music are among the most popular forms of music in the world. Rock and roll emerged in the United States in the early 1950s, combining African American blues, rhythm and blues and gospel with country music. It grew into rock, with electric guitars, bass, drums and powerful vocals, spawning many styles such as punk, metal and alternative. Pop music, short for popular music, focuses on catchy melodies and appeals to broad audiences. Both spread worldwide through radio, records, television, music videos and streaming, and artists everywhere, including in the Philippines, created their own versions.",
s:[["Rock and roll","In the early 1950s, artists such as Chuck Berry, Little Richard, Fats Domino and Bo Diddley, building on Black musical traditions, created rock and roll. Elvis Presley became its biggest star after 1956, with hits such as Heartbreak Hotel. Disc jockeys and radio spread the music to teenagers, and rock and roll became a symbol of youth culture."],["The British Invasion and the 1960s","In 1964 the Beatles arrived in the United States, leading a British Invasion of bands such as the Rolling Stones and the Who. The 1960s brought folk rock, psychedelic rock and guitar heroes such as Jimi Hendrix. The Woodstock festival of 1969 drew hundreds of thousands of people."],["Many styles","In the 1970s, rock divided into many styles: hard rock and heavy metal with bands such as Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath, the theatrical rock of Queen, punk rock with its fast, raw sound and later new wave. The 1990s brought grunge, led by Nirvana, and alternative rock."],["Pop stars","Pop music has produced global stars. Michael Jackson’s album Thriller, released in 1982, became the best-selling album of all time. Madonna, Whitney Houston, Beyoncé, Adele and Taylor Swift are among the best-selling artists. Music videos, MTV, launched in 1981, and later streaming platforms changed how people discovered music."]],
k:[["Origins","United States, early 1950s"],["Roots","Blues, rhythm and blues, gospel, country"],["Best-selling album","Michael Jackson’s Thriller (1982)"],["Famous festival","Woodstock, 1969"],["Pinoy rock pioneers","Juan de la Cruz Band"]],
d:[["1955","Chuck Berry releases Maybellene"],["1956","Elvis Presley becomes a star"],["1964","The Beatles lead the British Invasion"],["1969","The Woodstock festival"],["1981","MTV begins broadcasting"],["1982","Michael Jackson releases Thriller"]],
s2:[["Pinoy rock","Filipino rock grew from the 1960s and 1970s. The Juan de la Cruz Band, with songs such as Himig Natin, pioneered rock in Filipino in the early 1970s. In the 1990s, the Eraserheads, often called the Beatles of the Philippines, led a wave of Pinoy rock bands, including Rivermaya, Parokya ni Edgar and Sugarfree. Rock remains a major part of Original Pilipino Music."],["Rock and society","Rock music has often been linked to rebellion and social change, with songs about civil rights, war, freedom and everyday life. In some countries, rock was banned or restricted by governments. Rock concerts such as Live Aid in 1985 raised money for famine relief in Ethiopia."],["Women in rock and pop","Women have shaped rock and pop from the beginning. Sister Rosetta Tharpe, a gospel singer who played electric guitar in the 1930s and 1940s, is called the godmother of rock and roll. Later stars include Aretha Franklin, Janis Joplin, Joan Jett and many others."],["Streaming and global pop","Since the 2010s, streaming services have made music from all over the world available instantly. Artists from South Korea, Latin America, Nigeria and the Philippines reach global audiences. Taylor Swift’s Eras Tour, from 2023 to 2024, became the highest-grossing concert tour in history."],["Music technology","Rock was powered by the electric guitar and amplifier. Later technologies, such as synthesizers, drum machines, digital recording and auto-tune, shaped new sounds. Home recording and online platforms now allow musicians to create and share music without major record labels."],["Rock and roll hall of fame","The Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in Cleveland, Ohio, opened in 1995, honors influential musicians. Debates about who should be included reflect wider questions about which music counts as rock and how to recognize the African American artists who created it."]],
sp:["The Eraserheads","The Eraserheads formed in 1989 among students at the University of the Philippines Diliman. Ely Buendia, Raymund Marasigan, Buddy Zabala and Marcus Adoro wrote witty, heartfelt songs in Filipino and English about student life, love and Philippine society. Albums such as Ultraelectromagneticpop! of 1993 and Cutterpillow of 1995 sold hugely, and songs like Ang Huling El Bimbo became anthems. A reunion concert in 2022 drew tens of thousands of fans."],
g:[["Rock and roll","A music style of the 1950s combining blues and country"],["Pop music","Popular music with catchy melodies"],["Riff","A short repeated musical phrase"],["Pinoy rock","Filipino rock music"]],
f:["Rock and roll began in the United States in the 1950s.","Thriller is the best-selling album of all time.","The Eraserheads formed at UP Diliman."],
fun:"Sister Rosetta Tharpe, a gospel guitarist, is called the godmother of rock and roll.",
tl:"Ang rock at pop ay kabilang sa pinakapopular na musika sa mundo. Sumibol ang rock and roll sa Amerika noong 1950s. Sa Pilipinas, kilala ang Juan de la Cruz Band at ang Eraserheads sa Pinoy rock."});

ADDART({id:"kpop",t:"K-pop and the Korean Wave",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎤",w:"K-pop",r:["southkoreacountry","rockpop","tv","korea","socialmedia"],
p:[["Korean pop","K-pop is popular music from South Korea, known for catchy songs, dazzling choreography and polished videos."],["A global wave","K-pop is part of Hallyu, the worldwide spread of Korean music, dramas, films and culture."],["Fans everywhere","K-pop has millions of fans worldwide, including in the Philippines."]],
l:"K-pop is a genre of popular music from South Korea, combining pop, hip hop, rock, electronic and R&B influences with highly synchronized dance routines, colorful visuals and elaborate music videos. Most K-pop stars, called idols, are trained for years by entertainment companies before debuting in groups. Since the late 1990s, K-pop has spread across Asia and the world as part of the Korean Wave, or Hallyu, along with Korean dramas, films, food and fashion. Groups such as BTS and BLACKPINK have topped global charts.",
s:[["Origins","Modern K-pop is often traced to 1992, when the group Seo Taiji and Boys mixed rap, rock and dance music, changing Korean pop. In the mid-1990s, entertainment companies such as SM Entertainment, YG and JYP began recruiting and training young performers, creating groups such as H.O.T. that became hugely popular."],["The idol system","K-pop agencies recruit trainees, often as teenagers, and train them for years in singing, dancing, languages and media skills before debut. Groups often have members with specific roles, such as leader, main vocalist, main dancer and rapper. The system produces polished performances but has been criticized for its demanding schedules and pressure on young artists."],["The Korean Wave","From the late 1990s, Korean dramas and music became popular in China, Japan and Southeast Asia. This Hallyu, Korean Wave, grew through YouTube and social media. Korean films such as Parasite, which won the Academy Award for Best Picture in 2020, and series such as Squid Game reached global audiences."],["Global breakthroughs","In 2012 PSY’s Gangnam Style became the first YouTube video to reach a billion views. BTS, which debuted in 2013, became the first Korean act to top the US Billboard Hot 100 with Dynamite in 2020 and addressed the United Nations. BLACKPINK headlined the Coachella festival in 2023."]],
k:[["Origin","South Korea"],["Wider movement","Hallyu, the Korean Wave"],["Early milestone","Seo Taiji and Boys, 1992"],["First YouTube video to 1 billion views","Gangnam Style, 2012"],["First Korean Billboard Hot 100 number one","BTS, Dynamite, 2020"]],
d:[["1992","Seo Taiji and Boys transform Korean pop"],["1995","SM Entertainment is founded"],["2012","Gangnam Style reaches a billion views"],["2013","BTS debuts"],["2020","BTS tops the Billboard Hot 100; Parasite wins Best Picture"],["2023","BLACKPINK headlines Coachella"]],
s2:[["Fandoms","K-pop fans form highly organized communities, called fandoms, such as BTS’s ARMY. They stream songs, vote in award shows, translate content, raise money for charity and celebrate idols’ birthdays with events. Fans use social media to connect across countries and languages."],["K-pop in the Philippines","K-pop and Korean dramas are hugely popular in the Philippines, with sold-out concerts, fan clubs and dance groups. Korean culture has influenced Filipino fashion, food and language. Filipino idols have also joined K-pop groups, and K-pop inspired the rise of P-pop, Filipino pop groups such as SB19, which became the first Filipino act nominated at the Billboard Music Awards in 2021."],["Choreography","Synchronized group choreography is a hallmark of K-pop. Fans learn dances from videos and perform covers, sometimes in public flash mobs. Dance practice videos showing groups rehearsing are popular online."],["Soft power","The South Korean government has supported the cultural industries as a source of soft power, the ability to attract and influence others through culture. Hallyu has boosted tourism, interest in the Korean language and exports of Korean food, cosmetics and fashion."],["Criticism and challenges","The K-pop industry has faced criticism over long, restrictive contracts, intense training, pressure on appearance, lack of privacy and the mental health of idols. Some artists have spoken out, and reforms have been introduced, but debate continues."],["Korean culture abroad","K-pop has sparked global interest in learning Korean, eating Korean food such as kimchi and tteokbokki, and watching Korean dramas. In 2025 the animated film KPop Demon Hunters, about a fictional K-pop group, became a worldwide hit, showing the genre’s reach."]],
sp:["Gangnam Style","In July 2012 the South Korean singer PSY released Gangnam Style, a satirical song about the wealthy Gangnam district of Seoul, with a comic horse-riding dance. Its music video spread rapidly around the world, inspiring parodies and flash mobs. In December 2012 it became the first video on YouTube to reach one billion views, introducing millions of people to K-pop."],
g:[["K-pop","Korean popular music"],["Hallyu","The Korean Wave, the global spread of Korean culture"],["Idol","A K-pop performer trained by an agency"],["Fandom","A community of fans"]],
f:["Gangnam Style was the first YouTube video to reach a billion views.","BTS topped the Billboard Hot 100 in 2020.","SB19 was the first Filipino act nominated at the Billboard Music Awards."],
fun:"Gangnam Style became the first YouTube video to reach a billion views in 2012.",
tl:"Ang K-pop ay musikang popular mula sa Timog Korea na kilala sa nakaaaliw na awit at sayaw. Bahagi ito ng Korean Wave o Hallyu. Napakapopular nito sa Pilipinas at naging inspirasyon ng P-pop gaya ng SB19."});

ADDART({id:"indianmusic",t:"Indian Classical Music and Dance",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🪕",w:"Indian classical music",r:["drums","hindu","indiacountry","bollywood","beatles"],
p:[["Ancient traditions","Indian classical music and dance have roots going back more than 2,000 years."],["Raga and tala","Music is built on ragas, melodic frameworks, and talas, rhythmic cycles."],["Dancing stories","Classical dances tell stories through precise movements, expressions and hand gestures."]],
l:"Indian classical music and dance are among the oldest continuous artistic traditions in the world, rooted in ancient texts such as the Natya Shastra. Indian classical music has two main traditions: Hindustani in the north and Carnatic in the south. Both are based on ragas, melodic frameworks for improvisation, and talas, rhythmic cycles. India also has several classical dance forms, such as Bharatanatyam, Kathak and Kathakali, which combine rhythm, movement, facial expression and hand gestures to tell stories, often from Hindu epics.",
s:[["Raga","A raga is a set of notes with rules for how they are used, associated with particular moods, times of day or seasons. A performer improvises within the raga, developing it slowly, sometimes over an hour or more. There are hundreds of ragas, from the morning raga Bhairav to the evening raga Yaman."],["Tala","Tala is the rhythmic cycle that organizes music, such as teental, a cycle of 16 beats. Drummers playing the tabla in the north or the mridangam in the south interact with soloists, creating exciting rhythmic exchanges. Musicians learn complex rhythms by reciting syllables."],["Hindustani and Carnatic","Hindustani music, influenced by Persian traditions under the Mughals, features instruments such as the sitar, sarod, bansuri flute and tabla. Carnatic music, centered on compositions often devotional in nature, uses instruments such as the veena, violin and mridangam. Both traditions rely on a teacher passing knowledge to students, the guru-shishya tradition."],["Classical dance","India officially recognizes several classical dance forms, including Bharatanatyam from Tamil Nadu, Kathak from northern India, Kathakali from Kerala, with its elaborate makeup and costumes, Odissi from Odisha, Kuchipudi from Andhra Pradesh and Manipuri from Manipur. Dancers use mudras, hand gestures, and facial expressions to convey meaning."]],
k:[["Main music traditions","Hindustani and Carnatic"],["Melodic framework","Raga"],["Rhythmic cycle","Tala"],["Ancient text","Natya Shastra"],["Famous sitar player","Ravi Shankar"]],
d:[["c. 200 BCE–200 CE","The Natya Shastra is composed"],["1200s–1500s","Hindustani and Carnatic traditions develop distinct styles"],["1500s","Tansen performs at Akbar’s court"],["1700s–1800s","The Trinity of Carnatic music composes"],["1966","George Harrison studies sitar with Ravi Shankar"],["1998","M. S. Subbulakshmi becomes the first musician to receive the Bharat Ratna"]],
s2:[["Ravi Shankar","Ravi Shankar, the sitar virtuoso, brought Indian classical music to world audiences from the 1950s. George Harrison of the Beatles studied sitar with him, and Shankar performed at the Monterey Pop Festival in 1967 and at Woodstock in 1969. He collaborated with the violinist Yehudi Menuhin and won several Grammy Awards."],["Tansen","Tansen was a legendary musician at the court of the Mughal emperor Akbar in the 1500s. Legends say his singing could light lamps and bring rain. He is regarded as one of the greatest figures of Hindustani music, and a festival in his honor is held in Gwalior each year."],["M. S. Subbulakshmi","M. S. Subbulakshmi, a Carnatic singer from Tamil Nadu, became one of India’s most beloved musicians, performing devotional songs and classical music. In 1966 she sang at the United Nations General Assembly, and in 1998 she became the first musician to receive the Bharat Ratna, India’s highest civilian award."],["Mudras and storytelling","In Indian classical dance, mudras, symbolic hand gestures, represent objects, actions and emotions, such as a lotus, a deer or the sea. Combined with facial expressions, called abhinaya, and rhythmic footwork, they allow dancers to tell detailed stories from epics such as the Ramayana and Mahabharata."],["Kathakali","Kathakali, from Kerala, is a dramatic dance-theater with elaborate costumes, huge headdresses and painted faces in colors indicating character types: green for noble heroes, red and black for villains. Performances, traditionally all night, tell stories from Hindu epics, accompanied by drums and singers."],["Influence around the world","Indian classical music has influenced jazz, rock and world music, with musicians such as John Coltrane and the Beatles drawing on ragas. Yoga and meditation communities often use Indian music. Tabla and sitar sounds appear in film soundtracks, and Indian dance schools exist in many countries."]],
sp:["The guru and the student","Traditionally, Indian classical music and dance were passed down from guru, teacher, to shishya, student, often over many years of living and practicing together. Students learn by listening, imitating and absorbing not just techniques but the teacher’s style and values. Families of musicians, called gharanas in Hindustani music, preserve distinctive styles across generations. This oral tradition continues alongside music schools and online lessons."],
g:[["Raga","A melodic framework for improvisation in Indian music"],["Tala","A rhythmic cycle in Indian music"],["Mudra","A symbolic hand gesture in dance"],["Sitar","A long-necked Indian string instrument"]],
f:["Indian classical music is based on ragas and talas.","Ravi Shankar played at Woodstock in 1969.","Kathakali dancers wear painted faces and huge headdresses."],
fun:"Legends say the musician Tansen could make it rain with his singing.",
tl:"Ang klasikong musika at sayaw ng India ay may ugat na mahigit 2,000 taon. Batay ang musika sa raga at tala. Nagkukuwento ang mga klasikong sayaw sa pamamagitan ng galaw, ekspresyon at kumpas ng kamay."});

ADDART({id:"bollywood",t:"Bollywood and Indian Cinema",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🎬",w:"Cinema of India",r:["cinema","indiacountry","indianmusic","musicals","phcinema"],
p:[["The world’s biggest film industry","India produces more films than any other country."],["Song and dance","Indian films are famous for colorful song-and-dance numbers."],["Many cinemas","Bollywood, based in Mumbai, is one of several Indian film industries in different languages."]],
l:"Indian cinema is the largest film industry in the world by number of films, producing well over a thousand feature films a year in many languages. Bollywood, the Hindi-language film industry based in Mumbai, formerly Bombay, is the best known internationally, but industries in Telugu, Tamil, Malayalam, Kannada, Bengali and other languages are also huge. Indian films are known for blending drama, romance, action, comedy and music, with song-and-dance sequences. Indian cinema has a vast audience in South Asia and among the global Indian diaspora and is increasingly popular worldwide.",
s:[["Beginnings","In 1913 Dadasaheb Phalke released Raja Harishchandra, India’s first full-length feature film, a silent film based on a Hindu legend. In 1931 Alam Ara became India’s first sound film, with songs that became immensely popular. Music quickly became central to Indian films."],["The golden age","In the 1950s and 1960s, filmmakers such as Raj Kapoor, Guru Dutt and Bimal Roy made classic films exploring love, poverty and social change. Mother India, of 1957, was nominated for an Academy Award. In Bengali cinema, Satyajit Ray’s Pather Panchali of 1955 won international acclaim."],["Stars and spectacle","From the 1970s, the actor Amitabh Bachchan became a superstar playing the angry young man in action films. In the 1990s, Shah Rukh Khan became the king of romance, starring in Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge of 1995, which played in one Mumbai cinema for more than 25 years. Films grew more lavish, with foreign locations and big-budget dance numbers."],["Playback singing","In Indian films, songs are usually sung by professional playback singers, while actors lip-sync on screen. Singers such as Lata Mangeshkar, who recorded thousands of songs over seven decades, and Mohammed Rafi and Kishore Kumar became legends. Film songs dominate popular music in India."]],
k:[["Films per year","Well over 1,000"],["Hindi film center","Mumbai (Bollywood)"],["First feature film","Raja Harishchandra (1913)"],["First sound film","Alam Ara (1931)"],["Oscar-winning song","Naatu Naatu from RRR (2023)"]],
d:[["1913","Raja Harishchandra is released"],["1931","Alam Ara, the first Indian sound film, is released"],["1955","Satyajit Ray’s Pather Panchali is released"],["1957","Mother India is released"],["1995","Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge is released"],["2023","Naatu Naatu from RRR wins the Academy Award for Best Original Song"]],
s2:[["Many Indian cinemas","Besides Bollywood, India has major film industries in other languages: Telugu cinema, sometimes called Tollywood, based in Hyderabad; Tamil cinema in Chennai; Malayalam cinema in Kerala; and others. Telugu films such as Baahubali and RRR became huge hits across India and abroad."],["RRR and Naatu Naatu","The 2022 Telugu-language film RRR, directed by S. S. Rajamouli, became a global sensation for its spectacular action and dance. Its energetic song Naatu Naatu won the Academy Award for Best Original Song in 2023, the first song from an Indian film to win the award."],["Satyajit Ray","The Bengali director Satyajit Ray made realistic, humane films about Indian life, beginning with Pather Panchali in 1955, about a poor village family. His Apu Trilogy is considered one of the greatest achievements in cinema. In 1992 he received an honorary Academy Award shortly before his death."],["Indian cinema abroad","Indian films are popular across South Asia, the Middle East, Southeast Asia, Africa and Russia, and among the Indian diaspora in the United Kingdom, the United States and Canada. Streaming platforms have brought Indian films and series to new global audiences."],["The Filmfare Awards","India has many film awards, including the National Film Awards, given by the government since 1954, and the Filmfare Awards, started in 1954, often called the Indian Oscars. Award shows are major televised events."],["Cinema and society","Indian films reflect and influence society, addressing issues such as family, caste, religion, gender roles and national identity. Some films have sparked debates or been censored. Cinema is a powerful part of Indian culture, and film stars have become politicians, especially in southern India."]],
sp:["Lata Mangeshkar","Lata Mangeshkar, born in 1929, began singing for films as a teenager in the 1940s and continued for more than seven decades, recording thousands of songs in more than 30 languages. Her voice was heard on screen for generations of actresses. Called the Nightingale of India, she received the Bharat Ratna, India’s highest civilian award, in 2001. When she died in 2022, India declared two days of national mourning."],
g:[["Bollywood","The Hindi film industry based in Mumbai"],["Playback singer","A singer whose recorded voice is used for actors on screen"],["Tollywood","The Telugu film industry"],["Diaspora","People living outside their ancestral homeland"]],
f:["India produces more films than any other country.","India’s first sound film was Alam Ara in 1931.","Naatu Naatu won an Oscar in 2023."],
fun:"Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge played in one Mumbai cinema for more than 25 years.",
tl:"Ang India ang may pinakamaraming pelikulang ginagawa sa mundo. Kilala ang Bollywood ng Mumbai sa makukulay na awit at sayaw. Nanalo ng Oscar ang awiting Naatu Naatu mula sa pelikulang RRR noong 2023."});

ADDART({id:"asiantheatre",t:"Theatre of Asia",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"👺",w:"Asian theatre",r:["puppetry","theatre","opera","japan","phlit"],
p:[["Ancient stage traditions","Asia has some of the oldest and most elaborate theatre traditions in the world."],["Masks, makeup and music","Japanese Noh and kabuki and Chinese opera combine acting, music, dance and striking costumes."],["Philippine stage","Filipino traditions include the sarswela, the komedya and the senakulo."]],
l:"Asia has rich and varied theatre traditions, many centuries old, that combine acting, music, dance, poetry and elaborate costumes. Japan’s Noh, kabuki and bunraku, China’s many forms of opera, including Peking opera, India’s Sanskrit drama and Kathakali, Indonesia’s wayang and Thailand’s khon are among the most famous. Many traditions are recognized by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage. In the Philippines, theatre traditions include the komedya, the sarswela and the senakulo, alongside modern theatre companies.",
s:[["Noh","Noh is a Japanese musical drama developed in the 1300s by Kan’ami and his son Zeami. Performers, wearing carved wooden masks and rich robes, move slowly and gracefully on a simple wooden stage with a painted pine tree backdrop, accompanied by flute, drums and a chorus. Noh plays often feature ghosts, gods and warriors. It is one of the oldest theatre forms still performed."],["Kabuki","Kabuki began in Kyoto in 1603, when a woman named Izumo no Okuni performed dances. Later, women were banned from the stage, and male actors called onnagata specialized in female roles. Kabuki is known for dramatic makeup, spectacular costumes, stylized poses called mie, revolving stages and trapdoors. It was recognized by UNESCO in 2008."],["Chinese opera","Chinese opera combines singing, speech, mime, dance and acrobatics. There are hundreds of regional styles. Kunqu, from the 1500s, is one of the oldest. Peking opera developed in the late 1700s and 1800s, after opera troupes from Anhui came to Beijing in 1790. Performers wear painted faces whose colors show character: red for loyalty, black for integrity and white for cunning."],["South and Southeast Asia","India has ancient Sanskrit drama, such as Kalidasa’s Shakuntala, and dance-dramas such as Kathakali. Indonesia’s wayang puppet theatre and Bali’s masked dances tell stories from Hindu epics. Thailand’s khon, a masked dance-drama based on the Ramakien, was recognized by UNESCO in 2018."]],
k:[["Japanese forms","Noh, kabuki, bunraku"],["Kabuki founded","1603, Kyoto"],["Peking opera origins","1790, Beijing"],["UNESCO listings","Noh, kabuki, Kunqu, Peking opera, khon"],["Philippine forms","Komedya, sarswela, senakulo"]],
d:[["c. 300s–400s CE","Kalidasa writes Sanskrit dramas in India"],["1300s","Kan’ami and Zeami develop Noh"],["1603","Kabuki begins in Kyoto"],["1790","Opera troupes arrive in Beijing, leading to Peking opera"],["1900s","Sarswela flourishes in the Philippines"],["2010","Peking opera is recognized by UNESCO"]],
s2:[["Mei Lanfang","Mei Lanfang, who lived from 1894 to 1961, was the most famous Peking opera performer of the 20th century, a male actor celebrated for playing female roles. He toured Japan, the United States and the Soviet Union in the 1910s to 1930s, introducing Chinese opera to the world and influencing Western theatre practitioners."],["Komedya","The komedya, or moro-moro, is a Philippine theatre tradition from the Spanish era, telling stories of Christian and Muslim princes and princesses in verse, with sword fights, marches and romance. Performed at town fiestas, it is still staged in some communities, such as in Baliuag, Bulacan."],["Sarswela","The sarswela, from the Spanish zarzuela, is a musical play combining songs and spoken dialogue, often about love, family and social issues. In the early 1900s, Filipino playwrights such as Severino Reyes, with his Walang Sugat, used sarswela to express nationalist feelings under American rule. It was the most popular entertainment before films."],["Senakulo","The senakulo is a Philippine passion play performed during Holy Week, retelling the life, suffering and death of Jesus. Communities across the country stage senakulos on streets and in plazas, sometimes over several days, with local residents taking the roles."],["Modern Asian theatre","Asian countries also have vibrant modern theatre. In the Philippines, the Philippine Educational Theater Association, PETA, founded in 1967, Tanghalang Pilipino and Dulaang UP stage original Filipino plays and musicals. Japan’s Takarazuka Revue and contemporary companies across Asia blend tradition and innovation."],["Theatre and ritual","Many Asian theatre forms began as religious rituals honoring gods or spirits. Noh developed from temple and shrine performances, Balinese dances remain part of temple ceremonies and Philippine traditions such as the senakulo are linked to religious devotion."]],
sp:["Okuni, the founder of kabuki","In 1603 a woman named Izumo no Okuni, said to have been connected with the great Izumo shrine, began performing unusual, lively dances and comic sketches on the dry riverbed of the Kamo River in Kyoto, sometimes dressed as a man. Her performances, called kabuki, meaning something like strange or unconventional, drew huge crowds. Within decades, the government banned women from performing, and kabuki became an all-male art, but Okuni is remembered as its founder, and a statue of her stands in Kyoto."],
g:[["Noh","A classical Japanese masked musical drama"],["Kabuki","A Japanese theatre known for dramatic makeup and costumes"],["Onnagata","A male kabuki actor who plays female roles"],["Sarswela","A Philippine musical play with songs and dialogue"]],
f:["Kabuki began in Kyoto in 1603.","Peking opera face colors show character.","The senakulo is performed during Holy Week."],
fun:"Kabuki was started by a woman, Izumo no Okuni, though it later became all-male.",
tl:"May mayayamang tradisyon ng teatro ang Asya, gaya ng Noh at kabuki ng Hapon at opera ng Tsina. Pinagsasama nito ang pag-arte, musika at sayaw. Sa Pilipinas, kabilang dito ang komedya, sarswela at senakulo."});

;
/* data/arts7.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 7: children’s literature and the literatures of China, Russia, Africa and Latin America.
ADDART({id:"childlit",t:"Children’s Literature",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📚",w:"Children's literature",r:["fairy","novel","comics","phfolk","education"],
p:[["Stories for young readers","Children’s literature includes picture books, fairy tales, poems and novels written for children and young people."],["Classics and new favorites","From Alice in Wonderland to Harry Potter, children’s books are loved worldwide."],["Filipino stories","Filipino children’s literature includes the tales of Lola Basyang and books from publishers such as Adarna House."]],
l:"Children’s literature is the body of books, stories, poems and other writing created for children and young people, from picture books for toddlers to novels for teenagers. Children have always enjoyed stories, from fables and folktales told aloud to printed books. Books written especially for children became common from the 1700s, and classics such as Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland, The Little Prince and the Harry Potter series have been read by hundreds of millions of people. Children’s books help young people learn to read, imagine, understand feelings and discover the world.",
s:[["Early children’s books","For centuries, children heard fables, such as those of Aesop, and folktales. In 1658 the Czech educator John Amos Comenius published Orbis Pictus, often called the first picture book for children. In 1744 the English publisher John Newbery published A Little Pretty Pocket-Book, one of the first books written to entertain children."],["Fairy tales","Charles Perrault in France published fairy tales such as Cinderella and Sleeping Beauty in 1697. The Brothers Grimm in Germany collected folktales from 1812, and the Danish writer Hans Christian Andersen wrote original fairy tales, such as The Little Mermaid and The Ugly Duckling, from 1835."],["The golden age","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, many classics appeared: Lewis Carroll’s Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland in 1865, Carlo Collodi’s Pinocchio in 1883, Robert Louis Stevenson’s Treasure Island in 1883, Beatrix Potter’s The Tale of Peter Rabbit in 1902 and J. M. Barrie’s Peter Pan in 1904."],["Modern favorites","Twentieth-century classics include Antoine de Saint-Exupéry’s The Little Prince of 1943, Astrid Lindgren’s Pippi Longstocking of 1945, Dr. Seuss’s The Cat in the Hat of 1957 and Roald Dahl’s Charlie and the Chocolate Factory of 1964. J. K. Rowling’s Harry Potter series, beginning in 1997, has sold hundreds of millions of copies."]],
k:[["Early picture book","Orbis Pictus (1658)"],["Most translated children’s book","The Little Prince (hundreds of languages)"],["Top international award","Hans Christian Andersen Award"],["International Children’s Book Day","2 April"],["Filipino storyteller","Lola Basyang (Severino Reyes)"]],
d:[["1658","Comenius publishes Orbis Pictus"],["1697","Charles Perrault publishes his fairy tales"],["1865","Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland is published"],["1925","Severino Reyes begins the Lola Basyang stories"],["1943","The Little Prince is published"],["1997","The first Harry Potter book is published"]],
s2:[["Lola Basyang","In 1925 the Filipino writer Severino Reyes began publishing stories in the magazine Liwayway under the name Lola Basyang, an imaginary grandmother telling tales to children. The stories, full of princes, magic and adventure, often blended European and Filipino elements. They have been retold in books, television and theatre for generations."],["Filipino children’s books","Filipino children’s literature has grown since the 1970s and 1980s, with publishers such as Adarna House, founded in 1980, and Tahanan Books producing books in Filipino and English. Stories such as Ang Pambansang Bayani and books by writers like Virgilio Almario and Augie Rivera reflect Filipino life, history and folklore."],["Picture books","Picture books combine words and illustrations to tell stories, often for young children. Classics include Where the Wild Things Are by Maurice Sendak, published in 1963, and The Very Hungry Caterpillar by Eric Carle, published in 1969. Illustrators from around the world create picture books in many artistic styles."],["The Little Prince","Antoine de Saint-Exupéry, a French pilot and writer, published The Little Prince in 1943. It tells of a pilot stranded in the desert who meets a small prince from another planet. Its wisdom, such as what is essential is invisible to the eye, has made it one of the most translated books in the world, available in hundreds of languages and dialects."],["Why children’s books matter","Reading to and with children builds language, imagination and empathy and helps them succeed in school. Books that reflect children’s own cultures, as well as books about others, help them understand themselves and the world. Libraries and reading programs aim to give every child access to books."],["Young adult literature","Young adult, or YA, literature is written for teenagers and deals with themes such as identity, friendship, family, love and growing up. Popular YA books include The Hunger Games series and The Fault in Our Stars. Filipino YA fiction, in English and Filipino, has grown with online platforms such as Wattpad."]],
sp:["Alice in Wonderland","On 4 July 1862, the mathematician Charles Dodgson took three young sisters, including ten-year-old Alice Liddell, on a boat trip near Oxford and made up a story about a girl who fell down a rabbit hole. Alice asked him to write it down. Published in 1865 under the pen name Lewis Carroll as Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland, it was full of nonsense, wordplay and memorable characters such as the Mad Hatter and the Cheshire Cat, and it became one of the most famous children’s books ever written."],
g:[["Picture book","A book that tells a story with words and pictures"],["Fable","A short story with a moral, often with animals"],["Young adult literature","Books written for teenagers"],["Illustrator","An artist who creates pictures for books"]],
f:["Orbis Pictus of 1658 is often called the first picture book.","The Little Prince is one of the most translated books.","Lola Basyang stories began in 1925."],
fun:"Alice in Wonderland began as a story told on a boat trip in 1862.",
tl:"Ang panitikang pambata ay binubuo ng mga aklat, kuwento at tula para sa mga bata. Kilala sa buong mundo ang Alice in Wonderland at Harry Potter. Sa Pilipinas, tanyag ang mga kuwento ni Lola Basyang at mga aklat ng Adarna House."});

ADDART({id:"chineselit",t:"Chinese Literature",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🐒",w:"Chinese literature",r:["chinesephil","calligraphy","poetry","china","chineseart"],
p:[["Three thousand years","Chinese literature has a written history of about 3,000 years."],["Great poets","Tang dynasty poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu are among the greatest in the world."],["Four classic novels","Journey to the West, with the Monkey King, is one of four great classical novels."]],
l:"Chinese literature is one of the oldest continuous literary traditions in the world, with written works dating back about 3,000 years. It includes ancient poetry and philosophy, the great poetry of the Tang dynasty, plays, short stories and the Four Great Classical Novels, among them Journey to the West and Romance of the Three Kingdoms. In the 1900s, writers such as Lu Xun created modern Chinese literature in everyday language. Chinese literature has influenced the literature of Korea, Japan and Vietnam, and contemporary Chinese writers are read worldwide.",
s:[["Ancient classics","The Classic of Poetry, or Shijing, collects songs and poems from about 1000 to 600 BCE, about love, farming, war and ceremonies. Philosophical works such as the Analects of Confucius, the Daodejing and the Zhuangzi are also literary classics. The historian Sima Qian’s Records of the Grand Historian, around 100 BCE, set a model for history writing."],["Tang poetry","The Tang dynasty, from 618 to 907, is considered the golden age of Chinese poetry. Li Bai wrote free-spirited poems about wine, the moon and nature; Du Fu wrote about war, suffering and compassion; and Wang Wei wrote peaceful nature poems. Their poems are still memorized by Chinese schoolchildren."],["The four classic novels","Four novels from the Ming and Qing dynasties are considered China’s greatest: Romance of the Three Kingdoms, about wars in the 200s CE; Water Margin, about heroic outlaws; Journey to the West, about a monk’s pilgrimage to India with the Monkey King; and Dream of the Red Chamber, a story of love and family decline."],["Modern literature","In 1918 Lu Xun published A Madman’s Diary, one of the first modern Chinese stories written in everyday vernacular language rather than classical Chinese. Modern Chinese writers explored social change, revolution and personal life. Mo Yan won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2012, and Gao Xingjian, a French citizen writing in Chinese, won it in 2000."]],
k:[["History","About 3,000 years"],["Oldest poetry collection","Classic of Poetry (Shijing)"],["Golden age of poetry","Tang dynasty (618–907)"],["Four classic novels","Three Kingdoms, Water Margin, Journey to the West, Dream of the Red Chamber"],["Nobel laureate","Mo Yan (2012)"]],
d:[["c. 1000–600 BCE","Songs of the Classic of Poetry are composed"],["c. 100 BCE","Sima Qian writes the Records of the Grand Historian"],["701–762","Li Bai lives"],["1500s","Journey to the West is published"],["1918","Lu Xun publishes A Madman’s Diary"],["2015","Liu Cixin’s The Three-Body Problem wins the Hugo Award"]],
s2:[["The Monkey King","Journey to the West, attributed to Wu Cheng’en in the 1500s, tells of the monk Xuanzang traveling to India to bring back Buddhist scriptures, protected by the mischievous, shape-shifting Monkey King, Sun Wukong, along with the greedy Pigsy and the gentle Sandy. Sun Wukong, born from a stone and armed with a magic staff, is one of the most beloved characters in Asian culture."],["Li Bai","Li Bai, also known as Li Po, is one of China’s most celebrated poets. His short poem Quiet Night Thought, about seeing moonlight on the floor and thinking of home, is known by almost every Chinese speaker. Legend says he drowned trying to embrace the reflection of the moon in a river, though this is probably not true."],["Dream of the Red Chamber","Cao Xueqin’s Dream of the Red Chamber, from the 1700s, tells of the decline of a wealthy family and the love between Jia Baoyu and his cousin Lin Daiyu. With hundreds of characters, it is considered the masterpiece of Chinese fiction, and its study has its own name, redology."],["Lu Xun","Lu Xun, who lived from 1881 to 1936, is regarded as the father of modern Chinese literature. Originally a medical student, he turned to writing to change people’s minds. His stories, such as The True Story of Ah Q, criticized injustice and blind tradition in Chinese society."],["Science fiction","Contemporary Chinese science fiction has gained a global following. Liu Cixin’s The Three-Body Problem, about humanity’s contact with an alien civilization, won the Hugo Award for best novel in 2015, the first by an Asian writer, and was adapted for television."],["Chinese literature in Southeast Asia","Chinese communities brought stories such as the Three Kingdoms and the Monkey King to Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, where they appear in festivals, temples, comics and films. Translations into Malay, Thai, Vietnamese and other languages spread these tales widely."]],
sp:["Quiet Night Thought","Li Bai’s four-line poem Quiet Night Thought is perhaps the most famous poem in the Chinese language. In it, the poet sees bright moonlight before his bed and thinks for a moment that it is frost on the ground. He raises his head to gaze at the moon, then lowers it, thinking of his distant home. Its simple words and feeling of homesickness have made it beloved for over 1,200 years, and Chinese children learn it by heart."],
g:[["Classical Chinese","The old written form of Chinese used for literature"],["Vernacular","Everyday spoken language"],["Tang dynasty","A Chinese dynasty from 618 to 907"],["Redology","The study of Dream of the Red Chamber"]],
f:["Tang poetry is the golden age of Chinese verse.","The Monkey King appears in Journey to the West.","Mo Yan won the Nobel Prize in 2012."],
fun:"Dream of the Red Chamber is so studied that its research field has its own name, redology.",
tl:"Ang panitikang Tsino ay may halos 3,000 taong kasaysayan. Kabilang dito ang tula ng dinastiyang Tang nina Li Bai at Du Fu at ang apat na klasikong nobela, gaya ng Journey to the West na kinatatampukan ng Monkey King."});

ADDART({id:"russianlit",t:"Russian Literature",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📖",w:"Russian literature",r:["novel","russiacountry","russrev","existentialism","poetry"],
p:[["Great novels","Russian literature produced some of the world’s greatest novels, such as War and Peace and Crime and Punishment."],["The golden age","The 1800s were the golden age, with Pushkin, Gogol, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky and Chekhov."],["Writing under pressure","In the Soviet era, many writers faced censorship, exile or prison."]],
l:"Russian literature is renowned worldwide for its novels, short stories, plays and poetry, especially from the 1800s, its golden age. Alexander Pushkin, often called the father of modern Russian literature, was followed by Nikolai Gogol, Ivan Turgenev, Fyodor Dostoevsky, Leo Tolstoy and Anton Chekhov, whose works explored deep questions of faith, morality, love, suffering and society. In the 20th century, Russian writers produced great works despite revolution, war and censorship. Several have won the Nobel Prize in Literature.",
s:[["Pushkin","Alexander Pushkin, who lived from 1799 to 1837, wrote poems, plays and stories that shaped the modern Russian literary language. His novel in verse, Eugene Onegin, is a masterpiece. He died at 37 in a duel. Russians regard him as their national poet, and his great-grandfather was Abram Gannibal, an African brought to Russia who became a general."],["Dostoevsky","Fyodor Dostoevsky wrote intense psychological novels about faith, guilt, freedom and suffering. Crime and Punishment, of 1866, follows a poor student who commits murder and struggles with his conscience. The Brothers Karamazov, of 1880, explores faith and doubt. Dostoevsky had been sentenced to death for political activity, reprieved at the last moment and sent to a Siberian prison camp."],["Tolstoy","Leo Tolstoy’s War and Peace, published from 1865 to 1869, follows Russian families during Napoleon’s invasion of 1812, mixing history and philosophy. Anna Karenina, of 1878, begins with the famous line that all happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way. In later life, Tolstoy preached nonviolence, influencing Gandhi."],["Chekhov","Anton Chekhov, a doctor, wrote hundreds of short stories and plays such as The Seagull and The Cherry Orchard, showing ordinary people’s hopes, disappointments and everyday lives with subtlety and humor. He transformed the short story and modern drama."]],
k:[["Golden age","1800s"],["Father of modern Russian literature","Alexander Pushkin"],["Famous novels","War and Peace, Crime and Punishment, Anna Karenina"],["Nobel laureates","Bunin, Pasternak, Sholokhov, Solzhenitsyn, Brodsky"],["Famous playwright","Anton Chekhov"]],
d:[["1833","Pushkin completes Eugene Onegin"],["1866","Dostoevsky publishes Crime and Punishment"],["1869","Tolstoy completes War and Peace"],["1904","Chekhov’s The Cherry Orchard premieres"],["1958","Boris Pasternak is awarded the Nobel Prize"],["1970","Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn wins the Nobel Prize"]],
s2:[["Writing under the Soviets","After the Russian Revolution, Soviet authorities required writers to follow socialist realism, praising the state. Many writers were censored, imprisoned or killed in the purges of the 1930s. Others wrote secretly, and their works circulated in typewritten copies called samizdat, or were published abroad."],["Pasternak and Doctor Zhivago","Boris Pasternak’s novel Doctor Zhivago, about a doctor and poet during the revolution and civil war, was rejected in the Soviet Union and first published in Italy in 1957. When Pasternak was awarded the Nobel Prize in 1958, Soviet pressure forced him to decline it."],["Solzhenitsyn","Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn spent eight years in Soviet labor camps. His novel One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich, published in 1962, described life in a camp, and The Gulag Archipelago documented the vast prison camp system. He won the Nobel Prize in 1970 and was expelled from the Soviet Union in 1974."],["The Master and Margarita","Mikhail Bulgakov’s The Master and Margarita, a fantastical satire in which the devil visits Soviet Moscow, was written in the 1930s but published only in the 1960s, decades after his death. With its talking cat and blend of comedy and philosophy, it is one of the most beloved Russian novels."],["Poetry","Russia has a strong poetic tradition. Poets such as Anna Akhmatova, Marina Tsvetaeva, Osip Mandelstam and Joseph Brodsky, who won the Nobel Prize in 1987, wrote powerful poetry, often under persecution. Poetry readings drew huge crowds in the Soviet Union."],["Influence around the world","Russian novels have been translated into many languages and influenced writers worldwide, from Ernest Hemingway to Japanese and Latin American authors. Their deep exploration of human psychology and moral questions continues to attract readers."]],
sp:["Dostoevsky before the firing squad","In 1849 the young writer Fyodor Dostoevsky was arrested for belonging to a group that discussed banned political ideas. He and others were taken to a square in St Petersburg, dressed in white shirts and tied to posts before a firing squad. At the last moment, a messenger arrived with the news that the tsar had commuted their sentences. It had been a staged mock execution. Dostoevsky was sent to a prison camp in Siberia, an experience that deeply shaped his later novels."],
g:[["Golden age","A period of great achievement"],["Socialist realism","The official Soviet style of art and literature"],["Samizdat","Secretly copied and circulated banned writing"],["Gulag","The Soviet system of forced labor camps"]],
f:["Pushkin is Russia’s national poet.","Tolstoy wrote War and Peace.","Solzhenitsyn won the Nobel Prize in 1970."],
fun:"Dostoevsky faced a mock execution before being sent to Siberia.",
tl:"Kilala sa buong mundo ang panitikang Ruso, lalo na ang mga nobela gaya ng War and Peace at Crime and Punishment. Ang 1800s ang ginintuang panahon nito, kasama sina Pushkin, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky at Chekhov."});

ADDART({id:"africanlit",t:"African Literature",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🪘",w:"African literature",r:["achebe","africacolon","nigeriacountry","kenyacountry","poetry"],
p:[["Oral and written","African literature includes ancient oral epics, songs and proverbs as well as written works in many languages."],["Modern classics","Novels such as Chinua Achebe’s Things Fall Apart are read around the world."],["Nobel winners","African writers such as Wole Soyinka and Naguib Mahfouz have won the Nobel Prize."]],
l:"African literature includes the oral and written literature of the peoples of Africa, in hundreds of African languages and in Arabic, English, French, Portuguese and others. It ranges from ancient oral epics, praise poems, folktales and proverbs, preserved by storytellers called griots, to manuscripts in Ge’ez and Arabic and modern novels, plays and poems. In the 20th century, writers such as Chinua Achebe, Wole Soyinka and Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o explored colonialism, independence and identity. Today African writers are among the most celebrated in world literature.",
s:[["Oral traditions","For thousands of years, African peoples passed down history, law, values and entertainment through oral literature: epics, poems, songs, riddles, proverbs and folktales, such as stories of Anansi the spider from West Africa. Griots of West Africa, poets and musicians, preserve the history of families and kingdoms, such as the Epic of Sundiata about the founder of the Mali Empire."],["Written traditions","Africa also has ancient written literature. Ethiopia has texts in Ge’ez going back more than 1,500 years. In West Africa, scholars in Timbuktu wrote and collected thousands of manuscripts in Arabic and African languages on religion, science and history. Swahili poetry has been written in Arabic script for centuries."],["Literature and colonialism","In the 20th century, many African writers responded to colonial rule and its aftermath. Chinua Achebe’s Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, showed Igbo society before and during colonialism from an African point of view. The Négritude movement, led by poets such as Léopold Sédar Senghor of Senegal, celebrated African identity."],["Language debate","African writers have debated which language to write in. Achebe chose English, arguing it could carry African experience. The Kenyan writer Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o, after writing in English, switched to his native Gikuyu in the 1970s, arguing that African literature should be written in African languages."]],
k:[["Forms","Oral epics, folktales, poetry, novels, plays"],["Famous novel","Things Fall Apart (1958)"],["First African Nobel laureate in literature","Wole Soyinka (1986)"],["Oral historians","Griots"],["Famous trickster","Anansi the spider"]],
d:[["1200s","The Epic of Sundiata begins to be told"],["1958","Chinua Achebe publishes Things Fall Apart"],["1962","The African Writers Series is launched"],["1986","Wole Soyinka wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"],["1988","Naguib Mahfouz wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"],["2021","Abdulrazak Gurnah wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"]],
s2:[["Things Fall Apart","Chinua Achebe’s Things Fall Apart tells the story of Okonkwo, a proud Igbo wrestler and leader whose world is shaken by the arrival of British missionaries and colonial government. It has sold millions of copies, been translated into more than 50 languages and is one of the most widely read African novels."],["Nobel laureates","African writers who have won the Nobel Prize in Literature include Wole Soyinka of Nigeria in 1986, the first African laureate; Naguib Mahfouz of Egypt in 1988; Nadine Gordimer of South Africa in 1991; J. M. Coetzee of South Africa in 2003; Doris Lessing, raised in Zimbabwe, in 2007; and Abdulrazak Gurnah, born in Zanzibar, in 2021."],["Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o","Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o, born in Kenya in 1938, wrote novels such as Weep Not, Child and A Grain of Wheat. After being imprisoned without trial in 1977 for a play criticizing the government, he wrote a novel in Gikuyu on prison toilet paper. His book Decolonising the Mind argued for writing in African languages. He died in 2025."],["New voices","Contemporary African writers include Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie of Nigeria, whose novels Half of a Yellow Sun and Americanah and talk We Should All Be Feminists gained global audiences, Tsitsi Dangarembga of Zimbabwe and many others writing in African and European languages."],["The Timbuktu manuscripts","Timbuktu, in Mali, was a center of learning from the 1300s to the 1500s. Families preserved hundreds of thousands of manuscripts on subjects such as Islamic law, astronomy, medicine and poetry. In 2012 and 2013, when militants occupied the city, librarians smuggled many manuscripts to safety in a daring rescue."],["Anansi stories","Anansi, a clever spider trickster, is a hero of folktales from the Akan peoples of Ghana. Enslaved Africans carried Anansi stories to the Caribbean and the Americas, where they remain popular. In one tale, Anansi obtains all the world’s stories from the sky god by outwitting dangerous creatures."]],
sp:["Saving the manuscripts of Timbuktu","In 2012 armed groups occupied Timbuktu in northern Mali and began destroying historic shrines. Fearing for the city’s ancient manuscripts, librarians led by Abdel Kader Haidara organized a secret operation. Volunteers packed hundreds of thousands of manuscripts into metal trunks and smuggled them out on donkey carts, boats and cars to the capital, Bamako. Although some manuscripts were burned, most were saved, preserving centuries of African scholarship."],
g:[["Griot","A West African storyteller, poet and musician who preserves history"],["Oral literature","Literature passed down by speaking and singing"],["Négritude","A movement celebrating African identity"],["Trickster","A clever character who uses tricks, such as Anansi"]],
f:["Wole Soyinka was the first African Nobel laureate in literature.","Things Fall Apart was published in 1958.","Griots preserve history through song and story."],
fun:"Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o wrote a novel on prison toilet paper.",
tl:"Kasama sa panitikang Aprikano ang sinaunang epiko, awit at salawikain at ang mga akdang nakasulat sa maraming wika. Kilala ang Things Fall Apart ni Chinua Achebe. Nanalo ng Nobel Prize ang mga manunulat na Aprikano gaya ni Wole Soyinka."});

ADDART({id:"latamlit",t:"Latin American Literature",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🦋",w:"Latin American literature",r:["garciamarquez","novel","poetry","spanish","phlit"],
p:[["The fantastic and the everyday","Latin American writers became famous for magical realism, blending everyday life with the fantastic."],["World fame","In the 1960s, the Latin American Boom brought novels such as One Hundred Years of Solitude to the world."],["Nobel poets","Gabriela Mistral and Pablo Neruda of Chile won the Nobel Prize."]],
l:"Latin American literature is the literature of Mexico, Central and South America and the Spanish- and Portuguese-speaking Caribbean, written mostly in Spanish and Portuguese, as well as Indigenous languages. It includes Indigenous texts such as the Popol Vuh, colonial writers such as the poet Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz, the modernismo poetry of Rubén Darío and the novels of the Latin American Boom of the 1960s, such as Gabriel García Márquez’s One Hundred Years of Solitude. Latin American writers have won several Nobel Prizes and shaped world literature.",
s:[["Indigenous and colonial literature","The Popol Vuh, a sacred book of the K’iche’ Maya written down in the 1500s, tells of the creation of the world and the hero twins. In the colonial period, the Mexican nun Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz, of the 1600s, wrote brilliant poetry and defended women’s right to learn, earning the name the Tenth Muse."],["Modernismo","In 1888 the Nicaraguan poet Rubén Darío published Azul, launching modernismo, a movement that renewed Spanish-language poetry with musical language and new forms. It was the first literary movement from Latin America to influence Spain."],["The Boom","In the 1960s, a group of novelists gained worldwide fame in what was called the Latin American Boom: Gabriel García Márquez of Colombia, Mario Vargas Llosa of Peru, Julio Cortázar of Argentina and Carlos Fuentes of Mexico. Their experimental novels explored history, politics and identity."],["Magical realism","Magical realism presents extraordinary events, such as a woman rising to heaven while folding laundry, as if they were ordinary parts of life. García Márquez’s One Hundred Years of Solitude, published in 1967, is its most famous example. The style influenced writers around the world."]],
k:[["Main languages","Spanish and Portuguese"],["Famous style","Magical realism"],["Famous novel","One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967)"],["First Latin American Nobel laureate in literature","Gabriela Mistral (1945)"],["Other Nobel laureates","Asturias, Neruda, García Márquez, Paz, Vargas Llosa"]],
d:[["1500s","The Popol Vuh is written down"],["1691","Sor Juana writes her defense of women’s learning"],["1888","Rubén Darío publishes Azul"],["1945","Gabriela Mistral wins the Nobel Prize"],["1967","One Hundred Years of Solitude is published"],["2010","Mario Vargas Llosa wins the Nobel Prize"]],
s2:[["Jorge Luis Borges","The Argentine writer Jorge Luis Borges wrote short stories and essays about labyrinths, mirrors, infinite libraries and dreams, such as The Library of Babel, which imagines a library containing every possible book. Though he never won the Nobel Prize, he influenced writers worldwide and is considered one of the greatest writers of the 20th century."],["Pablo Neruda","The Chilean poet Pablo Neruda wrote passionate love poems, such as Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair, published in 1924, and the epic Canto General about Latin American history. He was also a diplomat and politician. He won the Nobel Prize in 1971."],["Isabel Allende","The Chilean-American writer Isabel Allende, niece of the former president Salvador Allende, published The House of the Spirits in 1982, a family saga with magical elements set against Chilean history. She is one of the most widely read Spanish-language authors."],["Brazilian literature","Brazil, where Portuguese is spoken, has its own rich literature. Machado de Assis, of the 1800s, wrote witty, ironic novels. Jorge Amado wrote colorful novels about Bahia, and Clarice Lispector wrote intense, philosophical fiction. Paulo Coelho’s The Alchemist, of 1988, is one of the best-selling books in the world."],["Literature and politics","Many Latin American writers were involved in politics, opposing dictatorships or supporting causes. Some, like Neruda, served as diplomats; Mario Vargas Llosa ran for president of Peru in 1990. Writers have used fiction to explore dictatorship, revolution, inequality and memory."],["Links with the Philippines","The Philippines and Latin America share a history of Spanish colonization, the Spanish language and Catholic culture. José Rizal wrote his novels in Spanish, and a Philippine literature in Spanish flourished in the late 1800s and early 1900s. Filipino readers and writers have found affinities with Latin American writers, especially magical realism."]],
sp:["One Hundred Years of Solitude","Gabriel García Márquez spent about 18 months writing One Hundred Years of Solitude, selling his car and pawning household items to support his family. Published in Buenos Aires in 1967, it tells the story of seven generations of the Buendía family in the fictional town of Macondo, where yellow butterflies follow a lovesick man and it rains for nearly five years. It has sold tens of millions of copies in dozens of languages and was adapted as a television series in 2024."],
g:[["Magical realism","A style blending realistic narrative with magical events"],["Modernismo","A Latin American poetry movement of the late 1800s"],["The Boom","The 1960s rise of Latin American novelists to world fame"],["Popol Vuh","A sacred K’iche’ Maya book"]],
f:["Gabriela Mistral was the first Latin American Nobel laureate in literature.","One Hundred Years of Solitude was published in 1967.","Rubén Darío launched modernismo in 1888."],
fun:"García Márquez sold his car to support his family while writing One Hundred Years of Solitude.",
tl:"Ang panitikang Latin Amerikano ay nakasulat karamihan sa Espanyol at Portuges. Kilala ito sa magical realism, gaya ng One Hundred Years of Solitude ni García Márquez. Nanalo ng Nobel Prize sina Gabriela Mistral at Pablo Neruda."});

