/* data/science6.js */
// Science articles, part 6: atoms and particles — the electron, the Standard Model, isotopes, particle accelerators and nuclear fission.
ADDART({id:"electron",t:"The Electron",c:"Science",i:"⚡",w:"Electron",r:["atom","elec","quantum","particles","light"],
p:[["A tiny particle","Electrons are tiny particles with a negative electric charge, found in every atom."],["Electricity in motion","An electric current is a flow of electrons."],["Chemistry’s glue","The way electrons are shared and swapped holds molecules together."]],
l:"The electron is a tiny particle with a negative electric charge that is found in every atom, moving around the central nucleus. Discovered in 1897 by the British physicist J. J. Thomson, it was the first subatomic particle to be identified. Electrons are far lighter than the protons and neutrons in the nucleus, yet they are responsible for electricity, magnetism, chemical bonds, light from atoms and the workings of all electronic devices, from light bulbs to computers.",
s:[["Discovery","In the 1800s scientists studied cathode rays, glowing beams inside glass tubes from which most of the air had been removed. In 1897 J. J. Thomson showed that these rays were made of particles much smaller than atoms, carrying negative charge. The name electron had been suggested earlier, in 1891, by the Irish physicist George Johnstone Stoney for a basic unit of electric charge."],["How small?","An electron has about 1/1,836 of the mass of a proton. As far as experiments can tell, it has no size at all and behaves as a point. In 1909 the American physicist Robert Millikan measured its electric charge by watching tiny charged oil drops floating between metal plates."],["Electrons in atoms","Every atom has a positively charged nucleus surrounded by electrons, which balance its charge. In 1913 Niels Bohr suggested that electrons travel only in certain energy levels, or shells. In quantum physics, electrons are described not as tiny planets in orbits but as clouds of probability around the nucleus."],["Electrons and electricity","In metals, some electrons are free to move between atoms. When a battery or generator pushes them, they flow through a wire as an electric current. Static electricity, such as the shock from a doorknob, comes from electrons building up on one object and jumping to another."]],
k:[["Electric charge","Negative"],["Mass","About 1/1,836 of a proton"],["Discovered by","J. J. Thomson (1897)"],["Found in","Every atom"],["Antiparticle","The positron"]],
d:[["1891","George Johnstone Stoney names the electron"],["1897","J. J. Thomson discovers the electron"],["1909","Robert Millikan measures its charge"],["1913","Niels Bohr describes electron energy levels"],["1931","The first electron microscope is built"],["1932","The positron, the electron’s antiparticle, is discovered"]],
s2:[["Chemical bonds","Atoms join to form molecules by sharing or swapping electrons. In a covalent bond, as in water, atoms share pairs of electrons; in an ionic bond, as in table salt, one atom gives an electron to another. The number of electrons in an atom’s outer shell largely decides how it reacts, which is why the periodic table groups elements with similar outer electrons together."],["Light from atoms","When an electron in an atom absorbs energy, it jumps to a higher energy level. When it falls back, it gives off light of a particular color. This is how neon signs, fireworks and the northern lights glow, and why each element has its own pattern of colors, which astronomers use to find out what stars are made of."],["Wave and particle","In the 1920s scientists discovered that electrons behave both like particles and like waves. Electron beams passed through crystals produce wave patterns. This strange double nature is a central idea of quantum mechanics."],["Electron microscopes","Because electrons have much shorter wavelengths than visible light, microscopes that use beams of electrons can show far smaller details. The first electron microscope was built in Germany in 1931 by Ernst Ruska and Max Knoll. Today electron microscopes reveal viruses, cells and even individual atoms."],["Electronics","Electronics is the technology of controlling the flow of electrons. Vacuum tubes, transistors and microchips switch and amplify electric currents, allowing radios, televisions, smartphones and computers to work. A modern computer chip contains billions of transistors."],["The positron","Every particle has an antimatter partner. The electron’s is the positron, which has the same mass but a positive charge. It was discovered in 1932 by Carl Anderson in cosmic rays. Positrons are used in hospitals in PET scans, which show how organs in the body are working."]],
sp:["Millikan’s oil drops","To measure the charge of a single electron, Robert Millikan and his student Harvey Fletcher sprayed a fine mist of oil drops between two metal plates and watched them through a microscope. Some drops picked up electric charges. By adjusting the voltage on the plates, Millikan could make a drop float in midair, balancing gravity with electric force. He found that the charges on the drops were always whole-number multiples of one tiny amount: the charge of one electron. His experiment, completed in 1909 and published over the following years, earned him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1923."],
g:[["Electron","A tiny negatively charged particle found in atoms"],["Nucleus","The center of an atom, made of protons and neutrons"],["Electric current","A flow of electric charge, usually electrons"],["Positron","The antimatter partner of the electron"]],
f:["The electron was discovered in 1897.","An electric current is a flow of electrons.","Electron microscopes can show individual atoms."],
fun:"As far as anyone can measure, an electron has no size at all.",
tl:"Ang electron ay napakaliit na partikulo na may negatibong karga at matatagpuan sa bawat atom. Natuklasan ito ni J. J. Thomson noong 1897. Ang daloy ng mga electron ang bumubuo sa kuryente."});

ADDART({id:"standardmodel",t:"The Standard Model of Particle Physics",c:"Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Standard Model",r:["particles","atom","electron","accelerators","antimatter"],
p:[["The building blocks","The Standard Model describes the smallest known building blocks of matter and the forces between them."],["Quarks and leptons","Everything around us is made of particles called quarks and leptons."],["The final piece","Its last missing piece, the Higgs boson, was found in 2012."]],
l:"The Standard Model of particle physics is the scientific theory that describes the smallest known particles and three of the four fundamental forces of nature: electromagnetism and the strong and weak nuclear forces. According to the model, all ordinary matter is made of particles called quarks and leptons, held together by force-carrying particles, while the Higgs field gives many particles their mass. Built up from the 1960s to the 1970s and confirmed by experiments, most recently the discovery of the Higgs boson in 2012, it is one of the most successful theories in science, though it does not explain gravity or dark matter.",
s:[["Matter particles","The Standard Model has two families of matter particles. Quarks combine to make protons and neutrons; a proton contains two up quarks and one down quark, and a neutron one up quark and two down quarks. Leptons include the electron and the ghostly neutrino. Each comes in three generations, with heavier versions such as the top quark and the muon."],["Force particles","Forces are carried by particles called bosons. The photon carries electromagnetism, gluons carry the strong force that holds quarks together inside protons and neutrons, and the W and Z bosons carry the weak force involved in radioactive decay."],["The Higgs boson","In 1964 Peter Higgs, François Englert and others proposed an invisible field filling all of space that gives particles mass. Its particle, the Higgs boson, was discovered on 4 July 2012 at the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva. Higgs and Englert shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2013."],["Antimatter","Every matter particle has an antimatter partner with the opposite charge, such as the positron for the electron. When a particle meets its antiparticle, they destroy each other in a burst of energy. Why the universe contains far more matter than antimatter is one of the great unsolved puzzles."]],
k:[["Describes","The smallest known particles and three forces"],["Matter particles","Quarks and leptons"],["Force carriers","Photon, gluons, W and Z bosons"],["Last piece found","The Higgs boson (2012)"],["Not included","Gravity and dark matter"]],
d:[["1897","The electron is discovered"],["1956","The neutrino is detected"],["1964","Quarks and the Higgs field are proposed"],["1983","The W and Z bosons are discovered at CERN"],["1995","The top quark is discovered at Fermilab"],["2012","The Higgs boson is discovered at CERN"]],
s2:[["Quarks","Quarks were proposed independently in 1964 by the American physicists Murray Gell-Mann and George Zweig. There are six kinds, called flavors: up, down, charm, strange, top and bottom. Quarks are never found alone; the strong force always binds them in groups, such as the three quarks of a proton."],["Neutrinos","Neutrinos are extremely light particles with no electric charge that barely interact with anything. Trillions pass through your body every second, most coming from the Sun, without any effect. They were first detected in 1956 by Clyde Cowan and Frederick Reines near a nuclear reactor. Their discovery that they have a tiny mass earned a Nobel Prize in 2015."],["How the model was tested","The Standard Model made predictions that experiments later confirmed, such as the existence of the W and Z bosons, found at CERN in 1983, the top quark, found at Fermilab near Chicago in 1995, and the Higgs boson. Particle accelerators smash particles together at high energy to create and study these short-lived particles."],["What it does not explain","The Standard Model does not include gravity, which is described by Einstein’s general relativity, and the two theories have not been combined. It also does not explain dark matter and dark energy, which make up most of the universe, or why neutrinos have mass. Physicists are searching for a deeper theory."],["A worldwide effort","Particle physics is one of the most international fields of science. CERN, founded in 1954, has member states across Europe and collaborators from more than 100 countries, including scientists from the Philippines, Asia, Africa and the Americas. Thousands of researchers worked on the experiments that discovered the Higgs boson."],["Why it matters","Understanding particles has led to technologies such as medical scanners, radiation treatments for cancer and the World Wide Web, invented at CERN in 1989 to help scientists share information. The Standard Model also helps scientists understand how the universe evolved after the Big Bang."]],
sp:["Three quarks for Muster Mark","When Murray Gell-Mann needed a name for his new particles, he first thought of a sound, something like kwork. Then he came across a line in James Joyce’s strange novel Finnegans Wake: Three quarks for Muster Mark! Since protons and neutrons are each made of three of the particles, the word seemed perfect, and quark became the name. George Zweig, who proposed the same idea independently, had called them aces, but Gell-Mann’s word stuck."],
g:[["Quark","A basic particle that makes up protons and neutrons"],["Lepton","A basic particle such as the electron or neutrino"],["Boson","A particle that carries a force"],["Higgs field","An invisible field that gives particles mass"]],
f:["The Higgs boson was discovered in 2012.","Protons and neutrons are made of quarks.","The Standard Model does not include gravity."],
fun:"Trillions of neutrinos pass harmlessly through your body every second.",
tl:"Ang Standard Model ay ang teoryang naglalarawan sa pinakamaliliit na partikulo ng materya, gaya ng quark at lepton, at sa mga puwersang nagbubuklod sa kanila. Natuklasan ang Higgs boson noong 2012."});

ADDART({id:"isotopes",t:"Isotopes",c:"Science",i:"🧪",w:"Isotope",r:["atom","radioact","nuclear","fission","archaeology"],
p:[["Same element, different weight","Isotopes are forms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons."],["Clocks in the rocks","Radioactive isotopes help date fossils, rocks and ancient objects."],["Medicine","Isotopes are used in hospitals to diagnose and treat disease."]],
l:"Isotopes are different forms of the same chemical element whose atoms have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons, giving them different masses. For example, carbon has isotopes called carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14. Isotopes of an element behave almost identically in chemical reactions, but some are radioactive, breaking down over time. Scientists use isotopes to date ancient objects, trace how substances move through living things and the environment, diagnose and treat diseases and produce nuclear energy.",
s:[["What makes an isotope","An element is defined by the number of protons in the nucleus of its atoms: every carbon atom has six. But the number of neutrons can vary. Carbon-12 has six neutrons, carbon-13 has seven and carbon-14 has eight. The number after the name is the total of protons and neutrons, called the mass number."],["Stable and radioactive","Many isotopes are stable and last forever. Others are radioactive: their nuclei are unstable and break down, giving off radiation and turning into other elements. The time it takes for half of a sample to decay is its half-life, which can be a fraction of a second or billions of years."],["Discovery","In 1913 the British chemist Frederick Soddy realized that some elements came in forms with different masses. The name isotope, meaning same place in Greek, because they share one place in the periodic table, was suggested to him by the doctor and writer Margaret Todd. The same year, J. J. Thomson found two forms of the gas neon."],["Isotopes of hydrogen","Hydrogen has three isotopes: ordinary hydrogen, with no neutron; deuterium, with one, discovered in 1931; and radioactive tritium, with two. Water made with deuterium is called heavy water and is used in some nuclear reactors. Deuterium and tritium are fuels for nuclear fusion."]],
k:[["Definition","Atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons"],["Named by","Frederick Soddy, on Margaret Todd’s suggestion (1913)"],["Example","Carbon-12, carbon-13, carbon-14"],["Half-life of carbon-14","About 5,730 years"],["Uses","Dating, medicine, energy, research"]],
d:[["1913","Frederick Soddy introduces the word isotope"],["1919","Francis Aston’s mass spectrograph finds many isotopes"],["1931","Deuterium is discovered"],["1946","Willard Libby proposes radiocarbon dating"],["1960","Libby wins the Nobel Prize for radiocarbon dating"],["1972","A natural nuclear reactor is found at Oklo, Gabon"]],
s2:[["Radiocarbon dating","Living things take in carbon, including a tiny amount of radioactive carbon-14, while they are alive. After death, the carbon-14 slowly decays, halving about every 5,730 years. By measuring how much is left, scientists can date bones, wood, cloth and other remains up to about 50,000 years old. The American chemist Willard Libby developed the method in the late 1940s and won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1960."],["Dating rocks","Isotopes with very long half-lives, such as uranium-238, which has a half-life of about 4.5 billion years, are used to date rocks and minerals. Such measurements show that the oldest minerals on Earth are about 4.4 billion years old and that Earth itself formed about 4.5 billion years ago."],["Isotopes in medicine","Radioactive isotopes help doctors see inside the body and treat disease. Technetium-99m is the most widely used isotope for medical scans. Iodine-131 is used to treat thyroid problems, and cobalt-60 has long been used in radiotherapy for cancer. Many medical isotopes are made in nuclear reactors or particle accelerators."],["Tracing nature","Stable isotopes act as natural labels. Scientists use isotopes of oxygen in ancient ice and shells to work out past temperatures, isotopes in hair and bones to learn what ancient people ate and where they lived, and isotopes in water to trace where rain and groundwater come from."],["Nuclear energy","Natural uranium is mostly uranium-238, with only about 0.7 percent uranium-235, the isotope that splits easily in nuclear reactors. Enriching uranium means increasing its share of uranium-235. Isotope separation is difficult and costly because isotopes behave almost the same chemically."],["Archaeology and the Philippines","Radiocarbon and other dating methods have helped date important Philippine finds, such as the Tabon Cave remains in Palawan, tens of thousands of years old, and the remains of Homo luzonensis from Callao Cave in Cagayan, announced in 2019, which are at least 50,000 years old."]],
sp:["Nature’s own nuclear reactor","In 1972, French scientists checking uranium from a mine at Oklo in Gabon, Central Africa, noticed something strange: it contained slightly less uranium-235 than uranium everywhere else. Investigation revealed that about two billion years ago, natural uranium deposits there, soaked by groundwater, had started nuclear chain reactions on their own, running on and off for hundreds of thousands of years. The Oklo deposits are the only known natural nuclear reactors on Earth, and isotopes left behind tell their story."],
g:[["Isotope","A form of an element with a particular number of neutrons"],["Neutron","A particle with no electric charge in the nucleus of an atom"],["Half-life","The time for half of a radioactive substance to decay"],["Mass number","The total number of protons and neutrons in a nucleus"]],
f:["Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.","Hydrogen has three isotopes.","Oklo in Gabon had natural nuclear reactors."],
fun:"About two billion years ago, a uranium deposit in Gabon became a natural nuclear reactor.",
tl:"Ang isotope ay anyo ng isang elemento na may parehong bilang ng proton ngunit iba ang bilang ng neutron. Ginagamit ang mga isotope sa pagtukoy ng edad ng mga fossil, sa medisina at sa enerhiyang nukleyar."});

ADDART({id:"accelerators",t:"Particle Accelerators",c:"Science",i:"🌀",w:"Particle accelerator",r:["standardmodel","particles","electron","antimatter","medimaging"],
p:[["Speeding up particles","Particle accelerators push tiny particles to enormous speeds using electric and magnetic fields."],["The biggest machine","The Large Hadron Collider is a ring 27 kilometers around, buried underground."],["Not just for physics","Most accelerators are used in hospitals and industry."]],
l:"A particle accelerator is a machine that uses electric and magnetic fields to speed up charged particles, such as electrons or protons, to very high speeds, often close to the speed of light. Physicists smash these particles into targets or into each other to study the smallest building blocks of matter. The largest, the Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva, is a ring 27 kilometers around. But most of the tens of thousands of accelerators in the world are smaller machines used in hospitals to treat cancer, in factories and in research on materials, medicines and ancient objects.",
s:[["How they work","Charged particles are pushed forward by electric fields, gaining energy with each push. In circular accelerators, powerful magnets bend the particles around a ring so they can be pushed again and again. In linear accelerators, particles travel in a straight line through a series of electric fields."],["Smashing atoms","In 1932 John Cockcroft and Ernest Walton in Cambridge used an accelerator to split atoms of lithium with protons, the first nuclear reaction caused by artificially accelerated particles. The same period saw Ernest Lawrence in California build the first cyclotron, a circular accelerator small enough to hold in one hand."],["Why go faster?","The faster particles move, the more energy they carry. When they collide, this energy can turn into new particles, following Einstein’s equation E = mc². Higher energies allow physicists to create heavier, rarer particles and to see smaller details inside matter."],["Detectors","Huge detectors, some as big as buildings, surround the collision points. They track the particles that fly out, measuring their paths, energies and charges. Computers sort through billions of collisions to find rare events."]],
k:[["Purpose","Speeding up charged particles"],["Largest","Large Hadron Collider, CERN (27 km ring)"],["First atom split by accelerator","1932, Cockcroft and Walton"],["Uses","Physics research, cancer treatment, industry"],["Particles used","Electrons, protons, ions"]],
d:[["1931","Ernest Lawrence builds the first cyclotron"],["1932","Cockcroft and Walton split the atom"],["1954","CERN is founded"],["1966","The two-mile linear accelerator at Stanford begins operating"],["2008","The Large Hadron Collider starts up"],["2012","The Higgs boson is discovered at the LHC"]],
s2:[["The Large Hadron Collider","The Large Hadron Collider, or LHC, lies about 100 meters underground beneath the border of France and Switzerland. Started in 2008, it sends two beams of protons around its 27-kilometer ring in opposite directions, kept on course by superconducting magnets cooled colder than outer space, and smashes them together. In 2012 its experiments discovered the Higgs boson."],["Fighting cancer","Linear accelerators in hospitals produce high-energy X-rays and electron beams to destroy cancer cells. Proton therapy uses accelerated protons, which deliver most of their energy at a precise depth, sparing healthy tissue. Thousands of hospitals around the world, including in the Philippines, use medical accelerators."],["Light sources","Synchrotrons are circular accelerators in which electrons give off intense beams of light, from infrared to X-rays, as they curve. Scientists use this light to study the structure of proteins, develop medicines, test materials and examine paintings, fossils and ancient manuscripts without damaging them."],["In industry","Accelerators are used to sterilize medical equipment and food, strengthen plastics, make computer chips and inspect cargo containers. Small accelerators also produce radioactive isotopes for medical scans."],["SESAME","SESAME, a synchrotron light source in Jordan that opened in 2017, is run jointly by countries including Cyprus, Egypt, Iran, Israel, Jordan, Pakistan, Palestine and Turkey. Modeled on CERN, it aims to promote science and cooperation in the Middle East among countries that often disagree."],["Future machines","Physicists are designing larger colliders to explore beyond the Standard Model, such as a proposed Future Circular Collider of about 91 kilometers at CERN and a proposed collider in China. Supporters say they could answer deep questions about the universe; critics question their great cost."]],
sp:["Lawrence’s pocket cyclotron","In 1930 the young American physicist Ernest Lawrence had an idea: instead of building a very long accelerator, he could use a magnet to send particles round and round in a spiral, giving them a push each time. His first working cyclotron, built with his student M. Stanley Livingston, was only about 11 centimeters across and was made partly of brass, wax and glass. Bigger cyclotrons followed, producing new isotopes for medicine, and Lawrence won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1939. The element lawrencium is named after him."],
g:[["Accelerator","A machine that speeds up charged particles"],["Collider","An accelerator in which particles crash into each other"],["Cyclotron","A circular accelerator that spirals particles outward"],["Synchrotron","A circular accelerator that keeps particles on a fixed ring"]],
f:["The LHC ring is 27 kilometers around.","Most accelerators are used in medicine and industry.","The first cyclotron fit in one hand."],
fun:"The LHC’s magnets are kept colder than outer space.",
tl:"Ang particle accelerator ay makinang nagpapabilis sa maliliit na partikulo gamit ang kuryente at magnet. Ang pinakamalaki ay ang Large Hadron Collider sa CERN. Ginagamit din ang mga accelerator sa ospital laban sa kanser."});

ADDART({id:"fission",t:"Nuclear Fission",c:"Science",i:"☢️",w:"Nuclear fission",r:["nuclear","radioact","isotopes","fusion","nukeweapons"],
p:[["Splitting atoms","Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy atom’s nucleus into smaller parts."],["Huge energy","Fission releases millions of times more energy than burning fuel."],["Power and weapons","It powers nuclear power stations and was used in the first atomic bombs."]],
l:"Nuclear fission is a reaction in which the nucleus of a heavy atom, such as uranium or plutonium, splits into two smaller nuclei, releasing a great amount of energy and several neutrons. Those neutrons can split more nuclei, starting a chain reaction. Discovered in 1938, fission was used within a few years to build the first nuclear reactor and the atomic bombs of World War II. Today nuclear power stations use controlled fission to produce about a tenth of the world’s electricity, without the carbon dioxide emissions of fossil fuels, though they raise concerns about safety and radioactive waste.",
s:[["Discovery","In December 1938, the German chemists Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann bombarded uranium with neutrons and found barium, a much lighter element. Lise Meitner, an Austrian physicist who had fled Nazi Germany, and her nephew Otto Frisch explained that the uranium nucleus had split in two. Frisch named the process fission, after the way cells divide."],["The chain reaction","When a uranium-235 nucleus absorbs a neutron, it splits and releases two or three more neutrons. If these split other nuclei, a chain reaction follows, releasing energy rapidly. If the reaction is controlled, it provides steady heat; if uncontrolled, it causes an explosion."],["Where the energy comes from","The pieces left after fission weigh slightly less than the original nucleus. The missing mass becomes energy, following Einstein’s equation E = mc². A kilogram of uranium-235 can release as much energy as burning millions of kilograms of coal."],["The first reactor","On 2 December 1942, a team led by the Italian-born physicist Enrico Fermi achieved the first controlled nuclear chain reaction in Chicago Pile-1, a stack of graphite blocks and uranium under the stands of a sports field at the University of Chicago."]],
k:[["What happens","A heavy nucleus splits into smaller nuclei"],["Common fuels","Uranium-235 and plutonium-239"],["Discovered","1938"],["First chain reaction","Chicago, 2 December 1942"],["Share of world electricity","About a tenth"]],
d:[["1938","Hahn and Strassmann discover fission"],["1939","Meitner and Frisch explain fission"],["1942","Enrico Fermi achieves the first controlled chain reaction"],["1945","Fission bombs are used on Hiroshima and Nagasaki"],["1954","The first nuclear power plant connects to a grid, at Obninsk in the Soviet Union"],["1986","The Chernobyl disaster"]],
s2:[["Nuclear weapons","Fearing that Nazi Germany might build a fission bomb, the United States, with Britain and Canada, set up the Manhattan Project. Its bombs destroyed the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August 1945, killing well over 100,000 people. Since then, the spread and testing of nuclear weapons have been major international concerns."],["Nuclear power stations","In a nuclear power station, controlled fission heats water to make steam, which turns turbines to generate electricity. Control rods absorb neutrons to slow the reaction. Countries such as France, the United States, China, Russia and South Korea produce large amounts of nuclear electricity."],["Safety and accidents","Serious accidents have occurred, including at Three Mile Island in the United States in 1979, Chernobyl in Ukraine in 1986 and Fukushima in Japan in 2011, after an earthquake and tsunami. Supporters point out that nuclear power has caused far fewer deaths per unit of electricity than fossil fuels; opponents stress the risks of accidents and long-lasting contamination."],["Radioactive waste","Used nuclear fuel stays dangerously radioactive for thousands of years and must be stored safely. Finland is building one of the world’s first permanent deep underground storage sites for spent fuel. Finding long-term solutions is one of the main challenges of nuclear power."],["The Bataan Nuclear Power Plant","The Philippines built the Bataan Nuclear Power Plant in Morong, Bataan, completed in 1984 under President Ferdinand Marcos. Following the Chernobyl disaster, concerns about safety, nearby fault lines and corruption, the government of President Corazon Aquino decided in 1986 not to operate it. Whether to revive it or build new nuclear plants remains debated in the Philippines."],["Lise Meitner","Lise Meitner played a key part in discovering fission, but only Otto Hahn received the 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it. Many historians see this as one of the great oversights in Nobel history. Meitner refused to work on the atomic bomb. The element meitnerium is named in her honor."]],
sp:["A walk in the snow","Over Christmas 1938, Lise Meitner, living in exile in Sweden, received a letter from Otto Hahn describing his puzzling discovery of barium in bombarded uranium. Her nephew, Otto Frisch, came to visit, and the two went for a walk in the snow, Frisch on skis and Meitner on foot. Sitting on a tree trunk, they scribbled calculations on scraps of paper and realized that the uranium nucleus must have stretched like a drop of liquid and split in two, releasing enormous energy. Their explanation, published in early 1939, named the process nuclear fission."],
g:[["Fission","The splitting of a heavy atomic nucleus"],["Chain reaction","A process in which each reaction causes more reactions"],["Control rod","A rod that absorbs neutrons to slow a reactor"],["Enrichment","Increasing the share of uranium-235 in uranium"]],
f:["Fission was discovered in 1938.","The first controlled chain reaction took place in Chicago in 1942.","The Bataan Nuclear Power Plant has never operated."],
fun:"Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch worked out how fission happens during a walk in the snow.",
tl:"Ang nuclear fission ay ang paghahati ng nucleus ng mabigat na atom, gaya ng uranium, na naglalabas ng napakalaking enerhiya. Ginagamit ito sa mga planta ng kuryenteng nukleyar at ginamit sa mga unang bombang atomika."});

;
/* data/tech7.js */
// Technology articles, part 7: artificial intelligence in daily life, self-driving cars, the Turing test, geothermal energy and hydrogen.
ADDART({id:"aieveryday",t:"AI in Everyday Life",c:"Technology",i:"📱",w:"Applications of artificial intelligence",r:["ai","mlearn","llm","smartphone","aisafety"],
p:[["All around us","Artificial intelligence quietly works in phones, apps and websites that people use every day."],["Helpful tools","AI helps translate languages, find routes, filter spam and suggest songs and videos."],["Using it wisely","Knowing how AI works helps people use it safely and spot its mistakes."]],
l:"Artificial intelligence, or AI, is now part of everyday life for billions of people, often without their noticing. It recommends videos and songs, finds the fastest route through traffic, translates languages, unlocks phones by recognizing faces, filters spam emails, helps doctors read scans and answers questions through chatbots and voice assistants. These systems learn patterns from huge amounts of data. They can be extremely useful, but they also make mistakes, can reflect unfair biases in their data and raise questions about privacy, jobs and trust.",
s:[["Recommendations","Video, music and shopping apps use AI to predict what each person might like, based on what they and similar users have watched, heard or bought. These recommendation systems keep people engaged, but critics warn they can trap users in bubbles of similar content or encourage spending too much time on screens."],["Maps and traffic","Navigation apps combine maps with location data from many phones to estimate traffic and suggest the quickest route. AI predicts travel times and can reroute drivers, riders and delivery workers around jams and accidents."],["Language","Translation apps use AI trained on millions of translated texts to translate between hundreds of languages, including Filipino and other Philippine languages. Voice assistants turn speech into text and respond, and phones suggest words as people type."],["Chatbots","Since the launch of ChatGPT in November 2022, chatbots based on large language models have become widely used for answering questions, writing, studying and coding. They can be very helpful, but they sometimes produce confident answers that are wrong, so important facts should be checked against reliable sources."]],
k:[["What it does","Learns patterns from data to make predictions"],["Common uses","Recommendations, maps, translation, chatbots"],["Voice assistants","Siri (2011), Alexa (2014)"],["Chatbot boom","From late 2022"],["Main concerns","Errors, bias, privacy, jobs"]],
d:[["1997","IBM’s Deep Blue defeats the world chess champion"],["2006","Google Translate is launched"],["2011","Apple’s Siri voice assistant is released"],["2016","Translation apps switch to neural networks"],["2017","Phones begin unlocking with 3D face recognition"],["2022","ChatGPT is launched"]],
s2:[["Faces and photos","Phones and photo apps use AI to recognize faces, sort pictures, sharpen images and remove backgrounds. Face recognition is also used at airports and by police in some countries, which raises concerns about privacy, surveillance and mistakes, especially as some systems have been less accurate for certain groups of people."],["Health","AI helps doctors spot signs of disease in X-rays, eye scans and skin photos, sometimes as accurately as specialists. Smartwatches use it to detect irregular heartbeats. Such tools can help in places with few doctors, but they must be tested carefully and used with human judgment."],["Work and business","Banks use AI to detect fraud, companies use it to answer customer questions, and farmers use it to monitor crops. In the Philippines, a major center for call centers and business process outsourcing, AI tools are changing many jobs, creating new opportunities while raising worries about job losses."],["Bias and fairness","AI systems learn from data created by people, so they can repeat unfair patterns, such as treating some groups differently in hiring or lending. Researchers and governments work to test AI for bias and to make its decisions more transparent."],["Privacy","Many AI services collect large amounts of personal data, such as locations, voices and messages. Data privacy laws, such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation and the Philippines’ Data Privacy Act of 2012, set rules on how personal information can be used."],["Using AI wisely","Experts advise using AI as a helper, not a replacement for thinking: check important information, be careful about sharing personal details, watch for fake images and videos made with AI and remember that AI does not truly understand the world as people do."]],
sp:["Spam, the first AI many people met","One of the earliest kinds of AI that millions of people used every day was the spam filter. In the early 2000s, email inboxes were flooded with unwanted advertising and scams. Programs began using a statistical method called Bayesian filtering, which learned which words and patterns appeared more often in spam than in real messages. As users marked emails as spam, the filters improved. Today, email services block the great majority of spam before people ever see it."],
g:[["Artificial intelligence","Computer systems that perform tasks that normally need human intelligence"],["Recommendation system","Software that suggests content based on past behavior"],["Chatbot","A program that converses with people in text or speech"],["Bias","An unfair leaning for or against certain people or ideas"]],
f:["ChatGPT was launched in November 2022.","Spam filters were among the first everyday AI tools.","The Philippines has had a Data Privacy Act since 2012."],
fun:"Spam filters were one of the first kinds of AI millions of people used daily.",
tl:"Ginagamit ang artificial intelligence araw-araw sa mga cellphone at app, gaya ng pagsasalin ng wika, mapa, at mga chatbot. Kapaki-pakinabang ito ngunit maaaring magkamali, kaya dapat suriin ang mahahalagang impormasyon."});

ADDART({id:"selfdriving",t:"Self-Driving Cars",c:"Technology",i:"🚘",w:"Self-driving car",r:["cars","ai","compvision","ev","robot"],
p:[["Cars that drive themselves","Self-driving cars use sensors and AI to steer, speed up and brake without a human driver."],["Robotaxis","Driverless taxis already carry passengers in some cities in the United States and China."],["Big questions","Safety, laws and jobs are major questions for self-driving vehicles."]],
l:"A self-driving car, also called an autonomous vehicle, is a car that can sense its surroundings and drive itself with little or no human control. Using cameras, radar, lidar and powerful computers running artificial intelligence, these cars detect roads, other vehicles, pedestrians and traffic signals and decide how to move. Experiments began in the 1980s, and today driverless robotaxis operate in parts of the United States and China. Supporters hope self-driving cars will reduce accidents and help people who cannot drive, but they also raise questions about safety, responsibility and jobs.",
s:[["How they see","Self-driving cars use several kinds of sensors. Cameras read signs, lanes and traffic lights; radar measures the distance and speed of other vehicles; and lidar uses laser pulses to build a detailed 3D map of the surroundings. Computers combine this information to understand the scene."],["How they decide","AI software predicts what other road users will do and plans a safe path. It controls steering, acceleration and braking many times a second. The cars are trained on millions of kilometers of real and simulated driving."],["Levels of automation","Engineers describe automation in six levels, from 0, no automation, to 5, a car that can drive anywhere without a human. Many new cars have level 1 or 2 features, such as lane keeping and adaptive cruise control, which still require an attentive driver. Robotaxis operating without drivers in certain areas are level 4."],["Early pioneers","In the 1980s and 1990s, the German engineer Ernst Dickmanns built vans that drove themselves on highways using cameras. In 1995 a team from Carnegie Mellon University drove a minivan across the United States with the computer steering for almost the whole way."]],
k:[["Also called","Autonomous vehicles"],["Sensors","Cameras, radar, lidar"],["Levels","0 (none) to 5 (full)"],["Early challenge","DARPA Grand Challenge (2004–2007)"],["Robotaxis","Operating in parts of the US and China"]],
d:[["1995","A Carnegie Mellon minivan steers itself across the United States"],["2004","No vehicle finishes the first DARPA Grand Challenge"],["2005","Stanford’s Stanley wins the second DARPA challenge"],["2009","Google begins its self-driving car project, later called Waymo"],["2018","A self-driving test car kills a pedestrian in Arizona"],["2020","Fully driverless robotaxi rides open to the public in Phoenix"]],
s2:[["The DARPA challenges","In 2004 the US Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency offered a prize for a robot vehicle that could cross the Mojave Desert. No vehicle finished; the best managed about 12 kilometers. In 2005, five vehicles completed the course, led by Stanford University’s Stanley. A 2007 Urban Challenge tested driving in city traffic, inspiring many engineers who later founded self-driving companies."],["Robotaxis today","Waymo, which began as Google’s self-driving project, operates driverless taxis in several US cities, and companies such as Baidu run robotaxis in Chinese cities such as Wuhan. Passengers summon them through apps. These services operate in mapped areas and in suitable conditions."],["Safety","Most road crashes are caused by human error, such as distraction, speeding and drunk driving, and supporters argue that self-driving cars could save many lives. However, self-driving systems can be confused by unusual situations. In 2018 a self-driving test car in Tempe, Arizona, struck and killed a pedestrian, the first such death, leading to tighter testing rules."],["Who is responsible?","When a self-driving car crashes, deciding who is responsible, the passenger, the owner, the manufacturer or the software company, is a new legal question. Governments are writing new laws on testing, insurance and safety standards."],["Jobs and cities","Self-driving trucks and taxis could change the work of millions of professional drivers, including jeepney, bus and delivery drivers. They could also change cities by reducing the need for parking. Many experts expect the change to be gradual, varying greatly between countries."],["Challenges in busy streets","Self-driving cars work best on well-marked roads with predictable traffic. Crowded streets with motorcycles, tricycles, pedestrians and street vendors, as in many Asian, African and Latin American cities, as well as heavy rain and flooding, remain difficult challenges."]],
sp:["Stanley crosses the desert","In October 2005, 23 robot vehicles lined up in the Mojave Desert for the second DARPA Grand Challenge, a 212-kilometer course of dirt roads, tunnels and mountain passes. A year earlier, none had finished. This time, a blue Volkswagen Touareg named Stanley, built by a team from Stanford University led by Sebastian Thrun, drove the whole course by itself in under seven hours, winning the $2 million prize. Stanley used lasers, cameras and machine learning, and it now rests in the Smithsonian’s National Museum of American History."],
g:[["Autonomous","Able to operate without human control"],["Lidar","A sensor that measures distance using laser light"],["Robotaxi","A self-driving taxi with no human driver"],["Adaptive cruise control","A system that keeps a safe distance from the car ahead"]],
f:["Stanley won the DARPA Grand Challenge in 2005.","Automation is described in six levels.","Robotaxis carry passengers in parts of the US and China."],
fun:"In the first DARPA desert challenge in 2004, no robot car got more than about 12 kilometers.",
tl:"Ang self-driving car ay sasakyang nagmamaneho nang mag-isa gamit ang mga sensor at artificial intelligence. May mga robotaxi na sa ilang lungsod sa Estados Unidos at Tsina, ngunit may mga tanong pa tungkol sa kaligtasan at trabaho."});

ADDART({id:"turingtest",t:"The Turing Test",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"Turing test",r:["ai","aihistory","llm","comp","turing"],
p:[["Can machines think?","In 1950, Alan Turing asked whether machines can think and proposed a test."],["The imitation game","If a judge chatting by text cannot tell a computer from a human, the computer passes."],["Still debated","Whether passing the test proves real intelligence is still argued about."]],
l:"The Turing test is a test of a machine’s ability to show intelligent behavior indistinguishable from that of a human. It was proposed in 1950 by the British mathematician Alan Turing, who called it the imitation game. In the test, a human judge holds text conversations with a hidden human and a hidden computer; if the judge cannot reliably tell which is which, the computer is said to pass. The test has inspired decades of chatbots and debate about what intelligence means, and modern AI chatbots have brought it back into the news.",
s:[["Turing’s question","In his 1950 paper Computing Machinery and Intelligence, published in the journal Mind, Alan Turing began with the question: can machines think? Finding it hard to define thinking, he replaced the question with a practical game that could be tested."],["How the game works","In Turing’s version, a judge types questions to two hidden players, a human and a machine, and reads their typed answers. The machine tries to seem human. Turing predicted that by about the year 2000, computers would fool an average judge at least 30 percent of the time after five minutes of questioning."],["Turing’s replies to critics","Turing considered objections, such as that machines can only do what they are programmed to do, or that they could never have feelings. He argued that we judge other people’s minds from their behavior, so we might judge machines the same way."],["ELIZA","In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT created ELIZA, a simple program that imitated a therapist by turning users’ statements into questions. Although it understood nothing, some users became emotionally attached to it, surprising and worrying Weizenbaum."]],
k:[["Proposed by","Alan Turing (1950)"],["Original name","The imitation game"],["Format","Text conversations judged by a human"],["Famous early chatbot","ELIZA (1966)"],["Reverse version","CAPTCHA"]],
d:[["1950","Alan Turing proposes the imitation game"],["1966","ELIZA is created"],["1980","John Searle presents the Chinese Room argument"],["1991","The first Loebner Prize competition is held"],["2003","The term CAPTCHA is introduced"],["2025","A study reports a chatbot judged human more often than real people"]],
s2:[["The Loebner Prize","From 1991 to the late 2010s, the Loebner Prize held yearly competitions in which chatbots tried to convince judges they were human. For years, no program came close to passing. Critics said the contest rewarded tricks, such as fake typing errors, rather than real intelligence."],["The Chinese Room","In 1980 the philosopher John Searle imagined a person in a room following a rulebook to answer questions in Chinese without understanding Chinese. To outsiders, the answers seem fluent. Searle argued that computers, like the person in the room, manipulate symbols without understanding, so passing the Turing test would not prove a machine has a mind. Others disagree, and the argument is still debated."],["CAPTCHA","A CAPTCHA, short for Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart, is a puzzle on websites, such as reading distorted letters or picking images, designed to block automated programs. It is a reverse Turing test, judged by a computer to identify humans."],["Chatbots today","Large language models can hold fluent conversations on almost any topic. In a 2025 study at the University of California, San Diego, judges chatting with a person and an AI at the same time picked the AI as the human more often than the real person when it was told to adopt a particular persona. Some called this passing the Turing test; others argued it shows the test measures imitation, not understanding."],["Is the test enough?","Many researchers now think the Turing test is not the best measure of intelligence. AI systems can be fluent yet make basic errors, and humans may be fooled easily. Scientists have proposed other tests, such as solving new problems, reasoning or performing practical tasks in the real world."],["Turing’s legacy","Alan Turing is considered a founder of computer science and artificial intelligence. His test remains one of the best-known ideas in AI and a starting point for thinking about whether machines can think, what intelligence is and how humans should relate to increasingly capable machines."]],
sp:["Eugene Goostman","In June 2014, at an event at the Royal Society in London, a chatbot called Eugene Goostman, presented as a 13-year-old boy from Ukraine, convinced 33 percent of judges that it was human in five-minute conversations. The organizers announced that the Turing test had been passed for the first time. Critics quickly objected: pretending to be a young teenager writing in his second language made odd or evasive answers seem natural. The episode showed how easily the test can be gamed and fueled debate about what it really measures."],
g:[["Turing test","A test of whether a machine can imitate human conversation"],["Chatbot","A program that converses with people"],["CAPTCHA","An online test that tells humans and computers apart"],["Chinese Room","A thought experiment questioning whether computers understand"]],
f:["Alan Turing proposed his test in 1950.","ELIZA was created in 1966.","A CAPTCHA is a reverse Turing test."],
fun:"Every time you solve a CAPTCHA, you are taking a kind of Turing test.",
tl:"Ang Turing test ay pagsubok kung kayang gayahin ng makina ang pakikipag-usap ng tao. Iminungkahi ito ni Alan Turing noong 1950. Pinagtatalunan pa rin kung ang pagpasa rito ay patunay ng tunay na talino."});

ADDART({id:"geothermal",t:"Geothermal Energy",c:"Technology",i:"♨️",w:"Geothermal energy",r:["renew","volc","phpower","iceland","kenyacountry"],
p:[["Heat from the Earth","Geothermal energy uses heat from deep inside the Earth."],["Steady power","Unlike sun and wind, it can produce electricity day and night."],["A Philippine strength","The Philippines is one of the world’s largest geothermal power producers."]],
l:"Geothermal energy is heat from inside the Earth, which comes from the planet’s formation and from the decay of radioactive elements in its rocks. In volcanic regions, this heat warms underground water into hot springs, geysers and steam. People have bathed in hot springs for thousands of years, and since the early 1900s geothermal steam has been used to generate electricity. Geothermal power is renewable, produces little carbon dioxide and runs around the clock. Leading producers include the United States, Indonesia, the Philippines, Turkey, New Zealand, Kenya, Mexico, Italy and Iceland.",
s:[["Where the heat comes from","The Earth’s core is about as hot as the surface of the Sun. Heat flows outward through the rocks. In most places it is too deep to reach easily, but near volcanoes and plate boundaries, such as the Pacific Ring of Fire and the East African Rift, hot rock lies close to the surface."],["Making electricity","Wells drilled one to three kilometers deep bring up hot water and steam. The steam spins turbines connected to generators. The cooled water is usually pumped back underground to be heated again, helping the resource last."],["Heating and cooling","Hot water from the ground can heat homes, greenhouses and swimming pools. Ground-source heat pumps use the steady temperature just below the surface to heat buildings in winter and cool them in summer, and they can work almost anywhere."],["Advantages","Geothermal plants produce electricity steadily, day and night, in all weather, unlike solar and wind power. They take up little land and release far less carbon dioxide than coal or gas plants."]],
k:[["Source","Heat inside the Earth"],["First geothermal electricity","Larderello, Italy (1904)"],["Main uses","Electricity, heating, bathing"],["Leading producers","United States, Indonesia, Philippines, Turkey, New Zealand, Kenya"],["Advantage","Runs day and night"]],
d:[["1904","Geothermal steam lights bulbs at Larderello, Italy"],["1958","Wairakei, New Zealand, opens"],["1960","The Geysers in California begins producing power"],["1979","Tiwi and Makban geothermal plants open in the Philippines"],["1981","Kenya opens its first geothermal plant at Olkaria"],["2023","A pilot enhanced geothermal project succeeds in the United States"]],
s2:[["Geothermal in the Philippines","Sitting on the Pacific Ring of Fire, the Philippines has long been one of the top geothermal power producers in the world. Major fields include Tiwi in Albay, Makban in Laguna and Batangas, Tongonan in Leyte, and Mount Apo in Mindanao. Geothermal has supplied a significant share of the country’s electricity, especially on the islands of Luzon, Leyte and Negros."],["Kenya and the Rift","Kenya, on the East African Rift, has become Africa’s leading geothermal producer. Its Olkaria fields in Hell’s Gate National Park provide a large share of the country’s electricity, and other Rift Valley countries, such as Ethiopia, are developing geothermal power too."],["Iceland","Iceland, a volcanic island, uses geothermal energy to heat most of its homes and to produce much of its electricity. Its capital, Reykjavík, is heated by water piped from geothermal fields, and the famous Blue Lagoon spa uses water from a geothermal plant."],["Drawbacks","Geothermal plants can only be built where heat is accessible, and drilling is expensive and risky. Some plants release gases such as hydrogen sulfide, which smells like rotten eggs, and injecting water underground can sometimes cause small earthquakes."],["Enhanced geothermal","Enhanced geothermal systems create reservoirs by pumping water into hot dry rock deep underground. In 2023 a pilot project in Nevada and Utah in the United States showed promising results. If successful on a large scale, such methods could make geothermal energy available in many more places."],["Hot springs through history","The ancient Romans built baths around hot springs, such as at Bath in England. In Japan, onsen hot-spring baths are a beloved tradition. Since the 1300s, the French town of Chaudes-Aigues has heated homes with hot spring water. In the Philippines, hot springs in Laguna, such as at Los Baños, and elsewhere are popular resorts."]],
sp:["Five light bulbs at Larderello","Larderello, in Tuscany, Italy, had long been known for its steaming vents and boric acid industry. On 4 July 1904, Prince Piero Ginori Conti connected a small generator to a steam-driven engine using natural steam from the ground and lit five light bulbs. It was the first time geothermal energy had produced electricity. By 1913 a commercial geothermal power plant was operating there, and Larderello still generates geothermal electricity more than a century later."],
g:[["Geothermal","Relating to heat from inside the Earth"],["Geyser","A hot spring that shoots water and steam into the air"],["Heat pump","A device that moves heat from one place to another"],["Ring of Fire","A zone of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific Ocean"]],
f:["Geothermal electricity was first produced in Italy in 1904.","The Philippines is a top geothermal producer.","Iceland heats most homes with geothermal water."],
fun:"The world’s first geothermal electricity lit just five light bulbs.",
tl:"Ang geothermal energy ay init mula sa loob ng Daigdig. Ginagamit ito sa paggawa ng kuryente at pagpapainit. Isa ang Pilipinas sa pinakamalalaking gumagamit ng geothermal na kuryente, gaya sa Tiwi, Makban at Tongonan."});

ADDART({id:"hydrogen",t:"Hydrogen Energy",c:"Technology",i:"💧",w:"Hydrogen economy",r:["renew","battery","ev","elec","climchange"],
p:[["A clean fuel","When hydrogen is used as a fuel, its only waste product is water."],["Made, not mined","Most hydrogen must be produced from other substances using energy."],["Many shades","Hydrogen is called grey, blue or green depending on how it is made."]],
l:"Hydrogen is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe, and it can be used as a fuel that releases energy without producing carbon dioxide, leaving only water. It can power vehicles through fuel cells, store energy from solar and wind power, and replace fossil fuels in industries such as steelmaking and fertilizer production. However, almost all hydrogen used today is made from natural gas, which releases carbon dioxide. Producing clean hydrogen cheaply, and storing and transporting it safely, are major challenges.",
s:[["Why hydrogen?","Hydrogen stores a lot of energy for its weight. When it combines with oxygen, in a flame or a fuel cell, it releases energy and makes water. This makes it attractive for cutting greenhouse gas emissions in areas where batteries are less practical, such as heavy industry, ships and long-distance trucks."],["How it is made","Hydrogen on Earth is mostly locked in water and other compounds, so it must be produced. Most is made from natural gas in a process that releases carbon dioxide. It can also be made by electrolysis, using electricity to split water into hydrogen and oxygen."],["Colors of hydrogen","People describe hydrogen by colors. Grey hydrogen is made from fossil fuels, with carbon dioxide released. Blue hydrogen is made from fossil fuels but with the carbon dioxide captured and stored. Green hydrogen is made by electrolysis using renewable electricity, producing almost no emissions."],["Fuel cells","A fuel cell combines hydrogen and oxygen to produce electricity directly, with water and heat as by-products. The idea was demonstrated in 1839 by the Welsh scientist William Grove. Fuel cells provided power and drinking water for astronauts on the Apollo missions."]],
k:[["Waste product when used","Water"],["Most common source today","Natural gas"],["Clean method","Electrolysis with renewable electricity"],["Fuel cell inventor","William Grove (1839)"],["Main uses today","Fertilizer and oil refining"]],
d:[["1766","Henry Cavendish identifies hydrogen as an element"],["1839","William Grove demonstrates the fuel cell principle"],["1913","Industrial production of ammonia from hydrogen begins"],["1937","The hydrogen-filled Hindenburg airship catches fire"],["1960s","Fuel cells power Apollo spacecraft"],["2018","Hydrogen fuel cell passenger trains enter service in Germany"]],
s2:[["Hydrogen today","Most hydrogen produced today is used to make ammonia for fertilizers, through the Haber-Bosch process developed in Germany in the early 1900s, and to refine oil. Ammonia fertilizers help grow food for a large part of the world’s population."],["Vehicles","Hydrogen fuel cell cars, such as the Toyota Mirai, launched in 2014, refuel in minutes and emit only water vapor. However, there are few hydrogen filling stations, and battery electric cars have become far more common. Hydrogen may be more useful for buses, trucks, trains and ships."],["Industry","Steelmaking produces large amounts of carbon dioxide because it uses coal. Using hydrogen instead to remove oxygen from iron ore could greatly reduce emissions, and pilot plants are being built in Sweden and elsewhere."],["Challenges","Hydrogen is difficult to store because it is very light and must be compressed or cooled to extremely low temperatures. It leaks easily and can make metals brittle. Making it by electrolysis loses energy, and green hydrogen is still more expensive than grey hydrogen."],["Safety","Hydrogen is highly flammable, but it also disperses quickly into the air. The 1937 fire of the airship Hindenburg in the United States made many people fear hydrogen, though modern engineers say hydrogen can be handled safely with proper precautions, as natural gas and gasoline are."],["Hydrogen around the world","Countries such as Japan, South Korea, Germany, Australia and Chile have national hydrogen strategies. Sunny and windy places, such as Australia, Chile, Namibia, Morocco and the Middle East, hope to produce green hydrogen for export. Whether hydrogen will play a large or small role in the future of energy is debated."]],
sp:["The village powered by natural hydrogen","In 1987, workers drilling for water near the village of Bourakébougou in Mali, West Africa, found something unexpected: a gas that flowed from the well. Decades later, in 2012, it was confirmed to be almost pure hydrogen, produced naturally underground, and a company began using it to generate electricity for the village. Bourakébougou became the first place known to use natural hydrogen as a fuel. The discovery sparked a worldwide search for underground deposits of natural, or white, hydrogen."],
g:[["Fuel cell","A device that turns hydrogen and oxygen into electricity"],["Electrolysis","Using electricity to split a substance such as water"],["Green hydrogen","Hydrogen made using renewable electricity"],["Ammonia","A compound of nitrogen and hydrogen used in fertilizers"]],
f:["Using hydrogen as fuel produces only water.","Most hydrogen today comes from natural gas.","Fuel cells powered the Apollo spacecraft."],
fun:"A village in Mali generates electricity from natural hydrogen found underground.",
tl:"Ang hydrogen ay maaaring gamitin bilang panggatong na tubig lamang ang dumi. Karamihan ng hydrogen ngayon ay gawa sa natural gas, ngunit ang green hydrogen ay gawa gamit ang kuryente mula sa araw at hangin."});

;
/* data/animals11.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 11: the Komodo dragon, sea snakes, albatrosses, flamingos, locusts and fireflies.
ADDART({id:"komodo",t:"The Komodo Dragon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Komodo dragon",r:["lizard","indonesiacountry","conservation","croc","dino"],
p:[["The largest lizard","The Komodo dragon is the biggest lizard alive today."],["Island giant","It lives only on a few islands in Indonesia."],["Venomous bite","Its bite delivers venom that stops blood from clotting."]],
l:"The Komodo dragon is the largest living lizard, growing up to about 3 meters long and weighing up to about 70 kilograms in the wild. It lives only on a handful of islands in southeastern Indonesia, including Komodo, Rinca and Flores. A powerful predator, it hunts deer, pigs and water buffalo, using sharp serrated teeth and venom that prevents blood from clotting. Known to Western science only since 1910, the Komodo dragon is now endangered, protected in Komodo National Park, a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["Appearance","Komodo dragons have long, heavy bodies, strong legs with curved claws, a muscular tail and rough, scaly skin reinforced with small bones called osteoderms. Their long yellow forked tongues flick in and out to taste the air and detect smells, even of dead animals kilometers away."],["Hunting","Komodo dragons often wait in ambush beside trails and then charge at prey with a burst of speed. They bite with sharp, serrated teeth like a shark’s and pull back to tear flesh. Prey that escapes may weaken from blood loss and venom, and the dragon follows it."],["Venom","Scientists once believed bacteria in the dragon’s mouth killed its prey. In 2009 researchers showed that Komodo dragons have venom glands in their lower jaw. The venom lowers blood pressure, causes heavy bleeding and prevents clotting, sending prey into shock."],["Eating","A Komodo dragon can eat up to 80 percent of its body weight in a single meal. It swallows large chunks, even bones and hooves, and can survive on a few big meals a year. Dragons gather at carcasses, where the largest eat first."]],
k:[["Length","Up to about 3 m"],["Weight","Up to about 70 kg in the wild"],["Home","A few islands in Indonesia"],["Diet","Deer, pigs, water buffalo and carrion"],["Status","Endangered"]],
d:[["1910","A Dutch officer reports the dragons to scientists"],["1912","The species is scientifically described"],["1980","Komodo National Park is established"],["1991","The park becomes a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2006","Virgin births are confirmed in captive dragons"],["2021","The Komodo dragon is listed as endangered"]],
s2:[["Life cycle","Females lay about 20 eggs, often in nests dug by megapode birds. The eggs hatch after about seven to eight months. Young dragons live in trees for their first years to avoid being eaten by adults, which sometimes eat young dragons. Komodo dragons can live for about 30 years."],["Virgin births","In 2006 scientists confirmed that female Komodo dragons in two British zoos had laid eggs that hatched without any male, a process called parthenogenesis. This ability may help a single female start a new population on an empty island."],["Island giants","Komodo dragons may have evolved large size from ancestors in Australia, where giant monitor lizards lived. On islands with few large predators, they became the top hunters. Fossils show close relatives once lived across a wider area."],["Komodo National Park","Komodo National Park, created in 1980, protects the dragons and the rich coral reefs around the islands. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991. Tourism brings income to local communities but must be managed to protect the animals and their habitats."],["Threats","Fewer than a few thousand Komodo dragons live in the wild. They face loss of habitat, fires, poaching of the deer they eat and the effects of climate change, as rising sea levels could flood parts of their small island homes. In 2021 they were listed as endangered."],["Dragons and people","Attacks on people are rare but can happen, and villagers living near the dragons take care. Local people on Komodo have stories that tell of a kinship between humans and the dragons, which they call ora. The dragon is a symbol of Indonesia’s natural heritage."]],
sp:["Teeth tipped with iron","In 2024, scientists studying Komodo dragon teeth discovered that the cutting edges and tips are coated with a thin layer of iron, which shows up as an orange color. The iron helps keep the jagged, saw-like edges sharp and strong for slicing through flesh. Komodo dragons replace their teeth throughout their lives, and the iron coating may help each tooth stay effective. Researchers think some meat-eating dinosaurs may have had similarly reinforced teeth."],
g:[["Monitor lizard","A family of large, active lizards to which the Komodo dragon belongs"],["Venom","A poison delivered by a bite or sting"],["Parthenogenesis","Reproduction without fertilization by a male"],["Carrion","The flesh of dead animals"]],
f:["The Komodo dragon is the largest living lizard.","It lives only in Indonesia.","Females can reproduce without males."],
fun:"Komodo dragon teeth are tipped with iron.",
tl:"Ang Komodo dragon ang pinakamalaking butiki sa mundo. Nakatira lamang ito sa ilang pulo sa Indonesia. May kamandag ang kagat nito, at nanganganib na itong maubos."});

ADDART({id:"seasnakes",t:"Sea Snakes",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐍",w:"Sea snake",r:["snake","reef","pacific","taal","ph"],
p:[["Snakes of the sea","Sea snakes are venomous snakes that live in warm coastal waters."],["Paddle tails","Flattened, paddle-shaped tails make them excellent swimmers."],["A Philippine lake","One rare sea snake lives only in freshwater Lake Taal."]],
l:"Sea snakes are venomous snakes that have adapted to life in the ocean, found in warm coastal waters of the Indian and Pacific Oceans. There are about 70 species of true sea snakes, which spend their entire lives at sea, and several species of sea kraits, which come ashore to rest and lay eggs. Related to cobras, most have powerful venom but are generally shy and rarely bite people. One remarkable species, the Taal sea snake, lives only in freshwater Lake Taal in the Philippines.",
s:[["Built for the sea","Sea snakes have flattened, paddle-shaped tails for swimming. Their nostrils are on top of their heads and can close underwater. Some can stay underwater for hours, and some absorb a little oxygen through their skin. Special glands under the tongue remove excess salt."],["Sea kraits","Sea kraits, often banded black and blue or white, hunt in the sea but return to land to rest, digest food, shed their skin and lay eggs. They are common around rocky islands and coral reefs in Southeast Asia and the Pacific."],["True sea snakes","True sea snakes live entirely in the water and give birth to live young at sea. On land, they are almost helpless. Some species stay near reefs, while the yellow-bellied sea snake drifts in the open ocean and has the widest range of any snake."],["Hunting","Most sea snakes hunt fish and eels, probing cracks in reefs. Some specialize in eating fish eggs and have very weak venom. Their venom works quickly on fish, which might otherwise swim away before dying."]],
k:[["Number of species","About 70 true sea snakes, plus sea kraits"],["Range","Warm Indian and Pacific Oceans"],["Relatives","Cobras and other elapid snakes"],["Breathing","Air, at the surface"],["Freshwater species","Taal sea snake, Philippines"]],
d:[["1700s","European naturalists begin describing sea snakes"],["1881","The Taal sea snake is scientifically described"],["1970s","Studies show sea snakes absorb oxygen through their skin"],["1990","Light-sensitive cells are found in a sea snake’s tail"],["2008","Sea kraits are shown to need fresh water to drink"],["2015","Two Australian sea snakes feared extinct are rediscovered"]],
s2:[["Venom","Many sea snakes have highly potent venom, among the strongest of all snakes. Fortunately, they are usually calm and reluctant to bite, and many bites deliver little venom. Most bites happen to fishers handling nets. Antivenom is available in some countries."],["The Taal sea snake","The Taal sea snake, Hydrophis semperi, lives only in Lake Taal in Batangas, a freshwater lake inside a volcano. It is one of only two sea snakes known to live entirely in freshwater, the other living in a lake on Rennell Island in the Solomon Islands. Scientists think its ancestors were trapped when the lake was cut off from the sea centuries ago. It is threatened by pollution, fishing nets and volcanic eruptions."],["Seeing with the tail","The olive sea snake of Australia has light-sensitive cells in its tail, discovered in 1990. This may help the snake know whether its tail is hidden while it shelters in reef crevices, keeping it safe from predators."],["Drinking fresh water","Although they live in the sea, sea snakes need fresh water to drink. Studies show that some become dehydrated during dry seasons and drink from layers of fresh rainwater floating on the sea surface after storms."],["Sea snakes in the Philippines","Philippine waters are home to many sea snakes. Banded sea kraits are often seen around reefs and islands such as Gato Island off Cebu, a popular dive site. Divers are advised to watch them calmly and never to touch or grab them."],["Threats","Sea snakes are caught in fishing nets, hunted for their skins and meat in some places and threatened by damage to coral reefs and by pollution. Some populations have declined sharply, and two Australian species were feared extinct until they were rediscovered in 2015."]],
sp:["Snakes that went back to sea","Millions of years ago, the ancestors of snakes lived on land. Sea snakes are descendants of land-dwelling venomous snakes, related to Australian cobras, that returned to the ocean. Over time they developed paddle tails, valve-like nostrils, salt glands and the ability to give birth in water. Sea kraits made the move separately and still keep a foot on land. This return to the sea mirrors the evolution of whales, dolphins and sea turtles, whose ancestors also lived on land."],
g:[["Sea krait","A sea snake that comes ashore to rest and lay eggs"],["Elapid","A family of venomous snakes including cobras and sea snakes"],["Antivenom","A medicine that counters snake venom"],["Reef","A ridge of coral or rock near the sea surface"]],
f:["There are about 70 species of true sea snakes.","Sea kraits lay eggs on land.","The Taal sea snake lives only in Lake Taal."],
fun:"One sea snake can sense light with its tail.",
tl:"Ang mga ahas-dagat ay makamandag na ahas na nakatira sa mainit na dagat ng Indian at Pacific Ocean. Ang Taal sea snake ay nakatira lamang sa tubig-tabang ng Lawa ng Taal sa Batangas."});

ADDART({id:"albatross",t:"Albatrosses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🕊️",w:"Albatross",r:["birds","birdmigr","penguin","antarc","plasticwaste"],
p:[["Giant wings","Albatrosses have the longest wingspans of any living birds."],["Ocean wanderers","They can glide for hours over the ocean without flapping."],["Long lives","Some albatrosses live for more than 70 years."]],
l:"Albatrosses are large seabirds famous for their enormous wings and their ability to travel vast distances over the ocean. The wandering albatross has the largest wingspan of any living bird, up to about 3.5 meters. Most of the roughly two dozen species live in the Southern Ocean around Antarctica, with a few in the North Pacific. Albatrosses can glide for hours without flapping, spend years at sea, mate for life and live for decades. Many species are threatened, especially by fishing lines and plastic pollution.",
s:[["Masters of gliding","Albatrosses use a technique called dynamic soaring, using differences in wind speed above the waves to gain energy and glide almost effortlessly. A special lock in their shoulders lets them hold their wings out without using muscle. Some albatrosses have been tracked flying around the world in under two months."],["Life at sea","Young albatrosses may stay at sea for several years before returning to land. They sleep on the water and probably sometimes while gliding. They drink seawater, removing the salt with glands above their eyes."],["Food","Albatrosses eat squid, fish and krill, catching them at the surface or by shallow dives. They have an excellent sense of smell, unusual among birds, which helps them find food over the vast ocean."],["Breeding","Albatrosses breed in colonies on remote islands. Pairs perform elaborate courtship dances with bowing, sky-pointing and bill-clapping, and usually mate for life. Most species lay a single egg, and both parents take turns incubating it and feeding the chick, sometimes flying thousands of kilometers on a single feeding trip."]],
k:[["Largest wingspan","Wandering albatross, up to about 3.5 m"],["Number of species","About 22"],["Main range","Southern Ocean and North Pacific"],["Lifespan","Often over 50 years"],["Main threat","Fishing lines and plastic"]],
d:[["1798","Coleridge’s poem The Rime of the Ancient Mariner features an albatross"],["1949","The short-tailed albatross is feared extinct"],["1956","The Laysan albatross Wisdom is first banded on Midway"],["2001","An international agreement to protect albatrosses is signed"],["2004","The agreement comes into force"],["2020s","Wisdom, over 70 years old, is still laying eggs"]],
s2:[["Wisdom","Wisdom, a Laysan albatross on Midway Atoll in the Pacific, was first banded by the scientist Chandler Robbins in 1956, when she was already at least five years old. Decades later, she was still returning to breed and raising chicks into her seventies, making her the oldest known wild bird in the world."],["Deadly fishing lines","Albatrosses dive for bait on longline fishing hooks and are dragged underwater and drowned. Tens of thousands die this way each year. Simple measures, such as setting lines at night, weighting hooks and flying streamers to scare birds away, can greatly reduce deaths."],["Plastic","Albatrosses mistake floating plastic for food and feed it to their chicks. On Midway Atoll, dead chicks are often found with stomachs full of bottle caps, lighters and other plastic, making them a symbol of ocean plastic pollution."],["Protection","Most albatross species are threatened. In 2001 countries signed the Agreement on the Conservation of Albatrosses and Petrels, which came into force in 2004 and works to reduce bycatch and protect breeding islands. Removing invasive rats and mice from islands has also helped."],["The short-tailed albatross","The short-tailed albatross of the North Pacific was hunted for its feathers by the millions and was feared extinct by 1949. A few birds were found on Torishima island in Japan, and with protection, the population has slowly recovered to thousands of birds."],["Albatrosses in stories","Sailors once believed albatrosses carried the souls of lost seafarers and that killing one brought bad luck. In Samuel Taylor Coleridge’s 1798 poem The Rime of the Ancient Mariner, a sailor shoots an albatross and is cursed, forced to wear its body around his neck. The phrase an albatross around one’s neck now means a heavy burden."]],
sp:["Wisdom, the oldest wild bird","When the American scientist Chandler Robbins placed a metal band on the leg of a Laysan albatross on Midway Atoll in 1956, he could not have imagined he would see her again 46 years later. In 2002, Robbins, then in his eighties, found the same bird, now named Wisdom, still breeding. Wisdom continued to return year after year, raising dozens of chicks and outliving several mates. In her seventies, she laid yet another egg, amazing scientists. She has probably flown millions of kilometers in her lifetime."],
g:[["Wingspan","The distance from one wingtip to the other"],["Dynamic soaring","Gliding by using changes in wind speed near the sea surface"],["Bycatch","Animals caught accidentally by fishing"],["Colony","A large group of birds nesting together"]],
f:["The wandering albatross has the largest wingspan of any living bird.","Albatrosses usually mate for life.","Wisdom is the oldest known wild bird."],
fun:"Some albatrosses can fly around the world in under two months.",
tl:"Ang albatross ay malaking ibong-dagat na may pinakamahabang pakpak sa lahat ng ibon. Kaya nitong lumipad nang matagal nang hindi ikinakampay ang pakpak. Nanganganib ang maraming uri nito dahil sa pangingisda at plastik."});

ADDART({id:"flamingo",t:"Flamingos",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦩",w:"Flamingo",r:["birds","wetlands","lakes","tanzaniacountry","birdmigr"],
p:[["Pink birds","Flamingos are tall wading birds famous for their pink feathers."],["Color from food","Their pink color comes from pigments in the algae and shrimp they eat."],["Upside-down feeding","They feed with their heads upside down, filtering food from water."]],
l:"Flamingos are tall wading birds with long legs, long curved necks and bright pink feathers. There are six species, living in warm regions of Africa, Asia, Europe and the Americas, usually in large flocks around shallow, salty or alkaline lakes and lagoons. Their pink color comes from pigments called carotenoids in the algae and tiny shrimp they eat. Flamingos feed with their heads upside down, using their bills to filter food from the water, and often stand on one leg.",
s:[["Pink from food","Flamingo chicks hatch with gray or white feathers. As they eat algae and brine shrimp containing carotenoid pigments, the same pigments that make carrots orange, their feathers turn pink, red or orange. Flamingos in zoos are given special food to keep their color."],["Filter feeding","A flamingo lowers its head into the water upside down and sweeps it from side to side. Its tongue pumps water through comb-like plates in its bent bill, trapping algae, shrimp and other tiny food while pushing water out. This is similar to how some whales feed."],["Standing on one leg","Flamingos often rest standing on one leg. A 2017 study found that their body structure lets them balance on one leg with almost no muscle effort, even when asleep. Tucking one leg up may also help them stay warm."],["Living in flocks","Flamingos are very social and live in flocks of dozens to many thousands. Large groups perform synchronized displays, marching, head-flagging and wing-saluting together, which help them pair up for breeding."]],
k:[["Number of species","Six"],["Color source","Carotenoid pigments in food"],["Feeding","Filtering food with an upside-down bill"],["Habitat","Shallow salty or alkaline lakes and lagoons"],["Group name","A flamboyance"]],
d:[["c. 30 million years ago","Early flamingo relatives appear"],["1811","The greater flamingo is scientifically named"],["1933","A greater flamingo arrives at Adelaide Zoo, Australia"],["1950s","Lesser flamingo breeding at Lake Natron is documented"],["2014","The Adelaide Zoo flamingo dies, aged over 80"],["2017","A study explains how flamingos balance on one leg"]],
s2:[["The six species","The six species are the greater, lesser, Chilean, Caribbean or American, Andean and James’s flamingos. The greater flamingo is the largest and most widespread, found in Africa, southern Europe and Asia. The lesser flamingo, the smallest, is the most numerous."],["Lake Natron","Most of the world’s lesser flamingos breed at Lake Natron in Tanzania, a lake so salty and alkaline that it can burn the skin of other animals. Few predators can reach the flamingos’ nests on its mudflats. Plans for industry at the lake have worried conservationists."],["Raising chicks","Flamingos build mud-mound nests and usually lay one egg. Both parents feed the chick a red liquid called crop milk produced in their throats. After a few weeks, chicks gather in large groups called crèches, watched over by a few adults."],["Andean flamingos","Three species, the Andean, James’s and Chilean flamingos, live on high salt lakes in the Andes Mountains of South America, at altitudes of up to about 4,500 meters, surviving freezing nights and thin air."],["Threats","Flamingos depend on a small number of special wetlands, which are threatened by mining, water diversion, pollution and climate change. Some species are near threatened. Protecting their lakes is key to their survival."],["Flamingos and people","Flamingos have long fascinated people. They appear in ancient Egyptian writing, and the flamingo is the national bird of the Bahamas. Plastic pink lawn flamingos, first made in the United States in 1957, became famous decorations."]],
sp:["Greater, the very old flamingo","In 1933 a greater flamingo arrived at Adelaide Zoo in Australia. Its exact age was unknown, but it was already an adult. Known simply as Greater, it lived at the zoo for more than 80 years, outliving its companions and becoming a local celebrity. When it died in 2014, it was believed to be at least 83 years old, making it one of the oldest known flamingos. Wild flamingos often live 20 to 30 years or more."],
g:[["Carotenoids","Natural pigments that give red, orange and yellow colors"],["Filter feeding","Straining food from water"],["Crop milk","A liquid food made in the throat of some birds"],["Flamboyance","The name for a group of flamingos"]],
f:["There are six species of flamingos.","Their pink color comes from their food.","Most lesser flamingos breed at Lake Natron."],
fun:"A group of flamingos is called a flamboyance.",
tl:"Ang flamingo ay matangkad na ibong may kulay rosas na balahibo. Ang kulay nito ay galing sa algae at maliliit na hipon na kinakain nito. Kumakain ito nang nakabaligtad ang ulo."});

ADDART({id:"locusts",t:"Locusts",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦗",w:"Locust",r:["insect","agri","grasslands","kenyacountry","climchange"],
p:[["Grasshoppers that swarm","Locusts are grasshoppers that can change form and gather into huge swarms."],["A farmer’s fear","Swarms can destroy crops over vast areas in days."],["An ancient plague","Locust plagues have been recorded for thousands of years."]],
l:"Locusts are species of grasshoppers that, under certain conditions, change their behavior and appearance and gather in enormous swarms. A single swarm can contain billions of insects, cover hundreds of square kilometers and travel great distances, eating crops and pastures and threatening the food supply of millions of people. Locust plagues have been recorded since ancient times, in Egypt, China and the Bible. Today the desert locust of Africa, the Middle East and South Asia is the most dangerous, and countries cooperate to monitor and control it.",
s:[["Two forms","Most of the time, locusts live alone, like ordinary grasshoppers, in a solitary phase. When rain brings plenty of plants and the population grows, crowding causes them to change into the gregarious phase: they become brightly colored, more active and attracted to each other. Scientists found that the chemical serotonin in their nervous system triggers this change."],["Hoppers and swarms","Young locusts, called hoppers, cannot fly. In the gregarious phase they march together in bands. When they become winged adults, they form swarms that fly with the wind, sometimes more than 100 kilometers a day."],["How much they eat","Each locust eats roughly its own weight in plants every day. A swarm of one square kilometer may contain 40 to 80 million locusts and can eat as much food in a day as tens of thousands of people."],["The desert locust","The desert locust lives in dry regions from West Africa to India. During quiet periods it stays in deserts, but after unusual rains, plagues can spread across dozens of countries, affecting a fifth of the Earth’s land surface."]],
k:[["What they are","Grasshoppers that swarm"],["Phases","Solitary and gregarious"],["Most dangerous species","Desert locust"],["Swarm size","Up to billions of insects"],["Monitoring","FAO Desert Locust Information Service"]],
d:[["c. 1400 BCE","Locust plagues are recorded in ancient Egypt"],["1875","A huge Rocky Mountain locust swarm crosses the United States"],["1902","The last Rocky Mountain locust is seen alive"],["1921","Scientists discover that locusts change phases"],["2009","Serotonin is shown to trigger swarming"],["2019–2021","A major desert locust upsurge hits East Africa and South Asia"]],
s2:[["The East African outbreak","From 2019 to 2021, heavy rains from cyclones caused desert locusts to multiply, and swarms spread across East Africa, the Arabian Peninsula and South Asia. Kenya suffered its worst outbreak in about 70 years. International efforts, including spraying and monitoring, helped limit the damage."],["Control","The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations runs the Desert Locust Information Service, which uses satellites, field reports and weather data to forecast outbreaks. Teams spray pesticides or natural fungal treatments on hopper bands before they can fly."],["Locusts in the Philippines","The Philippines has its own locust, the Oriental migratory locust, known locally as balang. Outbreaks have damaged rice, corn and sugarcane fields, especially in Mindanao and the Visayas. The Department of Agriculture monitors and controls outbreaks."],["The vanished locust","In 1875 a swarm of Rocky Mountain locusts crossing the western United States was estimated to cover an area larger than the state of California, possibly the largest swarm ever recorded. Yet by 1902 the species was extinct, possibly because farming destroyed the river valleys where it laid its eggs."],["Locusts as food","Locusts and grasshoppers are eaten in many parts of the world, including Africa, the Middle East, Asia and Mexico. They are rich in protein. In the Philippines, fried balang has been eaten in some provinces, and the Bible mentions John the Baptist eating locusts and honey."],["Climate change","Scientists warn that changing rainfall patterns, more frequent cyclones and warmer temperatures could make locust outbreaks more common or spread them to new areas, adding to threats to food security."]],
sp:["Albert’s swarm","In June 1875, Dr. Albert Child, a weather observer in Nebraska in the United States, watched a swarm of Rocky Mountain locusts pass overhead for days. Using telegraph reports and his own observations, he estimated that it was about 2,900 kilometers long and 180 kilometers wide, containing perhaps trillions of insects. It may have been the largest gathering of animals ever recorded. Remarkably, less than 30 years later, the Rocky Mountain locust had vanished entirely."],
g:[["Swarm","A large group of insects moving together"],["Gregarious","Living in groups"],["Hopper","A young locust that cannot yet fly"],["Food security","Having reliable access to enough food"]],
f:["Locusts are a kind of grasshopper.","Serotonin triggers locust swarming.","In the Philippines, locusts are called balang."],
fun:"The Rocky Mountain locust once formed perhaps the largest swarm ever recorded, then went extinct.",
tl:"Ang balang ay uri ng tipaklong na maaaring magkumpol sa napakalalaking kawan. Kayang sirain ng mga kawan ang mga pananim sa malalawak na lugar. Ang desert locust ang pinakamapanganib na uri sa Aprika at Asya."});

ADDART({id:"fireflies",t:"Fireflies",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"✨",w:"Firefly",r:["beetle","insect","wetlands","ph","conservation"],
p:[["Living lights","Fireflies are beetles that produce their own light."],["Flashing messages","They flash patterns of light to find mates."],["Trees of light","In Southeast Asia, thousands of fireflies flash together in mangrove trees."]],
l:"Fireflies, also called lightning bugs, are beetles that produce light from special organs in their abdomens through a chemical reaction called bioluminescence. There are about 2,000 species, found on every continent except Antarctica, especially in warm, humid places. Fireflies use flashing patterns to attract mates, and in parts of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, thousands of males gather in mangrove trees and flash in perfect unison. Fireflies are declining in many places because of light pollution, habitat loss and pesticides.",
s:[["Making light","Firefly light is made when a chemical called luciferin reacts with oxygen, helped by an enzyme called luciferase. The reaction is extremely efficient, producing almost no heat, which is why firefly light is called cold light. Fireflies control their flashing by adjusting the oxygen supply to their light organs."],["Talking with light","Each species has its own pattern of flashes, varying in color, length and timing. Males usually fly and flash while females watch from the ground or plants and flash back to signal interest. Firefly light can be yellow, green or pale orange."],["Life cycle","Fireflies spend most of their lives as larvae, living for a year or more in soil, leaf litter or water. Larvae also glow and are sometimes called glowworms. They eat snails, slugs and worms. Adults live only a few weeks, and many do not eat at all."],["Femme fatales","Females of some American Photuris fireflies imitate the flash patterns of other species. When males of those species approach, the females catch and eat them, gaining defensive chemicals from their prey."]],
k:[["Type of animal","Beetle"],["Number of species","About 2,000"],["Light reaction","Luciferin, luciferase and oxygen"],["Filipino name","Alitaptap"],["Main threats","Light pollution, habitat loss, pesticides"]],
d:[["1600s","Travelers describe synchronous fireflies in Southeast Asia"],["1885","Raphaël Dubois names luciferin and luciferase"],["1935","Scientists report on synchronized flashing in Southeast Asia"],["1986","Scientists insert the firefly light gene into a plant"],["2020","A study identifies the main threats to fireflies worldwide"],["2020s","Firefly tourism grows in Asia and the Americas"]],
s2:[["Synchronous fireflies","In mangrove swamps of Southeast Asia, males of Pteroptyx fireflies gather in thousands on single trees and flash together in perfect rhythm, making the trees pulse with light like a beating heart. Similar synchronized displays occur in the Great Smoky Mountains of the United States. Scientists study how the insects coordinate their timing."],["Fireflies in the Philippines","Filipinos call fireflies alitaptap. Firefly-watching tours along mangrove rivers, such as the Iwahig River in Palawan, the Donsol River in Sorsogon and the Abatan River in Bohol, attract visitors. The fireflies depend on healthy mangroves, which also protect coasts and fisheries."],["Why fireflies are declining","A 2020 study found that the greatest threats to fireflies are habitat loss, artificial light at night, which drowns out their signals, and pesticides. In some places, firefly tourism itself can harm them if boats, flashlights and trampling disturb their habitats."],["Fireflies in science","The firefly’s luciferase enzyme is widely used in medical and biological research. Scientists attach the firefly light gene to other genes to see when they are active, and luciferase tests can detect tiny amounts of bacteria and test drugs."],["Fireflies in culture","In Japan, firefly viewing, called hotaru-gari, is a summer tradition celebrated in poems and art. Fireflies appear in Filipino songs and stories, such as tales of fireflies leading lost travelers. For many people, fireflies are a cherished symbol of summer nights and childhood."],["Helping fireflies","People can help fireflies by turning off unnecessary outdoor lights, leaving natural areas and leaf litter in gardens, avoiding pesticides and protecting wetlands and mangroves. Communities in some countries have created firefly sanctuaries."]],
sp:["The glowing tobacco plant","In 1986, scientists at the University of California, San Diego, took the gene that lets fireflies make luciferase and inserted it into a tobacco plant. When the plant was watered with a solution containing luciferin, it glowed faintly in the dark. The famous photograph of the glowing plant appeared in the journal Science and showed that genes from one living thing could work in a completely different one. Today, the firefly gene is one of the most widely used tools in laboratories around the world."],
g:[["Bioluminescence","Light produced by a living thing"],["Luciferin","The chemical that produces light in fireflies"],["Luciferase","The enzyme that helps luciferin produce light"],["Larva","The young, wingless stage of an insect"]],
f:["Fireflies are beetles.","Their light produces almost no heat.","Filipinos call fireflies alitaptap."],
fun:"In Southeast Asian mangroves, thousands of fireflies flash together in perfect rhythm.",
tl:"Ang alitaptap ay salagubang na gumagawa ng sariling liwanag. Nagpapakislap ito upang makahanap ng kapareha. Sa mga bakawan sa Pilipinas, libu-libong alitaptap ang sabay-sabay na kumikislap."});

;
